#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""claude-accounts — cross-account Claude Max usage dashboard, auth-state prober + work router.

Successor to ~/.reso/bin/reso-quota (2026-07-04), promoted into claude-infrastructure with the
2026-07-10 research fixes. Reads the account map from ~/.claude/accounts.json (SSOT — launchers,
config dirs, emails, Dia profiles) and, per account: auth state from the macOS Keychain, live
limits from api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage, live session concurrency from ps. Routes work with
the adversarially-verified scorer (use-it-or-lose-it x Fable-sub-cap coupling x 5h-safety x
rate-spread), with the Fable window read LIVE from ~/.claude/model-config.yaml frontier_access
(never a hardcoded date — the reso-quota JUL7 bug: the operator extended the window Jul-9 and the
hardcode silently killed all Fable routing).

Network policy (read this before adding a call):
  - The usage sweep is `/api/oauth/usage` and nothing else. It reports each window as an INTEGER
    `percent` that ROUNDS UP and CLAMPS at 100, so `weekly 100%` is one rendering of three
    different states (99% used and allowed · exactly full · 102% used and rejected).
  - The WIRE (`anthropic-ratelimit-unified-*` response headers) carries the unclamped fraction and
    the server's own allowed/allowed_warning/rejected verdict, and it rides ONLY on `/v1/messages`
    — `count_tokens` carries none (measured). So this tool DOES make one minimal inference call
    (`max_tokens: 1`, body discarded) per account, but only where the integer is ambiguous
    (>= WIRE_NEAR_WALL), or on `--wire`. That is the tool's only inference, ~25 tokens against a
    weekly pp worth ~500k, and `CC_ACCOUNTS_WIRE=off` removes it. See the fetch_wire_limits block.

Auth policy (rotation-safe by construction):
  - NEVER mints or persists tokens itself. Staleness is read from the keychain payload's
    expiresAt (no network needed).
  - Healing a stale/expired token is delegated to the official binary: headless
    `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=... claude auth login`, which performs the refresh grant AND
    persists whatever (possibly rotated) tokens come back to the same keychain item. Heal runs
    ONLY when the account has zero live sessions (no rotation race with a running CC) and under a
    per-account lock. reso-quota's refresh-and-discard pattern is retired: refresh tokens MAY
    rotate (GH anthropics/claude-code#54443, unanswered) and a discarded rotation = logged out.
  - "logged-out" is only declared after a fresh keychain re-read (no item, or the binary's heal
    reports an invalid refresh token).
  - The LOGIN CLIFF (refreshTokenExpiresAt) is surfaced with lead time, not discovered on
    impact. A refresh grant renews the ACCESS token but does NOT extend the refresh token's
    expiry, so every account walks a hard, roughly-monthly, perfectly-predictable deadline at
    which only an interactive /login recovers it. Past it, healing cannot succeed by
    construction (invalid_grant), so the heal is skipped rather than burned. See login_warn_h
    in the SSOT.
  - Token-exposure note (decided trade-off): access tokens travel only in-process (urllib
    header — never curl argv). The ONE exception is the heal child's env
    (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN), visible to same-user `ps -E` for the <=90s heal — the
    official binary's only headless-auth contract, and equivalent exposure to the same user's
    existing keychain read access.

Usage:
  claude-accounts                     human table + route hints
  claude-accounts --json              machine-readable (rows + window + scores; tokens redacted)
  claude-accounts --wire              force the WIRE read on every account, not only the ones
      at the wall. The `/api/oauth/usage` percent is an INTEGER that rounds UP and clamps at 100,
      so `weekly 100%` is one rendering of three different states — 99% used and `allowed`,
      exactly full, and 102% used and `rejected`. The `anthropic-ratelimit-unified-*` response
      headers carry the unclamped fraction AND the server's own allowed/allowed_warning/rejected
      verdict, so they are what the router and the table use whenever present; each account at
      100% gets a sentence saying whether the server confirmed it is out.
      Cost: ONE `max_tokens: 1` call per account (~25 tokens; 1 weekly pp is ~500k). Un-forced it
      fires only at/above WIRE_NEAR_WALL, where the integer is ambiguous. Kill: CC_ACCOUNTS_WIRE=off.
  claude-accounts --readout           the /accounts chat readout, as finished markdown: the router
      headline, a fenced board-style grid, glyph-led flag lines (a Skipped line names any GENERAL-
      router exclusion no other line covers), then a drain line, a quota read-time line (with the Fable
      window only when it is news) and a provider summary (unless --no-agents; --agents prints
      the full table).
      THE renderer for that surface — paste it, do not rebuild it. Every formatting rule
      (column set, two-unit countdowns, the ➤ route marker, ⚠ on a login inside login_warn_h,
      `*` on inherited numbers, flag severity order) lives here in code, so it can be tested
      and cannot drift from the CLI table. commands/accounts.md keeps only the judgment:
      how to INTERPRET a stale row, an exclusion, or a cliff.
  claude-accounts --readout --narrow  the same readout at <=76 cols, for the SessionStart board.
      A MODE on the same renderer, never a second table: the board must not be able to claim a
      different pick, staleness or cliff than /accounts does. Written to disk (board_file, default
      alongside cache_file) by --keepwarm; read by hooks/accounts-board.sh, which never runs this
      program — --readout measured 5.33s, over a hook's 5s timeout.
  claude-accounts --agents            NON-Claude agent backends only (registry:
      ~/.claude/providers.json). Needs no keychain read, no usage sweep and no lock, so it
      answers "what else can I route to" without paying for a 4-account sweep. Add --json for
      the same rows as data. Three INDEPENDENT facts per backend — installed / authenticated /
      has a headless agent mode — because only the third decides routability, and a presence
      check silently skips it (an editor launcher on PATH is not an agent backend).
  claude-accounts --stranded [ACCT] [--hours N] [--json]  how much weekly limit was LEFT ON THE
      TABLE: every closed weekly window (default last 6 weeks), newest first, per account — final
      %, pp stranded, and when it reached 100% — plus a per-account total. ACCT is
      next|next2|next3|next4 or 1-4. History only, like --strand-score below.
  claude-accounts --strand-score [--hours N] [--json]  score the weekly-drain NOWCAST against
      what each completed weekly window actually stranded, stratified by how far from the reset
      the projection was made (96/48/24/12/6h). History only — reads the utilization series and
      nothing else, so it needs no keychain, no sweep and no accounts.json. THE POINT IS THAT IT
      CAN COME OUT BADLY: the score it replaces evaluated the projector at the horizon's close,
      where the arithmetic has already converged to the truth, and duly scored 8/8.
  claude-accounts --route general|fable|interactive   best account name (bare, on stdout)
      general      dispatch lane: headroom/T**2, deadline-DOMINANT — spend quota before it strands.
      interactive  a HUMAN's long-lived desk: a TWO-KEY rule — among 5h-safe accounts, take the
                   EARLIEST WEEKLY RESET — and the login cliff is never yielded. It is the same
                   spend-before-it-strands objective as `general`, with a 5-hour guard in front of
                   it; the lanes differ in the shape of that guard, not in their direction.
  claude-accounts --rank general|fable|interactive   ranked "name score" lines for wave spread
      (handoff). `interactive` is a real lane here and ranks by the desk rule above — it was
      absent from this line while being the lane a reader comparing the two would reach for.
  claude-accounts --assign ACCT [--src S] record one fire-time assignment (M7). Called by the
      consumer that COMMITS to launching a NEW session (handoff-fire, post-pick): the account
      is charged one phantom working session for ASSIGN_TTL_MIN, so fires inside the 90s cache
      TTL walk DOWN the ranking instead of stacking on rank[0]. Pure ledger append — never
      sweeps, never blocks a fire (callers `|| true`). Unknown account exits 64: a misspelled
      charge must never be a silent no-op the caller believes spread the load.
      v2 (lr-reconciler), all optional: --id ID (rows sharing an id charge ONCE, newest wins)
      --sid S (not charged once the account's census counts that session) --w N (weight,
      default 1) --ttl-s T (this row's lifetime; default ASSIGN_TTL_MIN). Bad value exits 64.
  claude-accounts --assign-many FILE  JSONL of {acct,id,sid,w,ttl_s}: every line validated
      first (one bad line ⇒ exit 64, nothing written), then ONE locked append, one shared t.
  claude-accounts --unassign ID       append {t,ts,void:ID}: that id stops charging at once.
      Both print the EXCLUDED accounts and why on stderr, on every invocation — a winner is
      "best of what we could see", and a transient throttle silently removes a candidate.
      Exit: 0 routable · 2 data was fine but nothing routable by POLICY (exhausted / 5h
      cutoff / window — callers must NOT fire blind) · 3 data unavailable for every account
      (callers may degrade to a proxy). A non-zero exit is an ANSWER, not a tool failure.
  claude-accounts --login-status [--window-h N]   accounts needing an interactive /login, now or soon.
      TSV: acct, REQUIRED|EXPIRING, reason, expiry as UTC ISO (2026-08-02T20:21:49Z),
      remaining as a bare decimal hour count (70.8), launcher — the last three are "—"
      when the verdict is not driven by the deadline. Fields 4 and 5 are MACHINE forms and
      stay that way: this surface is for hooks and pollers, and a consumer that has to
      parse a human rendering is coupled to how the table happens to look today.
      The EXIT CODE is the answer, so a hook can gate without parsing:
      0 all clear · 1 a login expires within the window · 2 action required NOW.
      --window-h N overrides login_warn_h FOR THIS CALL ONLY (default: login_warn_h, so every
      existing caller is byte-identical). It exists because this surface is a pre-FILTERED view
      and its consumer's policy is not this constant: cc-relogin-poll attempts from T−7d
      (168h) precisely because cc-relogin needs a k==0 moment and a 7-day window turns one
      fragile chance into ~168 hourly ones — yet reading a 72h-filtered view capped it at 72h,
      making 96 of its 168 declared hours structurally unreachable. It also produced a WRONG
      HUMAN verdict: RELOGIN_AUTOMATION_PLAN.md concluded "the cliff is closed until roughly
      2026-08-23" off this surface's exit 0 while `next` was 8 days from its cliff.
      A malformed N exits 64 (usage), deliberately OUTSIDE the 0/1/2 verdict range — a caller
      that asked for 168 must never silently receive a 72h answer, and must never read a usage
      error as a verdict.
      Disambiguator: this tool's own hard failures (unreadable SSOT, bad router block) also
      exit 1, via sys.exit("message") — but they print NOTHING on stdout and a diagnostic on
      stderr, whereas exit 1 here always prints at least one TSV line. So "exit 1 with empty
      stdout" is a tool failure, not an expiring login. The collision is fail-SAFE either way:
      only exit 0 means all-clear, and no failure path can produce it.
  claude-accounts --relogin-info NAME     JSON identity block for the account-relogin skill
  claude-accounts --relogin-status        per-account login-renewal board (+ --json)
      One row per account: state (OK|DUE|ESCALATED|UNKNOWN) · login_expires_at · hours · the
      last cc-relogin attempt (from the poller's log) · the exact next command. Cache-first
      and NEVER heals — a recurring check must not sweep both oauth endpoints every tick.
      TWO WINDOWS, ONE FACT — do not confuse this with --login-status above. That one answers
      "how much lead time does a HUMAN need" and is gated at login_warn_h (72h). This one
      answers "when should the unattended executor start trying" and is gated at the
      RENEWAL-ATTEMPT window, accounts.json `relogin_trigger_h` (168h) — wider because
      cc-relogin can only act at a k==0 moment and needs ~168 hourly chances at one. So an
      account can be DUE here and absent from --login-status; that is the two surfaces
      answering their own questions, not a contradiction.
      DUE IS A PROMISE: `next_action` is only printed for a band cc-relogin will ACCEPT.
      That is why the window is SSOT'd rather than owned here — when it was a local constant
      the two drifted (2026-07-29) and this board printed "NEXT ACTION: cc-relogin next" for
      72-168h while that command exited 2 REFUSED "no re-auth needed". The agreement is
      re-proved by EXECUTING this field in tests/cc-relogin-status.bats, not by asserting a
      number in two places.
      Exit: 0 all clear · 1 a renewal is DUE · 2 ESCALATED/overdue · 3 DETECTION-UNAVAILABLE.
      Version tolerance: the login_expires_* fields are not on every build, and a build that
      cannot see them reports UNKNOWN + a loud stderr line — never a confident wrong "OK".
  claude-accounts --fresh             bypass the shared cache
  claude-accounts --no-heal           never run the headless heal (pure read-only)
  claude-accounts --max-wait S        wall-clock ceiling for producing an answer. `--max-wait 0`
      is CACHE-ONLY: serve the cache or ABSTAIN (exit 3) — never take the single-flight lock,
      never sweep, never heal. This is the interactive-launcher contract: without it a launch can
      inherit the sweeper's tail, whose documented worst case is minutes and whose floor behind a
      wedged holder is 240s then exit 5. Composes with --route/--rank/--json.
      `--max-wait N` (N>0) is a REAL bound: a cache inside TTL is served; else the lock is tried
      until N, a miss serves the grace cache or exits 3, and a won sweep past N exits 3.
  claude-accounts --max-age S         staleness ceiling for a served cache (default cache_ttl_s).
      600 is free by measurement: at <=10 min staleness the stale pick is now-excluded in 0.8% of
      pairs (median score-ratio 1.000), and across 931 recorded routing decisions the grace band
      has never once been entered (max observed quota_age_s = 89).
      Malformed value on either flag ⇒ exit 64. Absent ⇒ unchanged behaviour.
  claude-accounts --reset-report [--days N]
                                      every limit reset the sweep detected in the last N days
      (default 30) — scheduled or banked, with no flag — one row each (lag, channel usage|wire,
      throttled polls and wire attempts it took), then median/max lag per kind and channel.
      Reads ~/.claude/logs/quota-reset-detect.jsonl. No network.
  claude-accounts --note-bank ACCT --left N --expires ISO [--source WHO]
                                      record a read of ACCT's banked limit resets (the CLI cannot
      read the bank; check it at clau.de/reset). ISO must carry an offset. Writes
      ~/.claude/state/limit-bank.json. While a grant is live and expires before ACCT's weekly
      reset, every lane ranks ACCT by the grant's expiry instead (effective_reset_h); an unscheduled
      weekly reset on ACCT is read as a redemption and decrements resets_left. N=0 or a past
      expiry is ignored by every reader.
  claude-accounts --bank-wall         accounts at their weekly wall that hold a live grant, one
      TAB-separated `acct key sentence` line each (autonomy-sweep pages each key once).
  claude-accounts --no-color         plain output (also: NO_COLOR env, pipes, TERM=dumb;
                                      FORCE_COLOR=1 forces color for screenshots)

Shared cache: /tmp/claude-accounts-cache.json (default TTL 90s, flock single-flight) so N
concurrent callers (handoff waves, statuslines, sessions) produce one endpoint sweep, not N —
both oauth endpoints rate-limit under load.
"""
import fcntl
import glob
import gzip
import json
import math
import os
import random
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import textwrap
import threading
import time
import traceback
import unicodedata
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor

# --max-wait's clock starts at PROCESS start on the CLI path, not inside get_data: under load the
# interpreter start and this module's import alone measured 0.7-1.15 s, so a bound started later
# overran its own ceiling by that much (W1 case j). main() sets _CLI; importers keep call-time.
_PROC_T0 = time.monotonic()
_CLI = False
from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
from hashlib import sha256

# ---- concurrency guards for the 5-way sweep (collect()) ---------------------------------------
# collect() fans the four account probes plus working_concurrency out over a thread pool. Every
# race those threads can reach is INTRA-PROCESS and new — collect() already runs under the
# process-level single-flight flock in get_data(), so nothing here is a cross-process guard.
#
# _REJECTED_LOCK guards the rejected-grant ledger's whole load -> mutate -> save, which is a
# non-atomic read-modify-write on ONE shared file: two threads marking DIFFERENT accounts both
# read the pre-state and the last writer replays it, silently dropping the other's record. That
# record is what suppresses a 90s re-heal of a grant already proven dead (see _load_rejected),
# so losing it costs a wasted heal per sweep, forever, on an account nothing can fix but /login.
#
# _HEAL_GATE serialises `claude auth login`. Mechanically the per-account heals look independent
# (per-account flock, per-account CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, per-account keychain item, and heal's
# rotation gate attributes on exactly that env var) — but it is an opaque third-party binary and
# its shared-state footprint OUTSIDE CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is unverified. Unverified is not safe, and
# the hedge is free: any sweep that heals is already >=90s, so the fast path never touches this.
_REJECTED_LOCK = threading.Lock()
_HEAL_GATE = threading.Semaphore(1)

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
CFG_PATH = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON", os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "accounts.json"))
# Event log. Env-overridable on the SAME discipline as the ledger below, and for the same reason:
# a module-level constant computed from HOME is not redirectable by a caller that has already
# redirected the SSOT, so every suite that exercises an error path wrote its fixtures into the real
# operator log. Measured 2026-09-11: 982 lines of `RuntimeError: simulated keychain explosion` — a
# TEST fixture (tests/claude-accounts-core.bats "collect: one account raising does not blank the
# others") in ~/.claude/logs/claude-accounts.log, plus ~250 keychain timeouts and 615 `ps
# UNAVAILABLE` rows of mixed provenance. That log is READ as evidence about the fleet, so a fixture
# in it is not noise, it is false testimony: 145 wave-plan passes were lost reasoning over it
# (docs/research/cloud-lane-redesign-2026-09-10/C3-local-refusals.md §7).
#
# The previous containment was per-call — `render_limit_gaps()` gates its own log line and says so
# ("LOG_PATH resolves at import; only some suites redirect it") — which fixes one emitter and
# leaves every other one leaking. Redirect the PATH and the whole class goes with it.
def _log_path():
    explicit = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_LOG")
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    logs = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "logs")
    # Same shape as _lastgood_path(): a scratch SSOT gets a sibling keyed on the config it points
    # at, and the DEFAULT keeps the unchanged path on purpose — deriving it unconditionally would
    # orphan the accumulated operator log the day an account is added or the SSOT moves.
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON"):
        h = sha256(os.path.realpath(CFG_PATH).encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
        return os.path.join(logs, f"claude-accounts-{h}.log")
    return os.path.join(logs, "claude-accounts.log")


LOG_PATH = _log_path()
# Last-good quota ledger: TTL-free, per-account, persisted OUTSIDE /tmp so a reboot/tmp-sweep
# never strands the last-known numbers. Env-overridable (mirrors CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON) so tests
# and scratch configs never touch the real ledger.
def _lastgood_path():
    explicit = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_LASTGOOD")
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    logs = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "logs")
    # CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_LASTGOOD is an INDEPENDENT variable that CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON does not
    # imply, so a scratch/test SSOT that overrode only the config still read and wrote the
    # REAL ledger — the cache is protected from exactly this by its cfg_key guard. Derive a
    # sibling ledger in that case. The default SSOT keeps the unchanged path on purpose:
    # deriving it unconditionally would orphan the whole accumulated ledger the day an
    # account is added or the SSOT moves, blanking "last-known" precisely when it matters.
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON"):
        h = sha256(os.path.realpath(CFG_PATH).encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
        return os.path.join(logs, f"claude-accounts-lastgood-{h}.json")
    return os.path.join(logs, "claude-accounts-lastgood.json")


LASTGOOD_PATH = _lastgood_path()


def log_event(msg):
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(LOG_PATH), exist_ok=True)
        with open(LOG_PATH, "a") as f:
            f.write(f"{datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()} {msg}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


def resolve_claude_bin(fallback):
    """The eval binary comes from bin/cc-claude-bin, never from a copy of the pin.

    accounts.json's `claude_bin` used to BE the answer, and on 2026-08-09 it was measured
    wrong: it pinned ~/.claude-219 (2.1.219) while `claude()` in ~/.zshrc launched
    ~/.claude-220. Four call sites read it — probe_account()'s certification, heal()'s
    headless grant, and cc-relogin's two login legs — so account certification and the
    relogin gate were both describing a build no session runs. That is verbatim the
    recurrence scripts/handoff-fire.sh:225-228 documents from the ~/.claude-183-vs-219 week:
    a hardcoded path CANNOT hold this invariant, because the party holding it is the one
    party that cannot detect the drift.

    So the literal is demoted to a FALLBACK for a stripped environment (no resolver on
    disk, a hermetic $HOME with no ~/.zshrc). The resolver is fail-closed and prints
    nothing on failure, so an empty result falls through to the literal rather than
    silently blanking the key -- every consumer here already handles a missing binary with
    a message, and none of them can handle an empty path.

    tests/account-fact-derivation.bats asserts this agrees with bin/cc-claude-bin and with
    scripts/handoff-fire.sh, which is the whole point: one derivation, checked.
    """
    override = os.environ.get("CC_CLAUDE_BIN")
    if override:
        return os.path.expanduser(override)
    # __file__ is UNDEFINED when this source is executed via `python3 -c` — which is exactly how
    # tests/claude-accounts-core.bats drives the --login-status e2e cases (it string-patches the
    # source and re-executes it). Reaching for it unguarded raised NameError inside load_cfg,
    # i.e. every invocation on that path died with an empty stdout. globals().get, not a bare
    # reference, and the live layer as the second candidate.
    here = globals().get("__file__")
    cands = []
    if here:
        cands.append(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(here)), "cc-claude-bin"))
    cands.append(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/bin/cc-claude-bin"))
    for resolver in cands:
        if not os.access(resolver, os.X_OK):
            continue
        try:
            p = subprocess.run([resolver], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            continue
        derived = (p.stdout or "").strip()
        if p.returncode == 0 and derived:
            return derived
    return fallback


def _num_flag(args, name):
    """Non-negative float value of a positional flag, or None when absent.

    Exit 64 (usage) on a missing or malformed value rather than defaulting: `--route fabel`
    already taught this file that a silently-defaulted flag value hands the caller a confident
    wrong answer. A bound the caller believes it set, and did not, is worse than no bound."""
    if name not in args:
        return None
    i = args.index(name)
    raw = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
    try:
        v = float(raw)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        print(f"claude-accounts: {name} requires a number of seconds (got {raw!r})",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(64)
    if v < 0:
        print(f"claude-accounts: {name} must be >= 0 (got {v})", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(64)
    return v


def _identity_module():
    """hooks/lib/identity.py from this checkout (bin/ is symlinked, so resolve through the link),
    else from the live layer. None when neither exists — personal fields then stay absent and
    probe rows degrade to blank labels, never to a crash."""
    import importlib.util
    me = globals().get("__file__")  # absent when a suite execs this source via `python3 -c`
    cands = [os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(me)), "..", "hooks", "lib", "identity.py")] if me else []
    cands.append(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/hooks/lib/identity.py"))
    for cand in cands:
        if os.path.isfile(cand):
            spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("cc_identity", cand)
            mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
            try:
                spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
            except Exception:  # a broken helper must not take the dashboard down
                return None
            return mod
    return None


def load_cfg(need_claude_bin=True):
    try:
        with open(CFG_PATH) as f:
            cfg = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:
        sys.exit(f"claude-accounts: cannot read SSOT {CFG_PATH}: {e}")
    # Personal fields (email, mailbox, dia_profile, keychain_account) live in the gitignored
    # identity overlay, not in this public repo's accounts.json — merged here, filling only what
    # the SSOT row lacks. A synthetic CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON fixture is never mixed with the real
    # overlay unless the suite also sets CC_IDENTITY_FILE (hooks/lib/identity.py).
    _ident = _identity_module()
    if _ident is not None and not _ident.overlay_suppressed("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON"):
        _ident.merge_accounts(cfg)
    for a in cfg["accounts"]:
        a["config_dir"] = os.path.expanduser(a["config_dir"])
    for k in ("claude_bin", "model_config_ssot", "dia_local_state"):
        cfg[k] = os.path.expanduser(cfg[k])
    # resolve_claude_bin() forks bin/cc-claude-bin (subprocess.run, timeout=10) and its only
    # consumers are heal()'s headless grant and probe_account()'s certification — both sweep-path.
    # A cache-only read (--max-wait 0) can never reach either, so paying the fork there is ~16 ms
    # of waste AND, worse, a 10 s hang surface on the one path this file advertises as
    # NON-BLOCKING. Skipped there; the SSOT literal rides through unresolved, which is safe
    # precisely because nothing on that path reads it.
    if need_claude_bin:
        cfg["claude_bin"] = resolve_claude_bin(cfg["claude_bin"])
    _validate_router(cfg)
    return cfg


ROUTER_KEYS = ("S_CUT", "S_SOFT", "SF_FLOOR", "KMAX", "KFLOOR", "MARGIN_H", "EPS_H",
               "WEEKLY_FLOOR", "FABLE_FLOOR", "JB_BONUS")

# The desk lane's constants (DESK_ROUTER_AND_STARTUP_V1 W1) are VALIDATED but deliberately not in
# ROUTER_KEYS. That tuple is a REQUIRED list — a key missing from it is a sys.exit inside every
# consumer of this tool at once — and ~/.claude/accounts.json is a symlink into a checkout that is
# older than the code for as long as a land takes to converge, so a required-key addition fails
# CLOSED across the whole fleet during exactly that window. M7's own additions (URGENCY_EXP,
# KWORK_WINDOW_MIN, ASSIGN_TTL_MIN, PROJ_LOOKAHEAD_H) are absent from ROUTER_KEYS for that reason
# and ride R.get() defaults. So: optional PRESENCE, range-checked when present — which is strictly
# more than ROUTER_KEYS does for most of its own keys, and it catches the plausible typo here: a
# floor written as 60 instead of 0.60 (a fraction entered as a percentage) would silently make
# every account 5h-safe forever, i.e. delete the guard while reading as a configured one.
ROUTER_OPTIONAL_RANGES = {
    "DESK_5H_FLOOR": (0.0, 1.0),        # projected 5h utilisation must be BELOW this to be safe
    "DESK_W_FLOOR": (0.0, 1.0),         # weekly headroom must be AT/ABOVE this to be safe
    "DESK_W_FLOOR_FULL_H": (0.0, 168.0),  # hours-to-reset at which DESK_W_FLOOR applies in full
    "DESK_HYST_MARGIN": (0.0, 1.0),     # fraction a rival must beat the incumbent by
    "DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN": (0.0, 10080.0),  # how long a launcher assignment names the incumbent
    "KMAX_RESIDENT": (1.0, 10000.0),    # resident-session cap (see k_cap); KMAX is the ACTIVE one
    "RECOVERY_W_FLOOR": (0.0, 1.0),     # weekly headroom a recovery target must still hold
    "RECOVERY_S_CEIL": (0.0, 1.0),      # projected 5h utilisation a recovery target must be UNDER
    "RECOVERY_F_FLOOR": (0.0, 1.0),     # f_eff a fable recovery target must still hold
    "DISPATCH_W_FLOOR": (0.0, 1.0),     # weekly headroom under which a dispatch pick is THIN
    # The strand rescue's three (2026-10-09, see dispatch_floor_row). The lower bounds are the
    # typo guards: 0.04 typed for 4pp, or 0.0042 for 0.42 pp/h, would lift every thin account.
    "DISPATCH_STRAND_PP": (1.0, 100.0),       # least forecast-dead weekly pp one new fire needs
    "DISPATCH_FIRE_BURN_PPH": (0.05, 5.0),    # weekly pp/h one fire is budgeted until the reset
    "DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN": (1.0, 10080.0),  # how long a placement claims its account's spare
    # The late-band shed's three (D2b, LR_SUSTAINED_LOAD W16; see shed_band_row). Tight on purpose
    # (D2 review D12): 0.97 typed for 97, or a 48 h trip, must be REFUSED, never bounded into a band
    # that opens at 50 % — the rejected option, which stranded 2.2-3.1 pp per wall.
    "SHED_LV_OPEN": (95.0, 100.0),     # weekly used % at which the band opens
    "SHED_TTW_OPEN_H": (0.0, 6.0),     # ...or hours to the wall at the FAST pace
    "SHED_GUARD_H": (1.0, 24.0),       # the SLOW pace must still wall this long before the reset
    # Display only: the 5h use at which the readout starts saying when the window resets (`△`),
    # below S_CUT's `▲`. 80 typed for 0.80 would silently hide the line, hence the range.
    "S_RESET_SHOW": (0.0, 1.0),
}
S_RESET_SHOW_DEFAULT = 0.80


def _validate_router(cfg):
    """Fail with a MESSAGE on a bad router block, never with a traceback.

    The constants are documented as operator-tunable (KMAX was hand-raised 4 -> 8), and they
    are consumed unguarded — _soft divides by (S_CUT - S_SOFT), so S_SOFT == S_CUT is a
    ZeroDivisionError inside every consumer of this tool at once, and a typo'd key is a
    KeyError with no hint which file caused it. Same fail-loud contract load_cfg already uses
    for an unreadable SSOT."""
    R = cfg.get("router")
    if not isinstance(R, dict):
        sys.exit(f"claude-accounts: {CFG_PATH} has no 'router' block")
    bad = [k for k in ROUTER_KEYS
           if not isinstance(R.get(k), (int, float)) or isinstance(R.get(k), bool)]
    if bad:
        sys.exit(f"claude-accounts: router constants missing or non-numeric in {CFG_PATH}: "
                 + ", ".join(bad))
    problems = []
    if not 0 <= R["S_SOFT"] < R["S_CUT"] <= 1:
        problems.append(f"require 0 <= S_SOFT ({R['S_SOFT']}) < S_CUT ({R['S_CUT']}) <= 1")
    if R["KMAX"] < 1:
        problems.append(f"KMAX must be >= 1 (got {R['KMAX']})")
    if R["EPS_H"] <= 0:
        problems.append(f"EPS_H must be > 0 (got {R['EPS_H']})")
    # A resident cap BELOW the active cap is incoherent — every active session is also resident,
    # so it would make the census fallback stricter than the instrument it degrades FROM, which is
    # the exact inversion k_cap exists to remove. Checked here rather than trusted at the call
    # site, because the two constants are hand-tuned in a JSON file by an operator.
    if "KMAX_RESIDENT" in R and isinstance(R["KMAX_RESIDENT"], (int, float)) \
            and not isinstance(R["KMAX_RESIDENT"], bool) and R["KMAX_RESIDENT"] < R["KMAX"]:
        problems.append(f"KMAX_RESIDENT ({R['KMAX_RESIDENT']}) must be >= KMAX ({R['KMAX']}): "
                        "every active session is also resident")
    # A recovery 5h ceiling at or ABOVE S_CUT is a LOOSENING wearing a guard's name: `_excluded`
    # already cuts every lane at S_CUT, so a "recovery 5h floor" of 0.90 would exclude strictly
    # nothing that was not already excluded — and it would read, in the SSOT and in every close,
    # as a survival guard that had been configured. Asserted at LOAD so a hand-tune cannot ship it.
    if "RECOVERY_S_CEIL" in R and isinstance(R["RECOVERY_S_CEIL"], (int, float)) \
            and not isinstance(R["RECOVERY_S_CEIL"], bool) and R["RECOVERY_S_CEIL"] >= R["S_CUT"]:
        problems.append(f"RECOVERY_S_CEIL ({R['RECOVERY_S_CEIL']}) must be < S_CUT ({R['S_CUT']}): "
                        "a recovery 5h ceiling at/above the shared cutoff excludes nothing")
    # Same shape, display side: the ▲ line takes every 5h reading at/above S_CUT first, so a △
    # threshold there would read as configured and never print.
    if "S_RESET_SHOW" in R and isinstance(R["S_RESET_SHOW"], (int, float)) \
            and not isinstance(R["S_RESET_SHOW"], bool) and R["S_RESET_SHOW"] >= R["S_CUT"]:
        problems.append(f"S_RESET_SHOW ({R['S_RESET_SHOW']}) must be < S_CUT ({R['S_CUT']}): "
                        "the ▲ line owns every 5h reading at/above the cutoff, so △ would never show")
    for key, (lo, hi) in ROUTER_OPTIONAL_RANGES.items():
        if key not in R:
            continue                    # optional by contract — the code default stands
        v = R[key]
        if not isinstance(v, (int, float)) or isinstance(v, bool) or not lo <= v <= hi:
            problems.append(f"{key} must be a number in [{lo}, {hi}] (got {v!r})")
    if problems:
        sys.exit(f"claude-accounts: invalid router constants in {CFG_PATH}: "
                 + "; ".join(problems))


# ---- keychain -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def keychain_service(config_dir):
    # CC 2.1.183: service = "Claude Code-credentials-" + sha256(NFC(config_dir))[:8] when
    # CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is set (all 4 accounts are); bare ~/.claude default gets no suffix.
    h = sha256(unicodedata.normalize("NFC", config_dir).encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
    return f"Claude Code-credentials-{h}"


KEYCHAIN_TIMEOUT_S = float(os.environ.get("CC_KEYCHAIN_TIMEOUT_S", "10"))


def _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, path, elapsed_ms, rc=None, stderr=None):
    """Name WHICH of read_creds' four failure returns fired, and with what evidence.

    🚨 WHY THIS EXISTS. `keychain-error` is a SINGLE label over four physically different
    events — a 10 s timeout, a non-zero `security` exit, unparseable stdout, and a non-object
    payload — and until 2026-09-01 the function logged NOTHING, discarded `p.stderr` (the one
    string carrying the OSStatus that names the real cause), and recorded no elapsed time. The
    label was therefore unfalsifiable: an 8-agent-hour reconstruction of the 2026-09-01T04:43Z
    fleet-wide outage could establish only that the failure was load-ASSOCIATED (62/69
    keychain-error sweeps carry a transcript-walk budget breach within ±90 s, vs 29.2% of 500
    control sweeps, Fisher p = 8.1e-13) and could NOT establish which return fired — so the
    tempting fix, raising the timeout, could not be evaluated at all, because nothing on this
    box can say whether the timeout has ever fired even once.

    That matters far past cosmetics: `probe_account` copies this state into `row["error"]`, and
    `_excluded()` tests `row["error"]` FIRST, ahead of every other rule and on all three lanes.
    So one unexplained `security` hiccup removes an account from routing outright — and when it
    hits all four, `--route` returns none and the `claude` launcher silently falls back to its
    pinned account. Measured: all 23 interactive `none` decisions in route.jsonl excluded all
    four accounts, 20 of them for a single uniform reason.

    Deliberately a LOG line, not a new auth state: `keychain-error` is consumed by
    ACTIONABLE_AUTH, LOGIN_FIXABLE, the glyph map and BAD_AUTH, and a new enum member would
    land in each of their fall-through arms unreviewed. Diagnose first; re-shape the enum only
    once the distribution is known."""
    detail = f"path={path} elapsed_ms={elapsed_ms:.0f}"
    if rc is not None:
        detail += f" rc={rc}"
    if stderr:
        detail += " stderr=" + " ".join(stderr.split())[:200]
    log_event(f"read_creds {os.path.basename(config_dir.rstrip('/'))}: keychain-error {detail}")


def creds_file_path(config_dir):
    return os.path.join(config_dir, ".credentials.json")


# config_dir -> "keychain" | "file": where the last read_creds() payload came from. Provenance
# rides BESIDE the (payload, state) contract rather than in it: a new state member would fall into
# every fail-closed arm (ACTIONABLE_AUTH, LOGIN_FIXABLE, BAD_AUTH, the glyph map) unreviewed.
_CREDS_SOURCE = {}


def creds_source(config_dir):
    return _CREDS_SOURCE.get(config_dir)


def read_creds_file(config_dir):
    """READ-ONLY fallback for a MISSING keychain item -> (claudeAiOauth dict | None, state).

    Claude Code itself runs from <config_dir>/.credentials.json when the keychain has no item
    (2026-10-11 ~22:12Z: next3's item vanished under load for ~34 min while 10+ sessions kept
    working off the file, and the board called the account logged-out). This opens the file for
    reading and nothing else: it never writes, creates, chmods or deletes it.

    - no file, or a file with no claudeAiOauth login (CC keeps mcpOAuth there on its own) ->
      (None, "no-keychain-item"), exactly as before;
    - unreadable, non-JSON, non-object, or a non-dict claudeAiOauth -> (None, "keychain-error"):
      the same rule as a corrupt keychain payload. A torn read while CC rewrites the file is not
      a logout, and logged-out would send the operator at a /login the account does not need."""
    path = creds_file_path(config_dir)
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            raw = f.read()
    except (FileNotFoundError, NotADirectoryError):
        return None, "no-keychain-item"
    except OSError as e:
        _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, f"file-unreadable({type(e).__name__}) {path}", 0)
        return None, "keychain-error"
    try:
        d = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:                           # JSONDecodeError and UnicodeDecodeError
        _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, f"file-json-decode {path}", 0)
        return None, "keychain-error"
    oa = d.get("claudeAiOauth") if isinstance(d, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(d, dict) or (oa is not None and not isinstance(oa, dict)):
        _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, f"file-non-object {path}", 0)
        return None, "keychain-error"
    if not oa:
        return None, "no-keychain-item"
    log_event(f"read_creds {os.path.basename(config_dir.rstrip('/'))}: keychain item missing — "
              f"read {path} (read-only fallback)")
    return oa, "present"


def read_creds(config_dir, kc_account, file_fallback=True):
    """-> (payload dict | None, state) — state in {present, no-keychain-item, keychain-error}.

    A MISSING item (rc 44) falls back to read_creds_file() unless file_fallback=False (the
    --relogin-info caller, whose consumers gate on the keychain itself). A keychain ERROR never
    falls back. creds_source(config_dir) names where a present payload came from."""
    _CREDS_SOURCE.pop(config_dir, None)
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            ["/usr/bin/security", "find-generic-password", "-s", keychain_service(config_dir),
             "-a", kc_account, "-w"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=KEYCHAIN_TIMEOUT_S)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, "timeout", (time.monotonic() - t0) * 1000)
        return None, "keychain-error"
    ms = (time.monotonic() - t0) * 1000
    if p.returncode == 44:                       # errSecItemNotFound
        if not file_fallback:
            return None, "no-keychain-item"
        creds, state = read_creds_file(config_dir)
        if creds is not None:
            _CREDS_SOURCE[config_dir] = "file"
        return creds, state
    if p.returncode != 0:                        # locked keychain / ACL / other
        _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, "returncode", ms, rc=p.returncode, stderr=p.stderr)
        return None, "keychain-error"
    try:
        d = json.loads(p.stdout)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, "json-decode", ms, rc=p.returncode)
        return None, "keychain-error"
    # A keychain password that is valid JSON but NOT an object (null, a list, a bare string)
    # used to reach .get() and raise AttributeError, which only json.JSONDecodeError was
    # catching — the traceback escaped main() and killed every mode. A non-object payload is
    # a corrupt item, not a missing one: "keychain-error", never "no-keychain-item" (the
    # latter maps to auth=logged-out at the caller and would trigger a pointless relogin).
    if not isinstance(d, dict):
        _log_keychain_failure(config_dir, "non-object", ms, rc=p.returncode)
        return None, "keychain-error"
    _CREDS_SOURCE[config_dir] = "keychain"
    return (d.get("claudeAiOauth") or None), "present"


# ---- frontier window (SSOT read — never hardcode) --------------------------------------------

def frontier_window(cfg):
    """Parse frontier_access.{active,end} from model-config.yaml without PyYAML.
    Returns {active: bool|None, end: str|None, deadline: dt|None}. active None = UNKNOWN
    (parse failure) — callers must treat unknown as un-routable, never as open or closed."""
    win = {"active": None, "end": None, "deadline": None, "permanent": False}
    try:
        text = open(cfg["model_config_ssot"]).read()
    except OSError:
        return win
    m = re.search(r"^frontier_access:\n((?:[ \t]+.*\n|\n)*?)(?=^\S|\Z)", text, re.M)
    if not m:
        return win
    block = m.group(1)
    ma = re.search(r"^\s*active:\s*(true|false)\b", block, re.M)
    me = re.search(r"^\s*end:\s*\"?(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\"?", block, re.M)
    mp = re.search(r"^\s*permanent:\s*(true|false)\b", block, re.M)
    if ma:
        win["active"] = ma.group(1) == "true"
    # The SSOT states this outright: "This flag is the truth; `end` is a sentinel only."
    # Fable became a standing plan inclusion on 2026-07-20 and `end` was pinned to a
    # far-future date purely so date-based consumers never raise a false "expired". Parsing
    # only `end` made the footer advertise "26826.6d left" — a 73-year countdown on a
    # window that no longer exists. deadline=None is score-neutral: score_fable's
    # window-ended check and JB_BONUS branch are both already guarded on deadline is None.
    if mp and mp.group(1) == "true":
        win["permanent"] = True
    if me:
        win["end"] = me.group(1)
        end = datetime.strptime(me.group(1), "%Y-%m-%d").replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
        if cfg["frontier"].get("end_date_inclusive", True):
            end += timedelta(days=1)             # available THROUGH the end date
        # A permanent entitlement has no deadline to press against — leave it None so no
        # surface derives urgency from the sentinel.
        win["deadline"] = None if win["permanent"] else end
    return win


# ---- live concurrency (all claude sessions, not just --resume) --------------------------------

ENV_TOK = re.compile(r"^[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*=")


class _Census(dict):
    """A per-account count dict that can carry a second per-account count beside it.

    It exists so `concurrency()` and `working_concurrency()` keep returning exactly the dict every
    caller and every test stub already uses, while collect() can read one more number off the
    same pass: `headless` (live `claude -p` processes) and `top` (top-level transcripts, the
    subset of the walk's total that is one-per-session). A stub returning a plain dict carries
    neither, and collect() then applies no bound — the pre-bound behaviour exactly.

    `sids` ({acct: set of top-level transcript stems}) and `at` (the walk's wall-clock start) name
    WHICH sessions the census counted, so apply_assignments can refuse to charge a phantom for a
    session the sweep already counts in k_work. Default empty/None: a stub skips nothing.

    The session-id join (bound_kwork's second bound) reads three more: `live_sids` and `unknown`
    on the process census ({acct: session ids of live processes}, {acct: live processes with no
    readable session id}) and `subs` on the walk ({acct: {parent session id: working subagents}}).
    Absent on a stub, and bound_kwork then applies the process-count bound alone."""

    def __init__(self, counts, **extra):
        super().__init__(counts)
        self.sids = {}
        self.at = None
        for k, v in extra.items():
            setattr(self, k, v)


def concurrency(cfg):
    """Live interactive-session count per account, or None when it CANNOT BE MEASURED.

    Counts processes whose argv[0] is the claude binary (excludes expect wrappers and node/MCP
    children that inherit the env), attributed via the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR env var that every
    launcher sets (ps -E appends env). A session with NO CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR — or ~/.claude, which
    is the stable `claude-prev` track's dir — counts toward 'next': .claude mirrors .claude-next.
    Headless one-shots (-p/--print) are skipped.

    THE None CONTRACT (was: an all-zero dict — scaling-bottlenecks-2026-08-09/07 §6.5).
    A `ps` that times out or fails used to return every account at 0, and no caller could tell
    that from a genuinely idle fleet. That fails OPEN in TWO directions at once:

      · KMAX stops binding — `_excluded`'s cap is compared against a fabricated 0, so the router
        admits an unbounded pile-on at exactly the moment the box is too loaded to run `ps`.
      · heal()'s rotation-safety gate (`k_live > 0`) reads 0 and redeems a refresh token
        underneath N live sessions. The loser of that rotation race holds a token the server has
        retired — a 400 invalid_grant with the calendar cliff still weeks away, i.e. a LOGOUT
        manufactured by an unmeasured `ps`.

    The None RIDES ON THE ROW, and only the two gates above may read it as a refusal. The
    ROUTER's charge degrades instead: inherit_k stamps the last sweep's count as `k_stale` when
    this census could not be taken, inside the 600 s grace, because a starved `ps` is fleet-wide
    (one census, four accounts) and excluding every account is how the router came to abstain on
    23 of 230 recorded desk decisions — 10% all-time, 19.7% over the 12 days before it was
    measured — and hand each of those launches to the pinned account
    (desk-router-abstention-2026-09-01 §2, §4). `row["k"]` stays None either way — that is what keeps
    heal() and handoff-fire.sh's relogin gate refusing on a count nobody took.

    tests/account-fact-derivation.bats used to PIN the all-zero return as a deliberate divergence
    from cc-relogin's `live_sessions() -> -1`, on the rationale that "concurrency() feeds an ALARM
    (keep rendering); live_sessions() feeds a GATE (refuse)". The premise was false when written:
    this function has fed BOTH — the display AND the two gates above — since heal()'s under-lock
    re-check (`:658`) was added. So the two implementations now agree on the UNKNOWN direction and
    the display path is the one that degrades, explicitly, at the renderer.

    Was "not currently at risk" (measured 67-95 ms / 118,665 B at 10 sessions, 07 §6.5). Re-measured
    2026-10-11: `ps -wwEo` emits 2.43 MB and took 0.5-10.2 s at load 84-780 against its 10 s
    timeout, and timed out 121 times that day; 11 of 25 desk decisions abstained on
    `concurrency-unmeasured`, all fleet-wide by construction (one census, four accounts). The
    instrument now starves, which is what the native census below exists for
    (docs/plans/ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS.md D1). With `census: "ab"` in accounts.json, a libproc
    census runs CONCURRENTLY with ps (no added serial time), is published through `_NATIVE_LAST`
    and logged beside ps as `census-ab:`; this function's return value and its None contract are
    unchanged, and so is `row["k"]`, which reads it. Heal's rotation gate no longer reads it
    directly: since the Phase 3 ruling (2026-10-19) it reads gate_counts(), which combines this
    count with the native census."""
    global _NATIVE_LAST
    if _census_mode(cfg) != "ab":
        return _ps_census(cfg)
    res, _NATIVE_LAST = _census_ab(cfg)
    return res


def _census_ab(cfg):
    """-> (ps counts | None, native _Census | None), taken concurrently, and the `census-ab:` line
    logged. Publishes nothing: concurrency() owns `_NATIVE_LAST`, so heal's re-check, which runs on
    a pool thread, can take its own pair without racing a sibling account's."""
    box = {}
    w0 = time.monotonic()
    # A daemon thread, started BEFORE the ps subprocess and joined after it: concurrent, so it
    # adds no serial time, and its result survives a ps failure — which is the case it is for.
    th = threading.Thread(target=lambda: box.update(zip(("n", "m"), _native_census(cfg))),
                          name="native-census", daemon=True)
    th.start()
    p0 = time.monotonic()
    res = _ps_census(cfg)
    ps_ms = int((time.monotonic() - p0) * 1000)
    th.join(max(0.0, NATIVE_WALL_BUDGET_S - (time.monotonic() - w0)))
    nat, meta = (box.get("n"), box.get("m")) if "m" in box else (None, {"reason": "join-timeout"})
    log_event(_census_ab_line(cfg, res, nat, meta, ps_ms))
    return res, nat


def gate_counts(cfg, ps, native):
    """The rotation gate's live-session count per account -> {name: int} | None.

    The Phase 3 ruling (2026-10-19, docs/plans/ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS.md § Phase 3), after a
    7-day soak at 99.4% exact agreement with a maximum per-account difference of 1: the HIGHER of
    the two counts when both were taken, whichever one was taken otherwise, None only when neither
    was. The max, never native alone, because both instruments fail by undercounting and an
    undercount OPENS this gate; the native census alone would trade ps's blindness for its own."""
    if ps is None and native is None:
        return None
    return {a["name"]: max(c.get(a["name"], 0) for c in (ps, native) if c is not None)
            for a in cfg["accounts"]}


def gate_census(cfg):
    """A fresh gate_counts() for heal's under-lock re-check. `census: ps` (or its rollback
    sentinel) keeps the gate on ps alone, through concurrency() as before."""
    if _census_mode(cfg) != "ab":
        return concurrency(cfg)
    return gate_counts(cfg, *_census_ab(cfg))


PS_JOIN_S = 15.0        # ps's own 10 s timeout plus a 5 s reap allowance


def _ps_census(cfg):
    """The `ps -wwEo` census: the body concurrency() always ran, now with a BOUNDED reap.

    subprocess.run's timeout kills ps at 10 s and then waits for it UNBOUNDED. Measured
    2026-10-12 at load 330: "timed out after 10 seconds", yet the call returned after 1,226 s — a
    SIGKILLed ps stuck in the kernel is not reaped until it leaves it. That held the whole sweep,
    and stamped the native census (taken at the scan's start) 20 minutes stale, past its grace. So
    the call runs on a daemon thread joined at PS_JOIN_S; a ps that will not die is abandoned to
    that thread (reaped whenever the kernel lets go, or at process exit)."""
    box = {}

    def _run():
        try:
            box["out"] = subprocess.run(["ps", "-wwEo", "pid=,command="], capture_output=True,
                                        text=True, timeout=10).stdout
        except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError) as e:
            box["err"] = e

    th = threading.Thread(target=_run, name="ps-census", daemon=True)
    th.start()
    th.join(PS_JOIN_S)
    try:
        if "out" in box:
            out = box["out"]
        else:
            raise box.get("err") or subprocess.TimeoutExpired(
                "ps", 10, output=f"killed, not reaped within {PS_JOIN_S:.0f}s")
    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError) as e:
        # Logged, never silent — same contract as _kwork_unmeasured below. An instrument that
        # changed mid-decision has to be answerable from disk afterwards.
        log_event(f"concurrency: ps UNAVAILABLE ({type(e).__name__}: {e}) — live-session count "
                  "UNMEASURED. The router charges 'unknown' (concurrency-unmeasured, a DATA "
                  "exclusion -> exit 3) and heal() REFUSES to redeem any refresh token this "
                  "sweep. Previously this returned all-zero, which disarmed both gates at once.")
        return None
    return _census_from_lines(cfg, ((line, None) for line in out.splitlines()))


def _expand_home(v, home):
    """`~`-expansion against a GIVEN home, the native census's per-process HOME."""
    return home + v[1:] if (v == "~" or v.startswith("~/")) else v


def _census_from_lines(cfg, entries, reg=None):
    """Classify `(line, home)` entries into a _Census — the ONE classifier both instruments share.

    A line is `"<pid> <argv…> <env…>"`, the shape `ps -wwEo pid=,command=` prints and the shape
    the native census builds from KERN_PROCARGS2. `home=None` means the module's HOME (the ps
    path, byte-identical to before the split); a native entry passes THAT PROCESS's own `HOME=`,
    so a scratch-HOME fixture's sessions are never attributed through the host's `~/.claude`
    mirror. In production every session's HOME is the module's, so both instruments agree.
    `reg` is the registry ({pid: sessionId}) when the caller has already read it."""
    counts = {a["name"]: 0 for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    headless = {a["name"]: 0 for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    # rstrip("/") on the KEYS as well as on the observed dir. The observed side has always been
    # rstripped (below), the key side was not, so a config_dir written with a trailing slash in
    # accounts.json could never match anything and that account scored 0 live sessions forever —
    # silently, in the undercount direction, which is the direction that OPENS the rotation gate.
    by_dir = {a["config_dir"].rstrip("/"): a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    mirror_next = next((a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]
                        if a["config_dir"].rstrip("/").endswith(".claude-next")), None)
    # setdefault, not assignment: an account whose config_dir IS ~/.claude owns that key. Plain
    # assignment overwrote it with mirror_next — or with None when no .claude-next account
    # exists — so that account's own sessions were credited elsewhere or dropped entirely.
    # ~/.claude mirrors .claude-next only when nothing more specific claims it.
    if mirror_next is not None:
        by_dir.setdefault(os.path.join(HOME, ".claude"), mirror_next)
    pids = {a["name"]: [] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    argv_sid = {}
    for line, home in entries:
        toks = line.split()
        # The pid column leads; a line without one (a test stub's bare `command=` output) still
        # counts, it just cannot be joined to a session id, so bound_kwork treats it as unknown.
        pid = int(toks.pop(0)) if toks and toks[0].isdigit() else None
        if not toks:
            continue
        t0 = toks[0]
        # `claude.exe` is NOT a third spelling to be thorough about — it is the argv[0] of every
        # session this repo's own dispatcher creates. Interactive launches exec the `.bin/claude`
        # SYMLINK (path ends in /claude, matched), while cc-pane-runner-dispatched workers,
        # teammates and research subagents exec the RESOLVED native binary. Measured on this
        # machine 2026-08-04: next2 counted 2 live sessions against a truth of 10 — the 8 missing
        # were all claude.exe. That matters here more than anywhere else, because this count is
        # the rotation-safety gate below (`k_live > 0`): a heal that reads 0 redeems a refresh
        # token concurrently with N sessions that rotate it, and the loser of that race holds a
        # token the server has retired — a 400 invalid_grant with the calendar cliff still weeks
        # away. Six sibling tools already match both spellings (cc-reaper, cc-reconcile,
        # cc-teardown, cc-wait, statusline.sh, session-register.sh); this was one of the last two.
        if not (t0 == "claude" or t0.endswith("/claude") or t0.endswith("claude.exe")
                or "cli.js" in t0):
            continue
        # Headless one-shot flags sit in the first few argv slots (`claude -p "…"`); scanning
        # the whole line would false-match a '-p' or '--print' inside a PROMPT argument.
        head = [t for t in toks[1:7] if t.startswith("-")]
        if "--version" in head:
            continue
        # ps -E appends env AFTER argv, so the LAST CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR= match is the real env
        # var — a first-match search could hit prompt text that merely mentions it.
        cfgs = re.findall(r"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=(\S+)", line)
        if home is None:
            d = os.path.expanduser(cfgs[-1]) if cfgs else os.path.join(HOME, ".claude")
        else:
            d = _expand_home(cfgs[-1], home) if cfgs else os.path.join(home, ".claude")
        name = by_dir.get(d.rstrip("/"))
        if name:
            # A headless one-shot is still kept out of the INTERACTIVE count (the pane census and
            # heal()'s rotation gate read that), but it is tallied: it is a live process writing a
            # transcript, and bound_kwork() needs it to cap the working census.
            if "-p" in head or "--print" in head:
                headless[name] += 1
            else:
                counts[name] += 1
            pids[name].append(pid)
            m = _RE_ARGV_SID.search(" ".join(toks[1:12]))
            if pid is not None and m:
                argv_sid[pid] = m.group(1)
    if reg is None:
        reg = _registered_sessions(by_dir)
    live_sids = {n: set() for n in pids}
    unknown = {n: 0 for n in pids}
    for n, plist in pids.items():
        for pid in plist:
            got = {s for s in (reg.get(pid), argv_sid.get(pid)) if s} if pid is not None else ()
            if got:
                live_sids[n].update(got)
            else:
                unknown[n] += 1
    return _Census(counts, headless=headless, live_sids=live_sids, unknown=unknown,
                   attributed={p for pl in pids.values() for p in pl if p is not None})


_RE_ARGV_SID = re.compile(r"(?:--resume|-r|--session-id)[= ]([0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-"
                          r"[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12})\b")


def _registered_sessions(by_dir):
    """{pid: sessionId} from Claude Code's own process registry, `<config_dir>/sessions/<pid>.json`,
    for every pid still alive. The harness writes the file at start (interactive AND `claude -p`),
    updates its sessionId on /clear or resume, and removes it on exit — so a finished headless run
    is absent by construction, and a crashed one is filtered by the pid check. realpath-deduped:
    ~/.claude-next/sessions is a symlink to ~/.claude/sessions. Any unreadable file is skipped —
    its process then lands in `unknown`, which bound_kwork charges conservatively."""
    return {pid: e["sid"] for pid, e in _registry_entries(by_dir).items()}


def _registry_entries(by_dir):
    """{pid: {sid, entrypoint, startedAt}} for every live registry entry — _registered_sessions'
    reader, which the native census also needs for its gap-fill and its canary."""
    out, seen = {}, set()
    for d in by_dir:
        sdir = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(d), "sessions"))
        if sdir in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(sdir)
        try:
            ents = [e for e in os.scandir(sdir) if e.name.endswith(".json")]
        except OSError:
            continue
        for e in ents:
            try:
                with open(e.path, "rb") as f:
                    j = json.loads(f.read(65536))
                pid, sid = int(j["pid"]), str(j["sessionId"])
            except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
                continue
            try:
                os.kill(pid, 0)
            except ProcessLookupError:
                continue
            except (PermissionError, OSError):
                pass
            out[pid] = {"sid": sid, "entrypoint": j.get("entrypoint"),
                        "startedAt": j.get("startedAt")}
    return out


# ---- native census (libproc + KERN_PROCARGS2; docs/plans/ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS.md D1) ---------
# Why: `ps -wwEo` serialises every process's argv AND env (2.43 MB on 2026-10-11) and starves under
# load — 121 timeouts that day, each one a fleet-wide `concurrency-unmeasured`. The native census
# reads argv only for claude-shaped processes: 354 ms against ps's 9,285 ms at load 738, same pid
# set (synth probe cmp4.py). Lazy by contract (C2): ctypes and both dylibs load inside the first
# call, never at import, because `--route`/`--rank` sit on the launch path and importing ctypes alone
# measured 11.6-13.8 ms of CPU. Since the Phase 3 ruling (2026-10-19, after a 7-day soak at 99.4%
# exact agreement) it is a gate instrument for heal's `k_live`, through gate_counts(); `row["k"]`
# stays ps-only.
_NATIVE_LIBS = None          # (ctypes, libproc, libc, BSDInfo), loaded on first use
_NATIVE_LAST = None          # the census concurrency() ran beside ps this sweep; collect() reads it
_NATIVE_META = None          # meta of the last native_census() call, a None result included
NATIVE_CPU_BUDGET_S = 1.0    # measured user 0.03-0.04 s for a whole census process
NATIVE_WALL_BUDGET_S = 10.0  # ps's own timeout: the native census must never be the slower one
_native_cpu_clock = time.thread_time      # seam: the CPU over-budget case stubs it
_PROC_PIDTBSDINFO = 3
_NO_TTY = 0xffffffff


class _NativeUnavailable(Exception):
    """The native census cannot run (ctypes or libproc failed to load); str() is the reason."""


def _native_libs():
    global _NATIVE_LIBS
    if _NATIVE_LIBS is None:
        try:
            import ctypes
            libproc = ctypes.CDLL("/usr/lib/libproc.dylib")
            libc = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True)
        except (ImportError, OSError) as e:
            raise _NativeUnavailable(f"libproc-load:{type(e).__name__}")
        u32, i32, u64 = ctypes.c_uint32, ctypes.c_int32, ctypes.c_uint64

        class BSDInfo(ctypes.Structure):
            # struct proc_bsdinfo, <sys/proc_info.h>. sizeof must equal what proc_pidinfo returns,
            # which is checked per call — a drifted struct reads as "no info", never as garbage.
            _fields_ = [("pbi_flags", u32), ("pbi_status", u32), ("pbi_xstatus", u32),
                        ("pbi_pid", u32), ("pbi_ppid", u32), ("pbi_uid", u32), ("pbi_gid", u32),
                        ("pbi_ruid", u32), ("pbi_rgid", u32), ("pbi_svuid", u32),
                        ("pbi_svgid", u32), ("rfu_1", u32), ("pbi_comm", ctypes.c_char * 16),
                        ("pbi_name", ctypes.c_char * 32), ("pbi_nfiles", u32), ("pbi_pgid", u32),
                        ("pbi_pjobc", u32), ("e_tdev", u32), ("e_tpgid", u32), ("pbi_nice", i32),
                        ("pbi_start_tvsec", u64), ("pbi_start_tvusec", u64)]
        vp, ci = ctypes.c_void_p, ctypes.c_int
        libproc.proc_listallpids.argtypes, libproc.proc_listallpids.restype = [vp, ci], ci
        libproc.proc_pidinfo.argtypes, libproc.proc_pidinfo.restype = [ci, ci, u64, vp, ci], ci
        libproc.proc_pidpath.argtypes, libproc.proc_pidpath.restype = [ci, vp, u32], ci
        libc.sysctl.argtypes = [ctypes.POINTER(ci), ctypes.c_uint, vp,
                                ctypes.POINTER(ctypes.c_size_t), vp, ctypes.c_size_t]
        libc.sysctl.restype = ci
        _NATIVE_LIBS = (ctypes, libproc, libc, BSDInfo)
    return _NATIVE_LIBS


def _native_proc_entries():
    """-> (listallpids count, [{pid, comm, start}]) for this uid's processes WITH a controlling
    terminal — exactly the set `ps -wwEo` lists without -a/-x. The seam the fail-closed cases
    stub. Raises _NativeUnavailable when libproc cannot load."""
    ctypes, libproc, _libc, BSDInfo = _native_libs()
    n = libproc.proc_listallpids(None, 0)
    if n <= 0:
        return n, []
    buf = (ctypes.c_int * (n + 256))()
    n = libproc.proc_listallpids(buf, ctypes.sizeof(buf))
    if n <= 0:
        return n, []
    uid, out, bi, size = os.getuid(), [], BSDInfo(), ctypes.sizeof(BSDInfo)
    for pid in buf[:n]:
        if pid <= 0:
            continue
        if libproc.proc_pidinfo(pid, _PROC_PIDTBSDINFO, 0, ctypes.byref(bi), size) != size:
            continue
        if bi.pbi_uid != uid or bi.e_tdev == _NO_TTY:
            continue
        out.append({"pid": pid, "comm": bi.pbi_comm.decode(errors="replace"),
                    "start": int(bi.pbi_start_tvsec)})
    return n, out


def _native_pidpath(pid):
    ctypes, libproc, _libc, _b = _native_libs()
    buf = ctypes.create_string_buffer(4096)
    return buf.value.decode(errors="replace") if libproc.proc_pidpath(pid, buf, 4096) > 0 else ""


def _native_procargs(pid):
    """-> (argv, env) from KERN_PROCARGS2, or None when the read fails (exited, or not ours).
    Layout: int32 argc, exec_path, NUL padding, argc argv strings, env strings, empty string."""
    ctypes, _lp, libc, _b = _native_libs()
    mib = (ctypes.c_int * 3)(1, 49, pid)               # CTL_KERN, KERN_PROCARGS2, pid
    size = ctypes.c_size_t(0)
    if libc.sysctl(mib, 3, None, ctypes.byref(size), None, 0) != 0 or size.value < 4:
        return None
    buf = ctypes.create_string_buffer(size.value)
    if libc.sysctl(mib, 3, buf, ctypes.byref(size), None, 0) != 0:
        return None
    raw = buf.raw[:size.value]
    argc = int.from_bytes(raw[:4], "little")
    i = raw.find(b"\0", 4)
    if i < 0:
        return None
    while i < len(raw) and raw[i] == 0:
        i += 1
    argv, env = [], []
    while i < len(raw):
        j = raw.find(b"\0", i)
        j = len(raw) if j < 0 else j
        s = raw[i:j]
        if len(argv) < argc:
            argv.append(s.decode(errors="replace"))
        elif not s:
            break
        else:
            env.append(s.decode(errors="replace"))
        i = j + 1
    return argv, env


def _native_wants(p):
    """The argv-read prefilter: claude-shaped comm, or a claude path (a versioned native binary's
    comm is its version string, e.g. `2.1.293`)."""
    c = p["comm"]
    return c.startswith("claude") or c == "node" or "claude" in _native_pidpath(p["pid"])


def _native_census(cfg):
    """-> (_Census | None, meta). The body of native_census(); see there."""
    t_wall, w0, c0 = time.time(), time.monotonic(), _native_cpu_clock()
    meta = {"as_of": t_wall, "ms": 0, "cpu_ms": 0, "reason": None, "tty_pop": 0, "reads": 0}

    def done(census, reason=None):
        meta["ms"] = int((time.monotonic() - w0) * 1000)
        meta["cpu_ms"] = int((_native_cpu_clock() - c0) * 1000)
        meta["reason"] = reason
        if census is not None:
            census.meta, census.as_of = meta, t_wall
        return census, meta

    def over():
        if _native_cpu_clock() - c0 > NATIVE_CPU_BUDGET_S:
            return "cpu-budget"
        if time.monotonic() - w0 > NATIVE_WALL_BUDGET_S:
            return "wall-budget"
        return None

    try:
        n_all, procs = _native_proc_entries()
    except _NativeUnavailable as e:
        return done(None, str(e))
    except Exception as e:                       # any ctypes surprise fails closed, never raises
        return done(None, f"lister:{type(e).__name__}")
    if n_all <= 0:
        return done(None, "listallpids-empty")
    meta["tty_pop"] = len(procs)
    if not procs:
        return done(None, "tty-pop-0")
    by_dir = {a["config_dir"].rstrip("/"): a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    mirror_next = next((a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]
                        if a["config_dir"].rstrip("/").endswith(".claude-next")), None)
    if mirror_next is not None:
        by_dir.setdefault(os.path.join(HOME, ".claude"), mirror_next)
    reg = _registry_entries(by_dir)
    by_pid = {p["pid"]: p for p in procs}

    def verified(pid):
        """A live registry pid whose recorded start matches the process now holding that pid."""
        e, p = reg.get(pid), by_pid.get(pid)
        sa = e.get("startedAt") if e else None
        if p is None or not isinstance(sa, (int, float)):
            return False
        return p["start"] - 1 <= sa / 1000.0 <= p["start"] + 600

    want = []
    try:
        for p in procs:
            if (why := over()):
                return done(None, why)
            if _native_wants(p) or verified(p["pid"]):     # registry gap-fill
                want.append(p["pid"])
    except Exception as e:
        return done(None, f"prefilter:{type(e).__name__}")
    entries, read = [], set()
    for pid in want:
        if (why := over()):
            return done(None, why)
        try:
            got = _native_procargs(pid)
        except Exception:
            got = None
        if got is None:
            continue
        argv, env = got
        read.add(pid)
        homes = [e[5:] for e in env if e.startswith("HOME=")]
        entries.append((f"{pid} " + " ".join(argv + env), homes[-1] if homes else HOME))
    meta["reads"] = len(read)
    census = _census_from_lines(cfg, entries, reg={p: e["sid"] for p, e in reg.items()})
    # The REGISTRY CANARY: a live, start-verified, tty-holding registry session that the scan did
    # not read or did not attribute means the scan is blind to something ps sees — refuse the whole
    # census rather than undercount (an undercount fails OPEN). A pid that exited mid-scan is churn.
    missed = []
    for pid, e in reg.items():
        if e.get("entrypoint") == "sdk-cli" or not verified(pid):
            continue
        if pid not in read or pid not in census.attributed:
            try:
                os.kill(pid, 0)
            except ProcessLookupError:
                continue
            except OSError:
                pass
            missed.append(pid)
    if missed:
        log_event(f"native census: registry canary — live registered pid(s) {sorted(missed)} not "
                  "read or not attributed; native count refused this pass (ps is unaffected)")
        return done(None, "registry-canary")
    if (why := over()):
        return done(None, why)
    return done(census)


def native_census(cfg):
    """Live-session census from libproc + KERN_PROCARGS2 -> _Census (with `.meta` and `.as_of`, the
    scan's start epoch), or None with `_NATIVE_META["reason"]` naming why. Fails CLOSED on: libproc
    or ctypes not loading, an empty pid list, an empty tty population, more than 1 s of thread CPU,
    more than 10 s of wall time, or the registry canary. Same classifier as ps
    (_census_from_lines), with each process's own HOME."""
    global _NATIVE_META
    census, _NATIVE_META = _native_census(cfg)
    return census


def _accounts_local_dir():
    return (os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_LOCAL_DIR")
            or os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "state", "accounts-local"))


def _census_mode(cfg):
    """'ps' | 'ab'. Env CC_ACCOUNTS_CENSUS, then the `census-ps` rollback sentinel, then
    accounts.json's top-level `census`, then 'ps'. Called on sweeps only — never on the launch path,
    where k_panes reads the env var alone (no file I/O, C2)."""
    env = os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_CENSUS")
    if env in ("ps", "ab"):
        return env
    if os.path.exists(os.path.join(_accounts_local_dir(), "census-ps")):
        return "ps"
    return "ab" if cfg.get("census") == "ab" else "ps"


def _fmt_counts(cfg, c):
    return "{" + ",".join(f"{a['name']}:{c.get(a['name'], 0)}" for a in cfg["accounts"]) + "}"


def _census_ab_line(cfg, ps, nat, meta, ps_ms):
    """The per-sweep `census-ab:` log line — counts only, never argv or env."""
    meta = meta or {}
    p = _fmt_counts(cfg, ps) if ps is not None else "UNAVAILABLE"
    n = _fmt_counts(cfg, nat) if nat is not None else f"None({meta.get('reason')})"
    agree = ("n/a" if ps is None or nat is None else
             "yes" if all(ps.get(a["name"], 0) == nat.get(a["name"], 0) for a in cfg["accounts"])
             else "no")
    return (f"census-ab: ps={p} native={n} agree={agree} ps_ms={ps_ms} "
            f"native_ms={meta.get('ms', '-')} native_cpu_ms={meta.get('cpu_ms', '-')}")


_RE_CENSUS_AB = re.compile(r"^(\S+) census-ab: ps=(\{[^}]*\}|UNAVAILABLE) "
                           r"native=(?:(\{[^}]*\})|None\(([^)]*)\)) agree=(\S+) ps_ms=(\S+) "
                           r"native_ms=(\S+) native_cpu_ms=(\S+)")
SOAK_MIN_DAYS, SOAK_MIN_PS_OK, SOAK_MIN_AGREE, SOAK_MAX_DIFF = 7.0, 500, 0.99, 1


def _parse_counts(s):
    out = {}
    for part in s.strip("{}").split(","):
        if ":" in part:
            k, v = part.split(":", 1)
            try:
                out[k] = int(v)
            except ValueError:
                pass
    return out


def census_ab_report(path=None, out=None):
    """`--census-ab-report`: the Phase 3 soak verdict over the `census-ab:` log lines. Read-only.
    PASS needs >= 7 days, >= 500 ps-OK lines, >= 99% exact agreement among ps-OK lines (a native
    None counts against it) and a max per-account difference of 1. The denominator always prints."""
    out = out or sys.stdout
    rows = []
    try:
        with open(path or LOG_PATH, errors="replace") as f:
            for line in f:
                if "census-ab:" not in line:
                    continue
                m = _RE_CENSUS_AB.match(line)
                if m:
                    rows.append(m)
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"census-ab-report: cannot read {path or LOG_PATH}: {e}", file=out)
    stamps = [t for t in (_as_epoch(m.group(1)) for m in rows) if t is not None]
    days = (max(stamps) - min(stamps)) / 86400.0 if stamps else 0.0
    ps_ok = [m for m in rows if m.group(2) != "UNAVAILABLE"]
    agree = sum(1 for m in ps_ok if m.group(5) == "yes")
    diff = 0
    for m in ps_ok:
        if m.group(3):
            p, n = _parse_counts(m.group(2)), _parse_counts(m.group(3))
            diff = max([diff] + [abs(p.get(k, 0) - n.get(k, 0)) for k in set(p) | set(n)])
    reasons = {}
    for m in rows:
        if m.group(4) is not None:
            reasons[m.group(4)] = reasons.get(m.group(4), 0) + 1
    nms = sorted(int(m.group(7)) for m in rows if m.group(7).isdigit())
    pct = (lambda q: nms[min(len(nms) - 1, int(q * len(nms)))] if nms else "-")
    rate = agree / len(ps_ok) if ps_ok else 0.0
    print(f"census-ab-report: window {days:.1f} d · {len(rows)} line(s) · ps-OK {len(ps_ok)} · "
          f"ps UNAVAILABLE {len(rows) - len(ps_ok)}", file=out)
    print(f"  exact agreement {agree}/{len(ps_ok)} ps-OK line(s) = {rate * 100:.1f}% · "
          f"max per-account |ps-native| {diff}", file=out)
    print("  native None: " + (" · ".join(f"{k} {v}" for k, v in sorted(reasons.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]))
                               if reasons else "0"), file=out)
    print(f"  native_ms p50 {pct(0.5)} · p90 {pct(0.9)}", file=out)
    miss = [w for ok, w in ((days >= SOAK_MIN_DAYS, f"window {days:.1f} d < {SOAK_MIN_DAYS:.0f} d"),
                            (len(ps_ok) >= SOAK_MIN_PS_OK,
                             f"ps-OK {len(ps_ok)} < {SOAK_MIN_PS_OK}"),
                            (rate >= SOAK_MIN_AGREE,
                             f"agreement {rate * 100:.1f}% < {SOAK_MIN_AGREE * 100:.0f}%"),
                            (diff <= SOAK_MAX_DIFF, f"max diff {diff} > {SOAK_MAX_DIFF}")) if not ok]
    print("soak: PASS" if not miss else f"soak: NOT-YET ({'; '.join(miss)})", file=out)
    return 0


KWORK_BUDGET_S = 15.0      # was 5.0, was 2.0 — see the budget note in working_concurrency's
                           # docstring, and docs/research/routing-policy-ruling-2026-09-22.md §4
KWORK_TIMEOUTS = 0         # per-process count, surfaced on route-meta as kwork_to=


def _kwork_unmeasured(budget_s, scanned):
    """The ONE return path for an over-budget walk: log it, count it, return None.

    Silence here was a real blind spot (R1 §5): the swing is `k_eff` 2 → 7 for one account, which
    is the difference between routable and `kmax-concurrency`, and nothing anywhere said the count
    had changed instrument mid-decision. It is logged rather than printed because the caller is a
    sweep, not a route — the route path surfaces the same fact per-decision as `k_src=` on
    route-meta, which is answerable from disk afterwards."""
    global KWORK_TIMEOUTS
    KWORK_TIMEOUTS += 1
    log_event(f"working_concurrency: walk exceeded budget_s={budget_s:.1f} after {scanned} "
              "project dirs — k_work UNMEASURED for this sweep, so the router charges the PANE "
              "census instead (measured swing k_eff 2 -> 7 on one account, R1 §5). The normal "
              "walk is 0.04-0.07s over 826 transcripts, so this means the box is pathologically "
              "loaded — i.e. exactly when the concurrency count matters most.")
    return None


# ---- WHAT "WORKING" MEANS (FLEET_V2 W6 D1.8) -----------------------------------------------------
# The walk used to call a transcript working when its MTIME fell inside the window. That counts
# THROUGHPUT, and on 2026-09-29 06:16:09Z it logged next=14 and next4=11 against KMAX 8 while the
# sessions actually in flight were ~3-5 and ~2 — mostly SUBAGENT files a finished agent had written
# minutes earlier (bound_kwork caps only the top-level term, so nothing capped them). Three rules:
#   1. A file is working from its LAST TIMESTAMPED USER OR ASSISTANT RECORD, not its mtime: hook
#      attachments, summaries and queue bookkeeping touch the file without a turn happening.
#   2. A session whose last turn record is an assistant tool_use is WAITING ON A TOOL and counts as
#      working however long the tool runs — up to KWORK_TOOLWAIT_MIN, which bounds a corpse killed
#      mid-tool (the top-level term is also capped at live processes by bound_kwork).
#   3. A SUBAGENT counts only while UNFINISHED. Finished = its final answer is written (last turn
#      record an assistant with no tool_use), OR its workflow run journal holds a `result`/`failed`
#      row for its agentId (a workflow agent ends ON a tool call, so rule 2 alone would hold it live
#      for the whole horizon), OR its parent recorded a task-notification for it at/after its last
#      record. The last two are the same serializer-only bare-key matches handoff-fire.sh's
#      live_subagents_of uses (memory: bare-json-key-is-unforgeable-by-content,
#      workflow-agent-settles-only-in-its-run-journal).
# A file with no parseable turn record at all is judged by the OLD mtime rule: no record to judge by
# is not evidence of idleness. Every record-reading helper takes `now` and ignores records after it,
# which is a no-op live and is what makes the census REPLAYABLE at a past instant (kmax-rebase.md).
# Kill switch: CC_ROUTE_KWORK_TURNS=off restores the mtime-only census.
KWORK_TOOLWAIT_MIN = 30.0
KWORK_TAIL_BYTES = (64 * 1024, 1024 * 1024)     # tail read, then one wider retry
_RE_TS = re.compile(rb'"timestamp":"([0-9][0-9:.TZ+-]*)"')
_RE_NOTIF = re.compile(rb'"(?:content|prompt)":"<task-notification>\\n<task-id>([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)</task-id>')


def _iso_epoch(s):
    if isinstance(s, bytes):
        s = s.decode("ascii", "replace")
    try:
        t = datetime.fromisoformat(str(s).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return None
    if t.tzinfo is None:
        t = t.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    return t.timestamp()


def _tail_lines(path, size, nbytes):
    """Complete lines of the last `nbytes` of the file, newest first, and whether the read reached
    the start of the file."""
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        off = max(0, size - nbytes)
        f.seek(off)
        data = f.read(nbytes)
    lines = data.split(b"\n")
    if off > 0:
        lines = lines[1:]                           # the first piece is a partial line
    return lines[::-1], off == 0


def _turn_state(lines, now=None):
    """(ts, waiting_on_tool, answered) of the newest user/assistant record in `lines` (newest
    first) whose timestamp is not after `now`, or None when there is none. `answered` = an
    assistant record with no tool_use block: the turn is over (a subagent's final answer, or a
    top-level session back at its prompt). A user record means the model owes a reply."""
    for ln in lines:
        if b'"type":"assistant"' not in ln and b'"type":"user"' not in ln:
            continue
        try:
            rec = json.loads(ln)
        except ValueError:
            continue                                # a line still being written
        if not isinstance(rec, dict) or rec.get("type") not in ("user", "assistant"):
            continue
        ts = _iso_epoch(rec.get("timestamp"))
        if now is not None and ts is not None and ts > now:
            continue
        if rec["type"] == "user":
            return ts, False, False
        content = (rec.get("message") or {}).get("content")
        tool = isinstance(content, list) and any(
            isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_use" for b in content)
        return ts, tool, not tool
    return None


def _last_turn(path, size, now=None):
    for nbytes in KWORK_TAIL_BYTES:
        lines, whole = _tail_lines(path, size, nbytes)
        st = _turn_state(lines, now)
        if st is not None or whole:
            return st
    return None


def _session_settled(sdir, now=None):
    """Agent ids under session dir `sdir` that the harness has recorded as ended:
    {id: ts_or_0}. Workflow journals (`result`/`failed` rows, keyed by bare agentId) settle
    unconditionally (ts 0); a parent task-notification settles an agent whose last record is not
    after it. Records after `now` are ignored."""
    out = {}
    wroot = os.path.join(sdir, "subagents", "workflows")
    try:
        runs = list(os.scandir(wroot))
    except OSError:
        runs = []
    for run in runs:
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(run.path, "journal.jsonl"), "rb") as f:
                for ln in f:
                    if b'"type":"result"' not in ln and b'"type":"failed"' not in ln:
                        continue
                    m = re.search(rb'"agentId":"([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)"', ln)
                    if not m:
                        continue
                    t = _RE_TS.search(ln)
                    ts = _iso_epoch(t.group(1)) if t else None
                    if now is not None and ts is not None and ts > now:
                        continue
                    out[m.group(1).decode()] = 0.0
        except OSError:
            continue
    parent = sdir + ".jsonl"
    try:
        size = os.path.getsize(parent)
        lines, _whole = _tail_lines(parent, size, KWORK_TAIL_BYTES[-1])
    except OSError:
        lines = []
    for ln in lines:
        if (b'"origin":{"kind":"task-notification"}' not in ln
                and b'"type":"queue-operation"' not in ln and b'"type":"queued_command"' not in ln):
            continue
        m = _RE_NOTIF.search(ln)
        if not m:
            continue
        t = _RE_TS.search(ln)
        ts = _iso_epoch(t.group(1)) if t else None
        if ts is None or (now is not None and ts > now):
            continue
        aid = m.group(1).decode()
        if out.get(aid) != 0.0:
            out[aid] = max(out.get(aid) or 0.0, ts)
    return out


def _file_working(path, mtime, size, now, window_s, sub=False, settled=None, turns=True):
    """THE predicate (see the block above). `settled` is a thunk returning _session_settled for
    this file's session, called only when a subagent is otherwise live."""
    age = now - mtime
    if not turns:
        return age <= window_s
    horizon_s = max(window_s, 60.0 * _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_KWORK_TOOLWAIT_MIN", KWORK_TOOLWAIT_MIN))
    if age > horizon_s:
        return False
    st = _last_turn(path, size, now)
    if st is None:
        return age <= window_s                      # no record to judge by: the old rule
    ts, waiting, answered = st
    if sub and answered:
        return False
    if ts is None:
        live = waiting or age <= window_s
    else:
        live = now - ts <= (horizon_s if waiting else window_s)
    if not live or not sub or settled is None:
        return live
    aid = os.path.basename(path)[len("agent-"):-len(".jsonl")]
    stop = settled().get(aid)
    if stop is None:
        return True
    return not (stop == 0.0 or ts is None or ts <= stop)


def working_concurrency(cfg, window_min=None, budget_s=None, now=None):
    """WORKING sessions per account: transcripts (projects/<slug>/*.jsonl) written within the
    window. concurrency() above counts every live interactive PROCESS — measured 28 on an
    account whose 5h window sat at 60%, i.e. mostly idle desks — so it over-states burn
    pressure exactly where panes accumulate. What the 5h window meters is sessions PRODUCING
    turns, and a producing session is appending to its transcript (active subagents append to
    their own .jsonl siblings and are burners too), so recently-written transcripts are the
    honest concurrent-burner census. Attribution mirrors concurrency()'s map exactly, incl.
    ~/.claude → the .claude-next account; realpath-deduped so a mirror symlink cannot count
    its target twice.

    Returns None — not {} — when the walk cannot finish inside budget_s: a partial count reads
    as 'nothing working', which is the dangerous direction (it would ADMIT a pile-on), so the
    consumer (k_eff) falls back to the stricter pane census instead.

    THE BUDGET, 5.0 -> 15.0 (2026-09-22, docs/research/routing-policy-ruling-2026-09-22.md §4).
    The 5.0 s sizing below reasons from the FOREGROUND walk and is correct about it — re-measured
    today it stats 4,269 transcripts across 3,706 dirs in 1.21 s. The walk does not run in the
    foreground. Its only caller is `com.claude.accounts-keepwarm`, a launchd job with
    `ProcessType Background`, and in that band the SAME walk measured 5.63 / 8.30 / 11.29 s —
    every sample over the old budget. So the bound was not ~70x the normal case, it was BELOW it:
    the walk timed out on 101 of 220 utilisation samples in 24 h (46%), and on each of those the
    active-concurrency gate silently vanished for that sweep and the router named the most-loaded
    account FIRST by 25x. A budget sized in the wrong QoS band is the repo's own
    `bound-must-fit-the-band-not-the-bench` lesson, arrived at independently.

    WHY 15 AND NOT MORE. The recycle's `--rank` call is bounded by `perl -e 'alarm 25'`
    (scripts/handoff-fire.sh), and this walk runs in a thread beside the four account probes, so a
    sweep costs ~max(probes 3-5 s, this budget). 15 keeps a >=10 s margin under that alarm; 30
    would trip it on a starved box and turn every loaded-hour recycle into a silent no-repick —
    i.e. it would break the very re-pick path this budget exists to feed. RESIDUAL: the extreme
    tail (walks reaching <500 dirs in 5 s) is the QoS band, not the budget. If the unmeasured
    fraction stays above 30%, the next lever is the plist's ProcessType — a launchd edit, operator-
    owned — never a router constant. PULLED 2026-09-24: the plist is ProcessType Standard, after a
    PRI-4 tick starved 3h40m at 0.04 s of CPU and left the SessionStart board withheld for hours.

    THE BUDGET, 2.0 -> 5.0 (W1.7, and it is not a "just raise the timeout" — the number was
    sized against the wrong distribution). The measured walk is 0.04-0.07 s over 826 transcripts,
    so 2.0 s is ~30x the normal case and 5.0 s is ~70x: neither is a real bound on healthy work,
    and the only walks the old bound cut were ones the box had already made pathological. What the
    cut BUYS is nothing — the fallback is the pane census, which R1 measured at 7 where the true
    working count was 2, i.e. the fallback excludes the account the routing goal most needs burned.
    The cost of raising it is bounded and paid on the SWEEP path only: `--max-wait 0` cache reads
    (the launcher's own call, `claude-launcher.zsh:59`) never reach this function at all, and a
    concurrent caller behind the sweep lock degrades to the grace cache rather than waiting. So
    the worst case is +3 s on a sweep that is already the slow path, in exchange for measuring the
    term instead of guessing it. Not unbounded: a genuinely hung filesystem still returns None,
    now loudly."""
    R = cfg.get("router") or {}
    window_s = 60.0 * (window_min if window_min is not None else
                       _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_KWORK_WINDOW_MIN",
                                  R.get("KWORK_WINDOW_MIN", KWORK_WINDOW_MIN)))
    if budget_s is None:
        budget_s = _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_KWORK_BUDGET_S",
                              R.get("KWORK_BUDGET_S", KWORK_BUDGET_S))
    deadline = time.monotonic() + budget_s
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    turns = _term_on("CC_ROUTE_KWORK_TURNS")
    settled_by_sdir = {}
    scanned = 0
    counts = {a["name"]: 0 for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    top = {a["name"]: 0 for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    sids = {a["name"]: set() for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    subs = {a["name"]: {} for a in cfg["accounts"]}    # {acct: {parent session id: n working}}
    by_dir = {a["config_dir"].rstrip("/"): a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    mirror_next = next((a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]
                        if a["config_dir"].rstrip("/").endswith(".claude-next")), None)
    if mirror_next is not None:
        by_dir.setdefault(os.path.join(HOME, ".claude"), mirror_next)
    seen = set()
    try:
        for d, name in by_dir.items():
            pdir = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(d), "projects")
            rp = os.path.realpath(pdir)
            if rp in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(rp)
            try:
                slugs = list(os.scandir(pdir))
            except OSError:
                continue
            for slug in slugs:
                if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                    return _kwork_unmeasured(budget_s, scanned)
                scanned += 1
                if not slug.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
                    continue
                # RECURSIVE, and the docstring above is why: it claims "active subagents append
                # to their own .jsonl siblings and are burners too". They are not siblings. A
                # subagent's transcript is written to
                # projects/<slug>/<session-uuid>/subagents/agent-*.jsonl, one level BELOW the
                # single-level scandir this replaced, so every one of them was invisible to the
                # census that names them. Measured 2026-09-04 under ~/.claude/projects: 582 files
                # at the shallow depth this walk used to see, 939 below it — the missed population
                # was the LARGER one, and it is the one that burns quota concurrently with its
                # parent. The deadline is still checked per directory AND per file, so the budget
                # (5.0s against a 0.04-0.15s walk) governs a deeper tree exactly as before, and an
                # over-budget walk still returns _kwork_unmeasured rather than a partial count.
                try:
                    for wroot, _wdirs, wfiles in os.walk(slug.path, followlinks=False):
                        if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                            return _kwork_unmeasured(budget_s, scanned)
                        for fname in wfiles:
                            if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                                return _kwork_unmeasured(budget_s, scanned)
                            if not fname.endswith(".jsonl"):
                                continue
                            # Below the session level only `agent-*.jsonl` is an agent. A
                            # Workflow also writes `subagents/workflows/<run>/journal.jsonl`
                            # (329 on disk, 2026-09-27) — run bookkeeping, not a burner — and
                            # counting it charged one phantom session per live workflow toward
                            # the KMAX exclusion.
                            if wroot != slug.path and not fname.startswith("agent-"):
                                continue
                            fpath = os.path.join(wroot, fname)
                            sub = wroot != slug.path
                            settled = None
                            if sub:
                                sdir = os.path.join(slug.path, os.path.relpath(
                                    wroot, slug.path).split(os.sep)[0])

                                def settled(_d=sdir):
                                    if _d not in settled_by_sdir:
                                        settled_by_sdir[_d] = _session_settled(_d, now)
                                    return settled_by_sdir[_d]
                            try:
                                st = os.lstat(fpath)
                                if _file_working(fpath, st.st_mtime, st.st_size, now, window_s,
                                                 sub=sub, settled=settled, turns=turns):
                                    counts[name] += 1
                                    if not sub:
                                        top[name] += 1
                                        sids[name].add(fname[:-len(".jsonl")])
                                    else:
                                        par = os.path.basename(sdir)
                                        subs[name][par] = subs[name].get(par, 0) + 1
                            except OSError:
                                continue
                except OSError:
                    continue
    except Exception as e:
        # Same instrument change as the budget path, different cause — and just as invisible
        # before this line: the sweep records k_work=None and the router silently re-charges
        # the pane census. Never fatal (a census is a valid, stricter answer), never silent.
        log_event(f"working_concurrency: walk aborted by {type(e).__name__}: {e} — k_work "
                  "UNMEASURED for this sweep, router charges the pane census")
        return None
    return _Census(counts, top=top, sids=sids, subs=subs, at=now)


# ---- usage endpoint (urllib in-process: no tokens in argv/ps) ---------------------------------

def _ssl_ctx():
    # python.org framework builds ship no CA bundle (CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED) — prefer
    # certifi, then the macOS system bundle, then whatever the default context finds.
    import ssl
    try:
        import certifi
        return ssl.create_default_context(cafile=certifi.where())
    except ImportError:
        pass
    if os.path.exists("/etc/ssl/cert.pem"):
        return ssl.create_default_context(cafile="/etc/ssl/cert.pem")
    return ssl.create_default_context()


SSL_CTX = None


def _ssl_warm():
    """Build both lazy urllib globals ON THE MAIN THREAD, before any fan-out.

    Two of them, and the second is the one that matters. (a) SSL_CTX is a lazy `global` in
    fetch_usage: four threads racing it is benign (an ssl.SSLContext is safe to share) but pays
    for four contexts and keeps whichever lands last. (b) urllib.request's module-global
    `_opener` is built lazily inside urlopen(), and on macOS build_opener() reaches
    getproxies_macosx_sysconf() — a SystemConfiguration FFI call. A concurrent FIRST build of
    that is the least-audited path in the whole fan-out, so it is simply never done concurrently:
    warm it once here and every worker finds it already installed."""
    global SSL_CTX
    if SSL_CTX is None:
        SSL_CTX = _ssl_ctx()
    if getattr(urllib.request, "_opener", None) is None:
        urllib.request.install_opener(urllib.request.build_opener())


def usage_429_retries():
    try:
        return max(0, int(os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_USAGE_429_RETRIES", "0")))
    except ValueError:
        return 0


def fetch_usage(cfg, token, retries=2):
    global SSL_CTX
    if SSL_CTX is None:
        SSL_CTX = _ssl_ctx()
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        cfg["usage_endpoint"],
        headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {token}",
                 "anthropic-beta": "oauth-2025-04-20",
                 "User-Agent": cfg.get("user_agent", "claude-cli")})
    for attempt in range(retries + 1):
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=12, context=SSL_CTX) as r:
                return r.status, json.loads(r.read().decode())
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
            body = {}
            try:
                body = json.loads(e.read().decode())
            except Exception:
                pass
            # NO 429 retry by default: the throttle is per token and sticky — a re-poll inside
            # 30 s is another 429 97.6% of the time (docs/research/quota-cache-freshness-
            # 2026-10-01 §2b) — so each retry spent the throttled token's budget for ~nothing
            # and made every 429 cost three requests. CC_ACCOUNTS_USAGE_429_RETRIES restores it.
            if e.code == 429 and attempt < min(retries, usage_429_retries()):
                # JITTER, and it is created by the fan-out, not inherited. Serially the four
                # calls self-stagger by ~340ms each, so a deterministic `1.5 * (attempt + 1)`
                # ladder never collided. Concurrently, four simultaneous 429s would back off by
                # the IDENTICAL interval and retry simultaneously, twice — a thundering herd
                # against the endpoint that just throttled us. Half-to-full randomisation keeps
                # the same expected delay while decorrelating the retries.
                time.sleep(1.5 * (attempt + 1) * (0.5 + random.random()))
                continue
            return e.code, body
        except (urllib.error.URLError, TimeoutError, json.JSONDecodeError, OSError):
            if attempt < retries:
                time.sleep(1.0 * (0.5 + random.random()))
                continue
            return None, None
    return None, None



# ---- the WIRE truth (anthropic-ratelimit-unified-* headers) ------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `/api/oauth/usage` reports each window as an INTEGER `percent`, and that
# integer is a THREE-WAY COLLAPSE at the top of the range: it rounds UP and it clamps at 100, so
# `weekly 100%` is the single rendering of "99% used, ~1pp left, server says allowed", "exactly
# 100%", and "102% used, in overage, server says rejected". Measured 2026-09-19 on all four
# accounts at once: `next` read `percent: 100` from the endpoint while the wire read
# `7d-utilization: 0.99  7d-status: allowed_warning` and a real request returned HTTP 200 — i.e.
# the fleet was excluding a WORKING account as `weekly-exhausted`. `next4` read `percent: 100` on
# the 5h window while the wire read `5h-utilization: 1.02  5h-status: rejected` (HTTP 429), which
# is the OTHER end of the same collapse: the endpoint cannot represent overage either. The
# binary's SDK doc says "values above 1 occur when usage legitimately runs past a window's cap";
# its "events are emitted when a window's rounded percentage or reset time moves" is about WHEN
# events fire and says nothing about the header's precision. The precision was measured instead
# (docs/research/sessionstart-readout-2026-10-06/README.md, R2): hundredths, rounded, held at 0.99
# until the server refuses — so a 0.99 reading bounds the remainder at roughly 1.5pp and can be 0.
#
# 1pp of a weekly window is not a rounding nicety. At the fitted exchange rate (K_FROZEN = 0.192
# weekly pp per session pp) it is up to ~5% of a whole 5-hour window (the in-band rate measured on
# 2026-10-06 was nearer 0.27, i.e. ~3.7%) — and it is worth MOST exactly when every meter reads 100
# and there is nothing else to route to.
# Measured over the recorded series, an account sits at a pinned `100` for a mean ~21 h before its
# weekly reset (max 56.5 h on `next`, 2026-09-13), hard-excluded from routing that entire time.
#
# THE ONE COST, STATED PLAINLY. These headers ride only on `/v1/messages` responses; the usage
# endpoint does not carry them and `/v1/messages/count_tokens` (measured) carries none either. So
# reading them means ONE minimal inference call — `max_tokens: 1` on the cheapest model, body
# discarded, headers kept. That is ~25 quota-bearing tokens against a weekly budget where 1pp is
# ~500k, i.e. ~4e-6 pp. It is nevertheless the first inference this tool has ever made, so it is
# GATED to the only band where the integer is ambiguous (WIRE_NEAR_WALL and up), forced only by
# `--wire`, and killable with CC_ACCOUNTS_WIRE=off. Away from the wall it never fires and the
# tool's cost is byte-identical to before.
#
# A 429 IS A SUCCESSFUL READ HERE, not a failure: the rejection carries the same headers and is
# precisely the case worth knowing about. Only a transport failure or a missing header set is a
# miss, and a miss degrades to exactly today's behaviour (the endpoint integer) — never to a
# guess, and never to a more permissive verdict than the endpoint would have given.

WIRE_MODEL = os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_WIRE_MODEL", "claude-haiku-5-5")
# THE BODY MUST CARRY CLAUDE CODE'S SYSTEM LINE, or the read is a silent miss on any current model.
# Measured 2026-10-07 on an OAuth token, `max_tokens: 1`: with no `system`, only Haiku 4.5 is served;
# claude-haiku-5-5, claude-sonnet-5-5 and claude-sonnet-4-6 answer 429 `rate_limit_error` carrying
# ZERO `anthropic-ratelimit-unified-*` headers, which fetch_wire_limits reads as "no reading" (None).
# With this line, claude-haiku-5-5 answers 200 with all 12 headers. A raw-API site resolves no
# alias, so the id is a full one; it moved off claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 at the Haiku 5.5 flip.
WIRE_SYSTEM = "You are Claude Code, Anthropic's official CLI for Claude."
WIRE_NEAR_WALL = 99.0      # endpoint percent at/above which the integer is ambiguous
WIRE_TIMEOUT_S = float(os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_WIRE_TIMEOUT_S", "15"))
WIRE_STATUSES = ("allowed", "allowed_warning", "rejected")
# Set once on the main thread from --wire, before any fan-out; read-only thereafter.
WIRE_FORCE = False
WIRE_FIELD = {"session_pct": "5h_util", "weekly_pct": "7d_util"}


def _wire_float(h, name):
    try:
        return float(h[name])
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def _wire_status(h, name):
    v = (h.get(name) or "").strip().lower()
    return v if v in WIRE_STATUSES else (v or None)


def fetch_wire_limits(cfg, token, timeout=None):
    """One minimal /v1/messages call -> the unified rate-limit headers, or None.

    Returns a dict with `5h_util` / `7d_util` as UNCLAMPED fractions (1.02 is a real answer) and
    `5h_status` / `7d_status` in {allowed, allowed_warning, rejected}. `status` is the account's
    currently-limiting verdict and `claim` names which window produced it.

    None means "could not read the wire" and callers MUST fall back to the endpoint integer. An
    HTTP error is not None: its headers are read and returned like any other."""
    if not token:
        return None
    global SSL_CTX
    if SSL_CTX is None:
        SSL_CTX = _ssl_ctx()
    body = json.dumps({
        "model": WIRE_MODEL, "max_tokens": 1, "system": WIRE_SYSTEM,
        "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "."}],
    }).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        cfg.get("messages_endpoint", "https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages"), data=body,
        headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {token}",
                 "anthropic-beta": "oauth-2025-04-20",
                 "anthropic-version": "2023-06-01",
                 "content-type": "application/json",
                 "User-Agent": cfg.get("user_agent", "claude-cli")})
    http = None
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout or WIRE_TIMEOUT_S,
                                    context=SSL_CTX) as r:
            http, hdrs = r.status, r.headers
            r.read()
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        http, hdrs = e.code, e.headers
        try:
            e.read()
        except Exception:
            pass
    except Exception:
        return None
    h = {k.lower(): v for k, v in (hdrs or {}).items()
         if k.lower().startswith("anthropic-ratelimit-unified-")}
    if not h:
        return None                              # no header set == no reading, never a guess
    w = {
        "http": http,
        "5h_util": _wire_float(h, "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-5h-utilization"),
        "7d_util": _wire_float(h, "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-utilization"),
        "5h_status": _wire_status(h, "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-5h-status"),
        "7d_status": _wire_status(h, "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-status"),
        "status": _wire_status(h, "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-status"),
        "claim": (h.get("anthropic-ratelimit-unified-representative-claim") or None),
        # the lower-priority ("fallback") lane the binary offers past a wall, and the overage
        # channel. Both are recorded because either one can make a `rejected` window still
        # serve work, and neither is visible from the usage endpoint at all.
        "fallback": (h.get("anthropic-ratelimit-unified-fallback") or None),
        "overage_status": _wire_status(h, "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-overage-status"),
    }
    if w["5h_util"] is None and w["7d_util"] is None and w["status"] is None:
        return None
    return w


def wire_enabled():
    return (os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_WIRE") or "").strip().lower() not in ("off", "0", "false")


def wire_wanted(sp, wp, force=False):
    """Is the endpoint integer AMBIGUOUS for this row? Only then is the probe worth its call."""
    if not wire_enabled():
        return False
    if force:
        return True
    return any(v is not None and v >= WIRE_NEAR_WALL for v in (sp, wp))


def weekly_used_frac(r):
    """Best-known weekly utilization as a fraction — wire when we have it, endpoint otherwise.

    UNCLAMPED on purpose: a wire 1.02 must stay 1.02 so `1 - used` is a real 0, not a rounded one."""
    w = r.get("wire") or {}
    if w.get("7d_util") is not None:
        return w["7d_util"]
    wp = r.get("weekly_pct")
    return None if wp is None else wp / 100.0


def weekly_headroom(r):
    """Weekly headroom as a fraction, from the best-known used figure. `None` when unknowable.

    The 0.98 target when usage credits are ON is unchanged and stays keyed on the same field; only
    the USED side gains resolution. At the wall that is the whole difference between a routable
    account and an excluded one: endpoint 100 gives 0.00 headroom and hard-excludes, while a wire
    0.99 gives 0.01 — above WEEKLY_FLOOR (0.005) — and the account keeps its last ~1pp."""
    used = weekly_used_frac(r)
    if used is None:
        return None
    return max(0.0, (0.98 if r.get("credits_on") else 1.00) - used)


def wire_rejects(r, window="7d"):
    """True only when the SERVER said it would refuse this window. Absent wire -> False: this
    predicate may only ever ADD a refusal the endpoint could not see, never invent one."""
    w = r.get("wire") or {}
    return w.get(f"{window}_status") == "rejected"

# ---- heal (delegated to the official binary; rotation-safe) -----------------------------------

def heal(cfg, acct, refresh_token, k_live):
    """Headless re-auth via `claude auth login` with the refresh token in env. The binary does
    the refresh grant AND persists the (possibly rotated) tokens to the same keychain item.
    Gated: only when the account has ZERO live sessions (no rotation race with a running CC) and
    under a per-account lock. Returns (ok, detail)."""
    # `k_live is None` = the sweep took no census: ps failed and, in `census: ab` mode, the native
    # census did too (gate_counts). Refuse BEFORE taking the lock, for the same reason the
    # under-lock re-check refuses: this gate is proven or it is not held.
    if k_live is None:
        return False, "skipped: live-session count UNMEASURABLE (no census) — CC may own the token"
    if k_live > 0:
        return False, f"skipped: {k_live} live session(s) — CC owns the token lifecycle"
    if not refresh_token:
        return False, "skipped: no refresh token in keychain"
    cbin = cfg["claude_bin"]
    if not os.path.exists(cbin):
        return False, f"skipped: claude binary missing at {cbin}"
    lock_path = f"/tmp/claude-accounts-heal-{acct['name']}.lock"
    with open(lock_path, "w") as lock:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except BlockingIOError:
            return False, "skipped: another heal in flight"
        # Re-check liveness UNDER the lock. k_live came from a single ps snapshot taken at
        # the start of the sweep, which can be minutes old by the time this account's turn
        # arrives (each earlier account may burn a 90s heal plus two retry ladders). This
        # gate is the rotation-safety invariant — a refresh racing a running CC can rotate
        # the token out from under it, and a discarded rotation means logged out — so it
        # must be evaluated against current state, not a stale reading.
        # UNKNOWN refuses. gate_census() returns None when no census could be taken, and this gate
        # is an invariant that must be PROVEN, not assumed: "I could not look" is not "there is
        # nobody there". The old all-zero return made an unreadable `ps` indistinguishable from a
        # quiet fleet and let the redeem proceed into a live rotation race (07 §6.5).
        live_now = gate_census(cfg)
        if live_now is None:
            return False, "skipped: live-session count UNMEASURABLE (no census) — refusing to " \
                          "redeem a refresh token that may be rotating under live sessions"
        if live_now.get(acct["name"], 0) > 0:
            return False, "skipped: a session started during the sweep — CC owns the token"
        env = os.environ.copy()
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = acct["config_dir"]
        env["CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN"] = refresh_token
        env["CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_SCOPES"] = cfg["oauth_scopes"]
        try:
            p = subprocess.run([cbin, "auth", "login"], env=env, capture_output=True,
                               text=True, timeout=90)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            log_event(f"heal {acct['name']}: TIMEOUT")
            return False, "heal timed out"
        out = (p.stdout + p.stderr).strip()
        ok = p.returncode == 0 and "Login successful" in out
        detail = out.splitlines()[-1] if out else f"rc={p.returncode}"
        log_event(f"heal {acct['name']}: {'OK' if ok else 'FAIL'} — {detail}")
        return ok, detail


_HEAL_STATUS_RE = re.compile(r"status code (\d{3})")
_HEAL_INVALID_RE = re.compile(
    r"invalid[_ ]?(grant|token|credential|refresh)"      # invalid_grant · "Invalid refresh token"
    r"|(grant|token|credential)\s+(is\s+)?invalid")      # ...and the reversed phrasing


def _heal_rejected(detail):
    """Was the refresh grant REJECTED (only an interactive /login recovers), or did the heal
    merely not happen (skipped / timed out / transport / server-side)?

    The old test was a substring hunt for "invalid"/"revoked" in the official binary's
    message. The message that binary actually emits for a dead refresh token contains
    NEITHER word — observed live on next3, 2026-07-24:

        Login failed: Request failed with status code 400

    so the row stayed auth="stale", whose documented reading is *benign*, and an account that
    genuinely needed /login was reported as fine. Classify on the HTTP status the string
    carries instead: per RFC 6749 §5.2 the token endpoint answers a bad, expired or revoked
    refresh token with 400 invalid_grant, and rejects bad client auth with 401. A 5xx, a 429
    or a timeout is transport, not a verdict — never terminal, or one flaky minute would
    demand an interactive login the account does not need."""
    d = (detail or "").lower()
    if d.startswith("skipped:") or "timed out" in d:
        return False
    # "invalid" must qualify a CREDENTIAL, not anything at all. The predecessor tested for a
    # bare substring, which fires on ordinary transport noise — "Invalid response from server",
    # "invalid JSON" — and the verdict it now reaches is "your account needs an interactive
    # login", so a false positive sends the operator at a problem that does not exist. Match
    # the OAuth error code and the credential nouns; leave every other use of the word alone.
    if _HEAL_INVALID_RE.search(d) or "revoked" in d:
        return True
    m = _HEAL_STATUS_RE.search(d)
    return bool(m and m.group(1) in ("400", "401", "403"))


# ---- collection ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def _iso_ms(ms):
    """Epoch MILLISECONDS (the keychain payload's units) → ISO-8601 UTC, else None."""
    if not ms:
        return None
    try:
        return datetime.fromtimestamp(float(ms) / 1000.0, timezone.utc).isoformat()
    except (ValueError, TypeError, OSError, OverflowError):
        return None


def _expired_ms(ms):
    """Has a millisecond stamp passed? None when there is NO stamp — an absent expiry is not
    an unexpired one, and collapsing the two would report a payload that never carried the
    field (an older CC, an API-key blob) as safely in-date forever."""
    if not ms:
        return None
    try:
        return float(ms) / 1000.0 <= time.time()
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return None


def hrs_until(iso):
    if not iso:
        return None
    try:
        dt = datetime.fromisoformat(str(iso).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
        if dt.tzinfo is None:
            dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
        return (dt - datetime.now(timezone.utc)).total_seconds() / 3600.0
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return None


def pick(u, kind, scope_name=None):
    for lm in (u.get("limits") or []):
        if lm.get("kind") != kind:
            continue
        if scope_name and ((lm.get("scope") or {}).get("model") or {}).get("display_name") != scope_name:
            continue
        return lm.get("percent"), lm.get("resets_at")
    return None, None


# ---- usage-schema drift ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `pick()` answers (None, None) for a kind the payload does not carry, and that answer is
# byte-identical to "this account genuinely has no such cap". Five of the endpoint's ~40 fields are
# read here, so a RENAMED kind — or a per-model sub-cap switched on under a name we do not model —
# lands as a clean exclusion: `_excluded` says "no-session-data", which names the SYMPTOM and says
# nothing about the cause, and a new sub-cap says nothing at all. These two lists make the cause
# legible. Neither of them moves anyone's eligibility; see `limits_drift`.
KNOWN_LIMIT_KINDS = frozenset(("session", "weekly_all", "weekly_scoped"))
# Only `session` is load-bearing for the error: `weekly_scoped` is legitimately absent for an
# account with no frontier scope, and `weekly_all` is recorded but left routable on purpose — see
# `limits_drift`.
REQUIRED_LIMIT_KINDS = ("session", "weekly_all")


def limits_drift(row, u, name=""):
    """Record which limit kinds the payload actually carried, against the ones we model.

    The error is set ONLY where the row was already unroutable for the SAME reason: a missing
    `session` limit is `_excluded`'s "no-session-data" either way (and `reason_class` keeps it
    'data' because `"error" in row`), so this changes the WORDS and never the eligibility set. A
    missing `weekly_all`, or an unmodeled kind, is recorded and left routable on purpose — if the
    endpoint renames weekly on all four accounts, erroring here would empty the fleet, which is a
    far worse failure than the silence it replaces.

    The log line fires only for an UNMODELED kind, which is the half no row field is rendered for
    and the half that changes what the fleet may spend. A missing kind is already visible in
    --json and (for `session`) in the table's error column, and gating the line this way keeps a
    fixture that simply omits a kind from writing into the real log (LOG_PATH resolves at import;
    only some suites redirect it)."""
    kinds = [lm.get("kind") for lm in (u.get("limits") or []) if isinstance(lm, dict)]
    unmodeled = sorted({str(k) for k in kinds if k not in KNOWN_LIMIT_KINDS})
    missing = [k for k in REQUIRED_LIMIT_KINDS if k not in kinds]
    if not (unmodeled or missing):
        return
    row["limits_unmodeled"] = unmodeled
    row["limits_missing"] = missing
    seen = ",".join(str(k) for k in kinds) or "none"
    if unmodeled:
        log_event(f"probe {name}: usage-schema drift — unmodeled limit kind(s) "
                  f"{','.join(unmodeled)} (payload kinds: {seen}). Routing is UNCHANGED; a new "
                  f"per-model sub-cap is not read by pick() until it is modeled here.")
    if "session" in missing and "error" not in row:
        row["error"] = f"usage schema drift: no session limit (kinds: {seen})"


# ---- last-good quota ledger (durable stale-quota visibility; NEVER influences routing) --------
# The oauth/usage endpoint yields quota ONLY on a 200. Every other outcome — logged-out /
# token-invalid / keychain-error / expired-stale-401 / no-data / 429 poll-throttle — leaves the
# row quota-blank and _excluded() drops it from routing. That exclusion is CORRECT and stays; the
# gap is that the STRANDED quota (how much weekly an unreachable account still holds) vanishes from
# --json / the table / a handoff successor. This ledger records the last good numbers per account
# so those surfaces can show "next3 logged-out — last-known weekly 31% @ 09:40" while the router
# still excludes the row (its error field is untouched). Generalizes the 429 poll-throttle's
# in-memory `prev` inheritance (≤ cache TTL) to a durable, TTL-free store.


def set_credits(row, is_enabled, used_credits):
    """Stamp the extra-usage (pay-as-you-go) fields on a row, in agreed units.

    `extra_usage.used_credits` from the oauth endpoint is denominated in CENTS, not in
    "credits" — verified 2026-07-26 against the claude.ai Usage panel, where an endpoint
    reading of 17691 rendered as "$176.91 spent". Every consumer that showed the raw number
    was therefore reporting a dollar figure inflated 100x with no unit label, so the derived
    `credits_used_usd` is emitted alongside it and is what human-facing surfaces must use.

    `credits_on` (is_enabled) is the TOGGLE, and it is NOT a proxy for whether money moved:
    the same 2026-07-26 account read false while $176.91 sat on the meter. Spend is
    `credits_used > 0`, independent of the toggle — see the alert in render_table().
    """
    row["credits_on"] = bool(is_enabled)
    row["credits_used"] = used_credits or 0.0
    row["credits_used_usd"] = row["credits_used"] / 100.0


def _rejected_path():
    """Sibling of the last-good ledger, DERIVED AT CALL TIME rather than bound at import.

    Not a style choice: every caller that isolates this module — the bats corpus, any scratch
    SSOT — redirects it by assigning `ca.LASTGOOD_PATH`. A module-level constant computed from
    the import-time value would keep pointing at the REAL ledger through all of that, so a test
    asserting on rejection state would read and write the live fleet's file and leak state
    between cases. Deriving here makes one override redirect both stores, which is the property
    the existing harness already assumes.

    Derived by EXTENSION, never by substring. The obvious spelling —
    `LASTGOOD_PATH.replace("-lastgood", "-rejected")` — is a silent no-op for any ledger whose
    basename lacks that exact hyphenated token, and the bats corpus uses
    `$BATS_TEST_TMPDIR/lastgood.json` (a slash, not a hyphen). The two stores then resolve to
    ONE file and the rejection ledger overwrites the quota ledger. Splitting the extension
    cannot collide for any input."""
    stem, ext = os.path.splitext(LASTGOOD_PATH)
    return f"{stem}-rejected{ext or '.json'}"


def _rt_fingerprint(refresh_token):
    """A refresh token identified WITHOUT being stored. sha256 prefix only — this file is
    world-readable-ish plain JSON in the logs dir, so it must never be able to leak a grant."""
    if not refresh_token:
        return None
    return sha256(refresh_token.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]


def _load_rejected():
    """-> {acct: {"rt": <fingerprint>, "ts": <iso>, "detail": <str>}}.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `_heal_rejected()` already decides correctly that a 400 is terminal —
    only an interactive /login recovers it — but that verdict lived in two keys on an in-memory
    row behind a 90-second cache and then evaporated. Two consequences, both measured on next2
    2026-08-03:

      * Nothing suppressed the retry. Every later sweep recomputed `stale` from expiresAt, found
        no memory of the rejection, and burned another 90s `auth login` against a token already
        proven dead — 20 identical 400s in 95 minutes.
      * Nothing could ESCALATE it. The recovery tool (`cc-relogin`) measures with `--no-heal` on
        purpose, so `heal()` never runs for it, so `_heal_rejected()` is never reached, so the
        row reads `auth="stale"` — benign — and `need_relogin()` answered *"healthy (auth=stale,
        login_expires_h=674.6)"* and refused to act, twice, while the account was unusable. The
        trigger and its own remedy were asking different questions about the same account.

    Keying on the refresh token's FINGERPRINT is what keeps this self-clearing rather than
    sticky. The record means "THIS grant was rejected", not "this account is broken": the moment
    the keychain holds a different refresh token — an operator /login, a successful heal, or a
    live session's own rotation — the fingerprint stops matching and the record is inert. A
    plain boolean flag would have needed someone to remember to clear it, and would have pinned
    a false `login-required` on an account that had already fixed itself (next2 did exactly that
    at 01:20:28Z, in-session, with no human)."""
    try:
        with open(_rejected_path()) as f:
            data = json.load(f)
        return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return {}


def _save_rejected(ledger):
    """Atomic replace; best-effort — a failed persist must never break a sweep.

    The temp name carries the THREAD ident as well as the pid. A pid-only name is unique across
    processes and identical across threads, so two concurrent savers opened the same temp path:
    the second `open(..., "w")` truncated the first's half-written bytes and both then
    os.replace'd one file, renaming a truncated or interleaved JSON into place (and the losing
    replace raised into the OSError swallow below, so it failed SILENTLY). Harmless while
    collect() was serial; a real corruption path the moment the sweep fans out."""
    try:
        path = _rejected_path()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.{threading.get_ident()}.tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(ledger, f, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError:
        pass


def _mark_rejected(acct_name, refresh_token, detail):
    # The LOCK spans load -> mutate -> save, not just the save: the lost update happens in the
    # gap between the read and the write, so guarding only _save_rejected would still let two
    # threads read the same pre-state and have the last writer replay it.
    with _REJECTED_LOCK:
        led = _load_rejected()
        led[acct_name] = {"rt": _rt_fingerprint(refresh_token), "detail": detail,
                          "ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())}
        _save_rejected(led)


def _clear_rejected(acct_name):
    with _REJECTED_LOCK:
        led = _load_rejected()
        if acct_name in led:
            del led[acct_name]
            _save_rejected(led)


def _rejected_for(acct_name, refresh_token):
    """The live record for THIS credential, or None. Returns None (and does not clear) when the
    fingerprint has moved on — the read side treats a superseded record as absent, and the next
    successful sweep removes it."""
    rec = _load_rejected().get(acct_name)
    if not rec:
        return None
    fp = _rt_fingerprint(refresh_token)
    return rec if (fp and rec.get("rt") == fp) else None


def load_lastgood():
    """-> {acct: {quota fields + quota_as_of}}. Never raises (missing/corrupt ⇒ {})."""
    try:
        with open(LASTGOOD_PATH) as f:
            data = json.load(f)
        return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return {}


def save_lastgood(ledger):
    """MERGE into the ledger on disk, under a flock; best-effort (a failed persist must never
    break a sweep). Two processes now write it — a sweep and the local pass's first read
    (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D4/D6) — so a plain replace of the in-memory copy a sweep loaded at
    its start would revert a first read that landed meanwhile. Per account, the entry with the
    newer `quota_as_of` wins; an equal stamp keeps the caller's (it carries this sweep's fields)."""
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(LASTGOOD_PATH), exist_ok=True)
    except OSError:
        return
    fd = _flock_open(LASTGOOD_PATH + ".lock", LASTGOOD_LOCK_WAIT_S)
    try:
        merged = load_lastgood()
        for acct, e in ledger.items():
            d = merged.get(acct)
            te = _as_epoch((e or {}).get("quota_as_of"))
            td = _as_epoch((d or {}).get("quota_as_of")) if isinstance(d, dict) else None
            if td is None or (te is not None and te >= td):
                merged[acct] = e
        # Same pid-is-not-unique-across-threads reason as _save_rejected. Called once per sweep
        # from the MAIN thread today, so this is prophylactic — but the two savers must not
        # differ, or the next caller to move inside the pool re-acquires the defect.
        tmp = f"{LASTGOOD_PATH}.{os.getpid()}.{threading.get_ident()}.tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(merged, f, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
        os.replace(tmp, LASTGOOD_PATH)
    except OSError:
        pass
    finally:
        _flock_release(fd)


LASTGOOD_LOCK_WAIT_S = 10.0


def _flock_open(path, wait_s):
    """-> an fd holding LOCK_EX on `path`, or None when it was not acquired within `wait_s`
    seconds (0 = one non-blocking try) or the file cannot be opened. Never raises."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o600)
    except OSError:
        return None
    deadline = time.monotonic() + max(0.0, wait_s)
    while True:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            return fd
        except BlockingIOError:
            if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                os.close(fd)
                return None
            time.sleep(0.05)
        except OSError:
            os.close(fd)
            return None


def _flock_release(fd):
    if fd is None:
        return
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
    except OSError:
        pass
    finally:
        try:
            os.close(fd)
        except OSError:
            pass


def inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev):
    """Merge an account's last-good quota into a quota-absent row — for VISIBILITY, with ONE
    routing exception. NEVER touches row['error'] → _excluded() still bails on it, EXCEPT for a
    429 poll-throttle whose inherited read is younger than THROTTLE_ADMIT_S (_throttle_admitted):
    that one row routes on these percents. `lastgood_age_s` records the read's age at sweep time
    for display; the admission recomputes it from `quota_as_of`, since a served cache ages.
    Absolute reset stamps are re-derived to *_reset_h at
    read time, so a day-old ledger shows a correct (possibly already-elapsed) countdown, not a
    frozen one. Prefers the durable ledger; falls back to the ≤TTL in-memory `prev` snapshot
    (percent-only) for the window before the first good sweep populates the ledger. Returns True
    if any quota was inherited."""
    e = ledger.get(row["acct"])
    if e and e.get("weekly_pct") is not None:
        # Per-bucket validation against that bucket's OWN reset stamp. A percent whose reset
        # has already passed is provably obsolete — that window rolled over to ~0 — so it must
        # not be presented as "last-known". The rollover is itself the useful fact: a stranded
        # account whose weekly has since reset is likely FRESH, which is exactly what decides
        # whether to bother relogging in. So record it; never blank the row (that would clear
        # stale_quota and leave the table with nothing to render at all).
        # A banked /limit-reset keeps the weekly reset DAY, so no stamp ever passes for it. That
        # case is caught by the READ, not here: a fresh usage poll reads the post-reset figure, and
        # a throttled one fires the wire (the stale reading is >= WIRE_SUBSTITUTE_PCT), whose lower
        # figure apply_wire_substitute takes as the reset evidence. No flag, no human, no agent.
        evidence = reset_evidence(e, row["acct"])
        rolled = []
        for pfx in ("session", "weekly", "fable"):
            stamp = e.get(f"{pfx}_reset_at")
            h = hrs_until(stamp)
            if pfx in evidence:
                row[f"{pfx}_pct"] = None
                # a passed stamp is void (a future one is kept, should evidence ever not be a stamp)
                keep = h is not None and h > 0
                row[f"{pfx}_reset_at"] = stamp if keep else None
                row[f"{pfx}_reset_h"] = h if keep else None
                rolled.append(pfx)
            else:
                row[f"{pfx}_pct"] = e.get(f"{pfx}_pct")
                row[f"{pfx}_reset_at"] = stamp
                row[f"{pfx}_reset_h"] = h
        if rolled:
            row["rolled_since"] = rolled
            row["reset_evidence"] = [evidence[p] for p in rolled]
        set_credits(row, e.get("credits_on"), e.get("credits_used"))
        row["stale_quota"] = True
        row["quota_as_of"] = e.get("quota_as_of")
        row["lastgood_age_s"] = lastgood_age_s(row)
        # A REJECTED wire verdict from the last good read rides along (never an `allowed` one —
        # see the ledger write): it can only veto a throttle admission, never enable one. It
        # does NOT outlive a reset of its own window: the verdict was about usage the reset
        # voided, and replaying it is what kept a freshly redeemed account "capped" on
        # 2026-10-01. Only positive evidence (reset_evidence) drops it; nothing else does.
        void = {"7d": "weekly", "5h": "session"}
        rej = [w for w in (e.get("wire_rejects") or []) if void.get(w) not in rolled]
        if rej:
            row["lastgood_wire_rejects"] = rej
        return True
    pr = (prev or {}).get("rows", {}).get(row["acct"])
    if pr and pr.get("weekly_pct") is not None:
        for f in ("session_pct", "weekly_pct", "fable_pct"):
            if pr.get(f) is not None:
                row[f] = pr[f]
        ts = (prev or {}).get("ts")
        row["stale_quota"] = True
        # Provenance must survive re-inheritance. get_data writes the inherited row back into
        # the cache and _prev_snapshot reads rows back out of it, so an account with no ledger
        # entry re-inherits its OWN previously-inherited percents every sweep. Stamping
        # prev["ts"] unconditionally re-dated that data to "just now" on each pass, so numbers
        # of any age reported as fresh, forever. Carry the original stamp forward; only a row
        # that was genuinely LIVE in that snapshot may be dated by the snapshot's own time.
        row["quota_as_of"] = (
            pr.get("quota_as_of") if pr.get("stale_quota")
            else (datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, timezone.utc).isoformat() if ts else None))
        row["lastgood_age_s"] = lastgood_age_s(row)
        return True
    return False


def lastgood_age_s(row):
    """Seconds since the row's inherited read (`quota_as_of`), NOW. None when undatable or dated
    in the future — an age we cannot state is never young enough to route on."""
    h = hrs_until(row.get("quota_as_of"))
    if h is None or h * 3600.0 > K_SKEW_TOL_S:   # same 1 s rounding slack inherit_k allows
        return None
    return round(max(0.0, -h * 3600.0), 1)


# ---- FRESHNESS: the 429 backoff, reset evidence, and the wire as the substitute read ----------
# docs/research/quota-cache-freshness-2026-10-01/ (receipts q1-output.txt, q2-output.txt). The
# 2026-10-01 incident: next4's banked reset was redeemed, every poll for five minutes was a 429,
# and every reader replayed "weekly 100%, server rejected" off the ledger. Measured:
#   · the 429 budget is PER TOKEN (1,186 of 1,373 throttle clusters hit exactly one account), so
#     there is no cross-account budget to re-spend — only a throttled token's own to stop wasting;
#   · it is STICKY: a re-poll of the same account within 30 s is another 429 97.6% of the time,
#     and an episode lasts p50 7.3 min, p90 91 min. In-call retries and `--fresh` both spend the
#     budget for ~nothing, so every caller now shares one per-account backoff instead;
#   · `/v1/messages` is a different endpoint, and its rate-limit headers (the WIRE) carry the
#     server's own live verdict — the one read a usage-endpoint throttle does not cover.
# Sidecars of the lastgood ledger, so every override of that path (tests, scratch SSOTs) moves
# them too and nothing here can touch the real files from a hermetic run.
USAGE_BACKOFF_BASE_S = 180.0      # one keepwarm interval
USAGE_BACKOFF_CAP_S = 600.0       # past 5 min the next poll's odds stop improving with the gap
WIRE_SUBSTITUTE_PCT = 90.0        # a throttled reading this close to a wall is worth a wire read
_POLLSTATE_LOCK = threading.Lock()


def _sidecar(suffix):
    return os.path.splitext(LASTGOOD_PATH)[0] + suffix


def _load_pollstate():
    try:
        with open(_sidecar(".pollstate.json")) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
        return d if isinstance(d, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def _save_pollstate(d):
    path = _sidecar(".pollstate.json")
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.{threading.get_ident()}.tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(d, f, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError:
        pass


def usage_backoff_s(streak):
    """Seconds every caller holds off an account's usage poll after its `streak`-th 429 in a row.
    CC_ACCOUNTS_USAGE_BACKOFF_S overrides the base; 0 turns the backoff off."""
    try:
        base = float(os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_USAGE_BACKOFF_S", USAGE_BACKOFF_BASE_S))
    except ValueError:
        base = USAGE_BACKOFF_BASE_S
    if not (base > 0 and math.isfinite(base)):
        return 0.0
    return min(base * 2 ** max(0, streak - 1), max(base, USAGE_BACKOFF_CAP_S))


def usage_backoff_left(acct):
    """Seconds left on this account's shared 429 backoff (0 = poll it)."""
    rec = _load_pollstate().get(acct) or {}
    try:
        until = float(rec.get("until") or 0)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return 0.0
    if usage_backoff_s(1) <= 0:
        return 0.0
    return max(0.0, until - time.time())


def note_usage_429(acct):
    # The threading.Lock serialises this process's threads; the flock serialises PROCESSES — a
    # sweep and the local pass's first read can now both note a 429 (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D4),
    # and an unlocked load-modify-save drops whichever lands first.
    with _POLLSTATE_LOCK:
        fd = _flock_open(_sidecar(".pollstate.json") + ".lock", POLLSTATE_LOCK_WAIT_S)
        try:
            d = _load_pollstate()
            rec = d.get(acct) or {}
            streak = int(rec.get("streak") or 0) + 1
            d[acct] = {"streak": streak, "at": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(),
                       "until": time.time() + usage_backoff_s(streak)}
            _save_pollstate(d)
        finally:
            _flock_release(fd)
    return d[acct]


def note_usage_ok(acct):
    with _POLLSTATE_LOCK:
        fd = _flock_open(_sidecar(".pollstate.json") + ".lock", POLLSTATE_LOCK_WAIT_S)
        try:
            d = _load_pollstate()
            if acct in d:
                del d[acct]
                _save_pollstate(d)
        finally:
            _flock_release(fd)


POLLSTATE_LOCK_WAIT_S = 5.0


def _reset_evidence_items(entry, acct):
    """-> {bucket: (epoch the evidence arose, text)} for bucket in session/weekly/fable.

    Stamps only. An UNSCHEDULED reset (a banked /limit-reset redemption, which leaves no local
    record) has no stamp to pass; it is detected by the next read instead — a usage poll, or the
    wire substitute a throttled near-wall reading always fires (apply_wire_substitute). Until
    2026-10-01 W2 a manual flag recorded it here; it was removed because the read already does
    the job with no one having to remember to run anything. `acct` is kept for callers."""
    out = {}
    for pfx in ("session", "weekly", "fable"):
        stamp = entry.get(f"{pfx}_reset_at")
        h = hrs_until(stamp)
        if stamp and h is not None and h <= 0:
            out[pfx] = (time.time() + h * 3600.0,
                        f"{pfx} window reset at {_fmt_asof(stamp) or stamp}")
    return out


def reset_evidence(entry, acct):
    """Which buckets of a last-good `entry` are KNOWN to be pre-reset, and why.

    -> ({bucket: evidence}) for bucket in session/weekly/fable. Only POSITIVE evidence counts: a
    reset stamp that has passed. Absence of evidence is not evidence of a reset, so an unknown
    stays exactly what it was; a fresh wire read is the other positive form, applied per sweep
    by apply_wire_substitute rather than stored."""
    return {b: text for b, (_at, text) in _reset_evidence_items(entry, acct).items()}


def reset_evidence_pending(cfg):
    """Accounts whose reset evidence arose AFTER the shared cache was written — i.e. the cache now
    serves a reading it does not know is pre-reset. Keepwarm sweeps at once for those instead of
    serving it. Evidence older than the cache is already folded into it (the sweep that wrote it
    applied the same rule), so an account whose stamp passed days ago — a logged-out one that
    cannot be re-read — does not force a sweep on every tick."""
    led = load_lastgood()
    age = _quota_age_s(cfg)
    cache_at = None if age is None else time.time() - age
    out = []
    for a in cfg.get("accounts", []):
        e = led.get(a["name"])
        if not e:
            continue
        items = _reset_evidence_items(e, a["name"])
        if items and (cache_at is None or any(at > cache_at for at, _t in items.values())):
            out.append(a["name"])
    return sorted(out)


def wire_substitute_wanted(row):
    """Is a throttled row's verdict worth one /v1/messages read? Only where it decides something:
    a stored rejection, evidence of a reset since the reading, or a reading near a wall."""
    if row.get("lastgood_wire_rejects") or row.get("rolled_since"):
        return True
    return any((row.get(k) or 0) >= WIRE_SUBSTITUTE_PCT for k in ("session_pct", "weekly_pct"))


def apply_wire_substitute(row, w):
    """Fold a FRESH wire read into a throttled row. The server's live verdict outranks the stored
    one in both directions: a window it still rejects stays rejected, a window it no longer
    rejects drops the stored rejection — positive evidence, read this second. A meter blanked by
    a reset takes the wire's figure, so the row can be routed on a reading instead of a guess."""
    row["wire"] = w
    row["wire_substitute"] = True
    row["wire_at"] = datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()
    rej = list(row.get("lastgood_wire_rejects") or [])
    keep = [win for win in rej if w.get(f"{win}_status") in (None, "rejected")]
    for win in rej:
        if win not in keep:
            row.setdefault("reset_evidence", []).append(
                f"server now allows {win} (wire {w.get(f'{win}_util')})")
    if keep:
        row["lastgood_wire_rejects"] = keep
    else:
        row.pop("lastgood_wire_rejects", None)
    # Usage only falls when a window resets, so the server reading a window materially LOWER than
    # the stored figure is itself positive evidence of a reset — the redemption case, whose weekly
    # stamp never passes. The endpoint integer rounds up by <1 pp, hence the 5 pp margin.
    for key, wkey in WIRE_FIELD.items():
        wv = w.get(wkey)
        if wv is None:
            continue
        old = row.get(key)
        if old is not None and wv * 100 <= old - 5:
            bucket = key.split("_")[0]
            row.setdefault("rolled_since", [])
            if bucket not in row["rolled_since"]:
                row["rolled_since"].append(bucket)
            row.setdefault("reset_evidence", []).append(
                f"server reads {bucket} at {wv * 100:.0f}% (stored {old:.0f}%)")
            old = None
        if old is None:
            row[key] = min(100, round(wv * 100))
    # A Fable figure older than the admission bound cannot ride along on a wire admission (the
    # wire carries no Fable window), so it is withheld: unknown is refused on the Fable lane.
    age = lastgood_age_s(row)
    if row.get("fable_pct") is not None and (age is None or age > throttle_admit_s()):
        row["fable_pct"] = None
        row["fable_unread"] = True


# ---- RESET DETECTION TELEMETRY (QUOTA_CACHE_FRESHNESS W2) ------------------------------------
# A reset — scheduled (its reset_at passed) or unscheduled (a banked /limit-reset) — is detected
# by a READ with no flag and nobody's action. This records each detection so "has it been fast?"
# is answerable later: one JSON line when a window's reading falls from >= RESET_HIGH_PCT to below
# RESET_LOW_PCT. Written from collect() only, i.e. from a sweep; it is a file write and never a
# network call, so nothing here touches session startup. The watch state (the last >= 99 reading
# per account and window, plus counters) lives in a lastgood sidecar; deleting the entry when an
# event is written is what makes one reset log once.
RESET_HIGH_PCT = 99.0
RESET_LOW_PCT = 90.0
RESET_WINDOWS = (("5h", "session"), ("7d", "weekly"))


def _reset_log_path():
    explicit = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_RESET_LOG")
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    # A redirected ledger takes its reset log with it (as the watch-state sidecar already does),
    # so a test can never write a fixture into the operator's record of real resets.
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_LASTGOOD"):
        return os.path.splitext(LASTGOOD_PATH)[0] + ".reset-detect.jsonl"
    logs = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "logs")
    # Same shape as _lastgood_path(): a scratch SSOT gets its own sibling.
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON"):
        h = sha256(os.path.realpath(CFG_PATH).encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
        return os.path.join(logs, f"quota-reset-detect-{h}.jsonl")
    return os.path.join(logs, "quota-reset-detect.jsonl")


RESET_LOG_PATH = _reset_log_path()


def _epoch(iso):
    try:
        return datetime.fromisoformat(str(iso).replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def _sweep_readings(row, now_iso):
    """-> ({window: pct}, channel, as_of) for a reading THIS sweep actually took, else ({}, None,
    None). An inherited (stale) figure is not a reading: replaying it would re-arm the watch on a
    number nobody measured."""
    if row.get("wire_substitute"):
        w = row.get("wire") or {}
        out = {win: float(w[f"{win}_util"]) * 100 for win, _b in RESET_WINDOWS
               if isinstance(w.get(f"{win}_util"), (int, float))}
        return out, "wire", row.get("wire_at") or now_iso
    if row.get("stale_quota") or row.get("error"):
        return {}, None, None
    out = {win: float(row[f"{b}_pct"]) for win, b in RESET_WINDOWS
           if isinstance(row.get(f"{b}_pct"), (int, float))}
    return out, "usage", now_iso


def watch_resets(rows, now=None):
    """Fold one sweep's rows into the watch state; append an event per detected reset.

    -> [event]. Never raises (telemetry must not break a sweep). Counters cover every sweep after
    the last >= 99 reading, the detecting sweep included, so a wire detection on the first
    throttled sweep reads throttled_polls_between=1, wire_attempts_between=1 — the ideal."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    now_iso = datetime.fromtimestamp(now, timezone.utc).isoformat()
    events = []
    try:
        path = _sidecar(".resetwatch.json")
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path + ".lock", "a") as lk:
            fcntl.flock(lk, fcntl.LOCK_EX)       # serialises racing sweeps: one reset logs once
            try:
                with open(path) as f:
                    st = json.load(f)
                st = st if isinstance(st, dict) else {}
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                st = {}
            for r in rows:
                acct = r.get("acct")
                if not acct:
                    continue
                reads, channel, as_of = _sweep_readings(r, now_iso)
                throttled = 1 if r.get("poll_throttled") else 0
                wired = 1 if (r.get("wire_substitute") or r.get("wire_miss")) else 0
                a = st.get(acct) or {}
                for win, bucket in RESET_WINDOWS:
                    w = a.get(win)
                    if w:
                        w["throttled_polls"] = int(w.get("throttled_polls") or 0) + throttled
                        w["wire_attempts"] = int(w.get("wire_attempts") or 0) + wired
                    v = reads.get(win)
                    if v is None:
                        continue
                    if v >= RESET_HIGH_PCT:
                        a[win] = {"stale_since": as_of, "pct": v,
                                  "reset_at": r.get(f"{bucket}_reset_at"),
                                  "throttled_polls": 0, "wire_attempts": 0}
                    elif v < RESET_LOW_PCT and w:
                        det = _epoch(as_of) or now
                        since = _epoch(w.get("stale_since")) or det
                        rst = _epoch(w.get("reset_at"))
                        scheduled = rst is not None and det >= rst
                        # Scheduled: lag from the stamp. Unscheduled: the redemption moment is not
                        # observable locally, so the lag is an UPPER bound — from the last reading
                        # that still saw the wall.
                        start = max(rst, since) if scheduled else since
                        events.append({
                            "ts": now_iso, "acct": acct, "window": win,
                            "kind": "scheduled" if scheduled else "unscheduled",
                            "stale_since": w.get("stale_since"), "detected_at": as_of,
                            "lag_s": round(max(0.0, det - start), 1), "channel": channel,
                            "throttled_polls_between": w["throttled_polls"],
                            "wire_attempts_between": w["wire_attempts"],
                            "from_pct": w.get("pct"), "to_pct": round(v, 1),
                            "reset_at": w.get("reset_at")})
                        del a[win]
                if a:
                    st[acct] = a
                else:
                    st.pop(acct, None)
            if events:
                os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(RESET_LOG_PATH), exist_ok=True)
                with open(RESET_LOG_PATH, "a") as f:
                    for e in events:
                        f.write(json.dumps(e, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
            tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.{threading.get_ident()}.tmp"
            with open(tmp, "w") as f:
                json.dump(st, f, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
            os.replace(tmp, path)
    except Exception as e:                        # never bare: KeyboardInterrupt/SystemExit pass
        log_event(f"reset-watch: FAILED {type(e).__name__}: {e}")
        return []
    for e in events:
        log_event(f"reset {e['acct']} {e['window']}: {e['kind']} reset detected via "
                  f"{e['channel']} — {e['from_pct']:.0f}% → {e['to_pct']:.0f}%, lag {e['lag_s']:.0f}s")
        # An UNSCHEDULED weekly reset on an account holding a banked grant is that grant being
        # redeemed — the only local trace a redemption leaves — so the bank is decremented here.
        if e["window"] == "7d" and e["kind"] == "unscheduled":
            try:
                if bank_redeemed(e["acct"], f"reset-detect {e['detected_at']}") is not None:
                    log_event(f"bank {e['acct']}: redemption inferred from the unscheduled weekly "
                              f"reset; resets_left decremented")
            except Exception as be:              # telemetry must not break a sweep
                log_event(f"bank {e['acct']}: decrement FAILED {type(be).__name__}: {be}")
    return events


def _fmt_lag(s):
    if s is None:
        return "-"
    s = int(round(s))
    if s < 60:
        return f"{s}s"
    if s < 3600:
        return f"{s // 60}m{s % 60:02d}s"
    return f"{s // 3600}h{(s % 3600) // 60:02d}m"


def reset_report(days=30.0, out=None):
    """`--reset-report`: one row per detected reset in the last `days`, then a summary — count,
    median and max lag per kind × channel — so "is detection working optimally?" is one command.
    A file read only. -> exit code (0)."""
    out = out or sys.stdout
    cutoff = time.time() - days * 86400.0
    evs = []
    try:
        with open(RESET_LOG_PATH) as f:
            for ln in f:
                try:
                    e = json.loads(ln)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                t = _epoch(e.get("ts"))
                if isinstance(e, dict) and t is not None and t >= cutoff:
                    evs.append(e)
    except OSError:
        pass
    print(f"reset detections, last {days:g} day(s) — {RESET_LOG_PATH}", file=out)
    if not evs:
        print("summary: 0 events (no window fell from >=99% to <90% in this span)", file=out)
        return 0
    print(f"{'detected (UTC)':<17} {'acct':<7} {'win':<3} {'kind':<11} {'channel':<7} "
          f"{'lag':>8} {'throttled':>9} {'wire':>4}  change", file=out)
    for e in sorted(evs, key=lambda x: x.get("ts") or ""):
        det = (e.get("detected_at") or e.get("ts") or "")[:16].replace("T", " ")
        fp, tp = e.get("from_pct"), e.get("to_pct")
        change = (f"{fp:.0f}% → {tp:.0f}%" if isinstance(fp, (int, float))
                  and isinstance(tp, (int, float)) else "?")
        print(f"{det:<17} {str(e.get('acct')):<7} {str(e.get('window')):<3} "
              f"{str(e.get('kind')):<11} {str(e.get('channel')):<7} {_fmt_lag(e.get('lag_s')):>8} "
              f"{str(e.get('throttled_polls_between', '-')):>9} "
              f"{str(e.get('wire_attempts_between', '-')):>4}  {change}", file=out)
    groups = {}
    for e in evs:
        if isinstance(e.get("lag_s"), (int, float)):
            groups.setdefault((e.get("kind"), e.get("channel")), []).append(float(e["lag_s"]))
    def _median(v):
        v = sorted(v)
        m = len(v) // 2
        return v[m] if len(v) % 2 else (v[m - 1] + v[m]) / 2.0

    parts = [f"{k}/{c} n={len(v)} median {_fmt_lag(_median(v))} max {_fmt_lag(max(v))}"
             for (k, c), v in sorted(groups.items(), key=lambda kv: (str(kv[0][0]), str(kv[0][1])))]
    print(f"summary: {len(evs)} event(s) · " + " · ".join(parts), file=out)
    return 0


K_GRACE_S = 600          # same band as cache_grace_s — see inherit_k's docstring
# Tolerance on the LOWER bound only. `k_stale_as_of` is an ISO string, so it carries microseconds
# and `datetime.fromtimestamp(...).isoformat()` can round the float stamp UP by up to half a
# microsecond — which makes the age of a just-written stamp very slightly NEGATIVE. That is
# rounding, not clock skew, and a strict `0 <=` floor turned it into a refusal that fired on the
# fractional part of the clock (caught as a 2-in-3 flake in the re-stamp mutant case, which is
# exactly the sub-second regime the arithmetic lives in). A second of slack cannot launder real
# skew: the skew this guard exists to refuse is a stamp from the FUTURE, which arrives whole
# seconds or hours early, and the grace band it sits inside is 600 s wide.
K_SKEW_TOL_S = 1.0


def _as_epoch(v):
    """A stamp -> epoch seconds: a number passes through, an ISO string is parsed (naive = UTC).
    None for anything else, so every caller fails toward 'no stamp'."""
    if isinstance(v, bool):
        return None
    if isinstance(v, (int, float)):
        return float(v)
    if not isinstance(v, str) or not v:
        return None
    try:
        t = datetime.fromisoformat(v.replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return (t if t.tzinfo else t.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)).timestamp()


def inherit_k(row, prev, now=None, grace_s=K_GRACE_S):
    """Carry the LAST sweep's pane census onto a row whose OWN `ps` census could not be taken.

    THE DEFECT (desk-router-abstention-2026-09-01 §4). `concurrency()` returns None — correctly,
    per its None contract — whenever `ps -wwEo command=` blows its 10 s timeout, which is not a
    rare event: the only sweeper runs at `ProcessType: Background` + `LowPriorityIO`, and over
    8,480 measured ticks the sweep ran p50 10.2 s / p90 34.0 s against a 1.85-2.53 s foreground
    bench. A None census is FLEET-WIDE by construction (one `ps` for all four accounts), so
    `_excluded` charged `concurrency-unmeasured` on every row at once and the router abstained.
    23 of 230 recorded desk decisions abstained (10% all-time, 19.7% over the last 12 days), and
    this is the single largest reason inside them: 68 of their 92 per-account exclusion records,
    i.e. 17 of the 23. The launcher then falls through to `_claude_pinned`, so a mechanism built
    to SPREAD load concentrates those launches on the most-drained account. Starved instrument,
    not a full fleet.

    The census that failed to be taken this sweep was taken 90 s ago and is on disk. Inheriting
    it is strictly better than abstaining on all four, and it is the same trade `inherit_lastgood`
    already makes for quota — with the same provenance discipline:

      · STAMPED, never silent. `k_stale_as_of` carries the time the count was MEASURED, so a row
        that re-inherits its own inherited count each sweep cannot re-date itself to "just now"
        (the exact trap inherit_lastgood's `quota_as_of` block documents). `k_src()` reports
        'panes-stale' and route-meta emits `k_stale_s=` per decision.
      · BOUNDED by the 600 s grace the cache already uses for a degraded read. Past it the row
        goes back to `unmeasured` and the router abstains as before, so a LONG `ps` outage still
        refuses — this widens the grace band, it does not remove the floor. The bound is checked
        TWICE, at write here and at charge in k_panes, because a cached row outlives the sweep
        that wrote it and `--route` charges rows it never swept (k_panes' docstring).
      · `row["k"]` STAYS None. That is the load-bearing half. Two gates read the measured count
        and must keep refusing on an unproven one: heal()'s rotation-safety gate, which reads the
        `k_live` ARGUMENT (never this row) and would redeem a refresh token underneath N live
        sessions; and handoff-fire.sh's Phase-1 relogin gate, which reads `.k` from --json and
        spells a null as the word `unmeasured` rather than 0. An inherited count cannot prove an
        account is idle NOW, so neither gate may see it. Only the router's CHARGE changes.

    Returns True if a count was inherited."""
    if row.get("k") is not None or not prev:
        return False
    pr = (prev.get("rows") or {}).get(row["acct"])
    if not pr:
        return False
    k, as_of = pr.get("k"), None
    if k is None:
        # The previous row was itself inherited: take its count AND its original stamp, so the
        # grace is measured from the last real MEASUREMENT and not from the last re-inheritance.
        # Without this a permanently-starved `ps` would inherit forever, one 600 s hop at a time.
        k, as_of = pr.get("k_stale"), pr.get("k_stale_as_of")
    elif _as_epoch(pr.get("k_as_of")) is not None:
        # Dated by the MEASUREMENT (the ps scan's start), not by the sweep's `ts`, which is stamped
        # after a 20-100 s sweep and would stretch the grace by exactly that much.
        as_of = datetime.fromtimestamp(_as_epoch(pr["k_as_of"]), timezone.utc).isoformat()
    elif prev.get("ts"):
        as_of = datetime.fromtimestamp(prev["ts"], timezone.utc).isoformat()
    if k is None or not as_of:
        return False
    try:
        t = datetime.fromisoformat(str(as_of).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return False
    if t.tzinfo is None:
        t = t.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    age = (now if now is not None else time.time()) - t.timestamp()
    # A negative age past the rounding tolerance is clock SKEW, not freshness — refuse it rather
    # than treat a stamp from the future as the freshest possible reading. Both bounds fail toward
    # `unmeasured`, i.e. toward the pre-change behaviour.
    if not -K_SKEW_TOL_S <= age <= grace_s:
        return False
    row["k_stale"] = k
    row["k_stale_as_of"] = as_of
    return True


POLL_LOCK_WAIT_S = 12.0      # fetch_usage's own timeout: a sweep never stalls longer than one fetch


def _poll_lock_path(cfg, name):
    cf = cfg.get("cache_file")
    return f"{cf}.poll-{name}.lock" if cf else None


def _fetch_usage_polllocked(cfg, name, token):
    """fetch_usage under the per-account poll lock (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D4): the sweep and
    the local pass's first read must not poll one token at the same instant — the 429 budget is
    per token and sticky. The sweep WAITS at most one fetch's timeout and then proceeds without
    the lock; the first read takes it non-blocking and stays pending when it is held."""
    lp = _poll_lock_path(cfg, name)
    fd = _flock_open(lp, POLL_LOCK_WAIT_S) if lp else None
    try:
        return fetch_usage(cfg, token)
    finally:
        _flock_release(fd)


def probe_account(cfg, acct, kc, k_live, ledger, prev, no_heal, first_reads=None):
    """Probe ONE account -> (row, lastgood_entry | None).

    Split out of collect() so that an UNEXPECTED exception can be contained per-account:
    before the split, one malformed keychain item raised out of the loop and blanked the
    dashboard for ALL FOUR accounts (and every consumer of --json/--route/--rank) instead
    of degrading the single bad row."""
    name = acct["name"]
    row = {"acct": name, "email": acct.get("email"), "dia_profile": acct.get("dia_profile"),
           "launcher": acct["launcher"], "k": k_live, "auth": None}
    # PROVENANCE (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D3): when this row's auth was read, which grant it read,
    # and (below) when its quota was. The local pass compares its own stamps against these, so a
    # sweep that read the keychain BEFORE a /login can never overwrite the login it did not see.
    row["auth_as_of"] = time.time()
    creds, kstate = read_creds(acct["config_dir"], kc)
    if creds and creds.get("refreshToken"):
        row["rt_fp"] = _rt_fingerprint(creds["refreshToken"])
    if kstate != "present":
        row["auth"] = "logged-out" if kstate == "no-keychain-item" else "keychain-error"
        row["error"] = row["auth"]
        inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev)
        return row, None
    if not creds:
        # The item exists but carries no usable claudeAiOauth blob (API-key auth, or a
        # half-written/cleared payload). read_creds returns (None, "present") for this by
        # design; the old code then called creds.get("refreshToken") -> AttributeError.
        # Short-circuit before the pointless heal + guaranteed-failing fetch, and give the
        # operator a label that names the real cause instead of "no data (http None)".
        row["auth"] = "no-oauth-blob"
        row["error"] = "keychain item present but carries no OAuth credentials"
        inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev)
        return row, None
    token = creds.get("accessToken")
    expires_ms = creds.get("expiresAt") or 0
    stale = expires_ms / 1000.0 <= time.time() + 60
    row["auth"] = "stale" if stale else "ok"
    # The LOGIN cliff, read from the same payload the access-token expiry comes from — no
    # network, no inference. Recorded on EVERY row (not only failing ones) because its whole
    # value is lead time: by the time it is a failure there is nothing left to warn about.
    rt_iso = _iso_ms(creds.get("refreshTokenExpiresAt"))
    if rt_iso:
        row["login_expires_at"] = rt_iso
        row["login_expires_h"] = hrs_until(rt_iso)
        row["login_expired"] = bool(_expired_ms(creds.get("refreshTokenExpiresAt")))
    # A grant can be dead LONG BEFORE its calendar cliff. The cliff guard below is a prediction;
    # this is an observation, and on 2026-08-03 the observation was the true one — next2's grant
    # answered 400 for 95 minutes with `login_expires_h` reading 674.6. Consulted BEFORE the
    # cliff branch and before the heal so that (a) a proven-dead grant is never re-redeemed on a
    # timer, and (b) the verdict is visible to `--no-heal` readers, which is every caller in the
    # relogin recovery path. Not a new alarm state: it reuses `login-required`, which
    # cc-relogin's need_relogin() has always treated as actionable — the state was simply
    # unreachable from the only code path that tool runs.
    rejected = _rejected_for(acct["name"], creds.get("refreshToken"))
    if rejected and not row.get("login_expired"):
        row["auth"] = "login-required"
        row["error"] = ("refresh REJECTED — run /login "
                        f"({rejected.get('detail')}; first seen {rejected.get('ts')})")
        inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev)
        return row, None
    if row.get("login_expired"):
        # Past the cliff every refresh grant answers invalid_grant, so a heal cannot succeed
        # by construction — skipping it saves a guaranteed-futile 90s subprocess and, more
        # importantly, reports the ONE action that works instead of a failure detail. The
        # usage fetch below still runs: the access token can be valid for hours yet, and
        # keeping quota readable right up to the cliff is the difference between an account
        # that needs a login and an account that has been blanked.
        row["auth"] = "login-required"
        row["error"] = "login EXPIRED — run /login (refresh token past expiry)"
    elif stale and not creds.get("refreshToken"):
        # 2026-09-27, next3: the keychain held no refresh token, so heal() could only answer
        # "skipped: no refresh token" and the row sat at benign `stale` while cc-relogin failed
        # 6x — nothing could ever heal it. Stale + no RT is a dead login; say so on BOTH the heal
        # and the --no-heal path (cc-relogin reads with --no-heal). A NON-stale token without an
        # RT still works, so it is left alone. The usage fetch still runs, as for login_expired.
        row["auth"] = "login-required"
        row["error"] = "no refresh token stored — run /login"
    elif stale and not no_heal and creds_source(acct["config_dir"]) == "file":
        # The keychain item is MISSING and this grant came from .credentials.json, the only
        # surviving copy. heal() redeems the refresh token via `claude auth login`, which rotates
        # the grant server-side and persists the new one to the KEYCHAIN — under the keychain
        # starvation that lost the item, that write is the step most likely to fail, and a lost
        # rotation leaves the file holding a retired token. Not ours to risk: CC refreshes its
        # own file-sourced grant on its next run; the row stays the benign `stale`.
        row["heal_note"] = ("skipped: grant read from .credentials.json (keychain item missing) "
                            "— not rotating the only stored copy")
    elif stale and not no_heal:
        rt_before = creds.get("refreshToken")
        # Only the opaque subprocess is serialised — see _HEAL_GATE. Everything around it stays
        # concurrent, and this costs the fast path nothing (a sweep that heals is already >=90s).
        with _HEAL_GATE:
            ok, detail = heal(cfg, acct, rt_before, k_live)
        if ok:
            creds, kstate = read_creds(acct["config_dir"], kc)
            token = (creds or {}).get("accessToken")
            # PROOF, NOT REPORT. `heal()` reads success as rc==0 plus "Login successful" on
            # stdout, and the 2.1.220 binary emits both even when the credential was never
            # stored: its persist helper returns {success:false} on a keychain write failure,
            # the caller discards that verdict, and `auth login` still exits 0. Worse, that
            # path WIPES the stored credential before writing the replacement — so a failed
            # write leaves nothing on disk while the server has already retired the old token.
            # A false OK here would clear the rejection record and re-arm the retry loop this
            # change exists to stop, so the claim is checked against the store: the grant moved
            # only if the refresh token actually changed.
            healed_rt = (creds or {}).get("refreshToken")
            if healed_rt and _rt_fingerprint(healed_rt) != _rt_fingerprint(rt_before):
                row["rt_fp"] = _rt_fingerprint(healed_rt)
                row["auth"] = "healed"
                _clear_rejected(acct["name"])
            else:
                ok = False
                detail = ("reported success but the stored refresh token did not change"
                          if healed_rt else
                          "reported success but no refresh token is stored (write lost)")
                log_event(f"heal {acct['name']}: UNPROVEN — {detail}")
                row["heal_note"] = detail
                row["auth"] = "stale"
        elif _heal_rejected(detail):
            # Persist BEFORE returning: this is the only moment the fleet ever learns that the
            # grant is dead, and until 2026-08-04 that knowledge died with the process.
            _mark_rejected(acct["name"], rt_before, detail)
            row["auth"] = "login-required"
            row["error"] = f"refresh REJECTED — run /login ({detail})"
            inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev)
            return row, None
        else:
            row["heal_note"] = detail
    # THE SHARED BACKOFF. A token that answered 429 is left alone by EVERY caller — keepwarm,
    # `--fresh`, `--rank` — until its backoff runs out; the row is a throttled row exactly as if
    # it had been polled, so nothing downstream can tell the difference except the request count.
    # THE SWEEP REUSE GUARD (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D6). The local pass polled THIS grant within
    # REPOLL_MIN_S (90 s): a re-poll of the same token within 30 s is a 429 97.6% of the time, so
    # the sweep reuses that read, dated honestly by when it happened (never now). record_utilization
    # writes a reused row as stale, so no duplicate burn sample is recorded.
    fr0 = (first_reads or {}).get(name) or {}
    ra0 = _as_epoch(fr0.get("read_at"))
    if (fr0.get("rt_fp") and fr0.get("rt_fp") == row.get("rt_fp") and ra0 is not None
            and 0 <= time.time() - ra0 <= _repoll_min_s()):
        _land_quota(row, fr0.get("fields") or {})
        row["quota_read_at"] = ra0
        row["quota_reused"] = True
        return row, (_ledger_entry(row, ra0) if row.get("weekly_pct") is not None else None)
    held = usage_backoff_left(name) if token else 0.0
    read_at = None
    if held > 0:
        status, u = 429, None
        row["usage_held_s"] = round(held)
    else:
        read_at = time.time()
        status, u = _fetch_usage_polllocked(cfg, name, token) if token else (None, None)
    if status == 401:
        # CC may have rotated the token between our read and the call — one fresh re-read.
        creds, kstate = read_creds(acct["config_dir"], kc)
        token = (creds or {}).get("accessToken")
        if (creds or {}).get("refreshToken"):
            row["rt_fp"] = _rt_fingerprint(creds["refreshToken"])
        read_at = time.time()
        status, u = _fetch_usage_polllocked(cfg, name, token) if token else (None, None)
        if status == 401:
            if row["auth"] == "login-required":
                pass                             # row["error"] already names the real cause
            elif row["auth"] == "stale":
                row["error"] = ("stale (expired token; --no-heal)" if no_heal
                                else "stale (expired token; heal skipped/failed — see heal_note)")
            else:
                row["auth"] = "token-invalid"
                row["error"] = "token-invalid (revoked? run /accounts relogin check)"
            inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev)
            return row, None
    if status == 429:
        # A 429 from the oauth/usage endpoint is ALWAYS a transient POLL-THROTTLE
        # (concurrent callers racing the 90s single-flight cache) — NEVER a usage cap.
        # A real weekly/5h/Fable cap returns HTTP 200 with percent≈100; status+endpoint
        # alone is the discriminator. Labeling this "rate-limited" made a downstream
        # session-classifier read a transient poll failure as a hard cap (false-limit
        # misdiagnosis 2026-07-16). Flag it distinctly + fall back to last-good percents.
        row["poll_throttled"] = True
        row["error"] = ("poll throttled ↻ (cached usage)"
                        if inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev) else "poll throttled ↻")
        if not held:
            row["usage_held_s"] = round(note_usage_429(name)["until"] - time.time())
        # THE SUBSTITUTE READ. /v1/messages is outside the usage endpoint's throttle and its
        # headers are the server's live verdict, so where the verdict decides something (a stored
        # rejection, a reset since the reading, a reading near a wall) one wire read replaces the
        # poll that cannot happen. A miss changes nothing: the row stays exactly as inherited.
        if token and wire_enabled() and row.get("stale_quota") and wire_substitute_wanted(row):
            w = fetch_wire_limits(cfg, token)
            if w:
                apply_wire_substitute(row, w)
                log_event(f"probe {name}: wire substitute for a throttled usage poll — 5h "
                          f"{w.get('5h_util')} {w.get('5h_status')} · 7d {w.get('7d_util')} "
                          f"{w.get('7d_status')}"
                          + (f" · {'; '.join(row['reset_evidence'])}"
                             if row.get("reset_evidence") else ""))
            else:
                row["wire_miss"] = True
        # THE INSTRUMENT. Until this line the 429 branch emitted nothing at all, so
        # ~/.claude/logs/claude-accounts.log held zero throttle entries across 5,413 lines —
        # which reads as "429 never fires" and is actually a null from a blind instrument. The
        # fan-out is what makes that unacceptable: it does not change the PER-TOKEN request rate
        # (four accounts, four bearer tokens, one request each) but it does compress four
        # requests from ~1.35s of self-staggering into ~0ms, so if the endpoint's throttle is
        # IP- or org-scoped rather than token-scoped, this change is what discovers it. Shipping
        # the fan-out without this line ships its one new failure mode unmeasurable.
        if held:
            log_event(f"probe {name}: usage poll held — 429 backoff, {held:.0f}s left "
                      f"(lastgood={'served' if row.get('stale_quota') else 'absent'})")
        else:
            log_event(f"probe {name}: 429 poll-throttled ↻ — transient endpoint throttle, NOT a "
                      f"usage cap (a real cap is HTTP 200 with percent≈100). "
                      f"lastgood={'served' if row.get('stale_quota') else 'absent'} "
                      f"backoff={row.get('usage_held_s')}s")
        return row, None
    if not u or "limits" not in u:
        row["error"] = f"no data (http {status})"
        inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev)
        return row, None
    note_usage_ok(name)
    row["quota_read_at"] = read_at          # D3: HTTP-200 reads only, never an inherited one
    sp, wp = _fill_quota(row, u, cfg, name)
    # THE WIRE, only where the endpoint integer is ambiguous (see the fetch_wire_limits block).
    # Never overwrites sp/wp/fp: the recorded series, the burn fit and `_rolled` all key on the
    # ENDPOINT meter, and a mid-series provenance switch would read as a window roll. The wire
    # rides beside them and is consumed by the router and the renderer, which want the truth.
    if wire_wanted(sp, wp, force=WIRE_FORCE):
        w = fetch_wire_limits(cfg, token)
        if w:
            row["wire"] = w
            log_event(f"probe {name}: wire — 5h {w.get('5h_util')} {w.get('5h_status')} · "
                      f"7d {w.get('7d_util')} {w.get('7d_status')} · claim {w.get('claim')} "
                      f"(endpoint read 5h {sp} / 7d {wp})")
        else:
            row["wire_miss"] = True
    # capture last-good (weekly is the durable stranded-quota signal; skip data-less rows).
    # Absolute reset stamps are stored (NOT the decaying *_reset_h) — inherit re-derives them.
    # THE WIRE IS DELIBERATELY NOT INHERITED. A `*_status` is a point-in-time verdict the SERVER
    # gave about one request; replayed onto a row we could not poll it would assert "allowed" on
    # an account that may have hit its wall since — the one direction this whole change refuses
    # to fail in. A stale meter reads as a stale number; a stale verdict reads as a fresh fact.
    entry = None
    if wp is not None:
        entry = _ledger_entry(row, time.time())
        # …but a REJECTED verdict is stored, because replaying it fails in the safe direction: it
        # can only stop a 429-throttled row from being admitted on these percents, never admit one.
        rej = [win for win in ("5h", "7d")
               if ((row.get("wire") or {}).get(f"{win}_status")) == "rejected"]
        if rej:
            entry["wire_rejects"] = rej
    return row, entry


def collect(cfg, no_heal=False, prev=None):
    """Sweep every account -> [row]. FIVE-WAY CONCURRENT: the N account probes plus the working
    census, which is the design's whole point.

    The obvious reading — "parallelise the per-account loop" — leaves most of the win on the
    table, because the loop was only 54% of the sweep. Measured cold, serially: load_cfg 44ms,
    concurrency() 221ms, working_concurrency() 865ms, 4 probes 1350ms = 2480ms. The single
    largest term was working_concurrency, and it is not consumed INSIDE the loop at all — its
    result is attached to each row after the probe returns. It is a pure 5th independent task,
    so co-scheduling it is free and it is what actually moves the floor: 2.6-4x, against ~40%
    for probes-only.

    Two things deliberately stay serial. concurrency() is a PREFIX, not a peer: k_live is the
    input to every probe's heal rotation-safety gate, so it must be complete before any probe
    starts. And every ledger write — `ledger[name] = entry`, save_lastgood — stays on this
    thread, below the pool, which is what keeps the shared dict and the dirty flag race-free
    without a lock.

    executor.map, never as_completed: row ORDER is load-bearing (render_table renders in row
    order, and tests index rows[0]), and as_completed would make both nondeterministic — a
    flaky-not-red suite, the worst failure shape available here."""
    kc = cfg["keychain_account"]
    # None (ps unreadable) rides through as None per row — NOT as 0. `.get(name, 0)` on an empty
    # dict would re-fabricate the very zero the None contract exists to abolish, so the unmeasured
    # case is spelled out rather than defaulted.
    global _NATIVE_LAST
    _NATIVE_LAST = None               # concurrency() publishes the native census here in 'ab' mode
    k_as_of = time.time()             # the ps scan's start epoch, stamped only when ps succeeded
    counts = concurrency(cfg)
    native = _NATIVE_LAST
    k_of_name =(lambda _n: None) if counts is None else (lambda n: counts.get(n, 0))
    # The probe's k_live is heal's rotation gate, and since Phase 3 it reads gate_counts(): the
    # higher of ps and native, whichever was taken, None only when neither was. `row["k"]` is put
    # back to the ps count right after the probe (C6), so the router's charge, the board and
    # handoff-fire's relogin gate see exactly what they saw before.
    gate = gate_counts(cfg, counts, native)
    gate_of = (lambda _n: None) if gate is None else (lambda n: gate.get(n, 0))
    _ssl_warm()                       # both lazy urllib globals, main thread, before fan-out
    ledger = load_lastgood()
    # The local pass's first reads, for probe_account's reuse guard (D6). Passed only when there
    # are any, so every existing 7-argument probe_account stub keeps working.
    first_reads = (load_local_overlay(cfg) or {}).get("first_read") or {}
    fr_kw = {"first_reads": first_reads} if first_reads else {}

    def _one(acct):
        """-> (row, entry). NEVER raises: the per-account containment that used to wrap the loop
        body moves inside the worker, because an exception escaping here would poison map()'s
        whole result and blank all four rows — the exact regression probe_account was split out
        of collect() to prevent."""
        name = acct["name"]
        try:
            row, entry = probe_account(cfg, acct, kc, gate_of(name), ledger, prev, no_heal,
                                       **fr_kw)
            row["k"] = k_of_name(name)
            return row, entry
        except Exception as e:                    # never bare: KeyboardInterrupt/SystemExit pass
            # Containment, not concealment — the traceback goes to the log so a real
            # regression is still discoverable rather than silently degrading forever.
            log_event(f"probe {name}: UNEXPECTED {type(e).__name__}: {e}\n{traceback.format_exc()}")
            row = {"acct": name, "email": acct.get("email"), "dia_profile": acct.get("dia_profile"),
                   "launcher": acct.get("launcher"), "k": k_of_name(name),
                   "auth": "probe-error",
                   "error": f"probe failed: {type(e).__name__} (see claude-accounts.log)"}
            inherit_lastgood(row, ledger, prev)
            return row, None

    accounts = list(cfg["accounts"])
    ex = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=len(accounts) + 1)
    try:
        # M7: the working census rides the sweep beside the pane census (same recorder-economics
        # as record_utilization — the sweep is already paid for). None = walk over budget →
        # k_eff falls back to the pane census for every row of this sweep, and that matters more
        # here than it looks: the walk now shares the box with N TLS threads, and a breach is not
        # a slow row, it is a fleet-wide k_work blackout for the WHOLE sweep — the routing signal
        # blinded by the very change meant to speed the sweep up.
        #
        # The budget is deliberately NOT overridden. The design note that specified this fan-out
        # said "raise budget_s to 3.0", correctly, against the 2.0 default it measured; the
        # default has since been raised to KWORK_BUDGET_S = 5.0. Passing 3.0 now would CUT the
        # budget by 40% and, worse, hard-code past _cliff_env's CC_ROUTE_KWORK_BUDGET_S — the
        # operator's own override — so the prescription is satisfied by leaving it alone.
        wf = (ex.submit(working_concurrency, cfg) if _term_on("CC_ROUTE_KWORK") else None)
        results = list(ex.map(_one, accounts))            # map ⇒ ORDER PRESERVED
        wcounts = wf.result() if wf else None
    finally:
        # cancel_futures so a Ctrl-C during a sweep does not join a retry ladder (~40s) or a
        # heal (~90s). A plain `with` block shuts down with wait=True and would.
        ex.shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True)

    lastgood_dirty = False
    rows = []
    for acct, (row, entry) in zip(accounts, results):
        # Uniformly, success and exception paths alike: absent (None) whenever the walk could
        # not measure, so k_eff's census fallback — not a fabricated 0 — is what cached rows
        # replay for the TTL.
        row["k_work"] = wcounts.get(acct["name"]) if wcounts is not None else None
        # WHICH sessions k_work counted, and when — apply_assignments' double-charge guard. Rides
        # the cached row like k_work does, so a cache replay skips exactly what this sweep saw.
        row["k_sids"] = (sorted(getattr(wcounts, "sids", {}).get(acct["name"], ()))
                         if wcounts is not None else None)
        row["k_sids_at"] = getattr(wcounts, "at", None) if wcounts is not None else None
        if counts is not None:
            row["k_as_of"] = k_as_of
        # The native census rides BESIDE `k`, never in it: row["k"] stays ps-only (C6), so
        # handoff-fire's relogin gate still refuses on a count ps did not take. The router's charge
        # (k_panes) reads k_native; heal's gate already had it, through gate_counts() above.
        if native is not None:
            row["k_native"] = native.get(acct["name"], 0)
            row["k_native_as_of"] = native.as_of
        # ps first, native as the fallback: bound_kwork only ever TIGHTENS, and a None census used
        # to leave k_work unbounded for the whole sweep.
        bound_kwork(row, acct["name"], counts if counts is not None else native, wcounts)
        # HERE, not at the probe's k_live argument, and that placement is the safety property:
        # heal()'s rotation gate reads the argument and must never see an inherited count
        # (inherit_k's docstring). This stamps a SEPARATE field on the row afterwards, so only the router's
        # charge learns the inherited count.
        inherit_k(row, prev, grace_s=cfg.get("cache_grace_s", K_GRACE_S))
        if entry:
            ledger[acct["name"]] = entry
            lastgood_dirty = True
        rows.append(row)
    if lastgood_dirty:
        save_lastgood(ledger)
    # Reset telemetry (W2): this is the sweep, so this is the one place it is recorded.
    watch_resets(rows)
    # One CLI-owned verdict per row for "does this need an operator action", so consumers stop
    # re-deriving it from a hand-copied auth-state list. handoff-fire.sh's copy had already
    # drifted — it matched 3 of the 5 actionable states, silently treating no-oauth-blob and
    # probe-error accounts as healthy — and any state added later un-covers every such copy.
    for r in rows:
        r["auth_actionable"] = r.get("auth") in ACTIONABLE_AUTH
        r["login_fixable"] = r.get("auth") in LOGIN_FIXABLE
    return rows


# ---- banked limit resets: THE BANK STATE (recorded, never read off the wire) -----------------
# A banked "Full reset" grant refills the 5-hour, weekly and Fable limits, can be redeemed only AT
# a limit (weekly 100%), and does NOT move the weekly reset day. So an unredeemed grant is LOST
# unless its account reaches 100% before the grant expires — for routing, the grant's expiry is a
# deadline exactly like a weekly reset, and the earlier of the two is the one that strands quota.
#
# THE STORE IS RECORDED BY HAND, and that is a measured constraint, not a shortcut: the CLI surface
# is not offered the bank (`/api/oauth/usage?cedar_ember=1` answers 200 with `eligible:false,
# ineligible_reason:"surface", grants:[]` on all four accounts, 2026-10-18), and redemption is
# web-only (clau.de/reset). A reader built on that endpoint would read "no grant" forever.
# Recorded with `--note-bank <acct> --left N --expires <ISO>`; decremented by the reset detector
# when it sees an UNSCHEDULED weekly reset on an account holding a grant (watch_resets), since a
# redemption leaves no other local record.
#
# An entry with resets_left 0, or past expires_at, is ignored by every reader: no cleanup needed,
# and an unreadable store reads as "no grant" — the pre-bank ranking, byte for byte.
BANK_REDEEM_URL = "clau.de/reset"
# Tie-break between EQUAL effective deadlines (operator ruling 2026-10-18: pure earliest-deadline
# first, goal zero unused weekly quota): the account whose REAL weekly reset is earlier goes first,
# because the refill it gets on redeeming expires sooner. Expressed as a sub-minute nudge on the
# effective deadline (<= 3.6 s at a full week), so it orders a tie and can never reorder anything
# a real deadline separates.
BANK_TIE_H = 1e-3


def bank_path():
    explicit = os.environ.get("CC_LIMIT_BANK_JSON")
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    # A redirected ledger or SSOT takes the bank with it (the _reset_log_path discipline), so a
    # suite exercising the router on real account names can never read or decrement the real bank.
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_LASTGOOD"):
        return os.path.splitext(LASTGOOD_PATH)[0] + ".limit-bank.json"
    state = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "state")
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON"):
        h = sha256(os.path.realpath(CFG_PATH).encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
        return os.path.join(state, f"limit-bank-{h}.json")
    return os.path.join(state, "limit-bank.json")


_BANK_MEMO = {}


def bank_load():
    """-> {acct: entry} from the bank store; {} when absent or unreadable. Memoised on the file's
    (path, mtime, size), so the router may ask once per row without re-reading."""
    p = bank_path()
    try:
        st = os.stat(p)
    except OSError:
        return {}
    key = (p, st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_size)
    if _BANK_MEMO.get("key") == key:
        return _BANK_MEMO["val"]
    try:
        with open(p) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        d = {}
    accts = d.get("accounts") if isinstance(d, dict) else None
    val = accts if isinstance(accts, dict) else {}
    _BANK_MEMO.update(key=key, val=val)
    return val


def bank_live(acct, now=None):
    """The account's unredeemed, unexpired grant -> its entry plus `expires_epoch` and
    `hours_left`; None otherwise (no entry, resets_left 0, expired, or an unparseable expiry)."""
    e = bank_load().get(acct) if acct else None
    if not isinstance(e, dict):
        return None
    left = e.get("resets_left")
    if not isinstance(left, int) or isinstance(left, bool) or left <= 0:
        return None
    exp = _epoch(e.get("expires_at"))
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    if exp is None or exp <= now:
        return None
    return dict(e, expires_epoch=exp, hours_left=(exp - now) / 3600.0)


def bank_live_all(rows, now=None):
    """-> [(row, grant)] for every row holding a live grant, in row order."""
    return [(r, g) for r in rows for g in [bank_live(r.get("acct"), now)] if g]


def bank_at_wall(r):
    """True when the row's weekly limit is spent: the one state in which a grant CAN be redeemed."""
    w = r.get("wire") or {}
    return w.get("7d_status") == "rejected" or (r.get("weekly_pct") or 0) >= 100


def effective_reset_h(r, R, field="weekly_reset_h"):
    """THE ROUTER'S WEEKLY DEADLINE, in hours: min(weekly reset, grant expiry) when the account
    holds a live grant that expires BEFORE its weekly reset; otherwise `weekly_reset_h` itself,
    returned unchanged (absent stays absent), so a fleet with no live grant ranks byte-identically
    to the pre-bank router. Every lane reads its weekly deadline through this one helper, then
    applies horizon() exactly as before — the only change is which instant is the deadline.
    `field="fable_reset_h"` gives the Fable lane the same cap: a redemption refills Fable too."""
    wrh = r.get(field)
    g = bank_live(r.get("acct"))
    if g is None:
        return wrh
    real = wrh if (isinstance(wrh, (int, float)) and wrh > 0) else R.get("NO_RESET_H", WEEK_H)
    if g["hours_left"] >= real:
        return wrh
    return g["hours_left"] + BANK_TIE_H * clamp(real / WEEK_H, 0.0, 1.0)


def _bank_write(mutate):
    """Apply `mutate(accounts_dict)` to the store under a lock; atomic replace. -> the new dict."""
    p = bank_path()
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p) or ".", exist_ok=True)
    with open(p + ".lock", "a") as lk:
        fcntl.flock(lk, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        try:
            with open(p) as f:
                d = json.load(f)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            d = {}
        d = d if isinstance(d, dict) else {}
        accts = d.get("accounts") if isinstance(d.get("accounts"), dict) else {}
        mutate(accts)
        d.update(version=1, accounts=accts)
        tmp = f"{p}.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(d, f, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
            f.write("\n")
        os.replace(tmp, p)
    return accts


def bank_note(acct, left, expires_at, source):
    """`--note-bank`: record one read of an account's bank."""
    now_iso = datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")

    def m(accts):
        accts[acct] = {"resets_left": left, "expires_at": expires_at,
                       "verified_at": now_iso, "source": source}
    return _bank_write(m)[acct]


def bank_redeemed(acct, source):
    """A redemption seen: decrement resets_left (never below 0). -> the new entry, or None when the
    account held no live grant (nothing to decrement, and nothing is written)."""
    if bank_live(acct) is None:
        return None
    out = {}

    def m(accts):
        e = dict(accts.get(acct) or {})
        e["resets_left"] = max(0, int(e.get("resets_left") or 0) - 1)
        e["redeemed_at"] = datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")
        e["source"] = source
        accts[acct] = e
        out.update(e)
    _bank_write(m)
    return out


def bank_wall_lines(rows):
    """-> [(acct, grant_key, sentence)] for each account AT its weekly wall holding a live grant.
    The key names the GRANT (account + expiry), so a pager can say it once per account per grant."""
    out = []
    for r, g in bank_live_all(rows):
        if bank_at_wall(r):
            out.append((r["acct"], f"{r['acct']}@{g.get('expires_at')}",
                        f"`{r['acct']}` is at its weekly limit and holds a banked full reset "
                        f"(expires {_bank_day(g)}): redeem it now at {BANK_REDEEM_URL}"))
    return out


def _bank_day(g):
    return datetime.fromtimestamp(g["expires_epoch"]).strftime("%b %-d")


def bank_readout_line(rows):
    """The one plain readout line while any grant is live and not yet at its wall; None otherwise.
    Accounts already at the wall are named by their own at-wall line instead."""
    live = [(r, g) for r, g in bank_live_all(rows) if not bank_at_wall(r)]
    if not live:
        return None
    days = {_bank_day(g) for _r, g in live}
    names = [f"`{r['acct']}`" for r, _g in live]
    who = names[0] if len(names) == 1 else ", ".join(names[:-1]) + " and " + names[-1]
    if len(days) == 1:
        when = f"expires {days.pop()}"
    else:
        who = ", ".join(f"`{r['acct']}` ({_bank_day(g)})" for r, g in live)
        when = "expiring as shown"
    them = "it" if len(live) == 1 else "them"
    return (f"◆ Banked full reset on {who}, {when}: work {them} to 100% first, "
            f"then redeem at {BANK_REDEEM_URL}")


def note_bank_main(args):
    """`--note-bank ACCT --left N --expires ISO [--source WHO]` -> exit 0 recorded · 64 bad args.
    The expiry must carry a UTC offset: "Oct 22" read off a web page is a local date, and a naive
    stamp would be read as UTC — five hours late on this box, i.e. past the real deadline."""
    def val(flag):
        i = args.index(flag) if flag in args else -1
        return args[i + 1] if 0 <= i < len(args) - 1 and not args[i + 1].startswith("--") else None
    names = [a["name"] for a in load_cfg(need_claude_bin=False)["accounts"]]
    acct, left, exp = val("--note-bank"), val("--left"), val("--expires")
    if acct not in names:
        print(f"claude-accounts: --note-bank needs an account ({'|'.join(names)}); got {acct!r}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 64
    try:
        n = int(left)
        if n < 0:
            raise ValueError
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        print(f"claude-accounts: --left needs a whole number >= 0; got {left!r}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 64
    try:
        t = datetime.fromisoformat(str(exp).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except ValueError:
        t = None
    if t is None or t.tzinfo is None:
        print(f"claude-accounts: --expires needs an ISO-8601 time WITH an offset "
              f"(e.g. 2026-10-22T00:00-05:00); got {exp!r}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 64
    e = bank_note(acct, n, t.isoformat(), val("--source") or "note-bank")
    print(f"bank {acct}: resets_left={e['resets_left']} expires_at={e['expires_at']} "
          f"source={e['source']!r} -> {bank_path()}")
    return 0


# ---- router (verified scoring math, 2026-07-04; audit fixes 2026-07-10) -----------------------

def clamp(x, lo, hi):
    return max(lo, min(hi, x))


def horizon(reset_h, R):
    """Hours of runway a bucket has left -> the urgency DENOMINATOR (score = headroom / time).

    `(reset_h or EPS_H)` was wrong in three ways at once: None (the endpoint omits resets_at
    on a freshly-rolled bucket), 0.0, and any NEGATIVE value (a reset stamp inherited from the
    last-good ledger that has since elapsed) all collapsed T to the EPS_H floor — 0.25h. That
    is MAXIMUM urgency, so the account with the least trustworthy timing data scored ~400x
    higher than a healthy one and won the route.

    A missing horizon must read as "far away", never "imminent": substitute the full bucket
    period (NO_RESET_H) so the row stays rankable by its real headroom (w_rem / f_eff still
    discriminate) without the urgency inflation. Excluding the row instead would fail CLOSED —
    all four accounts sit at 0% with a null resets_at right after a weekly reset, i.e. exactly
    when the fleet is maximally available, and the router would answer "none".

    The EPS_H clamp is retained: it is a correct divide-by-zero guard for a genuinely imminent
    MEASURED reset — it must simply never be the fallback for an absent one."""
    h = reset_h if (reset_h is not None and reset_h > 0) else R.get("NO_RESET_H", 168.0)
    return max(h - R["MARGIN_H"], R["EPS_H"])


def _soft(r, R):
    su = _su_projected(r, R)
    wu = (r.get("weekly_pct") or 0) / 100.0
    SF = clamp((R["S_CUT"] - su) / (R["S_CUT"] - R["S_SOFT"]), R["SF_FLOOR"], 1.0)
    # Same denominator as the cap that actually applies (k_cap): a census-charged row measured
    # against the ACTIVE integer would pin KF to KFLOOR at 8 residents while its real cap is 40,
    # i.e. the soft gradient would collapse well before the hard gate it is supposed to precede.
    KF = clamp(1 - k_eff(r) / k_cap(r, R), R["KFLOOR"], 1.0)
    CF = (1.0 if wu < 0.90 else 0.5) if r.get("credits_on") else 1.0
    return SF * KF * CF


# ---- the login-cliff routing term (ACCOUNT_ROUTING_V2 M1 — THE INVERSION) ----------------------
# Until this landed, every login_expires_* read in this file was in a PRODUCER (probe_account), a
# re-deriver (refresh_login_countdown) or a RENDERER — and the four functions that decide which
# account works never mentioned the cliff once. So the subsystem whose whole job is "which account
# works" treated the one fact that decides whether an account will STILL work as a display string.
#
# Two bands, both off the ABSOLUTE stamp's derived login_expires_h, reusing cc-relogin-poll's own
# constants (T-7d trigger / T-48h escalation) rather than inventing new numbers:
#   SOFT  (<= 168h)  score multiplier. The account keeps serving and keeps its quota; it loses
#                    ties. New-session inflow falls for ~5 days so k DECAYS instead of being
#                    fought at the deadline. A multiplier, not an exclusion: hard-excluding for a
#                    week discards ~25% of fleet capacity for 7 of every 30 days, per account.
#   DRAIN (<= 48h)   excluded from new work. Existing sessions finish, k -> 0, and cc-relogin
#                    finally gets the k==0 window it has never once found (all 4 accounts sit at
#                    k=5-6, so the autonomous path was 0-BY-CONSTRUCTION). Draining an account IS
#                    a routing decision — that is why this belongs here and not in a new daemon.
#   Every lane lifts SOFT (_desk_cliff_factor, _dispatch_cliff_factor) only when the account's
#   week is forecast to strand AND (its weekly reset comes before its drain point, or its drain
#   point is beyond any session's lifetime): a stranded pp is unrecoverable, a lost k==0 poll
#   tick is one of ~168. (_strand_cliff_factor; recycle_repick and the recovery lane read the
#   same general score, so they inherit it.)
#
# FAIL-OPEN on absence is not a nicety: login_expires_h is not on every build (see the
# --relogin-status detection note), and reading a missing field as "cliff imminent" would exclude
# the ENTIRE fleet on an older binary. Absence of the cliff fact is never evidence of a cliff.
CLIFF_SOFT_H = 168.0     # = cc-relogin-poll --trigger-days 7
CLIFF_DRAIN_H = 48.0     # = cc-relogin-poll --escalate-hours 48
CLIFF_SOFT_FACTOR = 0.25
CLIFF_DRAIN_REASON = "login-cliff-drain"


def _quota_age_s(cfg):
    """Age in whole seconds of the sweep this process read, from the cache's OWN recorded `ts`
    (cache_write stamps it) — never a file mtime, which answers a different question. None when
    unknowable, so a consumer can record `-` rather than a fabricated 0."""
    try:
        with open(cfg["cache_file"]) as f:
            ts = json.load(f).get("ts")
        return max(0, int(time.time() - float(ts))) if ts else None
    except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError, KeyError):
        return None


def _cliff_env(name, default):
    """Float from the env, else the default. A malformed value is IGNORED, never fatal and never
    zero: a typo in a tuning knob must not silently disable a safety term (nor crash a router)."""
    raw = os.environ.get(name)
    if raw:
        try:
            v = float(raw)
            if v >= 0:
                return v
        except ValueError:
            pass
    return default


def cliff_enabled():
    """R8 kill switch. CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_TERM=off restores byte-identical prior routing."""
    return os.environ.get("CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_TERM", "on").strip().lower() not in ("off", "0", "false")


def cliff_band(r):
    """-> 'drain' | 'soft' | 'none' for ONE row. 'none' whenever the term is off or the cliff is
    unknown — the fail-open rule above. Pure read; no side effects."""
    if not cliff_enabled():
        return "none"
    h = r.get("login_expires_h")
    if h is None:
        return "none"
    try:
        h = float(h)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return "none"
    if h <= _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_DRAIN_H", CLIFF_DRAIN_H):
        return "drain"
    if h <= _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_SOFT_H", CLIFF_SOFT_H):
        return "soft"
    return "none"


def _cliff_factor(r):
    """SOFT-band score multiplier (1.0 outside the band)."""
    if cliff_band(r) != "soft":
        return 1.0
    return _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_SOFT_FACTOR", CLIFF_SOFT_FACTOR)


# Upper bound on how long a desk session lives. 183 routed desk launches since 2026-09-01, joined to
# the transcript each started: median 2.9h, p90 26.6h, max 59.2h (a span is a LOWER bound on process
# life). 48h — the first value, from R2 §3's n=30 and a ps snapshot — was exceeded by 4 of 183.
DESK_LIFE_H = 60.0
# Weekly pp forecast to expire unused (wk_strand_pp) at/above which the desk lifts the soft factor.
# = STRAND_SCORE_FLOOR, S6's proposed rescue-alarm floor: below it there is nothing worth rescuing.
DESK_CLIFF_STRAND_PP = 4.0


def _desk_cliff_factor(r):
    """The SOFT-band factor as the DESK lane applies it: lifted only when quota is dying.

    ── Why the desk cannot use _cliff_factor as-is (measured 2026-10-03) ─────────────────────────
    The soft band cuts new-session inflow so cc-relogin-poll finds k==0: it attempts at EVERY hourly
    tick with k==0 from T-168h (~168 chances), and cc-relogin refuses whenever k != 0. A desk on a
    soft account therefore costs poll ticks for as long as it lives. That cost is worth paying back
    only for something unrecoverable, so the factor is lifted (1.0) iff BOTH hold:
      - the week is forecast to strand: wk_strand_pp >= DESK_CLIFF_STRAND_PP. Unknown => keep the
        factor (fail toward the existing protection). An account on pace to use its week gains
        nothing from the desk and keeps every tick.
      - the drain point is past any desk's life: login_expires_h - CLIFF_DRAIN_H > DESK_LIFE_H, so
        no desk opened now still holds k > 0 inside the drain band, the last relogin window.

    Applied unconditionally, x0.25 on the desk's 1/(1+T) key is exactly T' = 4T + 3 — it quadruples
    the time-to-reset, so it overrides the earliest-reset objective whenever the reset gap is under
    4x, not merely "loses ties". 2026-10-03T02:22Z: next4 (weekly 25% used, reset 30.2h, login
    128h) scored 2.008025 against next3 (reset 81.2h) at 2.012172, and the desk went to next3 while
    ~62pp of next4's week was forecast to expire unused. next4's drain point was 80h out — long
    after any desk opened then would have exited. Across 1,974 soft-band sweeps since 2026-08-24
    the factor flipped 683 desk picks: 196 in this next4 episode, and 487 for next2 in September,
    which cost 0pp (other lanes spent its week) — the case the strand condition keeps demoted.

    Kill: CC_ROUTE_DESK_CLIFF_WINDOW=off restores x0.25 across the whole soft band.
    Knobs: CC_ROUTE_DESK_LIFE_H, CC_ROUTE_DESK_CLIFF_STRAND_PP (malformed or negative => default)."""
    return _strand_cliff_factor(r, "CC_ROUTE_DESK_CLIFF_WINDOW")


def _dispatch_cliff_factor(r):
    """The SOFT-band factor as the DISPATCH lanes (general, fable) apply it: the desk's rule.

    ── Why dispatch needed the same lift (measured 2026-10-17T07:25Z) ────────────────────────────
    27987efa8 lifted the factor for the desk and left general/fable "unchanged" — a scope line, not
    a reasoned exclusion. On the urgency law w_rem / T**2, x0.25 is T' = 2T, so it overrides the
    earliest-reset objective whenever the reset gap is under 2x. Live: next (weekly 44% used,
    reset 20.0h, login 116.7h, ~45pp forecast to strand) scored 0.000288 against next4 (67% used,
    reset 25.0h, login 393h, ~12pp) at 0.000316 — every other term favoured next 3.64x — so
    handoff-fire --account auto placed 18 fires on next4 against 7 on next in 19h, breaking its
    own "soonest-expiring quota first" contract. The protection bought nothing either: the desk
    kept next at k>0 all band long, and cc-relogin-poll logged no k==0 tick for it.

    Same two conditions as the desk, same knobs (wk_strand_pp floor, DESK_LIFE_H): a fired session
    recycles in place and keeps its account until a repick beats it by REPICK_RATIO, so its life is
    unmeasured and the desk's 60h bound is the conservative one. An account on pace to use its week,
    or whose drain point a session opened now could reach, keeps x0.25 exactly as before.

    Kill: CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_CLIFF_WINDOW=off restores x0.25 on general and fable only."""
    return _strand_cliff_factor(r, "CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_CLIFF_WINDOW")


def _strand_cliff_factor(r, kill_env):
    """The soft factor, lifted to 1.0 iff the week strands AND either the weekly reset comes before
    the drain point or the drain is past DESK_LIFE_H.
    Shared body of _desk_cliff_factor and _dispatch_cliff_factor; kill_env is the lane's switch.

    ── The reset-first arm (weekly-strand post-mortem 2026-10-18 §4 item 3) ────────────────────────
    DESK_LIFE_H alone LAPSES while the week is still stranding: next's login fell to 108h at
    2026-10-17T16:12Z, its drain point moved inside the 60h desk life, and the factor came back
    with ~38pp still forecast to strand and its weekly reset 5h away (route.jsonl 22:54Z: desk ->
    next4, runner-up next at x0.25). The protection bought nothing there: the stranding quota
    expires at the reset, which lands BEFORE the drain band opens, so a session placed now on the
    dying week is on a fresh week — whose own wk_strand_pp decides afresh — by the time cc-relogin
    needs its window. Hence: reset < login_expires_h - CLIFF_DRAIN_H lifts regardless of DESK_LIFE_H.
    An unknown reset never lifts on this arm (fail toward the existing protection).
    Kill: CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_RESET_FIRST=off restores the DESK_LIFE_H-only rule in every lane."""
    f = _cliff_factor(r)                    # 1.0 outside soft, with the term off, or cliff unknown
    if f == 1.0 or not _term_on(kill_env):
        return f
    strand = r.get("wk_strand_pp")
    if not isinstance(strand, (int, float)) or \
            strand < _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_DESK_CLIFF_STRAND_PP", DESK_CLIFF_STRAND_PP):
        return f
    # float() is safe: cliff_band already parsed this field to answer "soft"
    drain_in = float(r["login_expires_h"]) - _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_DRAIN_H", CLIFF_DRAIN_H)
    wrh = r.get("weekly_reset_h")
    if _term_on("CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_RESET_FIRST") and _is_num(wrh) and 0 < wrh < drain_in:
        return 1.0
    return 1.0 if drain_in > _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_DESK_LIFE_H", DESK_LIFE_H) else f


# ---- utilization-pressure terms (ACCOUNT_ROUTING_V2 M7 — spread the 5h load, exhaust by deadline)
# One live snapshot exhibits every defect these terms fix (2026-08-10, plan doc §M7):
#   next3 held the fleet's soonest-expiring quota — weekly 81% used, reset in 27.8h, ~19% of the
#   week about to strand — and was EXCLUDED (k=28 ≥ KMAX) while its 5h window sat at 60%: `k`
#   counts PANES, and 28 mostly-idle desks read as burn that was not happening. Every fire inside
#   the 90s cache TTL then took rank[0] = next2 (weekly 13%, reset 5 DAYS out): same rows, same
#   top pick, nothing decrementing headroom between fires — concurrent working sessions stacking
#   onto one account's 5h window while the expiring account idled. Net: weekly quota strands at
#   reset (the operator's complaint verbatim) and 5h windows are slammed by bursts.
# The terms, each fail-soft (missing data ⇒ exactly the pre-M7 behavior) and kill-switched:
#   k_work      WORKING sessions — transcripts written inside KWORK_WINDOW_MIN — measured at sweep
#               time beside the pane census (working_concurrency). The census keeps its jobs
#               (display, heal's k==0 rotation gate); only the router's charge changes.
#               Kill: CC_ROUTE_KWORK=off.
#   k_phantom   fire-time assignments (`--assign`, written by handoff-fire at the moment of
#               choice) counted for ASSIGN_TTL_MIN — the router's short-term memory, so a burst
#               of fires walks DOWN the ranking inside the cache TTL instead of stacking.
#               Kill: CC_ROUTE_ASSIGN=off.
#   URGENCY_EXP score = headroom / T**γ, γ=2: deadline-DOMINANT urgency. Linear headroom/T was
#               indifferent between {19% left, resets 27.8h} and {87% left, resets 122h} — the
#               required burn RATES match — but stranding is not rate-symmetric: quota expiring
#               tomorrow has no later chance, quota with 5 days keeps every one of them. γ=2 is
#               the operator's rule as algebra — (headroom/T)·(1/T): the required rate, weighted
#               by how soon it stops being achievable. γ=1 restores the prior math exactly.
#               Kill: CC_ROUTE_URGENCY_EXP=1.
#   projection  SF evaluated at the 5h utilization the account is HEADING FOR at its own measured
#               recent burn (lookahead capped at the 5h reset — pressure cannot accrue past the
#               roll). Six working sessions at 40% score as the near-cutoff they will be within
#               the hour, not the 40% they are. Soften-ONLY by construction: a projection can
#               cost an account the tie, never trip an exclusion, never halt the fleet.
#               Kill: CC_ROUTE_PROJ=off.
# Constants live in the SSOT router block (all optional — R.get defaults below keep an
# un-migrated accounts.json fully working) with CC_ROUTE_* env overrides via _cliff_env.

KWORK_WINDOW_MIN = 10.0    # transcript-mtime window that counts a session as WORKING
ASSIGN_TTL_MIN = 15.0      # assignment lifetime ≈ cache TTL + engage + first sweep that sees it
ASSIGN_PRUNE_LINES = 400   # ledger rewrite threshold (kept to the newest half)
URGENCY_EXP_DEFAULT = 2.0

# ---- the RECYCLE RE-PICK threshold (ACCOUNT_ROUTING_V2 §14, backlog 2dc6906b6e0b) --------------
# §14 wanted a recycle to re-pick on "excluded OR pressured". Only the excluded half shipped:
# handoff-fire's recycle_repick moves a pane when the router names the current account in its
# exclusion map, and otherwise keeps it — its own comment says pressure short of exclusion "belongs
# with M7's scoring terms, where it can be scored against headroom, not here where the only input
# is a boolean."
#
# THIS CONSTANT IS WHY THE OTHER HALF CAN NOW SHIP WITHOUT A BOOLEAN. The measured magnitude is the
# M7 score itself — score_general already prices headroom, deadline urgency, projected 5h
# utilization and the login cliff into one number, and `--rank general` already emits it per
# account. So "pressured" needs no new sensor and no second definition of pressure: it is the RATIO
# between the best account's score and the incumbent's, and the only thing missing was a declared
# threshold for how much better is worth moving a pane for.
#
# It lives HERE, and rides route-meta, for the reason the backlog row insists on: a threshold
# hardcoded in handoff-fire would be a routing policy authored outside the router, free to drift
# from the scoring it is a threshold ON. The consumer reads the number the router declares; it does
# not get a vote.
#
# 4.0 — deliberately well clear of noise rather than tuned. Live spread measured 2026-08-17
# (`--rank general`): 0.005070 / 0.000062 / 0.000047 / 0.000039, i.e. the top account outscored the
# rest by ~80-130x. A real pile-up is orders of magnitude, not percent, so a 4x floor moves a pane
# only when the routing case is unambiguous and never on the jitter between two comparable
# accounts. Raising it makes the re-pick rarer, never wrong.
REPICK_RATIO_DEFAULT = 4.0


def repick_ratio(R):
    """The score ratio (best/incumbent) at or above which a recycle should re-pick, or None when
    the pressure half is switched off (the consumer then re-picks only on exclusion, i.e. v1).

    Takes the router config dict as a parameter, exactly like urgency_exp(R) — `R` is not a module
    global here, and reading it as one raised NameError on the first live call.

    TWO ENV KNOBS, NOT ONE, AND THE SPLIT IS THE POINT. The first draft documented
    `CC_ROUTE_REPICK_RATIO=off` as the kill switch and routed it through _cliff_env, which by
    deliberate design IGNORES a malformed value so that "a typo in a tuning knob must not silently
    disable a safety term". So `off` parsed as garbage and returned the 4.0 default: a kill switch
    that emitted `repick_ratio=4` and killed nothing. Measured, not reasoned — the claim was in the
    docstring for one revision before the live run refuted it.

    The polarity is what _cliff_env gets right and this term needs inverted. Ignoring a typo
    fails SAFE for a term that restrains routing; this term AUTHORISES AN ACTION (moving a pane),
    so its safe failure direction is off, not on. Hence the R8 `_term_on` primitive every other M7
    kill switch already uses:
        CC_ROUTE_REPICK=off|0|false     disable the pressure half entirely  → None → 'off'
        CC_ROUTE_REPICK_RATIO=<float>   tune the threshold (a typo still falls back to the default,
                                        which is correct HERE because a wrong threshold is a
                                        rarer/commoner re-pick, never an unguarded one)"""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_REPICK"):
        return None
    return _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_REPICK_RATIO", (R or {}).get("REPICK_RATIO", REPICK_RATIO_DEFAULT))


# ── D1 (LR_SUSTAINED_LOAD W15, packet 0223cae3d185): a recycle LEAVES an account held for new work ──
# The dispatch floor (dispatch_floor_row/_dispatch_partition) decides which thin accounts are HELD
# for new work; every fire obeys it and --recovery excludes a thin account outright. A recycle is a
# fresh session from disk, i.e. new work, and was the one automatic path that kept landing on a
# held account (3 of 9 at the 2026-10-20 next3 wall). The SETS and the SWITCH ride route-meta for
# repick_ratio's reason: handoff-fire's recycle_repick tests membership and owns no vocabulary.
#
# NOT every hold is a reason to leave (docs/research/lr-sustained-load-2026-10-20/impl/
# D1-repick-ruling.md, review R.3 changes 1-2):
#   - `claimed` is forecast to STRAND by one more fire, and the re-pick's own --assign creates it
#     on the account it lands on; leaving it would evict the residents of a week that dies unused.
#   - `flat` is every thin account once the strand rescue is switched off, so leaving it would turn
#     that kill switch into "leave every thin account" (the 2026-10-09 strand, in reverse).
#   - the data-quality holds read a STALE or ABSENT pace, which appears exactly when sweeps stall
#     under load: an unknown, so the pane stays and the stay row says `held-unknown`.
# So the leave set is the two verdicts that say "this account uses its own week": on-pace, and
# hold-credits (an overshoot is paid overage, never a strand).
REPICK_LEAVE_VERDICTS = ("on-pace", "hold-credits")
REPICK_UNKNOWN_VERDICTS = ("level-stale", "pace-unknown", "pace-stale")
# Most restrictive first: the order IS the precedence (repick_held_mode).
REPICK_HELD_MODES = ("off", "shadow", "on")
# The code default is OFF and stays off until the D1 ruling sets the mode: the stays are still
# written to disk (handoff-fire's recycle-repick-stay row), which is what REPORT B6 asked for
# whatever the ruling says.
REPICK_HELD_DEFAULT = "off"
# The strand-side stop rule (review R.3 change 5a): a held-basis move off an account whose week
# then closes with more than DISPATCH_STRAND_PP unused trips the mode to off. Moves are recorded by
# handoff-fire in their own ledger because handoffs.jsonl is trimmed to ~2.4 days and a thin window
# runs up to 71 h before its reset.
REPICK_STRAND_LOOKBACK_S = 8 * 86400.0     # a week plus a day: every move whose window can close
REPICK_STRAND_CHECK_EVERY_S = 3600.0       # the check reads ledgers; it runs on a sweep, hourly
REPICK_STRAND_ENGAGE_S = 3600.0            # a successor engages in minutes (M5: 103-269 s)
REPICK_STRAND_UTIL_BYTES = 16 << 20        # ~2.4 KB a sweep: 16 MB covers the 71 h worst case
REPICK_STRAND_RESET_SLACK_S = 300.0        # reset stamps differ in the fraction between sweeps


def repick_held_switch_path():
    """`~/.claude/state/repick-held`, one word. Keyed on $HOME and never on CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, so
    every account's sessions read the same switch."""
    return os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "state", "repick-held")


def repick_strand_trip_path():
    return os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "state", "repick-held.strand-trip")


def repick_moves_path():
    return os.environ.get("CC_REPICK_MOVES_LOG") or os.path.join(
        HOME, ".claude", "state", "repick-held-moves.jsonl")


def repick_held_mode(R):
    """off|shadow|on — whether handoff-fire's recycle_repick moves a pane off a HELD incumbent.

    The MOST RESTRICTIVE present value wins (off < shadow < on) across env CC_ROUTE_REPICK_HELD,
    the operator switch file (repick_held_switch_path) and SSOT router.REPICK_HELD, so
    `printf off > ~/.claude/state/repick-held` is fleet-wide even for a process that exported
    `on` (review R.3 change 8). A tripped strand stop rule is a present `off`. A present value
    outside the three reads `off`: this term AUTHORISES moving a pane, so its safe failure
    direction is off (repick_ratio's docstring). Empty or unreadable is absent. Nothing present
    is REPICK_HELD_DEFAULT."""
    try:
        with open(repick_held_switch_path()) as f:
            switch = f.read(64)
    except OSError:
        switch = None
    present = []
    for raw in (os.environ.get("CC_ROUTE_REPICK_HELD"), switch, (R or {}).get("REPICK_HELD")):
        if raw is None or not str(raw).strip():
            continue
        v = str(raw).strip().lower()
        present.append(v if v in REPICK_HELD_MODES else "off")
    if os.path.exists(repick_strand_trip_path()):
        present.append("off")
    if not present:
        return REPICK_HELD_DEFAULT
    return min(present, key=REPICK_HELD_MODES.index)


def repick_held_sets(cand, R):
    """(dispatch_held, repick_leave, repick_unknown) account lists off the partition's own stamps.
    Empty when the floor is off: _dispatch_partition's early return leaves old flags in place, and
    a stray thin_demoted must not speak for a floor that did not run (R-3)."""
    if not dispatch_w_floor(R or {}) > 0:
        return [], [], []
    held = [r for _s, r in cand if r.get("thin_demoted")]
    return ([r["acct"] for r in held],
            [r["acct"] for r in held if r.get("dispatch_floor") in REPICK_LEAVE_VERDICTS],
            [r["acct"] for r in held if r.get("dispatch_floor") in REPICK_UNKNOWN_VERDICTS])


def repick_strand_check(cfg, now=None, force=False):
    """The strand-side stop rule -> the trip evidence when it trips now, else None.

    An account that a held-basis re-pick moved a pane OFF, and whose week then CLOSED more than
    DISPATCH_STRAND_PP short of 100%, writes repick_strand_trip_path(); repick_held_mode then reads
    off until the operator removes the file and re-rules (packet 0223cae3d185). A move counts only
    once it LANDED: a recycle-engaged row for its pane within REPICK_STRAND_ENGAGE_S. A move older
    than handoffs.jsonl's window cannot be judged and counts as landed, toward the stop.
    Runs on a live sweep, at most hourly, and only while the moves ledger holds a recent move, so
    a fleet in mode off pays one failed open() per sweep."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    trip = repick_strand_trip_path()
    if os.path.exists(trip):
        return None
    moves = []
    try:
        with open(repick_moves_path()) as f:
            for ln in f:
                try:
                    m = json.loads(ln)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                t = _iso_epoch(m.get("ts")) if isinstance(m, dict) else None
                if t is not None and now - REPICK_STRAND_LOOKBACK_S <= t <= now and m.get("from"):
                    moves.append((t, m))
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not moves:
        return None
    stamp = trip + ".checked"
    try:
        if not force and now - os.path.getmtime(stamp) < REPICK_STRAND_CHECK_EVERY_S:
            return None
    except OSError:
        pass
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(stamp), exist_ok=True)
        with open(stamp, "w") as f:
            f.write(f"{now:.0f}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass
    # `oldest` = the first row the trimmed handoffs.jsonl still holds: an engagement before it is
    # unknowable, not absent.
    engaged, oldest = {}, None
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "logs", "handoffs.jsonl")) as f:
            for ln in f:
                try:
                    o = json.loads(ln)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                t = _iso_epoch(o.get("ts")) if isinstance(o, dict) else None
                if t is None:
                    continue
                oldest = t if oldest is None else min(oldest, t)
                if o.get("class") == "recycle-engaged" and o.get("engaged") is True \
                        and o.get("target_pane"):
                    engaged.setdefault(str(o["target_pane"]), []).append(t)
    except OSError:
        pass
    util = {}
    try:
        size = os.path.getsize(UTIL_PATH)
        for ln in _tail_lines(UTIL_PATH, size, REPICK_STRAND_UTIL_BYTES)[0]:
            try:
                u = json.loads(ln)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if not isinstance(u, dict) or u.get("stale") is True:
                continue
            t, rs, pct = _iso_epoch(u.get("ts")), _iso_epoch(u.get("weekly_reset_at")), u.get("weekly_pct")
            if t is not None and rs is not None and _is_num(pct):
                util.setdefault(u.get("acct"), []).append((t, rs, float(pct)))
    except OSError:
        return None
    strand_pp = dispatch_strand_pp(cfg.get("router") or {}) or DISPATCH_STRAND_PP_DEFAULT
    for t, m in sorted(moves, key=lambda x: x[0]):
        pane = str(m.get("target_pane") or "")
        landed = (oldest is None or oldest > t
                  or any(t <= e <= t + REPICK_STRAND_ENGAGE_S for e in engaged.get(pane, ())))
        if not landed:
            continue
        series = util.get(m["from"]) or []
        before = [x for x in series if x[0] <= t]
        if not before:
            continue
        reset = max(before)[1]
        if reset > now or reset <= t:
            continue                                    # not closed yet, or a pre-reset reading
        closing = max(p for _t, rs, p in series if abs(rs - reset) <= REPICK_STRAND_RESET_SLACK_S)
        if not 100.0 - closing > strand_pp:
            continue
        ev = {"ts": datetime.fromtimestamp(now, timezone.utc).isoformat(), "acct": m["from"],
              "week_reset_at": datetime.fromtimestamp(reset, timezone.utc).isoformat(),
              "closing_pct": closing, "unused_pp": round(100.0 - closing, 1),
              "strand_pp": strand_pp, "move": m, "packet": "0223cae3d185",
              "clear": "rm " + trip + " after the operator re-rules"}
        try:
            with open(trip, "w") as f:
                f.write(json.dumps(ev) + "\n")
        except OSError:
            return None
        print(f"claude-accounts: D1 STRAND STOP — {m['from']} closed its week at {closing:.0f}% "
              f"with a held-basis recycle move off it; repick_held reads off until the ruling is "
              f"re-opened ({trip})", file=sys.stderr)
        return ev
    return None


PROJ_LOOKAHEAD_H = 1.0     # how far ahead SF projects 5h burn (capped at the window reset)

# ---- the DESK lane's own terms (DESK_ROUTER_AND_STARTUP_V1 W1, R2 §7(ii)/§10) -------------------
# The desk lane is a two-key SORT, not a product of factors (see score_interactive), so it needs
# four constants the other lanes do not have. All optional in the SSOT, all kill-switched, all
# range-validated by _validate_router when present.
#   DESK_5H_FLOOR       projected 5h utilisation below which an account is "5h-safe".
#                       Kill: CC_ROUTE_DESK_5H_FLOOR=off (a NUMBER moves the floor; see below).
#   DESK_W_FLOOR        weekly headroom at/above which an account is "weekly-safe".
#                       Kill: CC_ROUTE_DESK_W_FLOOR=off.
#   DESK_W_FLOOR_FULL_H hours-to-weekly-reset at/above which DESK_W_FLOOR applies IN FULL; below it
#                       the floor ramps linearly to 0 (W2 — see desk_w_floor_at). Kill:
#                       CC_ROUTE_DESK_W_RAMP=off restores the flat floor at every horizon;
#                       CC_ROUTE_DESK_W_FULL_H overrides the value.
#   DESK_HYST_MARGIN    fraction by which a rival must beat the INCUMBENT desk account to take it
#                       (D-B, START_LATENCY_ROUTER.md:82-83 — specified, never built until now).
#                       Kill: CC_ROUTE_DESK_HYST=off; CC_ROUTE_DESK_HYST_MARGIN overrides the value.
#   DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN   how long a `--assign … --src claude-launcher` row still names the incumbent.
#                       300 min = one full 5-hour window: the bucket the desk's own burn lives in.
#                       Longer would pin the desk to an account whose quota state no longer has
#                       anything to do with the session the operator is sitting in.
# 1.00 = "projected to hit the 5h wall inside the lookahead" (_su_projected caps at 1.0, so su < 1.0
# is exactly "will not wall"). Was 0.60, sized by argument and never measured (see score_interactive).
# OPERATOR RULING 2026-10-17: the bottom line is exhausting the weekly limit before it resets (a week
# of a $200/mo plan is ~$50; unused weekly quota is gone), and a 5h level short of the wall has zero
# cost. Live that night: next at 46% 5h, burning 14%/h, projected 60.4% with BOTH its 5h window and
# its week resetting in 1.0h (~27pp of the week forecast to strand) fell to tier 0, and bare `claude`
# went to next4 (reset 6.1h). Below the wall the 5h level no longer decides the desk; the shared
# S_CUT=0.85 hard exclusion in _excluded still applies to every lane.
DESK_5H_FLOOR = 1.00
DESK_W_FLOOR = 0.15
DESK_W_FLOOR_FULL_H = 24.0
DESK_HYST_MARGIN = 0.15
DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN = 300.0
# `--src` values whose phantom charge the DESK lane does not pay (W1.5). A launcher assignment is
# the desk's own previous launch: charging it means the second bare `claude` inside the TTL is
# nudged AWAY from the account the operator is already sitting on. Right for dispatch (spread),
# wrong for a human desk (flapping). General/fable keep charging it — one store, two readings.
# Kill: CC_ROUTE_DESK_ASSIGN_EXEMPT=off.
DESK_EXEMPT_SRC = ("claude-launcher",)
ASSIGN_PATH = os.environ.get("CC_ASSIGN_LOG") or os.path.join(
    HOME, ".claude", "logs", "account-assignments.jsonl")


def _term_on(env_name):
    """M7 kill switches follow the R8 pattern (cliff_enabled): default on, off/0/false disables."""
    return os.environ.get(env_name, "on").strip().lower() not in ("off", "0", "false")


def _switch_on(env_name, off_file):
    """_term_on plus a FILE form, both read on every call (LR_SUSTAINED_LOAD W3). The env form
    serves interactive callers; `~/.claude/state/<off_file>` serves the reconciler's --place
    child, whose plist environment only a reload can change (B4 review F11)."""
    return _term_on(env_name) and not os.path.exists(os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "state", off_file))


def urgency_exp(R):
    return _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_URGENCY_EXP", R.get("URGENCY_EXP", URGENCY_EXP_DEFAULT))


# ---- the RECOVERY lane (LIMIT_RECOVER_100P W6a / research U13) ---------------------------------
# A recovery is NOT a dispatch. A dispatch places a sizeable NEW unit that can be cut to fit the
# quota; a recovery transplants an EXISTING long session that replays a cold, large context and
# keeps burning, and a re-limit costs a whole second recovery cycle. The asymmetry is ~10:1
# against a re-limit, so the recovery lane trades strand-avoidance for SURVIVAL.
#
# Measured 2026-09-19 (U13 §0-§1): `next` at weekly 98% scored 0.000068 against `next3` at weekly
# 11% scoring 0.000071 — the T**2 urgency denominator paid a 40x bonus for `next`'s bucket
# resetting in 11 h, almost exactly cancelling next3's 24x headroom lead. `next` was rank[0] of
# the fable lane at 17:04Z and hit weekly 100% at 18:58Z, 1 h 54 m later. It cleared the one
# weekly guard in that lane by a floating-point ULP: `f_eff <= FABLE_FLOOR` with
# `1.0 - 98/100 == 0.020000000000000018`.
#
# The fix is NOT a fourth score. The strand objective is right AMONG accounts that can host the
# session; only the eligible SET was wrong. So these are floors on ELIGIBILITY, and the modifier
# rides the existing lanes (a `recovery` KIND would need a recovery-general and a recovery-fable
# twin — the doubling is the tell).
RECOVERY_W_FLOOR_DEFAULT = 0.10   # weekly headroom floor: excludes weekly >= 90
RECOVERY_S_CEIL_DEFAULT = 0.60    # projected 5h ceiling: the desk floor's former 0.60 (desk moved to 1.00, 2026-10-17)
RECOVERY_F_FLOOR_DEFAULT = 0.05   # f_eff floor: fable >= 90 at coupling 0.5


def recovery_floors(R):
    """-> (w_floor, s_ceil, f_floor) for the recovery lane: env override > SSOT > code default.

    CC_ROUTE_RECOVERY=off NEUTRALISES all three (R8 kill-switch pattern, as cliff_enabled) so
    `--recovery` becomes byte-identical to pre-U13 routing. The neutral ceiling is +inf rather
    than 1.0 deliberately: a projected 5h of exactly 1.0 is reachable, and a DISABLED guard must
    never be the thing that excludes a row."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_RECOVERY"):
        return 0.0, float("inf"), 0.0
    return (_cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_RECOVERY_W_FLOOR",
                       R.get("RECOVERY_W_FLOOR", RECOVERY_W_FLOOR_DEFAULT)),
            _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_RECOVERY_S_CEIL",
                       R.get("RECOVERY_S_CEIL", RECOVERY_S_CEIL_DEFAULT)),
            _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_RECOVERY_F_FLOOR",
                       R.get("RECOVERY_F_FLOOR", RECOVERY_F_FLOOR_DEFAULT)))


def bound_kwork(row, name, counts, wcounts):
    """Cap the working census's TOP-LEVEL term at the live processes on this account.

    THE DEFECT (measured 2026-09-25). `working_concurrency` counts every transcript written in the
    last KWORK_WINDOW_MIN (10) minutes, which measures THROUGHPUT, not concurrency: a session that
    finished nine minutes ago still counts. Harmless for long interactive sessions, ruinous for a
    harness running short headless `claude -p` jobs back to back — the token-efficiency eval did,
    and every account read 18-21 top-level "working" transcripts against 2-4 live processes. All
    three healthy accounts were refused `kmax-concurrency` (KMAX 8), the router abstained, and a
    /handoff fire was hand-routed to the account whose weekly quota expires LAST — the opposite of
    the router's own deadline-dominant objective.

    The fix is a physical ceiling, not a new constant: a top-level transcript is one session, and a
    session with no live process cannot be burning, so the top-level term is min(written in the
    window, live processes incl. headless). SUBAGENT transcripts stay uncapped on purpose — they
    run inside their parent's process, so a process count cannot bound them, and counting them is
    the deliberate 2026-09-04 change. The raw walk count stays on the row as `k_work_raw`. Applied
    only when BOTH instruments carry their split (a stub or an older caller returns a plain dict,
    so no bound). Kill: CC_ROUTE_KWORK_LIVE_BOUND=off.

    THE SIBLING DEFECT (measured 2026-10-03T18:42:57Z). A process count is the right ceiling only
    while the account holds few processes. next4 held 13 resident, mostly idle panes when a hook
    probe ran 106 short `claude -p` calls on it: the walk read 59, this bound cut it to
    min(59, 13) = 13, still over KMAX 8, and a /handoff fire went to next3 (weekly reset 65 h out)
    instead of next4 (30% used, reset in 14.3 h — the quota the router's objective most wanted
    burned). The idle panes lent their PROCESS count to transcripts that were not theirs.

    So the bound now joins by SESSION ID rather than by count: a top-level transcript counts only
    when its session is a live process — concurrency() reads Claude Code's own `sessions/<pid>.json`
    registry (pid alive) plus any `--resume`/`--session-id` uuid in argv — which keeps a live pane
    that wrote recently and drops a finished `claude -p` the moment it exits. A live process whose
    session id cannot be read lends at most one transcript (`unknown`), so the join can only ever
    tighten the process-count bound above, never loosen it. A subagent counts only while its
    parent session is live; when any live process on the account is unidentified its parent
    cannot be ruled out, so subagents stay counted. Kill: CC_ROUTE_KWORK_SID_BOUND=off restores
    the process-count bound alone."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_KWORK_LIVE_BOUND") or row.get("k_work") is None or counts is None:
        return
    top = getattr(wcounts, "top", None)
    headless = getattr(counts, "headless", None)
    if top is None or headless is None or name not in top or name not in counts:
        return
    total = row["k_work"]
    live = counts[name] + headless.get(name, 0)
    top_term = min(top[name], live)
    sub_term = total - top[name]
    live_sids = (getattr(counts, "live_sids", None) or {}).get(name)
    unknown = (getattr(counts, "unknown", None) or {}).get(name)
    sids = (getattr(wcounts, "sids", None) or {}).get(name)
    if (_term_on("CC_ROUTE_KWORK_SID_BOUND") and live_sids is not None and unknown is not None
            and sids is not None and len(sids) == top[name]):
        matched = len(sids & live_sids)
        top_term = min(top_term, matched + min(top[name] - matched, unknown))
        subs = (getattr(wcounts, "subs", None) or {}).get(name)
        if unknown == 0 and subs is not None:
            sub_term -= sum(n for par, n in subs.items() if par not in live_sids)
    bounded = top_term + max(sub_term, 0)
    if bounded < total:
        row["k_work_raw"] = total
        row["k_work"] = bounded


def k_eff(r):
    """Concurrency the router CHARGES an account: working sessions where the sweep could measure
    them, the pane census where it could not (an older cache, a walk past its budget — absence
    degrades to the stricter count, never to zero), plus live fire-time phantoms. The census is
    a ceiling on burn, not a measure of it — routing on it excluded the account the goal most
    needed burned."""
    kw = r.get("k_work")
    base = kw if (kw is not None and _term_on("CC_ROUTE_KWORK")) else k_panes(r)
    return (base or 0) + (r.get("k_phantom") or 0)


KMAX_RESIDENT_DEFAULT = 40    # ceil(150/4) + headroom — see k_cap's docstring


def k_panes(r, now=None, grace_s=K_GRACE_S):
    """The PANE census this row is charged with: the sweep's own count, else the one INHERITED
    from the last sweep (`k_stale`, written by inherit_k) WHILE IT IS STILL INSIDE THE GRACE,
    else None.

    ONE derivation, for the same reason k_src is one: the charge, the cap, the exclusion reason
    and the display all have to be talking about the same number. It reads `k_stale` and never
    `k`'s absence as zero — a missing census still charges the STRICTER count, never a
    fabricated idle fleet.

    THE AGE IS CHECKED HERE AND NOT ONLY IN inherit_k, because the two bounds answer different
    questions. inherit_k bounds when a count may be WRITTEN, which keeps the cache and the board
    from carrying a number nothing may use. This bounds when it may be CHARGED — and a row
    OUTLIVES the sweep that wrote it: `--route` serves a cache up to `cache_grace_s` old and
    never runs inherit_k at all, so a census written at the edge of the grace would otherwise be
    charged at twice its age by a reader that never checked. The bound has to travel with the
    value, not with the moment it was written.

    `grace_s` defaults to the module constant rather than reading cfg["cache_grace_s"]: every
    caller is a pure row-level derivation with no cfg in hand, and the two are the same 600
    (accounts.json's key and K_GRACE_S). A row whose stamp is missing or unparseable is refused —
    the same fail-closed direction as an absent census.

    The NATIVE census (`k_native`, D2) is charged first when it is inside the same grace and either
    ps produced nothing or native is more than 60 s newer — it is a measurement, not an inheritance.
    The env lookup is the rollback for readers; it is the only thing read here (no file I/O, C2)."""
    kn = _k_native_pick(r, now, grace_s)
    if kn is not None:
        return kn
    k = r.get("k")
    if k is not None:
        return k
    age = _k_stale_s(r, now)
    return r.get("k_stale") if (age is not None and age <= grace_s) else None


K_NATIVE_LEAD_S = 60     # native must be this much newer than a ps count to displace it


def _k_native_pick(r, now=None, grace_s=K_GRACE_S):
    """The native count k_panes charges, or None: present, CC_ACCOUNTS_CENSUS != ps, stamped inside
    the grace, and either `k` is None or native is K_NATIVE_LEAD_S newer than the ps scan."""
    kn = r.get("k_native")
    if kn is None or os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_CENSUS") == "ps":
        return None
    age = _k_native_s(r, now)
    if age is None or not -K_SKEW_TOL_S <= age <= grace_s:
        return None
    if r.get("k") is not None:
        ps_at = _as_epoch(r.get("k_as_of"))
        if ps_at is None or _as_epoch(r.get("k_native_as_of")) <= ps_at + K_NATIVE_LEAD_S:
            return None
    return kn


def _k_native_s(r, now=None):
    """Age in seconds of the row's native census stamp, or None when there is none."""
    t = _as_epoch(r.get("k_native_as_of"))
    if r.get("k_native") is None or t is None:
        return None
    return (now if now is not None else time.time()) - t


def _k_stale_s(r, now=None):
    """Age in whole seconds of an INHERITED pane census, or None when the row's own was measured.

    Derived from `k_stale_as_of` (the time of the MEASUREMENT, carried across re-inheritances)
    rather than from the sweep that served it, which is the number a reader would otherwise
    assume and is wrong by exactly the amount that matters."""
    if r.get("k") is not None or not r.get("k_stale_as_of"):
        return None
    try:
        t = datetime.fromisoformat(str(r["k_stale_as_of"]).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return None
    if t.tzinfo is None:
        t = t.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    return max(0, int((now if now is not None else time.time()) - t.timestamp()))


def k_shown(r):
    """The pane census as the board RENDERS it: the measured count, `N*` when it was inherited
    from the last sweep (the same `*` suffix the quota columns already use for an inherited
    reading), `?` when neither instrument could produce one. Both renderers read this one
    derivation, so the markdown table and the color table cannot disagree about staleness.

    An expired inherited count renders `?`, not `N*`: the board must show what the router is
    charging, and past the grace it is charging nothing. A native count renders UNSTARRED: it is a
    measurement, not an inheritance."""
    kn = _k_native_pick(r)
    if kn is not None:
        return str(kn)
    if r.get("k") is not None:
        return str(r["k"])
    kp = k_panes(r)
    return f"{kp}*" if kp is not None else "?"


def k_src(r):
    """WHICH instrument produced this row's concurrency charge:
    'work' | 'panes-native' | 'panes' | 'panes-stale' | 'unmeasured'.

    One derivation, because the cap, the KF gradient, the exclusion reason and route-meta's
    `k_src=` field all have to agree about it. They previously each re-spelled the same ternary.

    'panes-stale' is the last sweep's pane census, inherited by inherit_k inside the 600 s grace
    because THIS sweep's `ps` could not be read. It is a distinct label rather than 'panes' for
    the reason §6 of desk-router-abstention-2026-09-01 documents: a policy that changes which
    accounts are routable must be answerable from disk afterwards, and route-meta reports this
    field per decision.

    'panes-native' is the libproc census (D2) when k_panes charges it; it takes the resident cap
    through the same `!= "work"` fall-through as every pane census."""
    if r.get("k_work") is not None and _term_on("CC_ROUTE_KWORK"):
        return "work"
    if _k_native_pick(r) is not None:
        return "panes-native"
    if r.get("k") is not None:
        return "panes"
    # k_panes, never `r.get("k_stale")` directly: an inherited count PAST the grace is not a
    # weaker instrument, it is no instrument — and this label decides both the cap and whether
    # `_excluded` refuses, so it has to expire on exactly the same predicate as the charge.
    return "panes-stale" if k_panes(r) is not None else "unmeasured"


def k_cap(r, R):
    """The concurrency cap that applies to THIS row's charge — derived from the INSTRUMENT that
    produced it, never one integer serving both.

    THE DEFECT (scaling-bottlenecks-2026-08-09 §5 P2 / 07 §6.1-6.2): `KMAX` is sized in
    accounts.json's own note against the PANE census — "observed 5-6 live sessions on primary
    accounts as deliberate operator practice" — i.e. it is a RESIDENT cap. M7 then re-keyed the
    charge onto `k_work`, an ACTIVE census, and left the integer alone. So one number now answers
    two different questions, and it is wrong for whichever one it is not answering:

      · against k_work (ACTIVE): 8 is KEPT (operator ruling, FLEET_V2 decision 8, 2026-09-30).
        The "5-6 infra-limiter band" once cited here came from GH#62426 and was never measured on
        this machine; what backs 8 is 0 failures in 40 probe first turns at N<=6, and that 8 is
        the only cap that builds up ACROSS placement passes (movers placed in the last 15 min
        count as phantoms against it, while every other floor resets per pass). Residency itself
        costs ~0 quota (07 §4).
      · against the pane census (RESIDENT): 8 x 4 accounts = 32, and the 33rd session fleet-wide
        is refused `kmax-concurrency` -> rc 2 -> handoff-fire HALTS. The 150-resident design point
        breaks 4.7x early, on one integer in one JSON file, on the path CLAUDE.md makes the
        DEFAULT execution locus.

    And the census is not the exotic path: `k_eff` falls back to it whenever the transcript walk
    goes over budget — which `_kwork_unmeasured` itself notes means "the box is pathologically
    loaded, i.e. exactly when the concurrency count matters most" — and whenever CC_ROUTE_KWORK is
    off. So the resident wall is reachable at any moment, from a term nothing surfaces.

    The split is 07 §6.2's table, unchanged: KMAX stays the ACTIVE gate at its current value (so
    every other consumer of `.router.KMAX` — cc-wave-plan's urgency bound reads the same integer
    and keeps the same meaning), and KMAX_RESIDENT is the resident gate at ceil(150/4)+headroom.
    OPTIONAL by contract with a matching code default, like every M7/W1 addition, because
    ~/.claude/accounts.json is a symlink into a checkout that is older than the code for as long
    as a land takes to converge — a required-key addition fails CLOSED fleet-wide in that window.

    'unmeasured' has no cap because it has no charge; `_excluded` refuses it before reaching here.
    The value returned for it is the resident cap purely so KF cannot divide by a None.
    'panes-stale' IS a pane census — an older one — so it takes the resident cap by the same
    argument, via the same `!= "work"` fall-through."""
    if k_src(r) == "work":
        return R["KMAX"]
    return R.get("KMAX_RESIDENT", KMAX_RESIDENT_DEFAULT)


def k_eff_desk(r):
    """Concurrency the DESK lane charges: k_eff minus the desk's own launcher phantoms (W1.5).

    The desk lane's two-key sort has no KF term, so this reaches routing through exactly ONE gate
    — `_excluded`'s KMAX cap — and that is the harsher half of the original complaint: a phantom
    that merely re-ordered the general lane can lock the desk OUT of the account the operator is
    already working in, for a whole ASSIGN_TTL_MIN, on the evidence of the operator's own previous
    launch. `k_phantom_desk` is stamped by apply_assignments; its ABSENCE (a module-level caller, an
    older cached row) degrades to the shared `k_phantom`, i.e. to pre-W1 behaviour — the exemption
    fails toward charging, never toward admitting a pile-on."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_DESK_ASSIGN_EXEMPT"):
        return k_eff(r)
    kw = r.get("k_work")
    base = kw if (kw is not None and _term_on("CC_ROUTE_KWORK")) else k_panes(r)
    ph = r.get("k_phantom_desk")
    if ph is None:
        ph = r.get("k_phantom")
    return (base or 0) + (ph or 0)


def _assignment_rows(path=None):
    """Every parseable assignment row, oldest first. One read, two readings (all-srcs for the
    dispatch lanes, launcher-exempt for the desk) — a second open() would race the appender and
    could hand the two lanes different ledgers for the same decision.

    Two row shapes: a CHARGE ({t, acct, ...}) and a VOID ({t, void: id}, `--unassign`). Voids are
    returned too — a void is only meaningful against the whole ledger — so every consumer must
    skip rows without `acct`."""
    out = []
    try:
        with open(path or ASSIGN_PATH) as f:
            for line in f:
                try:
                    e = json.loads(line)
                except json.JSONDecodeError:
                    continue
                if isinstance(e, dict) and isinstance(e.get("t"), (int, float)) and (
                        e.get("acct") or isinstance(e.get("void"), str)):
                    out.append(e)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


def assignment_counts(cfg, path=None, now=None, exempt_src=(), sweep=None, ttl_min=None):
    """Live per-account WEIGHTED count of assignments younger than their TTL. Read at INVOCATION
    time, not sweep time — acting inside the 90s cache TTL is this term's entire purpose. A
    malformed line or missing ledger is silence, never an error: spread is advisory.

    `exempt_src` drops rows by their `--src` (W1.5) — used only by the desk lane; the default is
    empty, so general/fable charging is byte-identical to pre-W1.

    The v2 ledger (lr-reconciler) adds four optional row fields, and each rule exists so a
    reconciler can RE-PLAN without inflating the charge: `w` weights a row (default 1 — one
    session); an `id` with ANY void row anywhere in the ledger charges nothing (a cancelled plan
    must stop repelling routing at once, not after its TTL); rows sharing an `id` charge ONCE, the
    newest winning (a re-plan replaces, never stacks); `ttl_s` bounds that row's own age, and a row
    without it keeps ASSIGN_TTL_MIN. A ledger holding no v2 fields reads exactly as before.

    `sweep` ({acct: (sids, sids_at)}, from the rows' k_sids/k_sids_at) drops a row whose `sid`
    the same account's census already counted in k_work AFTER the row was written — the session
    is live on disk, so charging its phantom too would count it twice.

    `ttl_min` replaces ASSIGN_TTL_MIN as the default row lifetime for THIS read: the dispatch
    floor's placement memory (`k_placed`, apply_assignments) asks the same ledger "what was placed
    here in the last five hours", not "what is too new for the census to have seen". None (every
    other caller) reads exactly as before."""
    counts = {}
    for e, w in _live_assignments(cfg, path, now, exempt_src, sweep, ttl_min):
        counts[e["acct"]] = counts.get(e["acct"], 0) + w
    return counts


def _live_assignments(cfg, path=None, now=None, exempt_src=(), sweep=None, ttl_min=None):
    """The ledger rows assignment_counts charges, each as (row, weight), by exactly its rules.
    --place reads it too, to find a mover's OWN phantom (gap-3 E4.3)."""
    out = []
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_ASSIGN"):
        return out
    ttl_s = 60.0 * (ttl_min if ttl_min is not None else
                    _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_ASSIGN_TTL_MIN",
                               cfg["router"].get("ASSIGN_TTL_MIN", ASSIGN_TTL_MIN)))
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    rows = _assignment_rows(path)
    voided = {e["void"] for e in rows if isinstance(e.get("void"), str)}
    live, newest = [], {}
    for e in rows:
        if not e.get("acct"):
            continue
        i = e.get("id")
        if i is None:
            live.append(e)
        elif i not in voided and (i not in newest or e["t"] >= newest[i]["t"]):
            newest[i] = e
    for e in live + list(newest.values()):
        t_ttl = e.get("ttl_s")
        if isinstance(t_ttl, bool) or not isinstance(t_ttl, (int, float)) or not t_ttl > 0:
            t_ttl = ttl_s
        if not 0 <= now - e["t"] <= t_ttl or e.get("src") in exempt_src:
            continue
        sid, seen = e.get("sid"), (sweep or {}).get(e["acct"])
        if sid and seen and seen[1] is not None and seen[1] > e["t"] and sid in seen[0]:
            continue
        w = e.get("w")
        if isinstance(w, bool) or not isinstance(w, int) or w < 1:
            w = 1
        out.append((e, w))
    return out


def desk_incumbent(cfg, path=None, now=None):
    """The account the DESK is currently on — the newest `--src claude-launcher` assignment inside
    DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN — or None. This is the hysteresis input (W1.4 / D-B).

    Why this store and not a new one: the launcher already records the fact at the moment of
    choice (`claude-launcher.zsh:84`), so the desk's own history is already on disk and a second
    store would be a second truth. Note the symmetry with W1.5 — the SAME rows the desk refuses to
    charge as load are the rows it reads as memory. That is the honest reading of a launcher
    assignment: it is not concurrent burn, it is where the operator is sitting.

    None whenever the term is off, the ledger is unreadable, or nothing is in window — hysteresis
    then simply does not apply, which is exactly pre-W1 behaviour."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_DESK_HYST"):
        return None
    ttl_s = 60.0 * _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN",
                              cfg["router"].get("DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN", DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN))
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    best = None
    for e in _assignment_rows(path):
        if not e.get("acct") or e.get("src") not in DESK_EXEMPT_SRC:
            continue
        if not 0 <= now - e["t"] <= ttl_s:
            continue
        if best is None or e["t"] >= best["t"]:
            best = e
    return best["acct"] if best else None


_ASSIGN_ID_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9:._-]+")
_ASSIGN_MANY_KEYS = ("acct", "id", "sid", "w", "ttl_s")


def assignment_row(cfg, acct, src=None, id=None, sid=None, w=None, ttl_s=None, t=None):
    """Build one validated charge row, or raise ValueError (the CLI's exit 64). A row carries only
    the fields GIVEN, in {t, ts, acct[, src][, id][, sid][, w][, ttl_s]} order, so a legacy
    `--assign next --src X` stays byte-identical to the pre-v2 row. The account name is NOT
    checked here: record_assignment never checked it (its caller does), and pinned tests rely on
    that; parse_assign_many checks it itself."""
    for k, v in (("id", id), ("sid", sid)):
        if v is not None and not (isinstance(v, str) and _ASSIGN_ID_RE.fullmatch(v)):
            raise ValueError(f"{k} must match [A-Za-z0-9:._-]+ (got {v!r})")
    if w is not None and (isinstance(w, bool) or not isinstance(w, int) or w < 1):
        raise ValueError(f"w must be a positive integer (got {w!r})")
    if ttl_s is not None and (isinstance(ttl_s, bool) or not isinstance(ttl_s, (int, float))
                              or not math.isfinite(ttl_s) or ttl_s <= 0):
        raise ValueError(f"ttl_s must be a positive number (got {ttl_s!r})")
    t = time.time() if t is None else t
    row = {"t": t, "ts": datetime.fromtimestamp(t, timezone.utc).isoformat(), "acct": acct}
    for k, v in (("src", src), ("id", id), ("sid", sid), ("w", w), ("ttl_s", ttl_s)):
        if v is not None and (k != "src" or v):
            row[k] = v
    return row


def append_assignments(rows, path=None):
    """Append rows in ONE write under ONE exclusive flock on `<ledger>.lock`, and prune inside the
    same hold. -> error string or None. The prune used to run unlocked after the append — "worst
    case one phantom is lost" — which was tolerable for a lone handoff-fire append and is not for
    a reconciler whose void or batched re-plan the unlocked rewrite could silently drop. Readers
    stay lockless: the prune swaps the file by os.replace, so a reader sees old or new, whole."""
    path = path or ASSIGN_PATH
    if not rows:
        return None
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path + ".lock", "a") as lock:
            # BOUNDED, like every flock in this file: a hold is one append plus a <=400-line
            # rewrite (milliseconds), so 5 s only ever expires behind a wedged holder — and then
            # the caller gets exit 5, never a fire stalled on the ledger.
            deadline = time.monotonic() + 5.0
            while True:
                try:
                    fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                    break
                except BlockingIOError:
                    if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                        return f"assign append failed: {path}.lock held for 5s"
                    time.sleep(0.02)
            with open(path, "a") as f:
                f.write("".join(json.dumps(r, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n" for r in rows))
            try:
                with open(path) as f:
                    lines = f.readlines()
                if len(lines) > ASSIGN_PRUNE_LINES:
                    tmp = path + f".{os.getpid()}.tmp"
                    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
                        f.writelines(lines[-(ASSIGN_PRUNE_LINES // 2):])
                    os.replace(tmp, path)
            except OSError:
                pass
    except OSError as e:
        return f"assign append failed: {e}"
    return None


def record_assignment(cfg, acct, src=None, path=None, *, id=None, sid=None, w=None, ttl_s=None):
    """Append one fire-time assignment — called by the consumer that COMMITS to launching a NEW
    session on the account (handoff-fire, post-pick). Returns an error string or None; the
    caller decides fatality (handoff-fire deliberately does not care — a lost append degrades
    spread by one phantom for one TTL, never a fire). Raises ValueError on an invalid v2 field
    (id/sid/w/ttl_s) — a malformed charge is a usage error, not a lost append."""
    return append_assignments([assignment_row(cfg, acct, src, id=id, sid=sid, w=w, ttl_s=ttl_s)],
                              path)


def record_void(assign_id, path=None):
    """Append {t, ts, void: id} — `--unassign`. Idempotent: assignment_counts treats ANY void for
    an id as final, so a repeat changes nothing. Raises ValueError on a malformed id."""
    if not (isinstance(assign_id, str) and _ASSIGN_ID_RE.fullmatch(assign_id)):
        raise ValueError(f"id must match [A-Za-z0-9:._-]+ (got {assign_id!r})")
    t = time.time()
    return append_assignments([{"t": t, "ts": datetime.fromtimestamp(t, timezone.utc).isoformat(),
                                "void": assign_id}], path)


def parse_assign_many(cfg, text):
    """Validate EVERY line of an --assign-many JSONL before anything is written -> [row], all
    sharing one t/ts. Raises ValueError naming the first bad line: all-or-nothing, because a
    half-applied re-plan is a charge the reconciler never meant to make."""
    t, out = time.time(), []
    for n, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        try:
            o = json.loads(line)
        except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
            raise ValueError(f"line {n}: not JSON ({e})")
        if not isinstance(o, dict) or set(o) - set(_ASSIGN_MANY_KEYS):
            raise ValueError(f"line {n}: want an object with keys ⊆ {_ASSIGN_MANY_KEYS}")
        if o.get("acct") not in {x["name"] for x in cfg["accounts"]}:
            raise ValueError(f"line {n}: unknown account {o.get('acct')!r}")
        try:
            out.append(assignment_row(cfg, o.get("acct"), id=o.get("id"), sid=o.get("sid"),
                                      w=o.get("w"), ttl_s=o.get("ttl_s"), t=t))
        except ValueError as e:
            raise ValueError(f"line {n}: {e}")
    return out


def _route_records_path():
    """`~/.claude/route/route.jsonl`, honouring cc-route's OWN env override.

    Read at CALL time, not import time, so a test (or a probe) that exports the variable after
    this module is loaded still lands in its sandbox — the alternative is a suite that silently
    appends fabricated fleet decisions to the operator's real record, which is exactly the trap
    the LOG_PATH note in tests/claude-accounts-core.bats documents."""
    return os.path.join(os.environ.get("CC_ROUTE_RECORDS_DIR")
                        or os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "route"), "route.jsonl")


def log_route_decision(kind, cand, reasons, cfg, cached, path=None):
    """Append ONE record per DESK decision to route.jsonl — the lane that had no recorder (W2.6).

    Every other routed decision on this box is on disk: cc-route writes {ts, slot, outcome, detail}
    plus its inputs for general and fable. The interactive lane wrote NOTHING. Its only footprint
    was one `--assign` row in a ledger it SHARES with handoff-fire and which prunes 400 -> 200
    (ASSIGN_PRUNE_LINES), so at the moment the routing incident was investigated exactly FOUR
    launcher-routed launches survived in the whole history — the incident was reconstructable only
    because it happened to be the ledger's last line. A lane whose decisions cannot be replayed is
    a lane whose policy cannot be falsified, which is how a measured-false premise (W1) shipped and
    stayed for a day.

    Written for the DESK only. general/fable already have a recorder in cc-route, and a second
    producer for the same slot would double-count every dispatch decision.

    Contract: {ts, slot, outcome, detail} FIRST and in cc-route's own spelling, so a consumer that
    selects .slot/.outcome/.detail reads both producers with one filter; the desk's own fields ride
    additively behind them. An ABSTENTION is recorded too (outcome "none") — "the desk refused and
    the launcher fell back to its pinned account" is a decision, and the silent version of it is
    indistinguishable from the router never having run.

    Never fatal, never noisy: this sits on the launcher's own critical path, so an unwritable
    records dir degrades to no record rather than to no launch. Kill: CC_ROUTE_DESK_LOG=off."""
    if kind != "interactive" or not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_DESK_LOG"):
        return None
    path = path or _route_records_path()
    w = cand[0][1] if cand else None
    run = cand[1][1] if len(cand) > 1 else None
    row = {"ts": datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
           "slot": "interactive",
           "outcome": "route" if w else "none",
           "detail": (f"desk -> {w['acct']}" if w else "desk -> none")
                     + (f" (runner-up {run['acct']})" if run else ""),
           "kind": kind,
           "acct": w["acct"] if w else None,
           "score": round(cand[0][0], 6) if cand else None,
           # The RUNNER-UP is the field that makes a decision re-judgeable rather than merely
           # readable: "next2 won" says nothing about whether it won by a nose or by a mile, and
           # the margin is exactly what the hysteresis term (W1.4) acts on.
           "runner_up": run["acct"] if run else None,
           "runner_up_score": round(cand[1][0], 6) if run else None,
           "desk_tier": desk_keys(w, cfg)[2] if w else None,
           "desk_incumbent": bool(w.get("desk_incumbent")) if w else False,
           "quota_cached": bool(cached),
           # The factor's role was only INFERABLE before these fields: route.jsonl carried no cliff
           # fact at all, so 683 soft-band flips had to be reconstructed by replay.
           "cliff_band": cliff_band(w) if w else None,
           "desk_cliff_factor": _desk_cliff_factor(w) if w else None,
           "runner_up_desk_cliff_factor": _desk_cliff_factor(run) if run else None,
           "excluded": dict(reasons or {})}
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(row, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n")
    except OSError as e:
        return f"route record append failed: {e}"
    return None


def _assignment_sweep(rows):
    """{acct: (sids, sids_at)} from rows whose sweep named its sessions (k_sids, as of k_sids_at)."""
    return {r.get("acct"): (set(r.get("k_sids") or ()), r.get("k_sids_at"))
            for r in rows if r.get("k_sids_at") is not None}


def apply_assignments(rows, cfg):
    """Stamp live phantom counts onto rows (cached or fresh — both are ≤TTL-old opinions about
    everything EXCEPT what this box fired seconds ago, which is exactly what phantoms carry).

    Three fields, one ledger read each by construction: `k_phantom` (every src — what general and
    fable charge, unchanged), `k_phantom_desk` (launcher-src exempt — W1.5), and `desk_incumbent`
    (W1.4). The desk facts are stamped HERE, beside the phantom count, rather than read inside
    score_interactive, because a scorer runs once per row and would otherwise re-read the ledger
    four times per decision and could see it change mid-ranking.

    A fourth, `k_placed`: the same count over DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN instead of ASSIGN_TTL_MIN —
    the dispatch floor's placement memory (dispatch_floor_row). No `sweep`: a session the census
    already counts still holds its claim on the account's spare quota, which is the point. The key
    is ABSENT with CC_ROUTE_ASSIGN off, and the floor then never promotes: a count of 0 that
    means "not tracked" would read as "nothing placed"."""
    # A row whose sweep named its sessions (k_sids, as of k_sids_at) lends that list to the
    # ledger read, so a phantom with a `sid` the SAME account's census already counts is not
    # charged a second time. Rows without k_sids_at (walk unmeasured, stub) skip nothing.
    sweep = _assignment_sweep(rows)
    counts = assignment_counts(cfg, sweep=sweep)
    desk_counts = assignment_counts(cfg, exempt_src=DESK_EXEMPT_SRC, sweep=sweep)
    inc = desk_incumbent(cfg)
    placed = (assignment_counts(cfg, ttl_min=dispatch_claim_ttl_min(cfg["router"]))
              if _term_on("CC_ROUTE_ASSIGN") else None)
    for r in rows:
        r["k_phantom"] = counts.get(r.get("acct"), 0)
        r.pop("k_placed", None)
        if placed is not None:
            r["k_placed"] = placed.get(r.get("acct"), 0)
        r["k_phantom_desk"] = desk_counts.get(r.get("acct"), 0)
        r["desk_incumbent"] = (inc is not None and r.get("acct") == inc)


def _su_projected(r, R):
    """5h utilization the router scores: measured, plus what the account's own recent burn rate
    accrues over the lookahead — capped at the window reset, past which pressure vanishes.
    Consumed only by _soft, so it is soften-only by construction."""
    su = (r.get("session_pct") or 0) / 100.0
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_PROJ"):
        return su
    # 🚨 THE UNIT IS THE HAZARD (S7). `burn_5h_ewma_ph` is %/h and `burn_5h_ph` is fraction/h,
    # and the arithmetic below is `su + b * ahead` with su in [0,1] — so a %/h value read on the
    # old key's terms saturates every row to 1.0 and renders as "the whole fleet is under 5h
    # pressure", a 100x error wearing an entirely plausible face. The two keys therefore stay
    # SEPARATE and the conversion happens here, at the one consumer, rather than the producer
    # writing a fraction under a name that says percent.
    b = r.get("burn_5h_ewma_ph")
    if isinstance(b, (int, float)):
        b = b / 100.0
    else:
        b = r.get("burn_5h_ph")     # the incumbent, still populated for one release
    if not isinstance(b, (int, float)) or b <= 0:
        return su
    ahead = _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_PROJ_LOOKAHEAD_H", R.get("PROJ_LOOKAHEAD_H", PROJ_LOOKAHEAD_H))
    srh = r.get("session_reset_h")
    if isinstance(srh, (int, float)) and srh >= 0:
        ahead = min(ahead, srh)
    return min(1.0, su + b * ahead)


def _reset_key(stamp):
    """Minute-ROUNDED reset-stamp key. The stamp jitters sub-second and STRADDLES the minute
    boundary (measured second-of-minute over 4,000 records: 00:1880 · 59:1427 · 01:13 · 17:1),
    so a string prefix or a truncation splits one window into two on 46.0% of adjacent pairs and
    the roll branch then injects an ABSOLUTE level as a delta. Rounding flips on 1.0% and agrees
    with ground truth (reset shift > 1 h) on 99 of 99. None means NO WINDOW OPEN — a distinct
    state, not missing data (15.0% of rows; 1,789 of 1,790 have session_pct == 0)."""
    if not stamp:
        return None
    try:
        t = datetime.fromisoformat(str(stamp).replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return None
    return round(t / 60.0)


def _rolled(a, b, stamp_field, pct_field):
    """True when the window between two samples RESET. Two independent witnesses, OR'd: the
    rounded key changed, or the meter went backwards. Neither alone is sufficient — a key
    comparison is blind to a same-key reset, and a pct decrease is blind to a reset that
    lands on the same value. A None key never counts as a roll: no window open is a state."""
    ka, kb = _reset_key(a.get(stamp_field)), _reset_key(b.get(stamp_field))
    if ka is not None and kb is not None and ka != kb:
        return True
    pa, pb = a.get(pct_field), b.get(pct_field)
    return pa is not None and pb is not None and pb < pa


UTIL_TAIL_SEED_B = 131072       # first tail slice; doubled until the SPAN is covered
UTIL_TAIL_MAX_B = 33554432      # 32 MiB — past the 25 MiB rotation bound, so never a real cap
UTIL_TAIL_CROSS_SLACK_S = 3600  # a slice "reached back" only past cutoff − this (B4 C3, review F3)


_UTIL_MEMO = None            # {"ident", "hours", "rows"} — this process's last _util_tail read
_UTIL_MEMO_ON = False        # set by --keepwarm, --local-refresh and --readout only (never --route)


def _util_ident(path):
    out = [path]
    for p in [path] + sorted(glob.glob(path + ".*")):
        try:
            st = os.stat(p)
            out.append((p, st.st_size, st.st_mtime_ns))
        except OSError:
            pass
    return out


def _util_tail(path=None, hours=48.0, max_bytes=None):
    """_util_tail_read behind a per-process memo (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D8). One render reads
    the 16 MB series twice (apply_burn 336 h, fleet_pace_line 72 h) — cProfile put it at 1.22 s of
    a 1.51 s render. A request no wider than the memo's, on the same file identity (size and
    mtime_ns of the file and its rotated siblings), is served by filtering the memo by its own
    cutoff, as copies. Off unless _UTIL_MEMO_ON: the launch path keeps the plain read."""
    global _UTIL_MEMO
    if not _UTIL_MEMO_ON or max_bytes:
        return _util_tail_read(path, hours, max_bytes)
    p = path or UTIL_PATH
    ident = _util_ident(p)
    m = _UTIL_MEMO
    if m and m["ident"] == ident and hours <= m["hours"]:
        cutoff = time.time() - max(0.0, hours) * 3600.0
        out = [dict(e) for e in m["rows"] if e["_t"] >= cutoff]
        span = ((max(r["_t"] for r in out) - min(r["_t"] for r in out)) / 3600.0
                if len(out) >= 2 else 0.0)
        return out, span
    out, span = _util_tail_read(p, hours, max_bytes)
    _UTIL_MEMO = {"ident": ident, "hours": hours, "rows": [dict(e) for e in out]}
    return out, span


def _util_tail_read(path=None, hours=48.0, max_bytes=None):
    """Newest `hours` of the utilization series (record_utilization's product), parsed and
    time-stamped, as `(rows, achieved_span_h)`.

    SELECTION IS BY TIME, NOT BY BYTES. The prior signature was `max_bytes=131072` and
    `apply_burn` called it with no argument, so the span was whatever 128 KiB happened to buy:
    against the live file (3,675,531 B, mean record 292.4 B) that is **407 rows spanning
    12.16 h** while the docstring claimed 48 h, and M2's 48 h lookback was unreachable. The
    coupling was undocumented and INVERTED — adding any field to the record silently SHORTENS
    the window. The byte seek survives only as a cheap pre-filter, doubled until the requested
    span is actually covered, so a record-size change can no longer move the window.

    RETURNS THE ACHIEVED SPAN, and every consumer's abstain rule is written against it. A
    consumer that asks for 48 h and receives 12 h must SEE 12 h and abstain on its own rule —
    it must never be told it got 48 (L2: abstain, never impute).

    Rotation: `config/store-bounds.manifest:47` rotates this file at 25 MiB and at 237 KiB/day
    that lands ~2026-12-11. Readers that open only the live path would silently drop to a series
    of length ~0 the day it rotates, so the `.gz` siblings are read newest-first until the
    requested span is covered.

    Stale rows (inherited/errored sweeps) are dropped — a rate needs two MEASUREMENTS, not a
    measurement and an echo.

    THE STOP RULE (B4 C3). The old test, "a KEPT row within 60 s after the cutoff", almost never
    held: the log is written in bursts (p90 gap 399 s), so on ~85% of calls the read doubled to
    the whole file and then walked every rotated sibling. A read now also stops once a slice holds
    a dated row older than cutoff − UTIL_TAIL_CROSS_SLACK_S. That keeps the same rows only on a
    time-ordered file, which the single writer gives by construction: record_utilization stamps
    `ts` under get_data's single-flight flock (_acquire_lock). The slack absorbs a small inversion
    straddling the cutoff (review F3, test U5), and the final sort is unconditional so row order
    never depends on which path stopped the read. It stops at the old rule's slice or an earlier
    one, never before a slice crosses the cutoff. Kill switch: CC_UTIL_TAIL_CROSS=off or
    ~/.claude/state/util-tail-cross.off."""
    path = path or UTIL_PATH
    cutoff = time.time() - max(0.0, hours) * 3600.0
    cross_at = cutoff - UTIL_TAIL_CROSS_SLACK_S if _switch_on(
        "CC_UTIL_TAIL_CROSS", "util-tail-cross.off") else None

    def _parse(lines, out):
        """-> True when a dated row older than cross_at was seen: the read reached back past the
        cutoff. A stale row counts for that (its stamp is still a time on the file)."""
        crossed = False
        for ln in lines:
            try:
                e = json.loads(ln)
            except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError):
                continue
            try:
                t = datetime.fromisoformat(str(e.get("ts")).replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
            except (ValueError, TypeError):
                continue
            if t < cutoff:
                crossed = crossed or (cross_at is not None and t < cross_at)
                continue
            if e.get("stale"):
                continue
            e["_t"] = t
            out.append(e)
        return crossed

    out, reached = [], False
    try:
        size = os.path.getsize(path)
    except OSError:
        size = 0
    if size:
        want = max_bytes if isinstance(max_bytes, int) and max_bytes > 0 else UTIL_TAIL_SEED_B
        while True:
            rows = []
            try:
                with open(path) as f:
                    if size > want:
                        f.seek(size - want)
                        f.readline()          # drop the probable fragment after a mid-file seek
                    reached = _parse(f.readlines(), rows)
            except OSError:
                rows, reached = [], False
            out = rows
            # Covered when we either reached back past the cutoff or read the whole file. A
            # FIXED byte cap cannot know which of those it hit; that is the defect being cured.
            if want >= size or want >= UTIL_TAIL_MAX_B or max_bytes:
                break
            if reached or (rows and min(r["_t"] for r in rows) <= cutoff + 60):
                break
            want *= 2

    # Still short of the requested span (or the live file is gone): walk the rotations.
    if not reached and (not out or min(r["_t"] for r in out) > cutoff + 60):
        try:
            sibs = sorted(glob.glob(path + ".*"),
                          key=lambda p: (os.path.getmtime(p), p), reverse=True)
        except OSError:
            sibs = []
        for sib in sibs:
            try:
                if sib.endswith(".gz"):
                    with gzip.open(sib, "rt") as f:
                        reached = _parse(f.readlines(), out)
                else:
                    with open(sib) as f:
                        reached = _parse(f.readlines(), out)
            except (OSError, EOFError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                continue
            if reached or (out and min(r["_t"] for r in out) <= cutoff + 60):
                break
    out.sort(key=lambda e: e["_t"])

    span = ((max(r["_t"] for r in out) - min(r["_t"] for r in out)) / 3600.0) if len(out) >= 2 else 0.0
    return out, span


K_FROZEN = 0.192              # pooled, 2026-08-10..25, n=11,026 pairs (Sds=4047, Sdw=779) -> 0.1925
K_MIN_SDS = 300.0             # session pp of movement below which the trailing fit is too thin
K_SANE = (0.175, 0.210)       # outside this the PLAN changed (or the meter did) -> abstain


def exchange_rate(samples, hours=168.0):
    """weekly pp per session pp, fitted on the trailing window. Ratio-of-sums over adjacent
    non-rolling pairs (dt <= 0.5 h) -- NEVER a mean-of-ratios (quantization biases that upward at
    small windows: 0.158 below 5 session pp vs 0.192 above 30) and NEVER binned by burn intensity
    (that is K's own denominator; a simulation with K exactly 0.192 reproduces the whole apparent
    trend). Returns (K, sum_ds, 'live'|'frozen'|None).

    WHY IT IS FITTED AND NOT A LITERAL. Re-fitting on trailing slices already reads 0.2000
    (from 08-21) and 0.2012 (from 08-22), ABOVE the shipped band's 0.198 ceiling, on Sds of
    1,350 and 1,019. A permutation test on the late-vs-early difference gives p = 0.0948 — so it
    is not proven to be a real change, and a FROZEN literal has no path to learn that it became
    one. Abstaining outside K_SANE is the only reading that stays honest under both hypotheses:
    a silently widened band launders a plan change into a number."""
    now = time.time()
    cutoff = now - max(0.0, hours) * 3600.0
    by = {}
    for e in samples or []:
        if e.get("_t") is not None and e["_t"] >= cutoff:
            by.setdefault(e.get("acct"), []).append(e)
    sum_ds = sum_dw = 0.0
    for ss in by.values():
        ss = sorted(ss, key=lambda e: e["_t"])
        for a, b in zip(ss, ss[1:]):
            dh = (b["_t"] - a["_t"]) / 3600.0
            if not (0 < dh <= 0.5):
                continue
            if _rolled(a, b, "session_reset_at", "session_pct"):
                continue
            if _rolled(a, b, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_pct"):
                continue
            sp_a, sp_b = a.get("session_pct"), b.get("session_pct")
            wp_a, wp_b = a.get("weekly_pct"), b.get("weekly_pct")
            if None in (sp_a, sp_b, wp_a, wp_b):
                continue
            ds, dw = sp_b - sp_a, wp_b - wp_a
            if ds < 0 or dw < 0:
                continue
            sum_ds += ds
            sum_dw += dw
    if sum_ds < K_MIN_SDS:
        return K_FROZEN, sum_ds, "frozen"
    k = sum_dw / sum_ds
    if not (K_SANE[0] <= k <= K_SANE[1]):
        return None, sum_ds, None
    return k, sum_ds, "live"


WEEKLY_TAIL_GAP_H = 3.0       # a window is "observed to its end" only if a sample lands this close


def completed_weekly_windows(samples, now=None):
    """Every weekly window in the series that has actually CLOSED and was observed to its close.

    Two conditions, and the second one is the whole point: the reset must be in the PAST, and the
    last sample must land within WEEKLY_TAIL_GAP_H of it. The synthesis's rule was the gap alone,
    which ADMITS THE CURRENTLY-LIVE WINDOW — a live window's newest sample is always some hours
    before its own reset. Measured at 2026-08-25T09:47:41Z the naive rule reported 51 pp over 9
    account-weeks (admitting next3's live window at 92%); the fixed rule reports 43 pp over 8,
    which is the baseline every weekly figure in USAGE_TELEMETRY_100P §5 rests on.

    The class is `gap-IS-the-mechanism`: a detector for "the window ended" that fires DURING the
    window. n = 8 is the binding limit on every weekly-side figure; it grows ~3.7/week."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    by = {}
    for e in samples or []:
        if e.get("_t") is None or e.get("weekly_pct") is None:
            continue
        key = _reset_key(e.get("weekly_reset_at"))
        if key is None:
            continue                      # no window open is a state, not a window
        by.setdefault((e.get("acct"), key), []).append(e)
    out = []
    for (acct, key), ss in by.items():
        reset_t = key * 60.0
        if reset_t >= now:                # LIVE window — its reset has not happened yet
            continue
        last = max(ss, key=lambda e: e["_t"])
        gap = reset_t - last["_t"]
        if not (0 <= gap <= WEEKLY_TAIL_GAP_H * 3600.0):
            continue                      # the series lost the window before it closed
        out.append({"acct": acct, "reset_t": reset_t, "final_pct": last["weekly_pct"],
                    "strand": max(0.0, 100.0 - last["weekly_pct"])})
    out.sort(key=lambda w: w["reset_t"])
    return out


def apply_burn(rows, cfg, samples=None):
    """Attach measured burn rates from the utilization series:
      burn_5h_ph  — 5h-window fraction/hour, newest adjacent pair ≤1.5h apart (older pairs
                    straddle window rolls) — feeds _su_projected.
      burn_wk_ppd — weekly %/day, widest pair inside 48h spanning ≥6h (short spans read desk
                    noise as pace) — feeds the pace line's 'recent' annotation.
    A negative delta is a window that ROLLED between samples — a reset crossing, not negative
    burn — and reads as unknown. Fail-soft: a thin series leaves the fields absent, which is
    bit-for-bit the pre-M7 behavior."""
    # BURST_LOOKBACK_H, not 48: burst_percentile ranks a demand against the account's OWN history
    # and needs BURST_MIN_WINDOWS=200 of them, which 48 h cannot supply at any grid point above
    # 6 h. Every consumer below still filters to its own span, so widening the read changes no
    # rate. It is also nearly free — measured on the live 3.6 MB series, 48 h reads in 66 ms and
    # 336 h in 73 ms, because the doubling seek amortises and the parse dominates.
    samples = _util_tail(hours=BURST_LOOKBACK_H)[0] if samples is None else samples
    if not samples:
        return
    now = time.time()
    by = {}
    for e in samples:
        by.setdefault(e.get("acct"), []).append(e)
    for r in rows:
        ss = by.get(r.get("acct"))
        if not ss:
            continue
        ss.sort(key=lambda e: e["_t"])
        recent = [e for e in ss
                  if now - e["_t"] <= 2.0 * 3600 and e.get("session_pct") is not None]
        if len(recent) >= 2:
            a, b = recent[-2], recent[-1]
            dh = (b["_t"] - a["_t"]) / 3600.0
            if 0.03 <= dh <= 1.5:
                d = (b["session_pct"] - a["session_pct"]) / 100.0
                if d >= 0:
                    r["burn_5h_ph"] = d / dh
        # S7/M1: the roll-aware form, under its OWN key and in %/h. `burn_5h_ph` stays populated
        # for one release — it is a published field and a silent disappearance is worse than a
        # duplicate — but `_su_projected` prefers this one. The span is stamped beside the value
        # because it is the abstain's REASON, and a null that cannot say why reads as a missing
        # measurement rather than as a refusal.
        v5, span5 = burn_5h_ewma_ph(ss, now)
        r["burn_5h_span_h"] = span5
        if v5 is not None:
            r["burn_5h_ewma_ph"] = v5
        wk = [e for e in ss
              if now - e["_t"] <= 48.0 * 3600 and e.get("weekly_pct") is not None]
        if len(wk) >= 2:
            # ROLL-AWARE ANCHOR, and it lands in the SAME change as _util_tail's time selection
            # by necessity, not by preference. The widest-pair form takes wk[0] as its anchor;
            # while the byte cap held the series to ~12 h that pair rarely straddled a weekly
            # reset, but at a TRUE 48 h span it straddles one for the whole first stretch of
            # every weekly window, `d < 0` leaves the field absent, and the estimator goes blind
            # exactly when the week's plan is set. Restoring the span alone is therefore a
            # REGRESSION (§5.5). Walking the anchor forward to the newest roll shortens the
            # window instead of discarding it: the same estimator, over the post-roll segment.
            i = len(wk) - 1
            while i > 0 and not _rolled(wk[i - 1], wk[i], "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_pct"):
                i -= 1
            a, b = wk[i], wk[-1]
            dh = (b["_t"] - a["_t"]) / 3600.0
            if dh >= 6.0:
                d = b["weekly_pct"] - a["weekly_pct"]
                if d >= 0:
                    r["burn_wk_ppd"] = d / (dh / 24.0)

    # Window-phase view, exported so consumers ROUTE on it instead of re-deriving it from a raw
    # percentage (the governed dispatcher, USAGE_TELEMETRY_100P §4 M1, admits work against this).
    # Absent keys mean "too early to say" — see wall_projection's abstain rule; a consumer that
    # treats a missing burn_ratio as 0 would read a fresh window as idle, which is the exact
    # inversion this metric exists to prevent.
    # M5: rank each account's REQUIRED rate inside its own realised history. Stamped here, beside
    # the other burn facts, because this is the one place that holds both the rows and the series;
    # pace_line renders what is stamped, so there is still exactly one renderer (L3).
    for r in rows:
        bp = burst_percentile(r, samples)
        if bp is not None:
            r["burst"] = bp

    # M2 + M3a: the roll-aware weekly EWMA and the strand nowcast it feeds. The span is stamped
    # beside the value because it is the ABSTAIN's reason, and a null that cannot say why reads
    # as a missing measurement rather than as a refusal.
    for r in rows:
        ss = by.get(r.get("acct"))
        if not ss:
            continue
        v, span = burn_wk_ewma_ph(ss, now, r.get("weekly_reset_h"))
        r["burn_wk_span_h"] = span
        if v is not None:
            r["burn_wk_ewma_ph"] = v
        s = wk_strand_pp(r)
        if s is not None:
            r["wk_strand_pp"] = s
        # The dispatch floor's cross-check on that EWMA (dispatch_floor_row): the flat endpoint
        # pace, and inside WK_EWMA_NEAR_H of the reset the faster of the 24h and 12h spans — a
        # burst a few hours old is diluted to nothing over 24h exactly when it matters most.
        # ABSENT whenever either span abstains, and the floor reads absence as "hold".
        wrh = r.get("weekly_reset_h")
        f24 = burn_wk_flat_ph(ss, now, WK_FLAT_LOOKBACK_H)
        f12 = (burn_wk_flat_ph(ss, now, WK_FLAT_NEAR_LOOKBACK_H)
               if _is_num(wrh) and wrh <= WK_EWMA_NEAR_H else 0.0)
        r.pop("burn_wk_flat_ph", None)
        if f24 is not None and f12 is not None:
            r["burn_wk_flat_ph"] = max(f24, f12)

    # S4/M4': the start-time constraint, and the FIRST consumer of S1c's exchange rate. K is
    # stamped on every row beside it because pace_line renders the coefficient in its header and
    # has no access to the series — this is the one place holding both. When exchange_rate
    # abstains (a fit outside K_SANE) the stamp is absent, `burst_start_by` returns None on every
    # row, and the third question simply goes unanswered; nothing downstream substitutes a
    # literal. That is the L2 shape: a refusal is visible as a missing clause, never as a number.
    k_live, _k_sds, ksrc = exchange_rate(samples)
    for r in rows:
        if k_live is not None:
            r["exchange_k"], r["exchange_k_src"] = k_live, ksrc
        sb = burst_start_by(r, k_live)
        if sb is not None:
            r["burst_start_by_h"] = sb["h"]
            r["burst_start_verdict"] = sb["verdict"]
            r["burst_unrecoverable_pp"] = sb["unrecoverable_pp"]
            r["burst_start"] = sb

    for r in rows:
        ratio, proj = wall_projection(r)
        if ratio is not None:
            r["burn_ratio"] = ratio
            r["proj_end_pct"] = proj
            r["wall_risk"] = proj >= 100.0


def pace_need_ppd(r):
    """%/day the weekly bucket must burn from NOW to reach 100% by its reset — the goal's own
    metric (quota stranded at reset is the one unrecoverable waste). None without data."""
    if r.get("weekly_pct") is None:
        return None
    wrh = r.get("weekly_reset_h")
    if not isinstance(wrh, (int, float)) or wrh <= 0:
        return None
    return max(0.0, 100.0 - r["weekly_pct"]) / (wrh / 24.0)


BURST_LOOKBACK_H = 336.0                     # 14 d — the span apply_burn reads for the ranking
BURST_H_GRID = (1.0, 3.0, 6.0, 12.0, 24.0)   # the horizons a demand can be expressed against
BURST_MIN_WINDOWS = 200                      # below this the account's own history cannot rank it
BURST_MIN_RESET_H = 0.5                      # inside the last half hour the rate is quantization
BURST_SPAN_LO, BURST_SPAN_HI = 0.9, 1.15     # a window counts only if it actually covers ~H hours


def burst_percentile(r, samples):
    """Is this account's required weekly burn rate ROUTINE for it, or a p95 stunt it has never
    pulled off? Returns None (abstain) or
    {"pct": float|None, "h": float, "n": int, "need_pph": float, "never": bool}.

    THE DEFECT IT CURES. The footer renders `needs 88%/d over 2.2h` — a rate quoted in a unit
    ELEVEN TIMES LONGER than the window it has to happen in, which is uninterpretable: nothing
    on the line says whether 88%/day is a Tuesday or a personal record. `pace_need_ppd` keeps
    its field, because it still answers "how hard would I have to push"; what is replaced is its
    rendering as a bare rate.

    Ranked first among the metrics because it is the only one no refutation touched. Its one
    out-of-sample datum has the right polarity: at 09:27Z on 2026-08-25 next3 needed a p95.5
    3 h burn to close 8 pp, did not perform it (92% -> 92% over the following 2.2 h), and its
    window closed short. One live case is not a validation, but it is the correct direction.

    Ranked against THIS ACCOUNT'S OWN history — never the fleet's — at the horizon the demand
    actually lives on: H is the nearest grid point to weekly_reset_h ON A LOG SCALE, because
    the grid is geometric and a linear nearest would send a 2.5 h deadline to the 1 h bucket.

    ABSTAIN (L2): None below BURST_MIN_WINDOWS qualifying windows for that account, and None
    inside BURST_MIN_RESET_H of the reset. When the demand exceeds every window the account has
    ever run, `never` is True and `pct` is None — the p100 is NOT extrapolated, because there is
    no evidence above the maximum and a rendered "p100" reads as a rate that has been achieved."""
    wp, wrh = r.get("weekly_pct"), r.get("weekly_reset_h")
    if wp is None or not isinstance(wrh, (int, float)) or wrh < BURST_MIN_RESET_H:
        return None
    need_pph = max(0.0, 100.0 - wp) / wrh
    h = min(BURST_H_GRID, key=lambda g: abs(math.log(g) - math.log(max(wrh, 1e-9))))
    ss = sorted((e for e in samples or []
                 if e.get("acct") == r.get("acct") and e.get("weekly_pct") is not None
                 and e.get("_t") is not None),
                key=lambda e: e["_t"])
    lo_s, hi_s = BURST_SPAN_LO * h * 3600.0, BURST_SPAN_HI * h * 3600.0
    rates = []
    j = 0
    for i, a in enumerate(ss):
        if j < i + 1:
            j = i + 1
        while j < len(ss) and ss[j]["_t"] - a["_t"] < lo_s:
            j += 1
        if j >= len(ss):
            break
        b = ss[j]
        dt = b["_t"] - a["_t"]
        if dt > hi_s:
            continue
        # INSIDE ONE weekly window, both witnesses: a window that straddles a reset would read
        # the new window's level as the old one's accrual.
        if _rolled(a, b, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_pct"):
            continue
        if _reset_key(a.get("weekly_reset_at")) != _reset_key(b.get("weekly_reset_at")):
            continue
        rates.append((b["weekly_pct"] - a["weekly_pct"]) / (dt / 3600.0))
    n = len(rates)
    if n < BURST_MIN_WINDOWS:
        return None
    if need_pph > max(rates):
        return {"pct": None, "h": h, "n": n, "need_pph": need_pph, "never": True}
    pct = 100.0 * sum(1 for x in rates if x < need_pph) / n
    return {"pct": pct, "h": h, "n": n, "need_pph": need_pph, "never": False}


def fmt_burst(bp):
    """The one rendering of burst_percentile, so the abstain states cannot be spelled two ways.

    THE 99 CAP IS THE SENTINEL'S OTHER HALF, and it is not cosmetic. `burst_percentile` refuses
    to extrapolate a p100 — above the observed maximum it returns the `never` state instead —
    but a demand sitting exactly AT the maximum scores 99.96, and `:.0f` rounds that to the
    literal string `p100`. Live on 2026-08-25 that is what next3 rendered. A reader cannot tell
    that string from the extrapolation the abstain exists to prevent, and it reads as a rate the
    account HAS achieved. The refusal has to hold at the string, not only at the number."""
    if not bp:
        return None
    if bp["never"]:
        return f"needs more than it has EVER burned in {bp['h']:.0f}h"
    return f"p{min(99.0, bp['pct']):.0f} of its own {bp['h']:.0f}h burns"


WEEK_H = 168.0          # the weekly bucket's span; reset stamps are exactly 7 d apart


# ---- M4' — the START-TIME constraint (USAGE_TELEMETRY_100P §5.2 S4) ----------------------------
# Constants from the burst table in §5.1. All three are read as module globals inside
# burst_start_by rather than bound as defaults, so a test can null P_WALL and prove the freeze
# term participates (RP-23) — a purely arithmetic implementation survives every other case.
BURST_SPPH = 22.87       # session pp/h a real burst delivers (mean over the 8 observed bursts)
P_WALL = 0.625           # P(a burst window walls) — a LOWER bound: walls under ~13 min are
                         # invisible to the detector, so the freeze this buys is under-counted
MEAN_WALL_H = 1.653      # mean frozen hours once a window walls
SESSION_WINDOW_H = 5.0   # the 5h grid: window N+1 cannot open before window N resets
START_SOON_H = 12.0      # slack at or below which the verdict escalates from SLACK to START SOON


def burst_start_by(r, k):
    """M4' — how many hours BEFORE the weekly reset the endgame burst must begin, or None.
    Returns {"h", "verdict", "unrecoverable_pp", "windows", "t_needed_h", "freeze_h",
    "need_spp", "deficit_pp"}; `h` <= 0 means ALREADY LATE.

    THE PRIMITIVE IS A START TIME, NOT A CAPACITY VERDICT, and that is the whole content of the
    change. The synthesis's M4 (`wk_reach_pp`) asked "could this account reach 100% if it tried",
    which is nearly algebraically fixed — all 8 observed burst windows delivered 13-20 weekly pp
    whether or not they walled, so the loss is never capacity WITHIN a window, it is the frozen
    tail plus the 5h grid. On the live 2026-08-25 shape M4 read next3 `16.9 pp reach vs 8 needed
    -- REACHABLE, 2.1x margin`; this reads LATE by 0.65 h with 2.83 pp already unrecoverable,
    and next3 in fact stranded. A rate-and-freeze-against-the-clock question can come out either
    way; a capacity question cannot, which is why M4 was deleted rather than shipped.

    THE 5H GRID IS WALKED, NOT DIVIDED. You cannot open the next session window before the
    current one resets, so a demand needing more than one window pays the roll wait — not the
    burn time alone — and the freeze is charged PER WINDOW, because each one can wall.

    ABSTAIN (L2), four arms:
      * `k` is None -- S1c's exchange_rate abstained (outside K_SANE), so session pp cannot be
        converted to weekly pp at all. A fabricated K would make every number below fiction.
      * no window is open -- a null `session_pct` / a null reset stamp is a distinct STATE, and
        collapsing it to `session_pct = 0` would read "no window" as "a fresh empty window",
        which is the most favourable possible reading of the least known one.
      * the deficit is already closed (`weekly_pct >= 100`) -- there is nothing to start.
      * `weekly_reset_h` outside (0, WEEK_H] -- no window to be early or late against.

    ON PROBATION, and the docstring is where that has to live. n = 8 burst windows is the entire
    evidence base for all three constants, and P_WALL is sized on the BURST denominator (5/8),
    never the all-windows one (5/252) -- a burst is exactly the regime that walls. §5.5's
    monitoring plan re-runs the burst-stratified wall rate on every completed weekly window."""
    if not isinstance(k, (int, float)) or k <= 0:
        return None
    wp, wrh = r.get("weekly_pct"), r.get("weekly_reset_h")
    sp, srh = r.get("session_pct"), r.get("session_reset_h")
    if wp is None or not isinstance(wrh, (int, float)) or not (0 < wrh <= WEEK_H):
        return None
    if sp is None or not isinstance(srh, (int, float)) or srh < 0:
        return None
    deficit_wk = max(0.0, 100.0 - wp)
    if deficit_wk <= 0:
        return None
    need_spp = deficit_wk / k
    roll_wait_h = SESSION_WINDOW_H - (100.0 / BURST_SPPH)   # grid pitch minus a full window's burn
    t = 0.0
    rem = need_spp
    windows = 0
    avail = 100.0 - sp                       # the OPEN window's remaining session pp
    if avail > 0:
        burn_h = min(min(avail, rem) / BURST_SPPH, srh)     # the window may die before the burn
        took = burn_h * BURST_SPPH
        rem -= took
        t += burn_h
        if took > 0:
            windows += 1
        if rem > 0:
            t = srh                          # wait out the roll: the clock, not the burn, governs
    while rem > 0:
        take = min(100.0, rem)
        t += take / BURST_SPPH
        rem -= take
        windows += 1
        if rem > 0:
            t += roll_wait_h
    freeze = windows * P_WALL * MEAN_WALL_H
    t_needed = t + freeze
    h = wrh - t_needed
    verdict = "SLACK" if h > START_SOON_H else ("START SOON" if h > 0 else "LATE")
    # The floor, and it is the number that makes LATE actionable rather than fatalistic: the
    # freeze is subtracted from the runway FIRST, and whatever weekly pp a perfect burst could
    # still buy in what remains is deducted from the deficit. What is left cannot be saved by
    # starting this instant, so it is already lost and should not be planned against.
    unrecoverable = max(0.0, deficit_wk - k * BURST_SPPH * max(0.0, wrh - freeze))
    return {"h": h, "verdict": verdict, "unrecoverable_pp": unrecoverable,
            "windows": windows, "t_needed_h": t_needed, "freeze_h": freeze,
            "need_spp": need_spp, "deficit_pp": deficit_wk}


def fmt_start_by(sb):
    """The one rendering of burst_start_by, for the same reason fmt_burst exists: the abstain and
    the alarm states must not be spelled two ways on two surfaces.

    THE LATE ARM CARRIES THE FLOOR AND THE OTHERS DO NOT, deliberately. `unrecoverable_pp` is 0
    everywhere the verdict is not LATE (a burst that still fits buys the whole deficit), so
    rendering it there would be a zero that reads like a measurement."""
    if not sb:
        return None
    if sb["verdict"] == "LATE":
        tail = (f" — {sb['unrecoverable_pp']:.1f}pp already unrecoverable"
                if sb["unrecoverable_pp"] >= 0.05 else "")
        return f"⚠ LATE by {-sb['h']:.1f}h{tail}"
    if sb["verdict"] == "START SOON":
        return f"⚠ start by T−{sb['t_needed_h']:.0f}h — START SOON ({sb['h']:.1f}h slack)"
    return f"start by T−{sb['t_needed_h']:.0f}h ({sb['h']:.0f}h slack)"


# The abstain floor, WIDENED 2026-09-01 from 0.05 (~8.4 h elapsed) to the day-6 mark (~24 h left).
# 0.05 was sized for the ARITHMETIC blowing up near zero elapsed; it said nothing about whether the
# linear divisor above it is TRUE, and measurement says it is not until the window's last day.
# The evidence, and what the widening costs, are in wall_projection's ABSTAIN section.
MIN_ELAPSED_FRAC = 6.0 / 7.0  # 0.857 — speak only where linear and empirical have converged


def wall_projection(r):
    """burn_ratio and projected end-of-window pct, or (None, None) when neither is honest.

    WHY THIS EXISTS (USAGE_TELEMETRY_100P §1). A point-in-time percentage against a
    periodically RESETTING counter is dominated by where you are in the window, not by how you
    are using it. On 2026-08-16 this renderer showed three accounts at 1-3% weekly and every
    `pace to 100%` line reading BEHIND, which reads as gross under-utilisation; the previous
    complete windows had closed at 91/92/100/85%. The readings were three windows that had just
    reset. Every figure was correct and the obvious interpretation was inverted.

      burn_ratio = (weekly_pct/100) / elapsed_fraction    1.0 = exactly on pace to end at 100%
      proj_end   = weekly_pct / elapsed_fraction          where the window lands if nothing changes

    ABSTAIN below MIN_ELAPSED_FRAC. This is the whole point rather than a nicety: at 1 h elapsed
    a 1% reading projects to 168%, so an un-floored projection would page on every fresh window —
    manufacturing the very false alarm the metric exists to remove. Returning None is the honest
    answer to "too early to say", per the repo's abstain-never-impute rule.

    THE FLOOR IS THE LINEAR MODEL'S OWN REFUTATION, not just a guard on small denominators
    (widened 0.05 -> 6/7 on 2026-09-01; weekly-reset-utilization-2026-08-25 §3/§6 +
    drain-telemetry-2026-08-25/axis-D-windows §4a/§5). Dividing by elapsed_fraction removes the
    PHASE error only if burn is LINEAR. Weekly burn is not: over 11,287 adjacent pairs across 12
    window instances (phase decorrelated from calendar, r = +0.030), phase 0.0-0.7 runs at
    10.9 pp/day and phase 0.7-1.0 at 25.2 pp/day — a 2.3x end-of-window acceleration. Backtesting
    THIS function at day 3: 12% -> 28% projected vs 98% actual; 17% -> 40% vs 100%; 25% -> 58% vs
    92%; 51% -> 119% "⚠ WALL" vs 99%. Mean absolute error 46 pp at day 3, 35 pp at day 5,
    18.3 pp at phase 0.9, 2 pp at day 7. A CONSTANT predictor (leave-one-out mean of the other
    windows' finals, ~94) scores 5.3 pp over the same 8 windows — mid-week this projection is
    8.7x worse than a number that ignores its input, i.e. mid-window weekly_pct carries
    essentially no information about where the window lands. So the floor now sits where linear
    and empirical have CONVERGED (day 6: -17 pp; day 7: -2 pp) and the projection is silent
    across the whole mid-week regime where it was measurably uninformative.

    WHY WIDEN RATHER THAN FIT THE CURVE. The shape-corrected divisor was built and backtested
    leave-one-out and LOSES — MAE 54.1 vs linear's 39.5 overall — because between-window shape
    variance dominates the mean shape and a small S amplifies it. Worse, the shape is a fossil of
    the operator's end-of-window rush: if the planner that reads this succeeds, the rush stops,
    the shape flattens toward linear, and an estimator fitted to today's shape fails in the
    direction of complacency. 4 windows refute the linear model; they cannot calibrate a curve.

    WHAT THE WIDENING COSTS, STATED. The mid-week `⚠ WALL` flag. On the one backtested window
    that raised it the flag was a false alarm (119% projected, 99% actual), and under the constant
    predictor EVERY window is a wall trajectory — so mid-week the flag carries no information in
    either direction. `wk_strand_pp` (M3a, a nowcast at the last 48 h of pace) is the surface that
    still speaks mid-week, and it makes no lead-time claim.
    """
    wp, wrh = r.get("weekly_pct"), r.get("weekly_reset_h")
    if wp is None or not isinstance(wrh, (int, float)):
        return None, None
    if not (0 < wrh <= WEEK_H):          # a stamp outside the bucket is bad data, not a signal
        return None, None
    frac = (WEEK_H - wrh) / WEEK_H
    if frac < MIN_ELAPSED_FRAC:
        return None, None
    return (wp / 100.0) / frac, wp / frac


# ---- M1 — the 5h rate, roll-aware and available (USAGE_TELEMETRY_100P §5.2 S7) ----------------
SU_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H = 6.0     # the trailing window: longer than the 5h meter's own, so a roll is
                             # always inside it and the estimator is never blind across one
SU_EWMA_HL_H = 1.0           # half-life; the 5h regime turns over in about an hour
SU_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H = 1.3     # below this a +-1 pp quantization step exceeds 25% of the 5h meter's
                             # realised mean, so the number would be reading its own rounding
SU_EWMA_MAX_DT_H = 1.0       # a longer gap is a sweep OUTAGE, not a measured interval


def burn_5h_ewma_ph(samples_for_acct, now):
    """Roll-aware EWMA of the 5h session meter, in %/h, as (value|None, measured_span_h).

    WHAT IT FIXES, AND IT IS AVAILABILITY, NOT ACCURACY. The incumbent `burn_5h_ph` is the
    newest adjacent pair inside 2 h: one interval, discarded outright when it straddles a window
    roll (`d < 0` reads as unknown). So the rate vanishes exactly at the roll — the moment the
    5h picture changes most — and a single quantization step is the entire measurement. Here a
    roll is not a discard: post-roll accrual is reconstructed as a LOWER BOUND by taking the new
    window's level as the increment, the same treatment `burn_wk_ewma_ph` gives the weekly meter.

    ABSTAIN (L2) on the RAW MEASURED SPAN — the sum of dt over usable pairs, never the requested
    lookback — below SU_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H, and on fewer than 2 usable pairs. NEVER on a roll.

    🚨 NAMED BLIND SPOT, and it must stay in this docstring because the metric is WRONG here and
    fail-safe rather than right: at `session_pct >= 40` the INCUMBENT is more accurate (MAE
    0.0617 against this estimator's 0.0797; the EWMA over-projects by +3.6 pp). That is the
    BURST regime — the one the weekly planner deliberately creates. The over-projection direction
    is soften-only through `_soft` (SF is floored at SF_FLOOR and can never exclude), so a wrong
    number there costs ranking, not availability; it must not be quoted as accurate. §5.6 Q3
    names the measurement that would fix it.

    🚨 ROLL SPELLING is `_reset_key`'s ROUNDING, via `_rolled`, and that is load-bearing rather
    than incidental. Under a truncation the roll branch fires on 46.0% of adjacent pairs and
    injects an absolute LEVEL where a delta belongs: MAE degrades 0.0282 -> 0.2110, i.e. 5.4x
    worse than the incumbent this replaces."""
    ss = sorted((e for e in samples_for_acct or []
                 if e.get("session_pct") is not None and e.get("_t") is not None
                 and now - e["_t"] <= SU_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H * 3600.0),
                key=lambda e: e["_t"])
    num = den = span = 0.0
    pairs = 0
    for a, b in zip(ss, ss[1:]):
        dt_h = (b["_t"] - a["_t"]) / 3600.0
        if not (0 < dt_h <= SU_EWMA_MAX_DT_H):
            continue
        if _rolled(a, b, "session_reset_at", "session_pct"):
            d = b["session_pct"]                      # the new window's level, a LOWER bound
        else:
            d = max(0.0, b["session_pct"] - a["session_pct"])
        w = 2.0 ** (-((now - b["_t"]) / 3600.0) / SU_EWMA_HL_H)
        num += w * d
        den += w * dt_h
        span += dt_h
        pairs += 1
    if pairs < 2 or span < SU_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H or den <= 0:
        return None, span
    return num / den, span


WK_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H = 48.0    # the pace the nowcast speaks for: "at the pace of the last 48 h"
WK_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H = 6.8     # below this, +-1 pp quantization exceeds 25% of the realised mean
WK_EWMA_HL_NEAR = 4.0        # weekly_reset_h <= 12: weight the last hours hard
WK_EWMA_HL_FAR = 8.0         # beyond 12 h the hl=4 form may NEVER be extrapolated
WK_EWMA_NEAR_H = 12.0

# The FLEET pace line (fleet_pace / fleet_pace_line). Every constant below was replayed against the
# whole utilization ledger by the telemetry judge, 2026-10-10 (docs/research/dispatch-strand-rescue-2026-10-09.md).
FLEET_WINDOW_H = 24.0         # the realised pace: endpoint deltas over the last day, roll-aware
FLEET_NEAR_H = 48.0           # resets owed by that pace; 72h fired 5h sooner this week but added two
                              # August episodes that realised only 9pp and 15pp
FLEET_SHORT_FLOOR_PP = 30.0   # healthy 09-09..10-02 peaked at a 20pp shortfall, 0 fired hours
FLEET_ANCHOR_GAP_H = 3.0      # an anchor this much older than now-24h is a ledger hole, not a pace
FLEET_MAX_AGE_H = 1.0         # a newest row older than this: the series is stale, no line at all
FLEET_SUSTAIN_H = 1.0         # the shortfall must hold now AND this long ago (two hourly evaluations)
FLEET_TAIL_H = 72.0           # the read: the day-ago pace needs an anchor at now-48h, plus the gap


def burn_wk_ewma_ph(samples_for_acct, now, weekly_reset_h):
    """Roll-aware EWMA of the weekly meter, in %/h, as (value|None, measured_span_h).

    IT SHIPS ON AVAILABILITY AND ROLL-AWARENESS, NOT ON ACCURACY. Against the incumbent
    `burn_wk_ppd` the two agree closely (live: 43.79 / 7.96 / 29.85 / 3.98 %/day against
    38.0 / 6.9 / 29.3 / 4.6). The win is that the incumbent's widest-pair form DISCARDS every
    window that crosses a reset, so it is blind for the first stretch of every weekly window —
    exactly when the week's plan is set. Here a roll is not a discard: post-roll accrual is
    reconstructed as a LOWER BOUND by taking the new window's level as the increment.

    ABSTAIN (L2) on the RAW MEASURED SPAN — the sum of dt over usable pairs, never the weighted
    span and never the requested lookback. Below WK_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H a +-1 pp quantization step
    exceeds 25% of the weekly meter's realised mean of 0.592 %/h, so the number would be reading
    its own rounding. Never abstain on a roll.

    THE HORIZON LICENCE IS REFUSED: hl is picked from weekly_reset_h, so a consumer projecting
    past WK_EWMA_NEAR_H cannot get the hl=4 form. That form is fitted to the end-of-window
    acceleration and extrapolating it across four days manufactures a wall."""
    if not isinstance(weekly_reset_h, (int, float)):
        return None, 0.0
    hl = WK_EWMA_HL_NEAR if weekly_reset_h <= WK_EWMA_NEAR_H else WK_EWMA_HL_FAR
    ss = sorted((e for e in samples_for_acct or []
                 if e.get("weekly_pct") is not None and e.get("_t") is not None
                 and now - e["_t"] <= WK_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H * 3600.0),
                key=lambda e: e["_t"])
    num = den = span = 0.0
    pairs = 0
    for a, b in zip(ss, ss[1:]):
        dt_h = (b["_t"] - a["_t"]) / 3600.0
        if not (0 < dt_h <= 1.0):
            continue
        if _rolled(a, b, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_pct"):
            d = b["weekly_pct"]                       # the new window's level, a LOWER bound
        else:
            d = max(0.0, b["weekly_pct"] - a["weekly_pct"])
        w = 2.0 ** (-((now - b["_t"]) / 3600.0) / hl)
        num += w * d
        den += w * dt_h
        span += dt_h
        pairs += 1
    if pairs < 2 or span < WK_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H or den <= 0:
        return None, span
    return num / den, span


WK_FLAT_LOOKBACK_H = 24.0        # the flat cross-check's span
WK_FLAT_NEAR_LOOKBACK_H = 12.0   # ...and the second, shorter one inside WK_EWMA_NEAR_H of the reset
WK_FLAT_MAX_AGE_H = 1.0          # = burn_wk_ewma_ph's own pair bound: older evidence is a gap


def _is_num(x):
    return isinstance(x, (int, float)) and not isinstance(x, bool)


def burn_wk_flat_ph(samples_for_acct, now, lookback_h=WK_FLAT_LOOKBACK_H):
    """Flat trailing weekly pace in %/h: an ENDPOINT DIFFERENCE over the last `lookback_h`.

    The cross-check on burn_wk_ewma_ph for a consumer that AUTHORISES something on a low pace (the
    dispatch floor's strand rescue). The EWMA skips every pair more than 1h apart, so burn inside
    a ledger hole is invisible to it — measured 2026-10-09: 36 such holes with the meter rising,
    45pp hidden. An endpoint difference cannot miss it.

    None when the newest sample is older than WK_FLAT_MAX_AGE_H (the series is stale: a quiet
    reading that old is no evidence the account is quiet NOW) or the roll-free span behind it is
    under WK_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H (the same quantization argument as the EWMA's abstain)."""
    ss = sorted((e for e in samples_for_acct or []
                 if e.get("weekly_pct") is not None and e.get("_t") is not None
                 and 0 <= now - e["_t"] <= lookback_h * 3600.0),
                key=lambda e: e["_t"])
    if len(ss) < 2 or now - ss[-1]["_t"] > WK_FLAT_MAX_AGE_H * 3600.0:
        return None
    i = len(ss) - 1
    while i > 0 and not _rolled(ss[i - 1], ss[i], "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_pct"):
        i -= 1
    span = (ss[-1]["_t"] - ss[i]["_t"]) / 3600.0
    if span < WK_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H:
        return None
    return max(0.0, ss[-1]["weekly_pct"] - ss[i]["weekly_pct"]) / span


def wk_strand_pp(r):
    """M3a — the weekly pp that DIE at this window's reset at the pace of the last 48 h.
    None when the nowcast cannot speak.

    THIS IS A NOWCAST AND CARRIES NO LEAD-TIME CLAIM. The synthesis published
    `4/4 recall / 0 FP / median ~20 h lead` for this arithmetic; that was refuted on four
    independent grounds and is withdrawn — most simply, the fire rule was non-empty only at the
    window's LAST sample, where the projection has already converged to `100 - weekly_pct`, i.e.
    to the true strand, so it agreed with the identity function on 8/8 windows. The estimator is
    a good NOWCASTER for exactly the reason it is a bad forecaster. Alarm semantics arrive with
    S6 and only after S5's scoring harness has been run and read.

    THE CLAMP IS LOAD-BEARING, not cosmetic. It discards the OVERSHOOT regime, where every
    projector measured here is badly wrong (on `next` at phase 0.32 the incumbent renders 154.6%
    and this EWMA renders 231.4% against a truth near 100% — the better estimator is worse on
    that figure), and keeps the SHORTFALL regime, where the arithmetic nowcasts a quantity that
    actually converges. A weekly projection above 100 is therefore never rendered as a number;
    the account is reported as being on a wall trajectory instead."""
    wp, wrh = r.get("weekly_pct"), r.get("weekly_reset_h")
    b = r.get("burn_wk_ewma_ph")
    if wp is None or not isinstance(wrh, (int, float)) or not (0 < wrh <= WEEK_H):
        return None
    if not isinstance(b, (int, float)):
        return None
    return max(0.0, 100.0 - (wp + b * wrh))


# ---- M3c — the scoring harness that CAN fail (USAGE_TELEMETRY_100P §5.2 S5) --------------------
STRAND_SCORE_BUCKETS = (96.0, 48.0, 24.0, 12.0, 6.0)   # hours before the reset, coarse -> fine
STRAND_SCORE_LOOKBACK_H = 1008.0                       # 6 weeks: ~4 completed windows per account
STRAND_SCORE_FLOOR = 4.0    # S6's own proposed alarm FLOOR. The agreement column scores the
                            # decision S5 exists to gate — "would the rescue alarm have been
                            # right at this horizon" — not the arithmetic. pace_line's 0.5 pp
                            # render threshold would have been useless here: essentially every
                            # window strands more than half a point, so agreement at 0.5 is ~1.0
                            # by construction and measures nothing.


def strand_score(samples, buckets=STRAND_SCORE_BUCKETS, now=None):
    """Score M3a's nowcast against what each completed weekly window actually stranded, stratified
    by how far from the reset the projection was made. Returns
    {"cells": [...], "buckets": [{"h", "n", "possible", "bias", "mae", "agree", "agree_n"}...],
     "windows": int, "accts": int}.

    WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY THE THING IT REPLACES WAS VACUOUS. The synthesis scored the alarm on
    8 binary cells evaluated at the horizon's CLOSE — the instant at which `100 - (weekly_pct +
    burn * weekly_reset_h)` has already converged to `100 - weekly_pct`, i.e. to the realised
    strand itself. A projector cannot lose against the identity function at the moment it becomes
    the identity function, and it duly scored 8/8. This evaluates the same estimator at FIXED
    DISTANCES from the reset — ~45 (window, horizon) cells — where it can be, and will be, wrong.
    That is the whole point: an instrument that cannot report a failure has not been validated,
    it has been asserted. `control-must-replay-the-real-artifact.md`,
    `verification-harness-vacuous-pass-traps.md`.

    IT REPLAYS THE REAL ARTIFACT, which is the trap the same research names. Two properties do
    that work, and dropping either turns this back into a tautology:

      * CAUSAL. The EWMA at a cell is computed over `x._t <= e._t` only. A harness that hands
        `burn_wk_ewma_ph` the whole series lets a sample from AFTER the projection instant into
        the average, and the score then measures hindsight.
      * THE ACCOUNT'S WHOLE SERIES, not the window's slice. `apply_burn` passes every sample the
        account has and lets the estimator's own roll-awareness handle the reset crossing. A
        harness that pre-filters to one weekly window is scoring a DIFFERENT function — a
        strictly better-conditioned one — at exactly the early-window horizons (96h, 48h) where
        the live instrument is thinnest.

    ABSTAIN, and the abstain is the honest half of the number. A (window, bucket) cell with no
    evaluable sample at `weekly_reset_h >= H`, or one where the EWMA itself abstains (span below
    WK_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H), is reported with `err = None` and EXCLUDED from its bucket's aggregate.
    It is never imputed, and never counted as a hit — imputing a miss as zero error is how a
    harness reports a bias of 0.0 in every cell and calls it agreement.

    `agree` scores THE DECISION S5 EXISTS TO GATE, not the arithmetic: whether the projection and
    the realised outcome fall on the same side of STRAND_SCORE_FLOOR — S6's own proposed 4 pp
    alarm floor. So the column reads "would the rescue alarm have been right at this horizon",
    which is the question S6 is blocked on. A bias of +3 pp on a window that stranded 40 is a
    different kind of wrong from one that flips the alarm."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    wins = completed_weekly_windows(samples, now=now)
    by_win, by_acct = {}, {}
    for e in samples or []:
        if e.get("_t") is None or e.get("weekly_pct") is None:
            continue
        by_acct.setdefault(e.get("acct"), []).append(e)
        key = _reset_key(e.get("weekly_reset_at"))
        if key is not None:
            by_win.setdefault((e.get("acct"), key), []).append(e)
    for ss in by_acct.values():
        ss.sort(key=lambda e: e["_t"])
    cells = []
    for w in wins:
        key = round(w["reset_t"] / 60.0)
        ss = sorted(by_win.get((w["acct"], key), []), key=lambda e: e["_t"])
        acct_ss = by_acct.get(w["acct"], [])
        for h in buckets:
            cell = {"acct": w["acct"], "reset_t": w["reset_t"], "h": h, "at_t": None,
                    "at_h": None, "realised": w["strand"], "projected": None, "err": None,
                    "why": None}
            usable = [e for e in ss if (w["reset_t"] - e["_t"]) / 3600.0 >= h]
            if not usable:
                cell["why"] = "no sample at that horizon"
                cells.append(cell)
                continue
            e = usable[-1]
            wrh = (w["reset_t"] - e["_t"]) / 3600.0
            cell["at_t"], cell["at_h"] = e["_t"], wrh
            v, span = burn_wk_ewma_ph([x for x in acct_ss if x["_t"] <= e["_t"]], e["_t"], wrh)
            if v is None:
                cell["why"] = f"ewma abstained (span {span:.1f}h)"
                cells.append(cell)
                continue
            p = wk_strand_pp({"weekly_pct": e["weekly_pct"], "weekly_reset_h": wrh,
                              "burn_wk_ewma_ph": v})
            if p is None:
                cell["why"] = "strand abstained"
                cells.append(cell)
                continue
            cell["projected"] = p
            cell["err"] = p - w["strand"]
            cells.append(cell)
    out = []
    for h in buckets:
        got = [c for c in cells if c["h"] == h and c["err"] is not None]
        poss = sum(1 for c in cells if c["h"] == h)
        if not got:
            out.append({"h": h, "n": 0, "possible": poss, "bias": None, "mae": None,
                        "agree": None, "agree_n": 0})
            continue
        agree = sum(1 for c in got
                    if (c["projected"] >= STRAND_SCORE_FLOOR) == (c["realised"] >= STRAND_SCORE_FLOOR))
        out.append({"h": h, "n": len(got), "possible": poss,
                    "bias": sum(c["err"] for c in got) / len(got),
                    "mae": sum(abs(c["err"]) for c in got) / len(got),
                    "agree": agree / len(got), "agree_n": agree})
    return {"cells": cells, "buckets": out, "windows": len(wins),
            "accts": len({w["acct"] for w in wins})}


def render_strand_score(sc, hours):
    """The one rendering of strand_score. Lines, never printed here — main() owns stdout.

    THE EXCLUSION IS PRINTED AS A RATIO (`n/possible`), not hidden behind a mean. §5.4's
    acceptance is explicitly not "it is accurate": it is that the table carries non-zero n at the
    24h, 12h and 6h buckets and reports a bias that COULD have been non-zero. A reader who cannot
    see how many cells were dropped cannot tell an accurate instrument from an empty one."""
    if not sc["windows"]:
        return [f"strand nowcast — no weekly window CLOSED and was observed to its close inside "
                f"the last {hours:.0f}h. Nothing to score.",
                "  (a window counts only once its reset is in the past AND a sample lands within "
                f"{WEEKLY_TAIL_GAP_H:.0f}h of it — completed_weekly_windows, S1d)"]
    lines = [f"strand nowcast — horizon-stratified error over {sc['windows']} completed weekly "
             f"window(s), {sc['accts']} account(s)",
             "  signed error = projected − realised, in weekly pp. A cell with no evaluable "
             "sample is EXCLUDED, never imputed.",
             "",
             "  horizon   cells      bias       MAE   alarm-agree"]
    for b in sc["buckets"]:
        if not b["n"]:
            lines.append(f"  {b['h']:6.0f}h   0/{b['possible']:<3d}         —         —   "
                         "(no evaluable cell)")
            continue
        lines.append(f"  {b['h']:6.0f}h   {b['n']}/{b['possible']:<3d}   "
                     f"{b['bias']:+7.2f}   {b['mae']:7.2f}   "
                     f"{b['agree_n']}/{b['n']} ({100.0 * b['agree']:.0f}%)")
    return lines


# ---- --stranded — "how much weekly limit did we leave on the table?" ------------------------------
STRANDED_ACCTS = ("next", "next2", "next3", "next4")


def _stranded_acct(tok):
    """next|next2|next3|next4, with 1-4 as aliases. None for anything else (the caller exits 64)."""
    if tok in STRANDED_ACCTS:
        return tok
    if tok in ("1", "2", "3", "4"):
        return STRANDED_ACCTS[int(tok) - 1]
    return None


def stranded_windows(samples, now=None):
    """completed_weekly_windows, newest first, plus `full_at`: the first sample in that window at
    weekly_pct >= 100 (None if it never got there). NOT a second window detector — the window set
    is completed_weekly_windows' verbatim, so a live window is excluded by its rule, not ours.

    Operator question 2026-10-11, "how much weekly limit did we leave on the table for account
    4?", took 8 tool calls because the answer existed only as strand_score's `realised`, a
    by-product of scoring forecasts. "0 pp, reached 100% 37 min before reset" is the answer."""
    wins = completed_weekly_windows(samples, now=now)
    by_win = {}
    for e in samples or []:
        if e.get("_t") is None or e.get("weekly_pct") is None:
            continue
        key = _reset_key(e.get("weekly_reset_at"))
        if key is not None:
            by_win.setdefault((e.get("acct"), key), []).append(e)
    out = []
    for w in wins:
        ss = by_win.get((w["acct"], round(w["reset_t"] / 60.0)), [])
        full = min((e["_t"] for e in ss if e["weekly_pct"] >= 100.0), default=None)
        out.append(dict(w, reset_at=_iso_z(w["reset_t"]), full_at_t=full,
                        full_at=_iso_z(full) if full is not None else None))
    out.sort(key=lambda w: w["reset_t"], reverse=True)
    return out


def _iso_z(t):
    return datetime.fromtimestamp(t, timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def render_stranded(wins, hours, acct=None):
    """One row per closed window, newest first, grouped by account, then a per-account total of
    the pp stranded over the windows shown. Lines, never printed here — main() owns stdout."""
    who = acct or "any account"
    if not wins:
        return [f"weekly limit left on the table — no weekly window of {who} CLOSED and was "
                f"observed to its close inside the last {hours:.0f}h.",
                "  (a window counts only once its reset is in the past AND a sample lands within "
                f"{WEEKLY_TAIL_GAP_H:.0f}h of it — completed_weekly_windows)"]
    lines = [f"weekly limit left on the table — {len(wins)} closed window(s) of {who}, last "
             f"{hours:.0f}h, newest first",
             "",
             f"  {'acct':<6} {'reset (local)':<22} {'reset (UTC)':<18} {'final':>5} {'left':>6}"
             "   reached 100%"]
    for a in sorted({w["acct"] for w in wins}, key=str):
        mine = [w for w in wins if w["acct"] == a]
        for w in mine:
            loc = datetime.fromtimestamp(w["reset_t"]).astimezone().strftime("%a %b %d %H:%M %Z")
            utc = datetime.fromtimestamp(w["reset_t"], timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%MZ")
            if w["full_at_t"] is None:
                full = "never"
            else:
                fu = datetime.fromtimestamp(w["full_at_t"], timezone.utc).strftime("%m-%d %H:%MZ")
                full = f"{fu} ({fmt_h((w['reset_t'] - w['full_at_t']) / 3600.0)} before reset)"
            lines.append(f"  {a:<6} {loc:<22} {utc:<18} {w['final_pct']:4.0f}% "
                         f"{w['strand']:4.0f}pp   {full}")
        tot = sum(w["strand"] for w in mine)
        lines.append(f"  {a} total: {tot:.0f}pp stranded over {len(mine)} window(s)")
        lines.append("")
    return lines[:-1]


def soonest_reset_line(rows, cfg):
    """The board's ONE drain line: the usable account whose weekly window resets first — the
    quota that expires soonest — with what is left, the burn needed to use it all, the 48h-pace
    verdict when the nowcast speaks, and WHY routing skips it when it does.

    It replaced a per-account strand column (2026-09-27) that was loudest on freshly reset
    accounts and blank on this one. The routing reason is here because the operator's question
    at startup was "why did this session not open on the account about to reset?", and the
    answer (`kmax-concurrency`) was on no surface they read. None when no account qualifies."""
    # ONE countdown per row, re-derived from the stamp (`_until`), for the choice, the printed
    # time and the rate alike — so this line agrees with the grid's `resets` column above it.
    def _h(x):
        return _until(x, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h")
    cands = [r for r in rows
             if r.get("weekly_pct") is not None
             and (_h(r) or 0) > 0
             and r.get("auth") not in ("login-required", "logged-out", "keychain-error")
             and not r.get("auth_broken")]
    if not cands:
        return None
    r = min(cands, key=_h)
    h = _h(r)
    left = max(0.0, 100.0 - r["weekly_pct"])
    parts = [f"next reset: {r['acct']} in {fmt_board_t(h)}, {left:.0f}% of the week left"]
    if left > 0:
        parts.append(f"needs {left / h:.1f}pp/h to use it")
    s = wk_strand_pp(r)
    if s is not None and left > 0:     # at 0% left there is no pace to report
        parts.append("on pace to use it all" if s < 0.5 else f"~{s:.0f}pp dies at 48h pace")
    _, why = score_general(r, cfg)
    if why == "kmax-concurrency":
        parts.append(f"not routed: {k_eff(r)} working ≥ cap {k_cap(r, cfg['router'])}")
    elif why == "weekly-exhausted":
        # Plain words. Whether the server CONFIRMED it is the per-account sentence's job, which
        # sits just above this line on the board; repeating it here only lengthened the board.
        parts.append("not used for new work: out of weekly quota")
    elif why:
        parts.append(f"not routed: {why}")
    else:
        # Routable, so the remaining question is the dispatch floor's: is this account held back
        # from NEW fires, and why — or is it first in line. Recomputed here, never read off a row
        # stamp: the stamp is whatever lane ranked last, and the readout ranks three.
        note = dispatch_floor_note(r, rows, cfg)
        if note:
            parts.append(note)
    return " · ".join(parts)


def _fleet_series(samples, accts=None):
    """{acct: [usable samples, oldest first]} — the fleet fleet_pace speaks for. With `accts`, an
    account with no usable sample is still a member (an empty list), so the fleet abstains on it
    rather than shrinking to the accounts that happen to be measured."""
    by = {a: [] for a in accts} if accts else {}
    for e in samples or []:
        a = e.get("acct")
        if accts and a not in by:
            continue
        if e.get("stale") or not _is_num(e.get("_t")) or not _is_num(e.get("weekly_pct")):
            continue
        by.setdefault(a, []).append(e)
    for ss in by.values():
        ss.sort(key=lambda e: e["_t"])
    return by


def _fleet_burn(by, now):
    """Realised fleet pace in pp/h at `now`, or None. Per account: the roll-aware sum of weekly
    deltas from the ANCHOR (its newest row at or before now - FLEET_WINDOW_H) to its newest row;
    the fleet's pace is the sum of those over the mean anchor age. A pair across a weekly reset
    adds the new window's level, never a negative delta. None when any account has no anchor, an
    anchor more than FLEET_ANCHOR_GAP_H older than the window start, or a newest row older than
    FLEET_MAX_AGE_H — a fleet sum missing one member is not a fleet pace."""
    if not by:
        return None
    start = now - FLEET_WINDOW_H * 3600.0
    tot, hrs = 0.0, []
    for ss in by.values():
        past = [e for e in ss if e["_t"] <= now]
        if not past or now - past[-1]["_t"] > FLEET_MAX_AGE_H * 3600.0:
            return None
        i0 = None
        for i, e in enumerate(past):
            if e["_t"] > start:
                break
            i0 = i
        if i0 is None or start - past[i0]["_t"] > FLEET_ANCHOR_GAP_H * 3600.0:
            return None
        for a, b in zip(past[i0:], past[i0 + 1:]):
            if _rolled(a, b, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_pct"):
                tot += b["weekly_pct"]
            else:
                tot += max(0.0, b["weekly_pct"] - a["weekly_pct"])
        hrs.append((now - past[i0]["_t"]) / 3600.0)
    mean_h = sum(hrs) / len(hrs)
    return tot / mean_h if mean_h > 0 else None


def fleet_pace(samples, now, accts=None):
    """The fleet's realised weekly burn against what its near resets need — pure, no I/O.

    Returns None when the pace abstains (see _fleet_burn), else {pace, needed, short_pp, by, by_h,
    near, left}. `near` is every account whose weekly window resets inside (0, FLEET_NEAR_H] hours,
    in reset order. Walking it, cum_j is the quota left on the first j of them; `needed` is the
    largest cum_j / h_j (the pace that would use it all) and `short_pp` the largest
    cum_j - pace x h_j (the pp that pace leaves unused by reset j), named by `by` / `by_h`. With
    nothing near, short_pp is 0.0 and `by` None.

    Why a SUM and not per account: the per-account nowcast (wk_strand_pp) answers each account
    against its own burn, so three accounts each needing a p93-p98 burn at once read as three
    separate facts; the fleet has one throughput, and the shortfall is against that."""
    by = _fleet_series(samples, accts)
    pace = _fleet_burn(by, now)
    if pace is None:
        return None
    near = []
    for a, ss in by.items():
        e = [x for x in ss if x["_t"] <= now][-1]       # exists: _fleet_burn checked it
        key = _reset_key(e.get("weekly_reset_at"))
        if key is None:
            continue
        wrh = (key * 60.0 - now) / 3600.0
        if 0 < wrh <= FLEET_NEAR_H:
            near.append((wrh, a, max(0.0, 100.0 - e["weekly_pct"])))
    near.sort()
    cum = needed = 0.0
    short, by_acct, by_h = 0.0, None, None
    for wrh, a, left in near:
        cum += left
        needed = max(needed, cum / wrh)
        v = cum - pace * wrh
        if by_acct is None or v > short:
            short, by_acct, by_h = v, a, wrh
    return {"pace": pace, "needed": needed, "short_pp": short, "by": by_acct, "by_h": by_h,
            "near": [a for _h, a, _l in near], "left": cum}


def fleet_pace_line(rows=None, samples=None, now=None, fmt=None):
    """The readout's one fleet-pace conclusion, or None. Fires only when the realised fleet pace
    leaves at least FLEET_SHORT_FLOOR_PP unused by some reset inside FLEET_NEAR_H, both now and
    FLEET_SUSTAIN_H ago; silent in a healthy week and whenever an input is stale.

    Replay (telemetry judge, 2026-10-10, docs/research/dispatch-strand-rescue-2026-10-09.md): first fires 2026-10-09T10:00Z and
    holds through 10-10T01:56Z; in the prior three weeks one episode, 10-03T05Z..10-04T07Z, after
    which next4 stranded 26pp; healthy 09-09..10-02 0 fired hours. Ten episodes in 59 days, each
    followed by a real strand.

    The pp figure is `~`, never a bound: at 10-03T18Z it read 58pp and 26pp died, so a `>=` would
    have been false. The parenthesis is the pace a day earlier, which is what states the FALL;
    a "since <time>" would report when an account crossed the 48h horizon, not when pace dropped.

    Readout renderers only: never apply_burn, never the --rank/--route path (that path runs under
    handoff-fire's 25 s alarm and this reads the ledger). Never in the status line either
    (operator ruling: no account quota there in any form). CC_ACCOUNTS_FLEET_PACE=off silences it.

    `fmt` is the chat grid's countdown formatter (2026-10-18). None keeps `fmt_h` on the series'
    own `by_h`, byte for byte, for the board and the plain table. Given one, the countdown is
    re-derived from the named account's own reset stamp and formatted the way the grid cell beside
    it is: the series' figure is as old as its last sample, so `7.4h` would sit under `7h 24m`."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ACCOUNTS_FLEET_PACE"):
        return None
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    if samples is None:
        samples = _util_tail(hours=FLEET_TAIL_H)[0]
    accts = [r["acct"] for r in rows or [] if r.get("acct")] or None
    cur = fleet_pace(samples, now, accts)
    prev = fleet_pace(samples, now - FLEET_SUSTAIN_H * 3600.0, accts)
    if cur is None or prev is None or cur["by"] is None:
        return None
    if cur["short_pp"] < FLEET_SHORT_FLOOR_PP or prev["short_pp"] < FLEET_SHORT_FLOOR_PP:
        return None
    day = _fleet_burn(_fleet_series(samples, accts), now - FLEET_WINDOW_H * 3600.0)
    ago = f" ({day:.1f} a day ago)" if day is not None else ""
    by_h = cur["by_h"]
    if fmt is None:
        fmt = fmt_h
    else:
        by_row = next((r for r in rows or [] if r.get("acct") == cur["by"]), None)
        h = _until(by_row, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h") if by_row else None
        by_h = h if h is not None else by_h
    return (f"⚠ {cur['pace']:.1f} pp/h fleet burn{ago} vs {cur['needed']:.1f} needed — "
            f"~{cur['short_pp']:.0f}pp dies by {cur['by']}'s reset in {fmt(by_h)}")


PACE_HEAD = "weekly drain — pp that DIE at reset (nowcast at the last 48h of pace):"
PACE_HEAD_K = ("weekly drain — pp that DIE at reset "
               "(K={k:.3f} {src} · nowcast at the last 48h of pace):")


def pace_line(rows, left=None):
    """The weekly-drain block: one row per account, SORTED BY pp AT RISK, descending.
    Returns a newline-joined string (the header plus one row per account), or "".

    `left` formats each row's `· N left` countdown. None keeps `fmt_h(weekly_reset_h,
    narrow=True)` for the board and the plain table; the chat readout passes the grid's own
    formatter, so an alarm row printed under the grid says `1d 22h` beside a `1d 22h` cell, not
    `1d` off the sweep's countdown.

    WHAT IT ANSWERS, AND WHY IT WAS RE-SORTED. The old line was sorted by SOONEST RESET, so on
    2026-08-25 it led with next3 — 5 pp at risk — and trailed the pair holding 119.5 pp with four
    days of runway. Reset proximity is urgency; pp at risk is the loss. Sorting on the first
    buried the second, and the fleet stranded 43 pp across 8 completed account-weeks with no
    surface naming which account was losing it. That inversion is this block's whole gain.

    Each row, left to right: how much dies (M3a `wk_strand_pp`), and whether the demand is
    routine for this account or a stunt it has never pulled (M5 `burst_percentile`).

    NOWCAST, NOT FORECAST — the caption says `at the last 48h of pace` and there is deliberately
    no lead-time claim anywhere on this surface. §5.1 LB-2.

    ZERO-STRAND ROWS STILL RENDER, and render last. `wall_projection`'s burn RATIO survives
    unchanged on them, because an account on a wall trajectory has no strand and is still the
    most important thing on the line; what does NOT survive is rendering a weekly projection
    ABOVE 100 as a number, which is the regime where every projector measured here is wrong by
    50-130 pp. An abstention renders as the word plus its reason, never as a zero.

    ...AND FROM 2026-09-01 THE RATIO ITSELF IS SILENT UNTIL THE WINDOW'S LAST DAY. `wall_projection`
    now abstains below 6/7 elapsed (its ABSTAIN section carries the measurement), so a mid-week
    zero-strand row falls to the bare `on pace to fill the window` arm — which is the STRAND
    nowcast speaking, a different instrument, not the projection. The ratio and the `⚠ WALL` flag
    return in the last ~24 h, where linear and empirical have converged. Mid-week they were wrong
    by a mean 46 pp, so what this row loses is a number that was misinforming it."""
    def _strand(x):
        # Computed here, not read off the stamp, for the same reason wall_projection is called
        # rather than read off `burn_ratio`: the renderer must not silently render nothing when
        # apply_burn has not run. The stamp exists for OTHER consumers, not for this one.
        return wk_strand_pp(x)

    def _key(x):
        s = _strand(x)
        return -(s if s is not None else -1.0)      # positive desc, then 0, then unknown LAST

    parts = []
    for r in sorted((x for x in rows if pace_need_ppd(x) is not None), key=_key):
        left_s = fmt_h(r["weekly_reset_h"], narrow=True) if left is None else left(r)
        deficit = max(0.0, 100.0 - r["weekly_pct"])
        ratio, proj = wall_projection(r)
        s = _strand(r)
        if s is None:
            span = r.get("burn_wk_span_h")
            why = (f"span {span:.1f}h < {WK_EWMA_MIN_SPAN_H}h"
                   if isinstance(span, (int, float)) and span > 0 else "no usable pairs")
            p = f"{r['acct']} strand unknown ({why})"
        elif s < 0.5:
            if ratio is not None and proj >= 100.0:
                # The ⚠ WALL flag survives (§5.2) — an account that reaches 100% is DOWN until
                # reset (next3 sat at exactly 100% for 11.2 h on 2026-08-11), and dropping a
                # warning glyph off a warning is a strict loss. What does NOT survive is
                # rendering the >100 PROJECTION as a number. 100% is the WALL, not the target.
                p = f"{r['acct']} no strand — {ratio:.2f}× burn, ⚠ WALL trajectory"
            elif ratio is not None:
                p = f"{r['acct']} no strand — {ratio:.2f}× burn, on pace to fill the window"
            else:
                p = f"{r['acct']} no strand — on pace to fill the window"
        else:
            p = f"{r['acct']} strand ~{s:.0f}pp of {deficit:.0f}"
        b = fmt_burst(r.get("burst"))
        if b and s is not None and s >= 0.5:
            p += f" · {b}"
        # S4/M4' — the third question, and it rides the SAME rows as the other two. A start-time
        # verdict on an account with no strand would be answering "when must you start saving it"
        # about pp that are not dying; the LATE arm in particular is a rescue call, not a status.
        sby = fmt_start_by(r.get("burst_start")) if s is not None and s >= 0.5 else None
        if sby:
            p += f" · {sby}"
        parts.append(f"  {p} · {left_s} left")
    if not parts:
        return ""
    # The K clause enters HERE, with its first consumer, and not before. S3 shipped this header
    # without it on purpose: M3a is pure weekly-space arithmetic and consumes no K at all, so
    # rendering the coefficient beside a number that does not use it would have been a fabricated
    # dependency. Now that burst_start_by converts session pp to weekly pp, the reader needs the
    # rate that conversion used — and needs to see WHICH rate, because `frozen` is the literal
    # and `live` is a trailing fit that has drifted above the shipped band's ceiling before.
    head = PACE_HEAD
    for r in rows:
        k, src = r.get("exchange_k"), r.get("exchange_k_src")
        if isinstance(k, (int, float)) and src:
            head = PACE_HEAD_K.format(k=k, src=src)
            break
    return "\n".join([head] + parts)


# THROTTLE ADMISSION (backlog 1c20dc1e92db). A 429 from the usage endpoint is a poll throttle, never a
# cap, yet `_excluded` returned the row's error first and dropped an account holding a minutes-old
# good read. Measured over 1,643 throttle events (docs/research/quota-429-drift-2026-09-30/): the
# quota moved p90 2pp between the last good read and the next one when that read was ≤ the 90 s
# cache TTL old — under the 5pp rule — so such a row routes on its inherited percents. Every other
# exclusion still applies on them, and a REJECTED wire verdict from that read still vetoes.
# Default = the measured bound; 0 disables admission (the pre-change behaviour).
THROTTLE_ADMIT_S_DEFAULT = 90.0
_THROTTLE_ADMIT_LOGGED = set()


def throttle_admit_s():
    try:
        v = float(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_THROTTLE_ADMIT_S", THROTTLE_ADMIT_S_DEFAULT))
    except ValueError:
        return THROTTLE_ADMIT_S_DEFAULT
    return v if v >= 0 and math.isfinite(v) else THROTTLE_ADMIT_S_DEFAULT


def throttle_admissible(r):
    """Pure predicate: may this poll-throttled row route on its inherited last-good percents?
    Renderers call this one (no side effect); the router calls _throttle_admitted, which logs."""
    if not r.get("poll_throttled") or not r.get("stale_quota"):
        return False
    bound = throttle_admit_s()
    if wire_admissible(r, bound):
        return True
    if r.get("session_pct") is None or r.get("weekly_pct") is None:
        return False
    if r.get("lastgood_wire_rejects"):
        return False
    age = lastgood_age_s(r)
    return age is not None and 0 < bound and age <= bound


def wire_admissible(r, bound=None):
    """The second door: a FRESH wire substitute (apply_wire_substitute) read inside the same bound,
    rejecting neither window, carrying both utilizations. It routes on the server's own figures
    (weekly_used_frac and the 5h check read the wire first), so the age of the inherited reading
    no longer matters — which is the whole point when that reading predates a reset. Every
    missing piece refuses: no wire, an undated or old one, a missing figure, a rejection."""
    bound = throttle_admit_s() if bound is None else bound
    w = r.get("wire") or {}
    if not r.get("wire_substitute") or not 0 < bound:
        return False
    if w.get("5h_util") is None or w.get("7d_util") is None:
        return False
    if w.get("5h_status") in (None, "rejected") or w.get("7d_status") in (None, "rejected"):
        return False
    if r.get("lastgood_wire_rejects") or r.get("session_pct") is None or r.get("weekly_pct") is None:
        return False
    h = hrs_until(r.get("wire_at"))
    return h is not None and -bound <= h * 3600.0 <= K_SKEW_TOL_S


def _throttle_admitted(r):
    """throttle_admissible, plus one countable log line per account per invocation."""
    if not throttle_admissible(r):
        return False
    bound, age = throttle_admit_s(), lastgood_age_s(r)
    acct = r.get("acct")
    if acct not in _THROTTLE_ADMIT_LOGGED:     # one line per account per invocation, countable
        _THROTTLE_ADMIT_LOGGED.add(acct)
        if wire_admissible(r, bound):
            w = r["wire"]
            log_event(f"route {acct}: wire-admitted — 429 poll-throttled, routing on a fresh wire "
                      f"read (5h {w.get('5h_util')} {w.get('5h_status')} · 7d {w.get('7d_util')} "
                      f"{w.get('7d_status')})")
        else:
            log_event(f"route {acct}: throttle-admitted — 429 poll-throttled, routing on last-good "
                      f"read {age:.0f}s old (≤ {bound:.0f}s)")
    return True


def _excluded(r, R, cliff=True, k_of=None, recovery=False):
    """`k_of` selects the concurrency charge (default k_eff). The desk lane passes k_eff_desk so its
    own launcher phantom cannot lock it out of the account the operator is already in (W1.5); every
    other caller keeps k_eff, so eligibility for general/fable is unchanged. Eligibility otherwise
    stays SHARED across lanes — a different objective over the same eligibility (C6), never a
    second opinion about who is eligible.

    `recovery` adds the SURVIVAL floors (recovery_floors / U13). It defaults False, so every
    existing caller is byte-identical; it is a MODIFIER on the lanes, not a lane."""
    if "error" in r and not _throttle_admitted(r):
        return r["error"]
    if r.get("session_pct") is None:
        return "no-session-data"
    # THE SERVER'S OWN VERDICT OUTRANKS BOTH METERS, and it is the only thing here that can say
    # "refused" as a FACT rather than as a threshold we chose. It can only ever ADD a refusal the
    # endpoint integer could not see (`wire_rejects` is False whenever the wire is absent), so no
    # account becomes routable through this branch and a missing probe degrades to today exactly.
    if wire_rejects(r, "7d"):
        return "weekly-exhausted"
    # THE EPS_H GRACE IS A RECOVERY-LANE ALLOWANCE, NOT A DISPATCH ONE (FLEET_V2 W6 D1.7). A session
    # parked until its source rolls is served by an account that rolls in minutes; a FRESH fire is
    # not — it starts at once, and the server refuses it until the reset actually happens. Measured
    # 2026-09-30 06:16-06:19Z: next2 wire-rejected with its reset 0.186-0.231 h away, the grace kept
    # it routable as the ONLY candidate, and all three new workers hit the limit within 3 s. So a
    # non-recovery lane refuses a wire-rejected 5h window for as long as its reset is still ahead,
    # read off the ABSOLUTE stamp where the row carries one (a cached countdown is as-of the sweep).
    if wire_rejects(r, "5h"):
        sr_now = hrs_until(r.get("session_reset_at"))
        if sr_now is None:
            sr_now = r.get("session_reset_h")
        if sr_now is None or sr_now >= R["EPS_H"] or (not recovery and sr_now > 0):
            return "5h-cutoff"
    # The 5h utilisation, unclamped where the wire carries it: the endpoint reports `percent: 100`
    # for a window measured at 1.02, and a ratio that cannot exceed 1 cannot express overage.
    wu5 = ((r.get("wire") or {}).get("5h_util"))
    su = wu5 if wu5 is not None else r["session_pct"] / 100.0
    sr = r.get("session_reset_h")
    if su >= R["S_CUT"] and (sr is None or sr >= R["EPS_H"]):
        return "5h-cutoff"
    if recovery:
        w_floor, s_ceil, _f_floor = recovery_floors(R)
        # These are SURVIVAL facts, so they sit HERE (eligibility) and not in the score: the
        # strand objective is correct among accounts that can host the session.
        # PROJECTED 5h, the same input the desk lane and _soft already use — a recovered session
        # resumes burning at once, so what matters is where the window is HEADING, not where it
        # is. Placed AFTER 5h-cutoff so a genuinely capped account still reads as capped.
        if _su_projected(r, R) >= s_ceil:
            return "recovery-5h-thin"
        wp = r.get("weekly_pct")
        if wp is None:
            # Unknown headroom is NOT headroom. DATA, not policy (DATA_UNAVAILABLE), so the
            # caller may degrade to a proxy rather than reading a measurement gap as a refusal.
            return "no-weekly-data"
        # The credits target (0.98) is deliberately NOT applied: this is a survival floor, and
        # 2pp of reserve is noise against a 10pp floor while the extra branch is not.
        if max(0.0, 1.0 - wp / 100.0) < w_floor:
            return "recovery-weekly-thin"
    # NEITHER instrument could measure this row's concurrency (ps failed AND the transcript walk
    # was unusable) AND no census was inheritable inside the grace. Refuse — but as DATA, not
    # policy, so it exits 3 and callers may degrade to a proxy rather than reading a measurement
    # failure as "the fleet is at capacity". The old all-zero `ps` return made this case admit
    # instead, which is the fail-open direction.
    #
    # A starved `ps` is fleet-wide by construction (one census for all four accounts), so this
    # reason used to empty the candidate set outright — the largest reason inside the 23 desk
    # abstentions on record (68 of their 92 exclusion records), and the launcher's answer to an
    # abstention is the PINNED account. inherit_k now supplies the
    # last sweep's count for up to `cache_grace_s`, which is what keeps that starvation from
    # excluding the whole fleet; past the grace this line fires exactly as before.
    if k_src(r) == "unmeasured":
        return "concurrency-unmeasured"
    if (k_of or k_eff)(r) >= k_cap(r, R):
        return "kmax-concurrency"
    # LAST of the exclusions, deliberately: a row with no live quota must still report its DATA
    # reason (that is what drives exit 3 rather than 2), and a genuinely capped account must still
    # read as capped. The cliff is the softest reason here — it is a preference, and ranked()
    # yields it entirely when it is what emptied the candidate set.
    if cliff and cliff_band(r) == "drain":
        return CLIFF_DRAIN_REASON
    return None


def score_general(r, cfg, cliff=True, recovery=False):
    R = cfg["router"]
    reason = _excluded(r, R, cliff, recovery=recovery)
    if reason:
        return None, reason
    if r.get("weekly_pct") is None:
        return None, "no-weekly-data"          # missing data is NOT headroom
    w_rem = weekly_headroom(r)
    if w_rem <= R["WEEKLY_FLOOR"]:
        return None, "weekly-exhausted"
    T = horizon(effective_reset_h(r, R), R)
    # T**γ (M7): deadline-dominant urgency — see the utilization-pressure block above. γ=1 is
    # the exact pre-M7 linear form.
    return (w_rem / (T ** urgency_exp(R))) * _soft(r, R) * (_dispatch_cliff_factor(r) if cliff else 1.0), None


DESK_TIER_SAFE = 2      # 5h-safe AND weekly-safe — the set the rule wants to pick from
DESK_TIER_5H = 1        # 5h-safe only (the weekly-headroom key degraded away)
DESK_TIER_ANY = 0       # eligible, neither key satisfied (both keys degraded away)
DESK_KEY_MAX = 0.999    # ceiling on the within-tier term: a tier boundary is never crossable


def _desk_floor(env, R, key, default, neutral):
    """A desk floor: env override > SSOT constant > code default, with `off` NEUTRALISING the key.

    `off`/`false`/`none` is the kill switch. The numeral 0 deliberately is NOT — this is where the
    knob departs from _term_on, because for the 5h key 0 does not mean "no floor", it means MAXIMAL
    strictness (nothing is ever 5h-safe), so reading 0 as off would invert the switch. A malformed
    value falls back to the default (_cliff_env's contract): a typo in a tuning knob must never
    silently disable a guard."""
    raw = (os.environ.get(env) or "").strip().lower()
    if raw in ("off", "false", "none"):
        return neutral
    return clamp(_cliff_env(env, R.get(key, default)), 0.0, 1.0)


def desk_5h_floor(R):
    return _desk_floor("CC_ROUTE_DESK_5H_FLOOR", R, "DESK_5H_FLOOR", DESK_5H_FLOOR, float("inf"))


def desk_w_floor(R):
    return _desk_floor("CC_ROUTE_DESK_W_FLOOR", R, "DESK_W_FLOOR", DESK_W_FLOOR, 0.0)


def desk_w_floor_full_h(R):
    """Horizon at which DESK_W_FLOOR applies in full. 0 disables the ramp (flat floor)."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_DESK_W_RAMP"):
        return 0.0
    return max(0.0, _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_DESK_W_FULL_H",
                               R.get("DESK_W_FLOOR_FULL_H", DESK_W_FLOOR_FULL_H)))


def desk_w_floor_at(R, weekly_reset_h):
    """DESK_W_FLOOR scaled by how much the weekly wall it guards against would actually COST (W2).

    ── Why the flat floor stranded quota (measured 2026-08-16, 1,251 sweeps / 5 resets) ──────────
    DESK_W_FLOOR exists to stop a pure earliest-reset sort handing the desk "an account with 2% of
    its week left and 3 hours to reset (a WEEKLY wall, the unrecoverable one)" — desk_keys' words.
    But it was a CONSTANT, so it fired identically whether the reset was six days away or six hours
    away, and the wall's cost is precisely the time until that reset. So it abandoned the tail:

      acct   headroom when demoted   hours to reset   stranded at reset
      next            0.14                 11.1              9 pp
      next2           0.09                  7.9              8 pp

    `next` is the clean instance — 0.14 against a 0.15 floor, ONE point below, eleven hours out.
    It fell to DESK_TIER_5H, the desk went to an account six days from its own reset, and it never
    came back: 0/112 sweeps on target across its final 12 hours. The general lane, which has no
    such floor, kept naming the same accounts down to the final hour (`w_rem / T**2` — the two
    lanes disagreed for the entire endgame and the desk was the wrong one). Weekly quota has no
    recovery, so those 17 pp are gone.

    ── The rule ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Reserve headroom in proportion to what a wall would cost: full floor at DESK_W_FLOOR_FULL_H
    hours out, ramping linearly to 0 at the reset. Equivalently — and this is the calibration that
    picked the constant — the floor is "what you could plausibly still BURN before the reset", at
    0.15/24h = 0.63 pp/h, inside the observed per-account range (0.45 pp/h on `next`, 1.9 pp/h on
    `next4`). Reserving more than you can spend protects nothing and strands the difference.

    Replayed over the same series, desk-time on an about-to-reset account holding headroom went
    24.7% -> 67.4% of its final-12h sweeps, with weekly-wall exposure flat at 0 sweeps for every
    FULL_H in {8, 12, 18, 24, 36, 48}. 24h is the SMALLEST value on the capture plateau (18h loses
    1.1 pt, 12h loses 6.9), i.e. full capture while retaining the guard over the longest horizon —
    the same "sized by argument, not by insensitivity" reasoning DESK_5H_FLOOR is stated under, and
    it carries the same caveat: the plateau is flat because the guard rarely binds, which is an
    absence of evidence about the far end, not evidence of robustness there.

    The horizon comes from horizon(), NOT the raw field, so this inherits one contract already
    relied on fleet-wide: an absent or elapsed reset reads as FAR AWAY, never imminent. That is the
    conservative direction here — unknown timing keeps the FULL floor, so bad data can never talk
    this into draining an account."""
    base = desk_w_floor(R)
    full_h = desk_w_floor_full_h(R)
    if base <= 0.0 or full_h <= 0.0:
        return base
    return base * clamp(horizon(weekly_reset_h, R) / full_h, 0.0, 1.0)


def desk_hyst_margin(R):
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_DESK_HYST"):
        return 0.0
    return clamp(_cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_DESK_HYST_MARGIN",
                            R.get("DESK_HYST_MARGIN", DESK_HYST_MARGIN)), 0.0, 1.0)


def desk_keys(r, cfg):
    """The desk lane's two keys and its TIER for one row -> (su, w_rem, tier).

    THE DEGRADATION LADDER, written out rather than left to emerge from a fallback that happens to
    fire (W1.1). Every ELIGIBLE row lands on exactly one rung, so the candidate set cannot be
    emptied by these keys — that is the property, and it is structural, not lucky:

      tier 2  SAFE SET  projected 5h utilisation < DESK_5H_FLOOR  AND  weekly headroom >= the
                        horizon-scaled weekly floor, desk_w_floor_at() (W2)
      tier 1  5H-SAFE   the 5h key holds; the weekly-headroom key does not
      tier 0  ELIGIBLE  neither key holds — still routable, still ranked

    The weekly key degrades FIRST because it is the cheaper guarantee to lose: it exists only to
    stop a pure earliest-reset sort from handing the desk an account with 2% of its week left and
    3 hours to reset (a WEEKLY wall, the unrecoverable one). The 5h key degrades second because a
    5-hour wall self-heals in <=5 h and has three escapes. Below both, the ladder still ranks by the
    objective; it never answers "none" while an eligible account exists.

    ⚠️ The weekly floor is a FUNCTION OF THE HORIZON, not a constant — desk_w_floor_at() carries
    the measurement. Read it before tuning DESK_W_FLOOR: as a constant this key strands the tail of
    every account it is supposed to protect, which is the defect it was changed to fix.

    (su, None, tier 0) when weekly_pct is absent — the caller's own data gate rejects that row
    before the tier is used; the tier is defined here anyway so route-meta can never index None.)"""
    R = cfg["router"]
    su = _su_projected(r, R)
    wp = r.get("weekly_pct")
    if wp is None:
        return su, None, DESK_TIER_ANY
    w_rem = weekly_headroom(r)
    if su < desk_5h_floor(R):
        w_floor = desk_w_floor_at(R, effective_reset_h(r, R))
        return su, w_rem, (DESK_TIER_SAFE if w_rem >= w_floor else DESK_TIER_5H)
    return su, w_rem, DESK_TIER_ANY


def score_interactive(r, cfg, cliff=True):
    """The DESK lane: a TWO-KEY rule — 5h-safe set first, then EARLIEST WEEKLY RESET.

    ── The reversal, recorded (2026-08-11, DESK_ROUTER_AND_STARTUP_V1 W1, docs/research/R2) ──────
    This function shipped on 2026-08-10 (`9a843f988`) as a SURVIVAL score — `w_rem * s_rem * KF *
    CF`, maximise runway, no 1/T term — on the argument preserved verbatim below. That argument's
    load-bearing claim is now MEASURED FALSE, so the objective is inverted here. The old reasoning
    is kept, not deleted, because it is exactly the plausible-but-wrong derivation the next reader
    would otherwise re-do from scratch (it was itself derived as agent finding "S8" in a research
    wave, on the same day the operator's own M7 /goal stated the opposite objective, and it was
    validated on a SYNTHETIC PAIR because the live snapshot could not discriminate the lanes):

      C1-C3 (still true): score_general is `w_rem / T**2`, deadline-DOMINANT by construction (M7),
        because a dispatched fire is SHORT and the objective there is to spend quota before it
        strands. A desk session does live for hours or days in WALL-CLOCK terms.
      C4 (MEASURED FALSE): "pointing bare `claude` at the dispatch score would place the operator
        NEAREST exhaustion and manufacture the 5-hour wall this lane exists to prevent."
        🚨 It would not, and the refutation is a CODE fact before it is a measurement:
        score_general already multiplies by `_soft` — which carries the hard S_CUT=0.85 exclusion,
        a PROJECTED 5h ramp from S_SOFT=0.50, the same KF and the same CF (see _soft above). Both
        lanes always shared the 5-hour brake; they differed in exactly two terms (the 1/T**2
        urgency, and the shape of the 5h term). "NEAREST exhaustion" under score_general means
        nearest its WEEKLY reset, which is a different bucket from the one the operator feels.
        Replayed over all 324 sweeps of the utilization series, scored on what actually happened in
        the following 6 h: the survival lane hit the 5h wall on 4.6% of picks and the dispatch lane
        on 3.4% — the survival lane was measurably WORSE at its own job, because w_rem dominance
        sent it to the roomiest WEEKLY account rather than the roomiest 5-HOUR one. Weekly
        exhaustion: 0/324, both lanes. The two lanes disagreed on 92% of sweeps (298/324), so the
        landing commit's "both lanes name next4 on today's live fleet" was a one-snapshot artifact.
      C5 (superseded): the two-key rule below scored 0.6% wall exposure on the same replay (5-8x
        better than either shipped lane) and 0.0% on the one genuinely stressed window, while
        picking the earliest-resetting account — i.e. it dominates the survival score on the
        survival axis too, which is why this is a reversal and not a trade.

    ── The rule ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Among _excluded-eligible accounts: prefer those with projected 5h utilisation < DESK_5H_FLOOR
    and weekly headroom >= DESK_W_FLOOR; among those, take the EARLIEST WEEKLY RESET. It is the
    operator's own sentence executed literally ("earlier weekly expiry and low 5-hour used"), and
    it is the M7 objective — spend quota before it strands — with a 5-hour guard in front of it.
    ~30 percentage-points of weekly quota is forecast to strand per week on this fleet, and stranded
    weekly quota has NO recovery, where a 5-hour wall self-heals in <=5 h and has three escapes.

    Expressed as a score so _rank_pass/ranked() stay one code path: `tier + within-tier key`, with
    the tier from desk_keys() (the explicit ladder) and the key `1/(1+T)` — largest for the
    soonest reset, bounded in (0,1) so it can never cross a tier boundary. Consequences worth
    naming: there is no KF term any more (concurrency binds only through _excluded's KMAX cap, and
    the desk does not pay its own launcher phantom — k_eff_desk, W1.5), and no CF term (the credits
    weekly target is already in w_rem). Ties inside a tier fall to accounts.json order, which is
    deterministic — a desk must not flap between two equal accounts.

    ⚠️ SUPERSEDED 2026-10-17: DESK_5H_FLOOR is now 1.00 (the projected wall) by operator ruling — see
    the constant. The paragraph below is the 0.60 record, kept.
    ⚠️ DESK_5H_FLOOR = 0.60 is UNTESTED BY THIS DATA, NOT VALIDATED BY IT. The replay was flat at
    0.6% for every floor in {0.50, 0.60, 0.70} x every weekly floor in {0, 0.15, 0.30} — but that
    is because the fleet almost never approaches 60% 5h utilisation (3 of 4 accounts never exceeded
    61% across 38 h), so the floor almost never binds and its insensitivity is an ABSENCE OF
    EVIDENCE, not evidence of robustness. It is insurance, sized by argument rather than by
    measurement: below S_CUT=0.85 with real margin, above the 61% ceiling three accounts observed,
    so it binds on pathology (the one next3-shaped case in 38 h, which is precisely when a naive
    earliest-reset sort would hand the operator a capped desk) and never on ordinary load. The
    weekly floor made no difference at all on this data (0/324) and is kept for the same reason.
    Re-run the replay after >=2 full weekly cycles before treating either number as durable.

    ⚠️ THE WEEKLY-FLOOR HALF OF THAT PARAGRAPH IS NOW MEASURED FALSE (W2, 2026-08-16), and the
    instruction above is the reason it was caught: "made no difference at all on this data (0/324)"
    was true of the W1 window and false of the very next one. Over 1,254 sweeps / 5 resets the FLAT
    floor was the largest single source of stranded weekly quota — it abandoned `next` at 0.14
    headroom with 11.1h to its reset (0/112 sweeps on target thereafter; 9pp expired), while the
    general lane, which has no such floor, kept naming that same account down to the final hour.
    The floor is now horizon-scaled: desk_w_floor_at() carries the measurement and the calibration.
    The replay is no longer something you must rebuild in order to run it —
    `scripts/desk-strand-replay.py` (`--sweep` for the plateau, `--flat` for the counterfactual).
    Full record: docs/research/R9-desk-weekly-strand.md.

    KEPT from the shipped lane, unchanged: every exclusion is shared with general (_excluded, C6 —
    a different objective over the same eligibility, never a second opinion about who is eligible),
    the login-cliff SOFT factor applies unless the week is stranding and drain is past any desk's
    life (_desk_cliff_factor),
    and the interactive lane NEVER YIELDS the cliff term (C7, see
    ranked() — past the cliff `invalid_grant` has no reset to wait for). That rule, not the score,
    is why this lane deserves to exist."""
    R = cfg["router"]
    reason = _excluded(r, R, cliff, k_of=k_eff_desk)
    if reason:
        return None, reason
    if r.get("weekly_pct") is None:
        return None, "no-weekly-data"
    su, w_rem, tier = desk_keys(r, cfg)
    if w_rem <= R["WEEKLY_FLOOR"]:
        return None, "weekly-exhausted"
    # EARLIEST WEEKLY RESET, as a bounded descending key: T is the same urgency denominator every
    # other lane uses (horizon() — so an absent or elapsed reset reads as FAR AWAY, never imminent),
    # and 1/(1+T) is in (0,1) for every T >= EPS_H, so a within-tier key can never reach the next
    # tier however the operator tunes EPS_H.
    key = 1.0 / (1.0 + horizon(effective_reset_h(r, R), R))
    # The cliff SOFT band demotes INSIDE the tier rather than across tiers (a soft-band account is
    # still a safer desk than one that failed the 5h key outright), and is lifted only when the
    # week is stranding — see _desk_cliff_factor for why x0.25 everywhere misrouted.
    key *= (_desk_cliff_factor(r) if cliff else 1.0)
    # HYSTERESIS (W1.4 / D-B, specified at START_LATENCY_ROUTER.md:82-83 and never built until
    # now): the incumbent desk account keeps the desk unless a rival beats it by DESK_HYST_MARGIN.
    # Inside the tier only, deliberately — stickiness must never hold the operator on an account
    # that has fallen out of the safe set, which is exactly when the desk SHOULD move. What it
    # prevents is the measured churn: consecutive-decision flips ran 20.6% lifetime and 43.4% over
    # the last 100 decisions, and a flip is not free — projects/sessions/history.jsonl are
    # per-account, so `--resume` cannot see across a move.
    if r.get("desk_incumbent"):
        key *= 1.0 + desk_hyst_margin(R)
    return tier + min(key, DESK_KEY_MAX), None


def score_fable(r, cfg, win, cliff=True, recovery=False):
    R, F = cfg["router"], cfg["frontier"]
    reason = _excluded(r, R, cliff, recovery=recovery)
    if reason:
        return None, reason
    if win["active"] is None:
        return None, "window-unknown"           # SSOT unreadable: fail loud, not open/closed
    if not win["active"]:
        return None, "window-inactive"
    if r.get("fable_pct") is None:
        return None, "no-fable-limit"           # missing scoped limit = no entitlement, not 100% headroom
    if r.get("weekly_pct") is None:
        return None, "no-weekly-data"
    h_deadline = None
    if win["deadline"] is not None:
        h_deadline = (win["deadline"] - datetime.now(timezone.utc)).total_seconds() / 3600.0 \
            - F["deadline_margin_h"]
        if h_deadline <= 0 and not F.get("credits_authorized"):
            return None, "window-ended"
        if h_deadline <= 0:
            h_deadline = None                   # credits mode: no window pressure, CF deprioritizes
    w_rem = weekly_headroom(r)
    f_eff = min(F["coupling"] * max(0.0, 1 - r["fable_pct"] / 100.0), w_rem)
    # The fable floor RISES for a recovery — max(), so it can only ever be stricter. This is the
    # one recovery floor that cannot live in eligibility: f_eff is the coupled fable/weekly
    # quantity `score_fable` itself computes. It also closes the float ULP at weekly 98
    # (1.0 - 98/100 == 0.020000000000000018 > FABLE_FLOOR), independently of the weekly floor.
    floor = max(R["FABLE_FLOOR"], recovery_floors(R)[2]) if recovery else R["FABLE_FLOOR"]
    if f_eff <= floor:
        # A genuinely exhausted bucket keeps its OWN reason — the recovery floor does not rename
        # what was already refused, it only names what IT refused.
        return None, ("recovery-fable-thin" if recovery and f_eff > R["FABLE_FLOOR"]
                      else "fable-exhausted")
    T_fable = horizon(effective_reset_h(r, R, "fable_reset_h"), R)
    H = max(min(T_fable, h_deadline), R["EPS_H"]) if h_deadline is not None else max(T_fable, R["EPS_H"])
    JB = R["JB_BONUS"] if (h_deadline is not None and T_fable > h_deadline) else 1.00
    # H**γ mirrors score_general — the scoped bucket strands at ITS reset by the same law.
    return (f_eff / (H ** urgency_exp(R))) * JB * _soft(r, R) * (_dispatch_cliff_factor(r) if cliff else 1.0), None


def _rank_pass(rows, cfg, win, kind, cliff, recovery=False):
    out, reasons = [], {}
    for r in rows:
        # The interactive lane takes no recovery modifier: it picks a HUMAN's desk, not a host
        # for a transplanted session, and main() refuses the combination before reaching here.
        s, why = (score_fable(r, cfg, win, cliff, recovery=recovery) if kind == "fable"
                  else score_interactive(r, cfg, cliff) if kind == "interactive"
                  else score_general(r, cfg, cliff, recovery=recovery))
        if s is not None and s > 0:
            out.append((s, r))
        else:
            reasons[r["acct"]] = why or "zero-score"
    out.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)
    return out, reasons


# ---- batch recovery placement (--place, LIMIT_RECOVER_FLEET_V2 C5/§5) ----
# `--route --recovery` answers "which ONE account for this session", so a wave of limited panes
# asked it N times and every answer was the same rank[0] — the router cannot see its own previous
# answers inside the cache TTL. --place answers for the whole batch at once: capacity is computed
# per account ONCE, every pick is charged against it before the next, and the per-pick floors
# charge each already-placed mover's burn, so the 7th mover sees the 6 before it.
PLACE_B_SESS_DEFAULT = 0.07       # fraction/h: a mover with no measured pre-death burn
PLACE_R_W_WORST_DEFAULT = 0.011   # weekly fraction/h per session: the worst measured rate (U13)
PLACE_EXIT_WAIT_DATA, PLACE_EXIT_BAD_ARGS = 3, 64


class _PlaceArgError(Exception):
    pass


def _place_epoch(v) -> "float | None":
    """Epoch seconds from an epoch number or an ISO-8601 string; None when absent or unreadable."""
    if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool):
        return float(v)
    if isinstance(v, str) and v.strip():
        try:
            t = datetime.fromisoformat(v.strip().replace("Z", "+00:00"))
        except ValueError:
            return None
        return (t if t.tzinfo else t.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)).timestamp()
    return None


def _place_read_facts(d: str) -> dict:
    """{acct: [fact, ...]} from `<acct>.<scope>.json`. An unreadable file is ignored with one
    stderr line: a torn write by a concurrent reporter must not abort a whole placement."""
    out: dict = {}
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.json"))):
        acct, _, rest = os.path.basename(p)[:-len(".json")].partition(".")
        try:
            with open(p) as fh:
                f = json.load(fh)
            if not isinstance(f, dict):
                raise ValueError("not an object")
        except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
            print(f"claude-accounts --place: ignoring unreadable fact {p}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        f.setdefault("scope", rest)
        out.setdefault(acct, []).append(f)
    return out


def _place_fact_live(f: dict, row: "dict | None", now: float) -> bool:
    """Unexpired = rejected, and before resets_at+60 (no resets_at never expires on time). A 5h/7d
    fact also dies when the row's wire for THAT window reads allowed at a read time later than
    observed_at+60 — the server has since said yes. No wire read time on the row ⇒ no such expiry:
    a wire of unknown age cannot outrank a dated refusal. A CONTRADICTED auth fact is dead: the
    account served a turn after it, and nothing else ever expires it, so next2/next3 got zero
    capacity on every call from 2026-10-05 (decision 508c47814658). An account whose auth really
    is broken is still refused, by its row's own error. A contradicted 5h/7d fact stays live to
    its reset (lr_recon facts.blocking, §C4)."""
    if f.get("status") != "rejected":
        return False
    ra = _place_epoch(f.get("resets_at"))
    if ra is not None and now >= ra + 60:
        return False
    scope = f.get("scope")
    if scope == "auth" and f.get("contradicted") is True:
        return False
    if scope in ("5h", "7d") and row is not None:
        w = row.get("wire") or {}
        read = _place_epoch(w.get("read_at") or w.get("at") or row.get("wire_at"))
        obs = _place_epoch(f.get("observed_at"))
        if w.get(f"{scope}_status") == "allowed" and read is not None and obs is not None \
                and read > obs + 60:
            return False
    return True


def _place_covers(f: dict, lane: str, model: "str | None") -> bool:
    s = str(f.get("scope") or "")
    if s in ("5h", "7d", "auth"):
        return True
    if s == "fable":
        return lane == "fable"
    if s.startswith("model:"):
        return bool(model) and s[len("model:"):] in model
    return False


def _place_load_inputs(args: list) -> tuple:
    def val(flag: str) -> str:
        if flag not in args or args.index(flag) + 1 >= len(args):
            raise _PlaceArgError(f"{flag} is required")
        return args[args.index(flag) + 1]
    lane = val("--lane")
    if lane not in ("general", "fable"):
        raise _PlaceArgError(f"--lane must be general|fable, not {lane!r}")
    movers, facts_dir = [], val("--facts")
    try:
        with open(val("--movers")) as fh:
            for ln in fh:
                if not ln.strip():
                    continue
                m = json.loads(ln)
                w = m.get("w", 1)
                if not isinstance(m, dict) or not m.get("sid") or not m.get("src") \
                        or m.get("kind") not in ("limited", "idle") \
                        or not isinstance(w, int) or isinstance(w, bool) or w < 1:
                    raise ValueError(f"bad mover line: {ln.strip()[:120]}")
                movers.append(dict(m, w=w))
        with open(val("--kwork")) as fh:
            kwork = json.load(fh)
        if not isinstance(kwork, dict):
            raise ValueError("--kwork is not a JSON object")
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError) as e:
        raise _PlaceArgError(str(e)) from e
    if not os.path.isdir(facts_dir):
        raise _PlaceArgError(f"--facts {facts_dir} is not a directory")
    return lane, movers, facts_dir, kwork


# B4 C2: --place reads three apply_burn outputs and nothing else: burn_5h_ph (2 h), burn_5h_ewma_ph
# (SU_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H) and wk_strand_pp (burn_wk_ewma_ph, WK_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H). The 336 h read exists
# for burst_percentile and exchange_rate, which only the board renders. The hour of slack covers
# the gap between the read's clock and apply_burn's, which the parent's ≤120 s kill bounds.
PLACE_BURN_LOOKBACK_H = max(SU_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H, WK_EWMA_LOOKBACK_H, 2.0) + 1.0


def _place_burn_samples():
    """The utilization rows --place's burn fields can see, or None (apply_burn's own 336 h read)
    with CC_PLACE_BURN_NARROW=off or ~/.claude/state/place-burn-narrow.off."""
    if not _switch_on("CC_PLACE_BURN_NARROW", "place-burn-narrow.off"):
        return None
    return _util_tail(hours=PLACE_BURN_LOOKBACK_H)[0]


def cmd_place(args: list, cfg: dict) -> int:
    """Batch recovery placement: stdout {sid: {acct, reason, eta_s, weight}}; exit 0 = a plan
    (unplaced movers included), 3 = WAIT_DATA (no servable cache), 64 = bad arguments/inputs.

    INPUTS. --movers: JSONL {sid, src (account name OR config-dir path), kind limited|idle, w (int,
    default 1), model?, burn_ph? (the mover's own pre-death 5h burn, pp/h), death_ts? (epoch),
    assign_id? (the ledger id of the mover's own phantom, never charged against its own placement),
    holds? ({acct: [config_dir, bool]}: the caller's own answer to "does that store hold a live
    projects/*/<sid>.jsonl", from an index that gives the glob's answer)}. An entry is trusted only
    when realpath(config_dir) equals realpath of the account's config_dir HERE, so a map computed
    over a different accounts.json can never answer (B4 review F5). An account the map omits, a
    malformed entry, or CC_PLACE_HOLDS_MAP=off / ~/.claude/state/place-holds-map.off globs as before.
    --kwork: JSON {acct: int|null} — missing/null is UNMEASURED and gets no seats, because a count
    nobody took is not zero. --facts: a dir of `<acct>.<scope>.json` {status:"rejected", scope,
    window, resets_at, first_sid, observed_at, src, contradicted?}; 5h/7d/auth cover every lane,
    fable the fable lane, model:<name> only movers whose model contains <name>.

    Capacity is KMAX − kwork − phantoms and never KMAX_RESIDENT: the pane census is a RESIDENT
    count, and charging it against a 40 cap placed 40−k movers on an account whose ACTIVE limit is
    8. A source store already holding the sid is exempt from the store skip — staying is resuming
    in place, not a second copy."""
    try:
        lane, movers, facts_dir, kwork = _place_load_inputs(args)
    except _PlaceArgError as e:
        print(f"claude-accounts --place: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return PLACE_EXIT_BAD_ARGS
    # W6 resolution 11: a cache taken before any mover's SOURCE reset is unservable here too.
    _dirs0 = {os.path.expanduser(a["config_dir"]).rstrip("/"): a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    _names0 = {a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    _srcs = tuple(sorted({str(m["src"]) if str(m["src"]) in _names0
                          else _dirs0.get(os.path.expanduser(str(m["src"])).rstrip("/"))
                          for m in movers} - {None}))
    try:
        rows, wj, _cached, _prev = get_data(cfg, max_wait=0, max_age=cfg.get("cache_grace_s", 600),
                                            src=_srcs)
    except CacheOnlyUnavailable:
        print(json.dumps({m["sid"]: {"acct": None, "reason": "wait-data", "eta_s": None,
                                     "weight": m["w"]} for m in movers}, sort_keys=True))
        return PLACE_EXIT_WAIT_DATA
    mark_self(rows, cfg)
    refresh_login_countdown(rows)
    apply_assignments(rows, cfg)
    apply_burn(rows, cfg, samples=_place_burn_samples())
    win = win_from_json(wj)
    R, now = cfg["router"], time.time()
    KMAX = R["KMAX"]
    w_floor, s_ceil, _f = recovery_floors(R)
    r_w_worst = _cliff_env("CC_ROUTE_RECOVERY_R_W_WORST",
                           R.get("RECOVERY_R_W_WORST", PLACE_R_W_WORST_DEFAULT))
    b_default = _cliff_env("CC_PLACE_B_SESS_DEFAULT", PLACE_B_SESS_DEFAULT)
    facts = _place_read_facts(facts_dir)
    dirs = {a["name"]: os.path.expanduser(a["config_dir"]).rstrip("/") for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    by_dir = {d: n for n, d in dirs.items()}
    mirror = next((n for n, d in dirs.items() if d.endswith(".claude-next")), None)
    if mirror is not None:
        by_dir.setdefault(os.path.join(HOME, ".claude"), mirror)
    snap = {r["acct"]: r for r in rows
            if r.get("acct") in dirs and r.get("acct") != "none"}

    def live(acct: str, pred) -> list:
        return [f for f in facts.get(acct, []) if pred(f) and _place_fact_live(f, snap.get(acct), now)]

    def acct_facts(acct: str) -> list:
        return live(acct, lambda f: not str(f.get("scope", "")).startswith("model:")
                    and _place_covers(f, lane, None))

    def phantom_w(a: str) -> int:
        return snap[a].get("k_phantom") or 0

    cap, why, free = {}, {}, {}
    for a, r in snap.items():
        kw = kwork.get(a)
        if not isinstance(kw, int) or isinstance(kw, bool):
            cap[a], why[a] = 0, "k-unmeasured"
            continue
        af = acct_facts(a)
        if af:
            cap[a], why[a] = 0, f"fact:{sorted(str(f.get('scope')) for f in af)[0]}"
            continue
        rc = dict(r, k_work=kw, k_phantom=phantom_w(a))
        x = _excluded(rc, R, recovery=True, k_of=lambda _r, _k=kw + phantom_w(a): _k)
        x = None if x == "kmax-concurrency" else x
        if not x:
            free[a] = KMAX - kw - phantom_w(a)
        cap[a] = 0 if x else max(0, free[a])
        why[a] = x or "kmax-concurrency"

    # gap-3 E4.3: a mover re-placed while its own phantom is still live (PLANNED, then BACKOFF,
    # inside the phantom's 1200 s TTL) was charged its own seats on the account it was planned on:
    # db755a94 (w=19 on next4) waited ~13 min for its own phantom. Each mover gets back exactly
    # what its own ledger id charges, on that account only, by the ledger's own rules.
    own_ids = {m["assign_id"] for m in movers
               if isinstance(m.get("assign_id"), str) and m["assign_id"]}
    own = {e["id"]: (e["acct"], ew)
           for e, ew in (_live_assignments(cfg, sweep=_assignment_sweep(rows)) if own_ids else ())
           if e.get("id") in own_ids}

    def own_w(m: dict, a: str) -> int:
        o = own.get(m.get("assign_id"))
        return o[1] if o is not None and o[0] == a else 0

    def room(m: dict, a: str) -> int:
        return max(0, free[a] + own_w(m, a)) if a in free else cap[a]

    def src_of(m: dict) -> "str | None":
        s = str(m["src"])
        return s if s in dirs else by_dir.get(os.path.expanduser(s).rstrip("/"))

    def src_facts(m: dict) -> list:
        s = src_of(m)
        return [] if s is None else live(s, lambda f: _place_covers(f, lane, m.get("model")))

    def order(m: dict) -> tuple:
        if not src_facts(m):
            return (0, 0.0, m["sid"])
        if m["kind"] == "limited":
            dt = m.get("death_ts")
            return (1, float(dt) if isinstance(dt, (int, float)) else float("inf"), m["sid"])
        return (2, 0.0, m["sid"])

    def floors_ok(a: str, n: int, b_sess: float) -> "str | None":
        r = snap[a]
        wu5 = (r.get("wire") or {}).get("5h_util")
        su = wu5 if wu5 is not None else (r.get("session_pct") or 0) / 100.0
        b = r.get("burn_5h_ewma_ph")
        b = b / 100.0 if isinstance(b, (int, float)) else r.get("burn_5h_ph")
        b_native = b if isinstance(b, (int, float)) and b > 0 else 0.0
        srh = r.get("session_reset_h")
        ahead = min(1.0, max(0.0, srh)) if isinstance(srh, (int, float)) else 1.0
        if su + (b_native + n * b_sess) * ahead >= s_ceil:
            return "recovery-5h-thin"
        w_rem = weekly_headroom(r)
        wrh = r.get("weekly_reset_h")
        span = min(max(0.0, wrh), 9.0) if isinstance(wrh, (int, float)) else 9.0
        if w_rem is None or w_rem - (n + 1) * r_w_worst * span < w_floor:
            return "recovery-weekly-thin"
        return None

    trust_holds = _switch_on("CC_PLACE_HOLDS_MAP", "place-holds-map.off")
    # --exclude a,b (D2b, W16): accounts no mover may be placed on, the source's own included. The
    # late-band shed names every banded and walled account, so an idle mover is never "placed" back
    # onto the account it is leaving (`stay`) or onto another one about to wall (review D17).
    excl = set()
    if "--exclude" in args and args.index("--exclude") + 1 < len(args):
        excl = {x for x in args[args.index("--exclude") + 1].split(",") if x}
    _real = {}

    def realp(p: str) -> str:
        if p not in _real:
            _real[p] = os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser(p))
        return _real[p]

    def holds(a: str, m: dict) -> bool:
        known = m.get("holds") if trust_holds else None
        ent = known.get(a) if isinstance(known, dict) else None
        if isinstance(ent, list) and len(ent) == 2 and isinstance(ent[0], str) and ent[0] \
                and isinstance(ent[1], bool) and realp(ent[0]) == realp(dirs[a]):
            return ent[1]
        return bool(glob.glob(os.path.join(dirs[a], "projects", "*",
                                           glob.escape(m["sid"]) + ".jsonl")))

    placed_w = {a: 0 for a in snap}
    placed_n = {a: 0 for a in snap}
    out = {}
    for m in sorted(movers, key=order):
        sid, w, src = m["sid"], m["w"], src_of(m)
        sf = src_facts(m)
        # A SUPPLIED burn is a floor-raiser, never an undercharge (W6 D3.6a): raw pre-death burn
        # bounded post-move burn for only 19 of 56 transplant pairs, max(pre, default) for 51.
        # No caller supplies burn_ph today, so this is defensive; the default is unchanged.
        b_sess = max(m["burn_ph"] / 100.0, b_default) \
            if isinstance(m.get("burn_ph"), (int, float)) else b_default
        reasons, zeroing, scored = set(), [], []
        for a in snap:
            if a in excl:
                reasons.add("excluded")
                continue
            mf = live(a, lambda f: str(f.get("scope", "")).startswith("model:")
                      and _place_covers(f, lane, m.get("model")))
            if room(m, a) - placed_w[a] < w:
                reasons.add(why[a])
                zeroing += acct_facts(a)
            elif a != src and holds(a, m):
                reasons.add("store-holds-sid")
            elif mf:
                reasons.add(f"fact:{sorted(str(f.get('scope')) for f in mf)[0]}")
                zeroing += mf
            elif a == src and sf:
                reasons.add("src-fact")
                zeroing += sf
            elif (miss := floors_ok(a, placed_n[a] + 1, b_sess)):
                reasons.add(miss)
            else:
                rc = dict(snap[a], k_work=kwork[a],
                          k_phantom=max(0, phantom_w(a) - own_w(m, a)) + placed_w[a])
                s, x = (score_fable(rc, cfg, win, recovery=True) if lane == "fable"
                        else score_general(rc, cfg, recovery=True))
                if s is None or s <= 0:
                    reasons.add(x or "zero-score")
                else:
                    scored.append((a, s))
        if not scored:
            ras = [t for t in (_place_epoch(f.get("resets_at")) for f in sf) if t is not None] \
                or [t for t in (_place_epoch(f.get("resets_at")) for f in zeroing) if t is not None]
            out[sid] = {"acct": None, "reason": ",".join(sorted(reasons)) or "no-account",
                        "eta_s": max(0, int(min(ras) - now)) if ras else None, "weight": w}
            continue
        if src in dict(scored):
            best, reason = src, "stay"
        else:
            best = min(scored, key=lambda t: (-t[1], placed_n[t[0]],
                                              sha256(f"{sid}\0{t[0]}".encode()).hexdigest()))[0]
            reason = "placed"
        placed_w[best] += w
        placed_n[best] += 1
        out[sid] = {"acct": best, "reason": reason, "eta_s": None, "weight": w}
    print(json.dumps(out, sort_keys=True))
    return 0


# ── the DISPATCH floor (2026-09-27): demote, never exclude ──────────────────────────────────────
# `handoff-fire.sh --account auto` takes `--rank general | head -1`, and on 2026-09-26 that put a
# code-writing fire on next4 at weekly 96-99 %; it hit the weekly limit before closing. The only
# general-lane weekly bar is WEEKLY_FLOOR (0.5pp), and the T**2 urgency term paid next4's ~12 h
# reset a ~180x bonus over next2's ~158 h one, so next4 outscored an almost-empty next2 4-6x.
# Urgency is right about STRANDING and blind to SURVIVAL: a new fire burns for hours, and the
# account it lands on must still exist at the end. So an account under this much weekly headroom
# is THIN, and a thin account is HELD: it ranks below every account that is not. It stays in the
# list, so it is still the pick when it is the only routable one; the score is untouched, so each
# group keeps the strand order. Not applied to --recovery (its own floor already EXCLUDES) or to
# the interactive lane (it has its own tiers).
#
# ── the STRAND RESCUE (2026-10-09): a thin account that will strand is not held ─────────────────
# The flat floor could not tell an account about to wall from one about to waste its week. On
# 2026-10-09 next2 sat at weekly 94 % with 13 h to its reset, burning 0.05 pp/h: ~5pp forecast to
# die unused, ranked LAST for every new fire by the rule above, for the whole of its last day. So
# a thin row is judged (dispatch_floor_row) and LIFTED out of the hold when its forecast-dead
# quota covers a whole new fire: spare >= max(DISPATCH_STRAND_PP, DISPATCH_FIRE_BURN_PPH x hours
# to the reset), once per fire already placed there inside DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN.
#
# Lifting alone changes nothing, which is why the general lane also PROMOTES. Replayed over that
# episode, a lifted next2 scored 0.39x-1.00x of the best rival and was line 1 in 0 of 407 sweeps:
# the urgency term cannot see 6pp against a rival's 45pp. `--account auto` takes line 1, so a
# lifted row goes FIRST, ahead of the roomy ones, and the claim above is what stops the second
# fire from following the first. The fable lane lifts without promoting (fable burn per session is
# unmeasured), and so does any row the promotion guards refuse (_dispatch_partition).
#
# Every missing, stale or malformed input fails toward the 2026-09-27 behaviour (HELD), and the
# reason is the row's token. Measured over the whole utilization ledger on 2026-10-09: 90 lifted
# sweeps in completed windows, all in windows that stranded and none in one that reached 99 %;
# line 1 differs from the flat floor's in 169 of 5,181 sweeps, none in a window that walled.
DISPATCH_W_FLOOR_DEFAULT = 0.10   # = RECOVERY_W_FLOOR: ~9 h at the worst measured burn rate
DISPATCH_STRAND_PP_DEFAULT = 4.0  # = STRAND_SCORE_FLOOR = DESK_CLIFF_STRAND_PP; 3.0 lifted 10
#                                   sweeps in windows that walled, 3.5-4.0 none
DISPATCH_FIRE_BURN_PPH_DEFAULT = 0.42   # one working session's weekly burn: 6h-block p75 0.39,
#                                         p90 0.61 pp/h over 2,845 session-hours
DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN_DEFAULT = 300.0  # one 5h window, as DESK_HYST_TTL_MIN. ASSIGN_TTL_MIN (15)
#                                         cannot hold it: line 1 takes a median of 4 fires an hour


def dispatch_w_floor(R):
    """Env override > SSOT > default; CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_W_FLOOR=off restores the pure score order."""
    return _desk_floor("CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_W_FLOOR", R, "DISPATCH_W_FLOOR",
                       DISPATCH_W_FLOOR_DEFAULT, 0.0)


def _dispatch_knob(env, R, key, default):
    """Env override > SSOT > default (_cliff_env: malformed or negative falls through), clamped to
    the key's ROUTER_OPTIONAL_RANGES entry so an env typo is bounded exactly as an SSOT one is."""
    lo, hi = ROUTER_OPTIONAL_RANGES[key]
    return clamp(_cliff_env(env, R.get(key, default)), lo, hi)


def dispatch_strand_pp(R):
    """The least forecast-dead weekly pp a new fire needs, or None when the whole rescue is OFF
    (CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_STRAND=off|0|false) and the floor is the flat 2026-09-27 one. A _term_on
    switch for repick_ratio's reason: this term AUTHORISES routing onto a thin account, so its
    safe failure direction is off and a number must never be what disables it."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_STRAND"):
        return None
    return _dispatch_knob("CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_STRAND_PP", R, "DISPATCH_STRAND_PP",
                          DISPATCH_STRAND_PP_DEFAULT)


def dispatch_fire_burn_pph(R):
    return _dispatch_knob("CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_FIRE_BURN_PPH", R, "DISPATCH_FIRE_BURN_PPH",
                          DISPATCH_FIRE_BURN_PPH_DEFAULT)


# ── the LATE warning band (D2b, packet 76a584cc935b; LR_SUSTAINED_LOAD W16): idle sessions may leave
# an account before its weekly wall ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Replay of 7 walls (10-08..10-20, 43 limited sessions; docs/research/lr-sustained-load-2026-10-20/
# impl/D2-warning-band-shedding.md §3.3): LV 97 + a 2 h guard avoided 15 at <=1.5 pp stranded in
# total; LV 90 with no guard avoided 24 and stranded 15.6-21.8 pp. That replay ran a SIMPLER rule
# than this one (review D13): it is re-run on this function before `on` is ever asked. This only
# answers "is the account in the band"; the controller (scripts/limit-recover/lr-shed.py) owns
# hysteresis, selection and the move. Shipped for shadow: nothing acts on it without shed/mode.
SHED_LV_OPEN_DEFAULT = 97.0      # weekly used % at which the band opens
SHED_TTW_OPEN_H_DEFAULT = 2.0    # ...or hours to the wall at the FAST pace
SHED_GUARD_H_DEFAULT = 2.0       # the SLOW pace must still wall this long before the reset
SHED_BAND_MAX_AGE_S = 1200       # its own cache grace (review E8): the level moves <= 1-2 pp/h

_SHED_KEYS = {"SHED_LV_OPEN": ("CC_ROUTE_SHED_LV_OPEN", SHED_LV_OPEN_DEFAULT),
              "SHED_TTW_OPEN_H": ("CC_ROUTE_SHED_TTW_OPEN_H", SHED_TTW_OPEN_H_DEFAULT),
              "SHED_GUARD_H": ("CC_ROUTE_SHED_GUARD_H", SHED_GUARD_H_DEFAULT)}


def shed_knob(R, key):
    """Env > SSOT > default. NOT _dispatch_knob: that one CLAMPS, and a clamp moves a typo toward
    shedding (0.97 for 97 became a band from 50 %, review D12). An env value that is malformed,
    non-finite or outside the key's range is ignored and the SSOT/default stands; an SSOT value
    outside it is refused at load (_validate_router)."""
    env, default = _SHED_KEYS[key]
    lo, hi = ROUTER_OPTIONAL_RANGES[key]
    base = R.get(key, default)
    raw = os.environ.get(env)
    if raw:
        try:
            v = float(raw)
        except ValueError:
            return base
        if math.isfinite(v) and lo <= v <= hi:
            return v
    return base


def _shed_finite(x):
    return _is_num(x) and math.isfinite(x)


def shed_band_row(r, R, facts=None, now=None):
    """D2b -> (token, facts). PURE apart from the clock: reads apply_burn's stamps and the caller's
    live 5h/7d facts for this account, no I/O.
       off              CC_ROUTE_SHED=off
       walled           used >= 100 %, or the server or a live fact rejects the 5h/7d window
                        (review D7): the at-wall population is the reconciler's and D2a's, one driver
                        per phase, and a dead source would turn background jobs and /goal holds
                        into moves
       out              used < SHED_LV_OPEN and the fast-pace wall is > SHED_TTW_OPEN_H away
       band             in the late band, and the SLOW pace still walls >= SHED_GUARD_H before
                        the reset: idle sessions (who add nothing to the measured pace) may leave
       band-guard       in the late band, but the slow pace lands the wall inside SHED_GUARD_H of
                        the reset, or never: shedding could strand, so nothing is shed
       hold-credits     extra usage is on: an overshoot is overage, not a wall
       unknown:<why>    level-stale | no-reset | pace-unknown | pace-stale. The controller
                        neither opens nor closes a band on it (our blindness is not a verdict)
    `trip` says which arm opened it ("level" or "ttw"): only the TTW arm flaps, so only it needs
    the controller's confirmation (review E2). Every input passes math.isfinite (review D4: NaN
    reached `band` through max(0.0, nan) and min() silently dropping it)."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_SHED"):
        return "off", {}
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    used = weekly_used_frac(r)
    ra = r.get("weekly_reset_at")
    key = {"reset_at": ra, "reset_key": _reset_key(ra)}
    if (_shed_finite(used) and used >= 1.0) or wire_rejects(r, "7d") or wire_rejects(r, "5h") \
            or any(str(f.get("scope")) in ("5h", "7d") and _place_fact_live(f, r, now)
                   for f in (facts or [])):
        return "walled", dict(key, used_pct=round(100.0 * used, 2) if _shed_finite(used) else None)
    if r.get("stale_quota") or r.get("error"):
        return "unknown:level-stale", key
    if r.get("credits_on"):
        return "hold-credits", key
    wrh = _until(r, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h")
    if not _shed_finite(used) or not _shed_finite(wrh) or not (0 < wrh <= WEEK_H):
        return "unknown:no-reset", key
    ewma, flat = r.get("burn_wk_ewma_ph"), r.get("burn_wk_flat_ph")
    if not _shed_finite(ewma):
        return "unknown:pace-unknown", key
    if not _shed_finite(flat):
        return "unknown:pace-stale", key        # flat is None when the ledger is > 1 h old
    left = max(0.0, 100.0 * (1.0 - used))
    hi, lo = max(ewma, flat), min(ewma, flat)
    ttw_hi = left / hi if hi > 0 else math.inf
    ttw_lo = left / lo if lo > 0 else math.inf
    out = dict(key, used_pct=round(100.0 * used, 2), left_pp=round(left, 2), reset_h=round(wrh, 2),
               pace_hi=round(hi, 4), pace_lo=round(lo, 4),
               ttw_hi_h=None if ttw_hi == math.inf else round(ttw_hi, 2),
               ttw_lo_h=None if ttw_lo == math.inf else round(ttw_lo, 2))
    lv, tt, gh = (shed_knob(R, k) for k in ("SHED_LV_OPEN", "SHED_TTW_OPEN_H", "SHED_GUARD_H"))
    level = 100.0 * used >= lv
    if not (level or ttw_hi <= tt):
        return "out", out
    out["trip"] = "level" if level else "ttw"
    if not (lo > 0 and ttw_lo <= wrh - gh):
        return "band-guard", out
    return "band", out


def cmd_shed_band(args, cfg):
    """--shed-band --json [--facts DIR]: the D2b verdict per account, and the recovery-lane targets
    that are not themselves banded or walled. CACHE-ONLY (max_wait 0), dispatched before the
    get_data anchor like --place, so it never sweeps. Its own grace (CC_SHED_BAND_MAX_AGE_S,
    default 1200 s, review E8). Each account carries its cache and ledger age, so a blind reading
    can be told from an empty one (review E7), and its swept k_work, which the controller hands to
    --place. exit 0 answered · 3 WAIT_DATA (no servable cache)."""
    max_age = _cliff_env("CC_SHED_BAND_MAX_AGE_S", SHED_BAND_MAX_AGE_S)
    try:
        rows, wj, _c, _p = get_data(cfg, max_wait=0, max_age=max_age)
    except CacheOnlyUnavailable:
        print(json.dumps({"v": 1, "wait_data": True}))
        return PLACE_EXIT_WAIT_DATA
    facts = {}
    if "--facts" in args and args.index("--facts") + 1 < len(args):
        fd = args[args.index("--facts") + 1]
        facts = _place_read_facts(fd) if os.path.isdir(fd) else {}
    now = time.time()
    try:
        with open(cfg["cache_file"]) as f:
            cts = json.load(f).get("ts")
        cache_age = round(now - cts, 1) if _shed_finite(cts) else None
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError, KeyError):
        cache_age = None
    samples = _util_tail(hours=PLACE_BURN_LOOKBACK_H)[0]
    newest, kw_led = {}, {}
    for e in samples:
        a, t = e.get("acct"), e.get("_t")
        if _shed_finite(t) and t > newest.get(a, 0.0):
            newest[a] = t
            kw_led[a] = e.get("k_work")
    mark_self(rows, cfg)
    refresh_login_countdown(rows)
    apply_assignments(rows, cfg)
    apply_burn(rows, cfg, samples=samples)
    R, win = cfg["router"], win_from_json(wj)
    accts = []
    for r in rows:
        if r.get("acct") in (None, "none"):
            continue
        tok, f = shed_band_row(r, R, facts.get(r["acct"]), now)
        # The swept row's k_work, else the newest ledger sample's when that sample is inside this
        # read's own grace: a cache-only sweep leaves the row's null (measured on the live cache,
        # 2026-10-21, every account), and --place gives an unmeasured account no seats at all.
        age = now - newest[r["acct"]] if r["acct"] in newest else None
        kw = r.get("k_work")
        if not _is_num(kw) and age is not None and age <= max_age:
            kw = kw_led.get(r["acct"])
        accts.append(dict(f, acct=r["acct"], verdict=tok, cache_age_s=cache_age,
                          ledger_age_s=None if age is None else round(age, 1),
                          k_work=kw if _is_num(kw) else None))
    held = {a["acct"] for a in accts if a["verdict"] in ("band", "band-guard", "walled")}
    out, reasons = _ranked(rows, cfg, win, "general", recovery=True)
    print(json.dumps({"v": 1, "ts": now, "accounts": accts,
                      "targets": [r["acct"] for _s, r in out if r["acct"] not in held],
                      "excluded": reasons,
                      "knobs": {k: shed_knob(R, k) for k in _SHED_KEYS}}, sort_keys=True))
    return 0


def dispatch_claim_ttl_min(R):
    return _dispatch_knob("CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN", R, "DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN",
                          DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN_DEFAULT)


def dispatch_floor_row(r, R):
    """ONE derivation of a row's dispatch-floor verdict -> (token, spare_pp|None, need_pp|None).
    PURE: it reads what apply_burn and apply_assignments stamped and does no I/O. The partition,
    route-meta and the drain line all call this, so they cannot disagree.

    Ranks by score:  roomy         at/above the floor, or the floor is off
                     lifted        thin, and its forecast-dead quota covers one more new fire
    HELD (demoted):  flat          the rescue is switched off
                     level-stale   the weekly figure itself is inherited (stale_quota / error)
                     hold-credits  extra-usage is on: an overshoot is paid overage, not a wall
                     pace-unknown  no reset horizon, no nowcast or no EWMA to judge it on
                     pace-stale    no flat cross-check: ledger over 1h old or under 6.8h of span
                     on-pace       spare < need — it will use its week, or too little would die
                     claimed       spare covers a fire, but not one more than is already placed

    spare = min(wk_strand_pp, 100 x weekly_headroom - max(EWMA, flat pace) x hours to reset),
    floored at 0. Re-based on weekly_headroom because the nowcast counts from the endpoint integer
    and to 100, while the floor judges the wire figure against the credits target; the faster of
    the two paces because each is blind where the other is not (burn_wk_flat_ph).
    need = max(DISPATCH_STRAND_PP, DISPATCH_FIRE_BURN_PPH x horizon): a fire burns until the
    reset, so the further out the reset the more it needs. Weekly 96 / 13.6h / zero pace needs
    5.5pp and holds 4.0 — and `1.00 - 0.96` is 0.040000000000000036, which a bare 4.0 would pass.

    Every comparison is `not x >= y`, so a NaN that reaches one HOLDS. min() and max() DROP a NaN
    instead of propagating it, hence the explicit test before them."""
    floor, w = dispatch_w_floor(R), weekly_headroom(r)
    if floor <= 0.0 or w is None or w >= floor:
        return "roomy", None, None
    base = dispatch_strand_pp(R)
    if base is None:
        return "flat", None, None
    if r.get("stale_quota") or r.get("error"):
        return "level-stale", None, None
    if r.get("credits_on"):
        return "hold-credits", None, None
    # The RAW field, not horizon(): horizon() reads an unknown reset as far away, which is the
    # right fallback for a score and the wrong one for a product with a pace.
    wrh = r.get("weekly_reset_h")
    strand, ewma, flat = r.get("wk_strand_pp"), r.get("burn_wk_ewma_ph"), r.get("burn_wk_flat_ph")
    if not _is_num(wrh) or not (0 < wrh <= WEEK_H) or not _is_num(strand) or not _is_num(ewma):
        return "pace-unknown", None, None
    if not _is_num(flat):
        return "pace-stale", None, None
    need = max(base, dispatch_fire_burn_pph(R) * horizon(wrh, R))
    if strand != strand or ewma != ewma or flat != flat or w != w:
        return "on-pace", None, need
    spare = max(0.0, min(strand, 100.0 * w - max(ewma, flat) * wrh))
    if not spare >= need:
        return "on-pace", spare, need
    # A fire already placed here holds a claim on the same spare pp for DISPATCH_CLAIM_TTL_MIN,
    # long after ASSIGN_TTL_MIN stopped charging it as a phantom: the weekly meter is an integer
    # and has not moved yet. An absent count is 0 claims — the one input that does not fail
    # closed, and the reason promotion separately requires k_placed to be present.
    kp, kpl = r.get("k_phantom"), r.get("k_placed")
    kp, kpl = kp if _is_num(kp) else 0, kpl if _is_num(kpl) else 0
    if kp != kp or kpl != kpl or not spare >= need * (1 + max(kp, kpl, 0)):
        return "claimed", spare, need
    return "lifted", spare, need


def _dispatch_first(s, r, R, roomy):
    """May this LIFTED row go ahead of the roomy ones? All must hold, and each failure leaves it
    lifted into plain score order instead:
      - CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_STRAND_FIRST is on;
      - k_placed is on the row: the placement memory is what bounds a pile-on, so with it absent
        (CC_ROUTE_ASSIGN off, or a row that never went through apply_assignments) nothing would;
      - no login cliff in play — no drain, and no soft factor left on its score after the strand
        lift (_dispatch_cliff_factor: a stranding week whose reset precedes its drain point is
        not "in play"; post-mortem 2026-10-18 §4 item 3) — and 5h-cold — promotion must not carry
        a row past the soft terms that only its score expresses (10 of 258 rescued rows replayed
        above S_SOFT);
      - no roomy row both outscores it AND resets sooner: that one is the more urgent strand."""
    if not _term_on("CC_ROUTE_DISPATCH_STRAND_FIRST") or not _is_num(r.get("k_placed")):
        return False
    if cliff_band(r) == "drain" or _dispatch_cliff_factor(r) != 1.0 \
            or not _su_projected(r, R) < R["S_SOFT"]:
        return False
    t = horizon(effective_reset_h(r, R), R)
    return not any(s2 > s and t2 < t for s2, t2 in roomy)


def _dispatch_partition(out, R, promote):
    """Stable three-way partition of ranked (score, row) pairs: FIRST + KEEP + HELD.

    FIRST  lifted rows that may be promoted (`promote`, and _dispatch_first) — 'lifted-first'
    KEEP   roomy rows, and lifted rows that may not
    HELD   every other verdict, flagged thin_demoted
    Each group keeps its score order; no row is dropped and no score changes. The verdict is
    stamped on the row (`dispatch_floor`, `dispatch_spare_pp`) and CLEARED on every call: rows are
    shared across the lanes one readout ranks, so a flag left behind would speak for another."""
    if dispatch_w_floor(R) <= 0.0 or not out:
        return out
    judged = []
    for s, r in out:
        for key in ("thin_demoted", "dispatch_floor", "dispatch_spare_pp"):
            r.pop(key, None)
        tok, spare, _need = dispatch_floor_row(r, R)
        r["dispatch_floor"] = tok
        if spare is not None:
            r["dispatch_spare_pp"] = spare
        judged.append((s, r, tok))
    roomy = [(s, horizon(effective_reset_h(r, R), R)) for s, r, tok in judged if tok == "roomy"]
    first, keep, held = [], [], []
    for s, r, tok in judged:
        if tok == "lifted" and promote and _dispatch_first(s, r, R, roomy):
            r["dispatch_floor"] = "lifted-first"
            first.append((s, r))
        elif tok in ("roomy", "lifted"):
            keep.append((s, r))
        else:
            r["thin_demoted"] = True
            held.append((s, r))
    return first + keep + held


DISPATCH_FLOOR_NOTE = {
    "flat": "held for new work: under {floor:.0f}% of the week left",
    "level-stale": "held for new work: its quota reading is stale",
    "hold-credits": "held for new work: extra-usage credits are on",
    "pace-unknown": "held for new work: no pace reading yet",
    "pace-stale": "held for new work: its pace reading is stale",
    "claimed": "held for new work: a recent fire already claims that spare quota",
    "lifted": "open to new work",
    "lifted-first": "first in line for new work",
}


def dispatch_floor_note(r, rows, cfg):
    """soonest_reset_line's clause for a THIN row — why new fires skip it, or that they do not —
    or None when the floor has nothing to say. `rows` is the fleet, to answer "first in line" the
    way the general lane's partition does."""
    R = cfg["router"]
    tok, spare, need = dispatch_floor_row(r, R)
    if tok == "roomy":
        return None
    if tok == "on-pace":
        if spare is None or need is None:
            return "held for new work: on pace to use it"
        return (f"held for new work: {spare:.1f}pp would die unused, "
                f"a new task needs {need:.1f}")
    if tok == "lifted":
        s = score_general(r, cfg)[0]
        roomy = [(s2, horizon(effective_reset_h(x, R), R)) for x in rows
                 if x is not r and dispatch_floor_row(x, R)[0] == "roomy"
                 for s2 in [score_general(x, cfg)[0]] if s2]
        if s and _dispatch_first(s, r, R, roomy):
            tok = "lifted-first"
    return DISPATCH_FLOOR_NOTE[tok].format(floor=100.0 * dispatch_w_floor(R))


def ranked(rows, cfg, win, kind, recovery=False):
    """`_ranked` below, with the DISPATCH floor applied to the general and fable lanes. Since the
    strand rescue, line 1 of the general lane is NOT always the highest score: a promoted thin
    account leads on a lower one. The recycle re-pick compares line 1's score with the incumbent's
    (repick_ratio), so a promoted line 1 moves no live pane — the conservative direction."""
    out, reasons = _ranked(rows, cfg, win, kind, recovery=recovery)
    if kind in ("general", "fable") and not recovery:
        out = _dispatch_partition(out, cfg["router"], promote=(kind == "general"))
    return out, reasons


def _ranked(rows, cfg, win, kind, recovery=False):
    """Rank routable accounts, with the login-cliff term YIELDING rather than starving the fleet.

    🚨 The RECOVERY floors are NEVER yielded the way the cliff is, and the asymmetry is the whole
    argument: a cliff-drained account STILL WORKS, so refusing it is a preference; a THIN account
    does not survive the session being placed on it, so refusing it is a fact. If the floors empty
    the candidate set that is a real refusal — POLICY, exit 2 — and the caller must park loudly
    rather than fire blind.

    A cliff-drained account STILL WORKS — the cliff has not passed. So refusing to route to it is
    a PREFERENCE, and a preference must never be reported as "no account has headroom": the drain
    reason classifies as policy, policy maps to --route exit 2, and cc-route turns exit 2 into a
    fleet-wide QUOTA CLIFF (exit 4) that row 5 consumes as a hard stop. Left un-yielded, this term
    would convert a survivable, scheduled 4-account cliff calendar into a dispatch OUTAGE — the
    exact shape of the capacity gate that scored REFUSE against every sampled load.

    So: if the cliff term is what emptied the candidate set, re-rank without it and say so. The
    fleet degrades to pre-cliff-term behaviour under pressure, never below it.

    The override is recorded on the ROW (`cliff_yielded`), never as a synthetic entry in the
    reasons map: that map is account-keyed and its consumers count it (`route_line` renders
    "N excluded") and index it by account, so a non-account key would silently inflate a
    rendered count."""
    out, reasons = _rank_pass(rows, cfg, win, kind, True, recovery=recovery)
    if out or not any(w == CLIFF_DRAIN_REASON for w in reasons.values()):
        return out, reasons
    # 🚨 The INTERACTIVE lane never yields the cliff term. Yielding is right for dispatch — a
    # drained account still works, the cliff has not passed, and refusing it would turn a
    # scheduled 4-account cliff calendar into a fleet outage. It is wrong for a human: once the
    # refresh token expires, `invalid_grant` has NO reset to wait for, so a yielded pick hands the
    # operator a desk that dies on auth mid-session and whose only recovery is the interactive
    # /login this whole lane exists to keep them away from. Better to abstain and let the launcher
    # fall back to its pinned account, which the operator can at least reason about.
    if kind == "interactive":
        return out, reasons
    yielded, y_reasons = _rank_pass(rows, cfg, win, kind, False, recovery=recovery)
    if not yielded:
        # The cliff was NOT the binding constraint — something else would have excluded these rows
        # anyway. Report the reasons that actually BIND, never the drain that merely masked them.
        # This is not cosmetic: `_excluded` returns the drain before score_general can reach
        # "no-weekly-data", and the data-vs-policy split drives the exit code. Reporting the drain
        # here would turn a DATA-unavailable fleet (exit 3, callers may degrade to a proxy) into a
        # POLICY refusal (exit 2, callers must not fire blind) — the wrong verdict, in the
        # direction that hides an outage.
        return yielded, y_reasons
    for _s, r in yielded:
        if reasons.get(r["acct"]) == CLIFF_DRAIN_REASON:
            r["cliff_yielded"] = True
    return yielded, y_reasons


# Exclusion reasons meaning "we could not SEE the data", as opposed to "policy says no".
# This drives the --route/--rank exit code, which callers branch on: 3 = data unavailable
# (degrade to a proxy), 2 = data was fine and policy refused (do NOT fire blind). The old
# test — `all("error" in r for r in rows)` — keyed on a row field these reasons never set,
# so a fleet excluded purely for missing data reported as a policy QUOTA CLIFF.
# Deliberately EXCLUDES "no-fable-limit": a missing scoped limit is an entitlement fact, not
# a gap in our knowledge. Classifying it as data-unavailable would make cc-route hard-refuse
# (RT-d) instead of taking its designed Opus down-tier when no account carries Fable.
# "concurrency-unmeasured" belongs here for the same reason: it says the INSTRUMENT failed, not
# that policy refused. Classifying it as policy would turn an unreadable `ps` into rc 2, which
# handoff-fire.sh:5266-5269 turns into a hard HALT — a measurement failure would then read exactly
# like a fleet at capacity. As data it exits 3 and callers keep their designed degrade path.
DATA_UNAVAILABLE = {"no-session-data", "no-weekly-data", "window-unknown",
                    "concurrency-unmeasured"}


def reason_class(row, why):
    """-> 'data' | 'policy' for one row's exclusion (see DATA_UNAVAILABLE)."""
    return "data" if ("error" in row or why in DATA_UNAVAILABLE) else "policy"


# ---- shared cache (single-flight) -------------------------------------------------------------

def _cfg_key(cfg):
    # Cache entries are keyed to the config identity so a scratch/test CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON
    # sharing the default cache_file can neither poison real routing nor read real rows.
    ident = os.path.realpath(CFG_PATH) + "|" + ",".join(sorted(a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]))
    return sha256(ident.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]


BAD_AUTH = ("stale", "logged-out", "token-invalid", "keychain-error", "no-oauth-blob",
            "probe-error")


CACHE_SRC = ()     # accounts whose reset a served cache must not predate — set by main() from --src


def _cache_predates_reset(c, src, now=None):
    """(acct, window, reset_at) for the first SOURCE account whose 5h or weekly reset fell between
    the cache's sweep and now, else None. FLEET_V2 W6 resolution 11 (D1.3 router side).

    A cache is a picture of every account AS OF ITS SWEEP, and a reset is the one event that
    invalidates that picture wholesale for the account it hits: the source that read `5h-cutoff`
    a minute ago now has a fresh window. Measured 2026-09-30: the recovery lane's timeouts sat on
    the 06:30Z reset boundary, and a pre-reset cache moved a cohort OFF its source 5 s after the
    source had already rolled. The reset instants are read from the cached rows themselves (the
    ABSOLUTE stamps, never the as-of-the-sweep countdowns), so no caller has to supply them."""
    if not src:
        return None
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    ts = c.get("ts", 0)
    for r in c.get("rows", []):
        if not isinstance(r, dict) or r.get("acct") not in src:
            continue
        for window in ("session", "weekly"):
            at = r.get(f"{window}_reset_at")
            h = hrs_until(at)
            if h is None:
                continue
            t = now + h * 3600.0
            if ts < t <= now:
                return r["acct"], window, at
    return None


def cache_read(cfg, want_heal=False, grace_s=None, src=None):
    """grace_s overrides the TTL — used ONLY by the lock-degrade path, which prefers a
    slightly-expired sweep over blocking behind another caller's multi-minute one.

    `src` (default CACHE_SRC) names the accounts a caller is moving work OFF; a cache taken before
    any of their resets is unservable on EVERY path — TTL, grace and cache-only alike."""
    try:
        with open(cfg["cache_file"]) as f:
            c = json.load(f)
        # Valid JSON that is not an object (a stray `null`, `[]`, or a bare string written by
        # something else sharing /tmp) reached .get() and raised AttributeError, which the
        # handler below does not catch — every invocation then died until the file was
        # removed by hand. A malformed cache must degrade to "no cache", never wedge the tool.
        if not isinstance(c, dict):
            return None
        if c.get("cfg_key") != _cfg_key(cfg):
            return None
        ttl = grace_s if grace_s is not None else cfg.get("cache_ttl_s", 90)
        if time.time() - c.get("ts", 0) > ttl:
            return None
        # A no-heal sweep that saw auth trouble must not satisfy a heal-enabled caller —
        # that would defer the heal for the whole TTL and mislabel the state.
        if want_heal and c.get("no_heal") and any(
                (r.get("auth") in BAD_AUTH or "error" in r) for r in c.get("rows", [])):
            return None
        stale = _cache_predates_reset(c, CACHE_SRC if src is None else src)
        if stale:
            acct, window, at = stale
            msg = (f"cache from {datetime.fromtimestamp(c.get('ts', 0), timezone.utc).isoformat()} "
                   f"predates {acct}'s {window} reset at {at} — refused (a pre-reset rank would "
                   f"move work off a source that has already rolled)")
            log_event(f"cache_read: {msg}")
            print(f"claude-accounts: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
            return None
        return c
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        pass
    return None


# CC_UTIL_LOG: same override pool-floor.sh already honors — and a hermeticity requirement, not a
# convenience. apply_burn() made every CLI invocation a READER of this series, so a bats fixture
# with real account names was inheriting the REAL fleet's burn rates and flipping its own
# orderings (core suite test 26, first red 2026-08-10).
UTIL_PATH = os.environ.get("CC_UTIL_LOG") or os.path.join(
    HOME, ".claude", "logs", "account-utilization.jsonl")
UTIL_MIN_INTERVAL_S = 300


def record_utilization(rows, path=None, min_interval_s=UTIL_MIN_INTERVAL_S):
    """Append one row per account per LIVE sweep — the series nothing was keeping.

    Every live sweep already measures the four numbers a capacity question needs (per-account
    5h %, weekly %, Fable %, and k live sessions) and then throws them away: the /tmp cache is a
    single-slot overwrite carrying exactly ONE prior sample, and the last-good ledger is
    overwritten per account. So the box has produced this data continuously for months and
    retained a depth of one, which is why "how many concurrent sessions does the four-
    subscription pool sustain" has no answer that is not a guess.

    This costs NOTHING to collect. It rides the sweep that already happened — no extra network
    call, no extra quota, no new daemon — because the expensive part (four OAuth usage fetches)
    is already paid for by whoever asked. That is the whole design: the cheapest recorder is the
    one that stops discarding a measurement, not one that takes a new one.

    Only on the fresh-sweep path, and rate-limited to one batch per min_interval_s, so a desk
    hammering --fresh cannot turn this into its own growth problem (~4 rows / 5 min worst case).

    BLAST RADIUS: a side-car must fail no wider than itself. Every error is swallowed — this is
    a recorder bolted onto the tool the whole fleet routes with, and a full disk or a bad
    permission must degrade the SERIES, never break `claude-accounts --route`.
    """
    path = path or UTIL_PATH
    try:
        if time.time() - os.stat(path).st_mtime < min_interval_s:
            return 0
    except OSError:
        pass                                    # absent store: first write
    ts = datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()
    n = 0
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "a") as f:
            for r in rows:
                if r.get("session_pct") is None and r.get("weekly_pct") is None:
                    continue                    # a failed probe carries no measurement
                f.write(json.dumps({
                    "ts": ts, "acct": r.get("acct"), "k": r.get("k"),
                    # WHICH INSTRUMENT measured k, and the active count beside the resident one.
                    # Without these a replay cannot tell which CAP applied — k_cap is KMAX (8) for
                    # a work-charged row and KMAX_RESIDENT (40) for a pane-charged one, so the
                    # exclusion threshold differs 5x and `kmax-concurrency` becomes unattributable.
                    # That is not hypothetical: the W2 replay (2026-08-16) had to assume "work" for
                    # every row, which is the strict end, and so could not settle whether next4's
                    # 15pp strand was a real concurrency lockout or an artifact of that assumption.
                    # Free to record — same sweep, same row, both fields already on it.
                    "k_work": r.get("k_work"), "k_src": k_src(r),
                    # D2: the native census and its scan stamp, so a panes-native record replays
                    # as charged (scripts/desk-strand-replay.py) rather than as unmeasured.
                    "k_native": r.get("k_native"), "k_native_as_of": r.get("k_native_as_of"),
                    "session_pct": r.get("session_pct"), "weekly_pct": r.get("weekly_pct"),
                    "fable_pct": r.get("fable_pct"),
                    "session_reset_at": r.get("session_reset_at"),
                    "weekly_reset_at": r.get("weekly_reset_at"),
                    # THE WIRE, recorded beside the endpoint meters and NEVER in place of
                    # them: the replay tools key window-rolls on a meter going backwards, so a
                    # mid-series provenance switch would read as a reset. These are the fields
                    # that make "was that pinned 100 real?" answerable AFTER the fact, which is
                    # the question this series could not answer for its first 30,926 rows.
                    "wire_5h_util": ((r.get("wire") or {}).get("5h_util")),
                    "wire_7d_util": ((r.get("wire") or {}).get("7d_util")),
                    "wire_5h_status": ((r.get("wire") or {}).get("5h_status")),
                    "wire_7d_status": ((r.get("wire") or {}).get("7d_status")),
                    "credits_on": r.get("credits_on"), "credits_used": r.get("credits_used"),
                    "auth": r.get("auth"),
                    # inherited/errored rows are KEPT and flagged, never dropped: a gap in the
                    # series is indistinguishable from a quiet period, and the analysis needs to
                    # tell "we could not measure" from "nothing was happening".
                    # A reused first read (D6) is the local pass's sample, not a new one.
                    "stale": bool(r.get("stale_quota") or r.get("error") or r.get("quota_reused")),
                }, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n")
                n += 1
    except OSError:
        return 0
    return n


def cache_write(cfg, payload):
    tmp = cfg["cache_file"] + f".{os.getpid()}.tmp"
    try:
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(payload, f)
        os.replace(tmp, cfg["cache_file"])
    except OSError:
        pass


# ---- write coordination (docs/plans/ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS.md D4) ------------------------------
# Two processes now write the cache: the sweep (get_data) and the 20 s local pass
# (`--local-refresh`). Every cache and board writer holds `<cache_file>.wlock` for its critical
# section only — there is NO write-anyway bypass except the sweep's, after a wait longer than the
# local pass can live (its SIGALRM is 60 s), which is unreachable unless a holder survives that.
CACHE_WLOCK_SWEEP_S = 65.0


def _wlock_path(cfg):
    return cfg["cache_file"] + ".wlock"


def cache_commit(cfg, payload):
    """The sweep's cache write: under the wlock, re-apply the local overlay (D5 rules a-d, never f)
    to a COPY of the sweep's rows, write, and return the merged rows. get_data still hands its
    caller the sweep's own rows, so `--fresh`/`--json` stay pure measurement. `ts`, `window`,
    `prev`, `no_heal` and `cfg_key` are the sweep's, untouched (C5)."""
    fd = _flock_open(_wlock_path(cfg), CACHE_WLOCK_SWEEP_S)
    if fd is None:
        log_event(f"cache_commit: wlock wedged — not acquired in {CACHE_WLOCK_SWEEP_S:.0f}s "
                  "(the local pass's own alarm is 60 s); writing anyway")
    try:
        rows = payload["rows"]
        ov = load_local_overlay(cfg)
        if ov is not None:
            rows = json.loads(json.dumps(rows))
            apply_local_overlay(rows, ov, payload.get("ts"), writer="sweep")
        cache_write(cfg, dict(payload, rows=rows))
        return rows
    finally:
        _flock_release(fd)


def local_overlay_path(cfg):
    """`/tmp/claude-accounts-local.json` beside the cache — derived from cache_file exactly as
    board_path is, so a fixtured cache gets a fixtured overlay."""
    cf = cfg.get("cache_file")
    if not cf:
        return None                      # a config with no cache has no overlay (never the live one)
    return cf[:-len("-cache.json")] + "-local.json" if cf.endswith("-cache.json") else cf + ".local.json"


# ---- the local overlay (docs/plans/ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS.md D5) ------------------------------
# The 20 s local pass (`--local-refresh`) writes `/tmp/claude-accounts-local.json`; its facts reach
# the cache two ways: the pass's own CAS-guarded write, and cache_commit re-applying rules (a)-(d) to
# every sweep payload, so a sweep that read the keychain BEFORE a /login cannot revert the login.
# Every rule fires only when the overlay's stamp is newer than the row's matching stamp. It never
# touches `ts`, `window`, `prev`, `no_heal` or `cfg_key` (C5), nor `row["k"]` (C6).
LOCAL_OVERLAY_MAX_AGE_S = 600
PENDING_RE = re.compile(r"^quota pending — logged in \d\d:\d\d, first read not landed$")
QUOTA_FIELDS = ("session_pct", "session_reset_at", "weekly_pct", "weekly_reset_at", "fable_pct",
                "fable_reset_at", "credits_on", "credits_used", "credits_used_usd")
T2_FROM = ("logged-out", "no-oauth-blob")          # recover on any good verdict
T2_FROM_IF_NEW_GRANT = ("login-required", "token-invalid")   # only with a different refresh token


def _epoch_iso(t):
    return datetime.fromtimestamp(t, timezone.utc).isoformat() if t is not None else None


def _pending_marker(t):
    # Deliberately avoids `throttled` and `cached`: bin/cc-lr and lr-move-batch.sh string-match
    # those. Any errored row is data class (reason_class), so a pending row exits 3, never routes.
    return (f"quota pending — logged in {time.strftime('%H:%M', time.localtime(t))}, "
            "first read not landed")


def _read_json_obj(path):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
        return d if isinstance(d, dict) else None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def _write_json_atomic(path, obj, mode=0o644):
    tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.{threading.get_ident()}.tmp"
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path) or ".", exist_ok=True)
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, mode)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            json.dump(obj, f, separators=(",", ":"))
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        return True
    except OSError:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return False


def load_local_overlay(cfg, max_age_s=LOCAL_OVERLAY_MAX_AGE_S):
    """The local pass's overlay, or None when absent, for another config, or older than 600 s."""
    path = local_overlay_path(cfg)
    ov = _read_json_obj(path) if path else None
    if not ov or ov.get("v") != 1 or ov.get("cfg_key") != _cfg_key(cfg):
        return None
    ts = _as_epoch(ov.get("local_ts"))
    if ts is None or time.time() - ts > max_age_s:
        return None
    return ov


def _copy_login(r, au):
    for k in ("login_expires_at", "login_expired"):
        if k in au:
            r[k] = au[k]
    if au.get("login_expires_at"):
        r["login_expires_h"] = hrs_until(au["login_expires_at"])


def apply_local_overlay(rows, overlay, payload_ts, writer):
    """Apply the overlay's rules to `rows` in place -> [(acct, rule)] for what changed.

    (a) census: k_native/k_native_as_of (never k); a `strip` census removes them (the ps rollback).
    (b) T1: the keychain item is GONE -> logged-out, quota marked stale (dated by its real read).
    (c) T2: a logged-out/no-oauth-blob row, or a login-required/token-invalid row whose refresh
        token CHANGED, with a good local verdict -> recovered but PENDING: errored until a quota
        read lands, so it is never routed on pre-logout quota (C7).
    (d) T4: an ok row whose refresh token changed -> refresh the login_* fields.
    (e) a failed local observation is never in the overlay, so it changes nothing.
    (f) writer=="local" ONLY: a first read lands on a pending row, if it read THIS grant, is no
        older than the payload, and is no older than anything the row already holds."""
    out = []
    census = overlay.get("census") or {}
    auth = overlay.get("auth") or {}
    frs = overlay.get("first_read") or {}
    c_at = _as_epoch(census.get("as_of"))
    for r in rows:
        a = r.get("acct")
        touched = False
        if census.get("strip"):
            if r.pop("k_native", None) is not None or r.pop("k_native_as_of", None) is not None:
                out.append((a, "census-strip"))
        elif (census.get("counts") is not None and c_at is not None
              and c_at > (_as_epoch(r.get("k_native_as_of")) or 0)):
            r["k_native"] = census["counts"].get(a, 0)
            r["k_native_as_of"] = c_at
        au = auth.get(a)
        au_at = _as_epoch((au or {}).get("as_of"))
        if au and au_at is not None and au_at > (_as_epoch(r.get("auth_as_of")) or 0):
            ks, verdict, cur = au.get("kstate"), au.get("verdict"), r.get("auth")
            new_grant = bool(au.get("rt_fp")) and au.get("rt_fp") != r.get("rt_fp")
            if ks == "no-keychain-item" and cur != "logged-out":
                if not r.get("stale_quota"):
                    when = _as_epoch(r.get("quota_read_at")) or payload_ts
                    r["quota_as_of"] = _epoch_iso(when)
                r["auth"] = r["error"] = "logged-out"
                r["stale_quota"] = True
                r["auth_as_of"] = au_at
                out.append((a, "T1"))
                touched = True
            elif ks == "present" and verdict in ("ok", "stale") and (
                    cur in T2_FROM or (cur in T2_FROM_IF_NEW_GRANT and new_grant)):
                r["auth"] = verdict
                r["rt_fp"] = au.get("rt_fp")
                _copy_login(r, au)
                r["auth_recovered_at"] = au_at
                r["error"] = _pending_marker(au_at)
                r.pop("poll_throttled", None)        # a pending row is never throttle-admitted
                r["auth_as_of"] = au_at
                out.append((a, "T2"))
                touched = True
            elif ks == "present" and verdict in ("ok", "stale") and cur == "ok" and new_grant:
                r["rt_fp"] = au.get("rt_fp")
                _copy_login(r, au)
                r["auth_as_of"] = au_at
                out.append((a, "T4"))
                touched = True
        fr = frs.get(a)
        if (writer == "local" and fr and PENDING_RE.match(str(r.get("error") or ""))
                and fr.get("rt_fp") and fr.get("rt_fp") == r.get("rt_fp")):
            ra = _as_epoch(fr.get("read_at"))
            floor = max(_as_epoch(r.get("quota_read_at")) or 0, _as_epoch(r.get("quota_as_of")) or 0,
                        _as_epoch(r.get("wire_at")) or 0)
            if ra is not None and payload_ts is not None and ra >= payload_ts and ra >= floor:
                _land_quota(r, fr.get("fields") or {})
                r["quota_read_at"] = ra
                r["quota_src"] = "first-read"
                for k in ("stale_quota", "quota_as_of", "poll_throttled", "usage_held_s", "error"):
                    r.pop(k, None)
                for k in [k for k in r if k.startswith("lastgood_") and k != "lastgood_wire_rejects"]:
                    r.pop(k, None)
                out.append((a, "first-read"))
                touched = True
        if touched:
            r["auth_actionable"] = r.get("auth") in ACTIONABLE_AUTH
            r["login_fixable"] = r.get("auth") in LOGIN_FIXABLE
    return out


def _land_quota(r, fields):
    """Copy a stored quota read onto a row: re-derive each covered countdown from its absolute
    stamp, and drop the roll evidence for the buckets the read covers (it supersedes them)."""
    r.update(fields)
    covered = [p for p in ("session", "weekly", "fable") if f"{p}_reset_at" in fields]
    for p in covered:
        r[f"{p}_reset_h"] = hrs_until(fields.get(f"{p}_reset_at"))
    rolled, ev = r.get("rolled_since") or [], r.get("reset_evidence") or []
    keep = [i for i, p in enumerate(rolled) if p not in covered]
    if keep:
        r["rolled_since"] = [rolled[i] for i in keep]
        r["reset_evidence"] = [ev[i] for i in keep if i < len(ev)]
    else:
        r.pop("rolled_since", None)
        r.pop("reset_evidence", None)


def _fill_quota(row, u, cfg, name):
    """The HTTP-200 usage body -> quota fields on `row`. ONE implementation for the sweep's probe
    and the local pass's first read, so both paths are identical by construction."""
    sp, sr = pick(u, "session")
    wp, wr = pick(u, "weekly_all")
    fp, fr = pick(u, "weekly_scoped", cfg["frontier"]["scoped_display_name"])
    limits_drift(row, u, name)
    eu = u.get("extra_usage") or {}
    row.update({
        # session_reset_at is emitted alongside the *_reset_h countdown for the SAME reason
        # weekly/fable are: a countdown decays the moment it is serialized, so any consumer
        # rendering an absolute wall-clock time (the /accounts chat readout does) had to do
        # now + session_reset_h arithmetic itself. The absolute stamp is the durable form.
        "session_pct": sp, "session_reset_h": hrs_until(sr), "session_reset_at": sr,
        "weekly_pct": wp, "weekly_reset_h": hrs_until(wr), "weekly_reset_at": wr,
        "fable_pct": fp, "fable_reset_h": hrs_until(fr), "fable_reset_at": fr,
    })
    set_credits(row, eu.get("is_enabled"), eu.get("used_credits"))
    return sp, wp


def _ledger_entry(row, read_at):
    """A last-good ledger entry from a filled row, dated by WHEN the read happened."""
    return {
        "session_pct": row.get("session_pct"), "session_reset_at": row.get("session_reset_at"),
        "weekly_pct": row.get("weekly_pct"), "weekly_reset_at": row.get("weekly_reset_at"),
        "fable_pct": row.get("fable_pct"), "fable_reset_at": row.get("fable_reset_at"),
        "credits_on": bool(row.get("credits_on")), "credits_used": row.get("credits_used") or 0.0,
        "credits_used_usd": (row.get("credits_used") or 0.0) / 100.0,
        "quota_as_of": _epoch_iso(read_at),
    }


# ---- the first read after a login (D6) -----------------------------------------------------------
FIRST_READ_MIN_GAP_S = 90.0
FIRST_READ_MAX_GAP_S = 600.0
REPOLL_MIN_S_DEFAULT = 90.0


def _repoll_min_s():
    try:
        return float(os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_REPOLL_MIN_S", REPOLL_MIN_S_DEFAULT))
    except ValueError:
        return REPOLL_MIN_S_DEFAULT


def _at_fp(token):
    return sha256(token.encode()).hexdigest()[:16] if token else None


def first_read(cfg, name, token, rt_fp, state, overlay):
    """ONE OAuth usage GET for a just-logged-in account -> outcome string. Never a /v1/messages
    call (C3), never heal, never a recorder. Gates in order: the kill switch; an attempt memo keyed
    on the access token (>= 90 s between attempts, doubling after a non-200, capped at 600 s); the
    per-account poll lock, non-blocking. The shared 429 backoff is NOT a gate: the new token may
    not have earned it (the budget is per token)."""
    if (os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_FIRST_READ") == "off"
            or os.path.exists(os.path.join(_accounts_local_dir(), "first-read-off"))):
        return "off"
    memo = state.setdefault("fr_memo", {})
    key = _at_fp(token)
    m = memo.get(key) or {}
    now = time.time()
    if now - (m.get("at") or 0) < (m.get("wait") or 0):
        return "memo"
    lp = _poll_lock_path(cfg, name)
    fd = _flock_open(lp, 0) if lp else None
    if lp and fd is None:
        return "poll-locked"
    try:
        read_at = time.time()
        status, u = fetch_usage(cfg, token, retries=0)
    finally:
        _flock_release(fd)
    if status == 200 and u and "limits" in u:
        row = {}
        _fill_quota(row, u, cfg, name)
        save_lastgood({name: _ledger_entry(row, read_at)})
        overlay.setdefault("first_read", {})[name] = {
            "read_at": read_at, "rt_fp": rt_fp,
            "fields": {k: row[k] for k in QUOTA_FIELDS if k in row}}
        note_usage_ok(name)
        memo[key] = {"at": now, "wait": FIRST_READ_MIN_GAP_S}
        return "200"
    if status == 429:
        note_usage_429(name)
    prev_wait = m.get("wait")
    memo[key] = {"at": now, "wait": (min(FIRST_READ_MAX_GAP_S, prev_wait * 2) if prev_wait
                                     else FIRST_READ_MIN_GAP_S)}
    return str(status)


# ---- `claude-accounts --local-refresh` (D7) ------------------------------------------------------
LOCAL_DEADLINE_S_DEFAULT = 60
LOCAL_KC_DEADLINE_S = 8.0
LOCAL_ATTR_REUSE_S = 300
# An unchanged census is re-committed once the cached stamp is older than this. It sits BELOW the
# 20 s tick on purpose: every pass then refreshes `k_native_as_of`, so the router's count is never
# older than one tick plus a pass. Measured live 2026-10-12 against DoD 15's 60 s bound: 120 (D7 step
# 3) reached 118 s, and 40 still reached 74 s (a strict `> 40` skips two 20 s ticks, then the pass
# itself takes seconds). Cost: one ~4-row CAS write per pass. A same-pass re-run (age < 15 s) skips.
LOCAL_CENSUS_RECOMMIT_S = 15
LOCAL_MIN_BUDGET_S = 15
LOCAL_HEARTBEAT_S = 600
LOCAL_WLOCK_WAIT_S = 2.0


def _local_stamp_path():
    return (os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_LOCAL_STAMP")
            or os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "logs", "accounts-local.stamp"))


def _keychain_db_path():
    return (os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_KEYCHAIN_DB")
            or os.path.join(HOME, "Library", "Keychains", "login.keychain-db"))


def _touch(path):
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "a"):
            pass
        os.utime(path, None)
    except OSError:
        pass


def _kc_ident():
    try:
        st = os.stat(_keychain_db_path())
        return [st.st_ino, st.st_mtime_ns]
    except OSError:
        return None


_RE_KC_DATE = {k: re.compile(r'"%s"<timedate>=\S*\s+"(\d{14}Z)' % k) for k in ("cdat", "mdat")}


def _kc_attrs(svc, kc):
    """Tier 1: the item's ATTRIBUTES only -> ("absent", None) | ("present", {cdat, mdat}) |
    ("error", reason). NO `-w`, never `-g`: no secret is read here."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["/usr/bin/security", "find-generic-password", "-s", svc, "-a", kc],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=LOCAL_KC_DEADLINE_S)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return "error", "timeout"
    except OSError as e:
        return "error", type(e).__name__
    if p.returncode == 44:
        return "absent", None
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return "error", f"rc={p.returncode}"
    got = {k: rx.search(p.stdout) for k, rx in _RE_KC_DATE.items()}
    if not all(got.values()):
        return "error", "unparsed"
    return "present", {k: m.group(1) for k, m in got.items()}


def _bounded_parallel(fns, deadline_s):
    """{key: fn} run on daemon threads joined to ONE deadline -> {key: result}. A key whose fn did
    not finish is absent: a timeout observation, retried on the next pass (R6)."""
    out = {}

    def run(k, fn):
        try:
            out[k] = fn()
        except Exception as e:                   # an observation failure, never a crash
            out[k] = ("error", type(e).__name__)
    ths = [threading.Thread(target=run, args=(k, fn), daemon=True) for k, fn in fns.items()]
    for t in ths:
        t.start()
    end = time.monotonic() + deadline_s
    for t in ths:
        t.join(max(0.0, end - time.monotonic()))
    return dict(out)


def _auth_verdict(it, as_of):
    """Stored item metadata -> the overlay's auth entry, with stale/expired re-derived at NOW."""
    if not it.get("present"):
        return {"as_of": as_of, "kstate": "no-keychain-item", "verdict": "logged-out"}
    if not it.get("has_creds"):
        return {"as_of": as_of, "kstate": "present", "verdict": "no-oauth-blob"}
    stale = (it.get("expiresAt") or 0) / 1000.0 <= time.time() + 60
    d = {"as_of": as_of, "kstate": "present", "verdict": "stale" if stale else "ok",
         "rt_fp": it.get("rt_fp")}
    rte = it.get("refreshTokenExpiresAt")
    if _iso_ms(rte):
        d["login_expires_at"] = _iso_ms(rte)
        d["login_expired"] = bool(_expired_ms(rte))
    # Mirror probe_account: past the cliff, or stale with no refresh token, is a dead login.
    if d.get("login_expired") or (stale and not it.get("has_rt")):
        d["verdict"] = "login-required"
    return d


def _creds_item(creds, t, cdat=None, mdat=None, src="keychain"):
    """A present item's stored metadata (never the secret itself) -> the dict _auth_verdict reads."""
    creds = creds or {}
    rt, tok = creds.get("refreshToken"), creds.get("accessToken")
    return {"present": True, "src": src, "cdat": cdat, "mdat": mdat, "has_creds": bool(creds),
            "expiresAt": creds.get("expiresAt"),
            "refreshTokenExpiresAt": creds.get("refreshTokenExpiresAt"),
            "has_rt": bool(rt), "rt_fp": _rt_fingerprint(rt), "at_fp": _at_fp(tok),
            "checked_at": t}


def _file_item(a, t):
    """A MISSING keychain item -> (stored item, access token) from <config_dir>/.credentials.json,
    read-only via read_creds_file (the sweep's own fallback, so both writers agree), or
    (None, None) when the file is unreadable/corrupt — an observation error, retried next pass."""
    creds, st = read_creds_file(a["config_dir"])
    if st == "keychain-error":
        return None, None
    if creds is None:
        return {"present": False, "checked_at": t}, None
    return _creds_item(creds, t, src="file"), creds.get("accessToken")


def _local_auth(cfg, state, overlay, tokens, ident):
    """Step 4: refresh the overlay's auth entries. Tier 1 reads attributes (4 reads, 8 s); tier 2
    reads a secret only for an item that appeared or whose cdat/mdat moved. An unchanged keychain
    file inside 300 s reuses the stored metadata. Identity advances on DEFINITIVE results only.

    A MISSING item consults .credentials.json (decision 46fdb85e2f23), and is re-read on EVERY
    pass, reuse window included: the file is local and cheap, and the keychain identity that keys
    the reuse does not move when CC rewrites or removes that file."""
    items = state.setdefault("items", {})
    kc = cfg["keychain_account"]
    now = time.time()
    errors = []

    def absent(a, t):
        it, tok = _file_item(a, t)
        if it is None:
            errors.append(f"{a['name']}:credentials-file")
            return
        items[a["name"]] = it
        if tok:
            tokens[a["name"]] = tok
        overlay["auth"][a["name"]] = _auth_verdict(it, t)

    if (ident is not None and ident == state.get("kc_ident")
            and now - (state.get("last_attr_check") or 0) < LOCAL_ATTR_REUSE_S):
        for a in cfg["accounts"]:
            it = items.get(a["name"])
            if it is not None and (not it.get("present") or it.get("src") == "file"):
                absent(a, now)
            elif it is not None:
                overlay["auth"][a["name"]] = _auth_verdict(it, now)
        if errors:
            state["last_attr_check"] = 0
        return errors
    attrs = _bounded_parallel(
        {a["name"]: (lambda a=a: _kc_attrs(keychain_service(a["config_dir"]), kc))
         for a in cfg["accounts"]}, LOCAL_KC_DEADLINE_S)
    t1, need = time.time(), []
    for a in cfg["accounts"]:
        name, r = a["name"], attrs.get(a["name"])
        if r is None or r[0] == "error":
            errors.append(f"{name}:{'timeout' if r is None else r[1]}")
            continue
        prev = items.get(name) or {}
        if r[0] == "absent":
            absent(a, t1)
        elif (prev.get("present") and "has_creds" in prev and prev.get("cdat") == r[1]["cdat"]
              and prev.get("mdat") == r[1]["mdat"]):
            prev["checked_at"] = t1
            overlay["auth"][name] = _auth_verdict(prev, t1)
        else:
            need.append((a, r[1]))
    if need:
        secrets = _bounded_parallel({a["name"]: (lambda a=a: read_creds(a["config_dir"], kc))
                                     for a, _ in need}, LOCAL_KC_DEADLINE_S)
        t2 = time.time()
        for a, at in need:
            name, r = a["name"], secrets.get(a["name"])
            if r is None or r[1] not in ("present", "no-keychain-item"):
                errors.append(f"{name}:secret-{'timeout' if r is None else r[1]}")
                continue
            creds, kstate = r
            if kstate == "no-keychain-item":
                items[name] = {"present": False, "checked_at": t2}
            elif creds_source(a["config_dir"]) == "file":
                # The item vanished between tier 1 and tier 2 and read_creds fell back to the file:
                # store it as file-sourced (no cdat/mdat), so the next pass re-reads, not reuses.
                items[name] = _creds_item(creds, t2, src="file")
            else:
                items[name] = _creds_item(creds, t2, at["cdat"], at["mdat"])
            tok = (creds or {}).get("accessToken")
            if tok:
                tokens[name] = tok
            overlay["auth"][name] = _auth_verdict(items[name], t2)
    if not errors:
        state["kc_ident"], state["last_attr_check"] = ident, now
    else:
        state["last_attr_check"] = 0               # a failed read is retried next pass
    return errors


def _read_cache_any_age(cfg):
    """The cache TTL-IGNORED and cfg-key-checked (the read _prev_snapshot does), or None."""
    c = _read_json_obj(cfg["cache_file"])
    return c if c and c.get("cfg_key") == _cfg_key(cfg) and isinstance(c.get("rows"), list) else None


def _local_commit(cfg, overlay):
    """Step 7: the pass's own cache write -> [(acct, rule)] applied, or None when skipped. Under the
    wlock (2 s, else skip), re-read, apply (writer=local), CAS on the file identity, replace with
    ts/window/prev/no_heal/cfg_key exactly as read. Never creates a cache."""
    fd = _flock_open(_wlock_path(cfg), LOCAL_WLOCK_WAIT_S)
    if fd is None:
        log_event("local: wlock busy past 2 s — cache write skipped this pass")
        return None
    try:
        path = cfg["cache_file"]
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                st = os.fstat(f.fileno())
                c = json.load(f)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return None
        if not isinstance(c, dict) or c.get("cfg_key") != _cfg_key(cfg) \
                or not isinstance(c.get("rows"), list):
            return None
        applied = apply_local_overlay(c["rows"], overlay, _as_epoch(c.get("ts")), writer="local")
        blob = json.dumps(c)
        try:
            st2 = os.stat(path)
        except OSError:
            return None
        if (st2.st_ino, st2.st_mtime_ns) != (st.st_ino, st.st_mtime_ns):
            log_event("local: cache changed under the wlock (a writer without it) — CAS abort")
            return None
        tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.local.tmp"
        try:
            with open(tmp, "w") as f:
                f.write(blob)
            os.replace(tmp, path)
        except OSError:
            return None
        return applied
    finally:
        _flock_release(fd)


def _census_commit_wanted(cache, census):
    rows = cache.get("rows") or []
    if census.get("strip"):
        return any("k_native" in r for r in rows)
    counts = census.get("counts")
    if counts is None:
        return False
    for r in rows:
        if r.get("k_native") != counts.get(r.get("acct"), 0):
            return True
        if time.time() - (_as_epoch(r.get("k_native_as_of")) or 0) > LOCAL_CENSUS_RECOMMIT_S:
            return True
    return False


def local_refresh(cfg, t0=None):
    """`--local-refresh`: one pass of the 20 s local job. See D7 for the twelve steps. Never calls
    heal, fetch_wire_limits, record_utilization, watch_resets, log_route_decision or ps, and never
    takes the sweep's single-flight lock."""
    t0 = time.monotonic() if t0 is None else t0
    stamp = _local_stamp_path()
    try:
        deadline = float(os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_LOCAL_DEADLINE_S", LOCAL_DEADLINE_S_DEFAULT))
    except ValueError:
        deadline = LOCAL_DEADLINE_S_DEFAULT

    def budget():
        return deadline - (time.monotonic() - t0)
    lock = _flock_open(cfg["cache_file"] + ".local.lock", 0)
    if lock is None:
        _touch(stamp)
        print("local: busy")
        return 0
    # SIG_DFL: the alarm KILLS the pass. Every write is atomic, so a killed pass leaves nothing
    # torn, and the 65 s sweep-side wlock bound is sized against exactly this.
    signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, signal.SIG_DFL)
    signal.alarm(max(1, int(deadline)))
    lines = []
    try:
        state_path = _sidecar(".local-state.json")
        state = _read_json_obj(state_path) or {}
        key = _cfg_key(cfg)
        old = _read_json_obj(local_overlay_path(cfg)) or {}
        if old.get("cfg_key") != key:
            old = {}
        ov = {"v": 1, "cfg_key": key, "local_ts": time.time(), "census": old.get("census") or {},
              "auth": old.get("auth") or {}, "first_read": old.get("first_read") or {}}
        # 3. census first, written before any keychain work (R6: a stalled keychain cannot hold it)
        if _census_mode(cfg) == "ps":
            ov["census"] = {"as_of": time.time(), "strip": True}
        else:
            n = native_census(cfg)
            meta = _NATIVE_META or {}
            ov["census"] = {"as_of": meta.get("as_of") or time.time(),
                            "counts": dict(n) if n is not None else None,
                            "headless": getattr(n, "headless", None), "ms": meta.get("ms"),
                            "reason": meta.get("reason")}
            if n is None:
                lines.append(f"local: census unmeasured ({meta.get('reason')})")
        _write_json_atomic(local_overlay_path(cfg), ov)
        cache = None
        for rnd in (0, 1):
            ident = _kc_ident()
            tokens = {}
            errs = _local_auth(cfg, state, ov, tokens, ident)
            if errs:
                lines.append("local: keychain " + " ".join(errs))
            ov["local_ts"] = time.time()
            cache = _read_cache_any_age(cfg)                         # 5
            if cache is None:
                _write_json_atomic(local_overlay_path(cfg), ov)
                break
            preview = apply_local_overlay(json.loads(json.dumps(cache["rows"])), ov,
                                          _as_epoch(cache.get("ts")), writer="local")
            _write_json_atomic(local_overlay_path(cfg), ov)           # 6
            if preview or _census_commit_wanted(cache, ov["census"]):
                applied = _local_commit(cfg, ov)                      # 7
                for a, rule in applied or ():
                    if rule != "census-strip":
                        lines.append(f"local: {rule} {a}")
            # 8. the first read, for pending rows whose local verdict is ok
            cache = _read_cache_any_age(cfg) or cache
            for r in cache.get("rows") or []:
                a = r.get("acct")
                au = ov["auth"].get(a) or {}
                if not PENDING_RE.match(str(r.get("error") or "")) or au.get("verdict") != "ok":
                    continue
                if budget() < LOCAL_MIN_BUDGET_S:
                    lines.append(f"local: first-read {a} skipped (budget)")
                    continue
                tok = tokens.get(a)
                if tok is None:
                    acct = next((x for x in cfg["accounts"] if x["name"] == a), None)
                    got = _bounded_parallel(
                        {a: lambda: read_creds(acct["config_dir"], cfg["keychain_account"])},
                        LOCAL_KC_DEADLINE_S).get(a) if acct else None
                    if got and got[1] == "present":
                        tok = (got[0] or {}).get("accessToken")
                if not tok:
                    continue
                res = first_read(cfg, a, tok, au.get("rt_fp"), state, ov)
                if res not in ("memo", "off"):
                    lines.append(f"local: first-read {a} {res}")
                if res == "200":
                    _write_json_atomic(local_overlay_path(cfg), ov)
                    for a2, rule in _local_commit(cfg, ov) or ():
                        if rule == "first-read":
                            lines.append(f"local: routable {a2} (first read landed)")
            # 9. prune first reads a later real read has superseded, or older than 600 s
            cache = _read_cache_any_age(cfg) or cache
            by = {r.get("acct"): r for r in cache.get("rows") or []}
            for a, fr in list(ov["first_read"].items()):
                ra = _as_epoch(fr.get("read_at")) or 0
                if ((_as_epoch((by.get(a) or {}).get("quota_read_at")) or 0) > ra
                        or time.time() - ra > LOCAL_OVERLAY_MAX_AGE_S):
                    del ov["first_read"][a]
            _write_json_atomic(local_overlay_path(cfg), ov)
            # 11. launchd drops a WatchPaths fire that lands during a tick: look once more
            if rnd == 0 and _kc_ident() != ident and budget() >= LOCAL_MIN_BUDGET_S:
                continue
            break
        # 10. the board (D8)
        if cache is not None and budget() >= LOCAL_MIN_BUDGET_S:
            msg = _local_board(cfg, state)
            if msg:
                lines.append(msg)
        memo = state.get("fr_memo") or {}
        state["fr_memo"] = {k: v for k, v in memo.items() if time.time() - (v.get("at") or 0) < 86400}
        if not lines and time.time() - (state.get("heartbeat_at") or 0) >= LOCAL_HEARTBEAT_S:
            c = ov.get("census") or {}
            lines.append("local: ok · census " + (
                "ps" if c.get("strip") else
                ",".join(f"{k}:{v}" for k, v in (c.get("counts") or {}).items()) or "unmeasured"))
        if lines:
            state["heartbeat_at"] = time.time()
        _write_json_atomic(state_path, state, mode=0o600)
    finally:
        signal.alarm(0)
        _touch(stamp)
        _flock_release(lock)
    for ln in lines:
        print(ln)
    sys.stdout.flush()
    return 0


def local_refresh_main():
    global _UTIL_MEMO_ON
    _UTIL_MEMO_ON = True
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    ld = _accounts_local_dir()
    try:
        os.makedirs(ld, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError:
        pass
    if os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_LOCAL") == "off" or os.path.exists(os.path.join(ld, "off")):
        _touch(_local_stamp_path())
        print("local: disabled")
        return 0
    return local_refresh(load_cfg(need_claude_bin=False), t0)


def board_path(cfg):
    """Where the pre-rendered SessionStart board lives.

    DERIVED FROM cache_file, not a second independent constant, and for the same reason cache
    entries are keyed by `_cfg_key`: a fixtured `CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS_JSON` pointing at a scratch cache
    must get a scratch board too, or a test run would overwrite the live operator's board with
    fixture accounts — and the board is a surface nobody re-reads adversarially, so that would be
    believed. `CC_ACCOUNTS_BOARD` overrides both, and is the seam the hook's tests fixture: the
    hook must be able to read a board this producer wrote WITHOUT either of them touching
    accounts.json, because the hook is forbidden from loading config at all (it may not fork this
    program — see hooks/accounts-board.sh).
    """
    env = os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_BOARD")
    if env:
        return env
    if cfg.get("board_file"):
        return os.path.expanduser(cfg["board_file"])
    cf = cfg.get("cache_file") or "/tmp/claude-accounts-cache.json"
    return cf[:-len("-cache.json")] + "-board.txt" if cf.endswith("-cache.json") else cf + ".board.txt"


BOARD_WLOCK_WAIT_S = 5.0


def write_board(cfg, lines, sig=None, keep_mtime=False, rendered_from_ts=None, producer=None,
                variants=None, ladder=None):
    """Atomically publish the rendered board. Returns the path, or None on failure.

    os.replace, never a truncate-then-write: the reader is a SessionStart hook that may fire at
    any instant, including the microseconds this producer is mid-write. A torn board is worse
    than a stale one — stale is labelled by the hook's age check, whereas a half-written table
    looks like a complete table with fewer accounts in it.

    Two producers now write it (keepwarm and the local pass, ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D8), so the
    write holds the cache wlock (5 s, else skip and log) and records `<board>.meta.json`
    {sig, rendered_from_ts, producer, at} — which the hook never reads. `keep_mtime` is the local
    re-render: the board's mtime is the hook's PRODUCER-LIVENESS signal (accounts-board.sh), so a
    re-render keeps the existing mtime, never creates a missing board, and aborts if the cache
    moved on since it rendered — a dead keepwarm still reads STALE exactly as before.

    `variants` ({width: lines}, _board_variants) are written beside it as `<board>.w<width>`, each
    atomically and with the board's own mtime: the hook accepts a variant only when its mtime
    matches the board's, so its age label stays true of whichever one it prints.

    `ladder` (_board_ladder) goes beside it as `<board>.ladder.json`, the same way: atomic, board
    mtime. hooks/accounts-board.sh hands it to mods/accounts-readout only when that mtime matches.
    """
    path = board_path(cfg)
    tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
    fd = _flock_open(_wlock_path(cfg), BOARD_WLOCK_WAIT_S)
    if fd is None:
        log_event(f"board: wlock busy past {BOARD_WLOCK_WAIT_S:.0f}s — board write skipped "
                  f"({producer or 'board'})")
        return None
    try:
        st = None
        if keep_mtime:
            try:
                st = os.stat(path)
            except OSError:
                return None
            if (_read_json_obj(cfg["cache_file"]) or {}).get("ts") != rendered_from_ts:
                return None
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            f.write("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
        if st is not None:
            os.utime(tmp, ns=(st.st_atime_ns, st.st_mtime_ns))
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        bst = os.stat(path)
        for n, vl in (variants or {}).items():
            vtmp = f"{path}.w{n}.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
            with open(vtmp, "w") as f:
                f.write("\n".join(vl) + "\n")
            os.utime(vtmp, ns=(bst.st_atime_ns, bst.st_mtime_ns))
            os.replace(vtmp, f"{path}.w{n}")
        if ladder is not None:
            ltmp = f"{path}.ladder.json.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
            with open(ltmp, "w") as f:
                json.dump(ladder, f, ensure_ascii=False)
            os.utime(ltmp, ns=(bst.st_atime_ns, bst.st_mtime_ns))
            os.replace(ltmp, path + ".ladder.json")
        _write_json_atomic(path + ".meta.json", {"sig": sig, "rendered_from_ts": rendered_from_ts,
                                                 "producer": producer, "at": time.time()})
        return path
    except OSError:
        # A producer that cannot write the board must not take the CACHE sweep down with it: the
        # cache is what the interactive launch path depends on, the board is a convenience. The
        # hook reports the resulting absence itself, so this failure is visible without an alarm
        # here that only launchd's log would ever see.
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return None
    finally:
        _flock_release(fd)


def _board_sig(rows):
    """What a board row shows that the local pass can change: auth, errored, live count."""
    return [[r.get("acct"), r.get("auth"), "error" in r, k_shown(r)] for r in rows]


def _render_board(cfg, rows, wj, cached, quota_ts=None):
    """The narrow board from cache rows, on a COPY: k_phantom and burn_* are render-time
    decorations and must never reach the cache."""
    rows = json.loads(json.dumps(rows))
    refresh_login_countdown(rows)
    apply_assignments(rows, cfg)
    apply_burn(rows, cfg)
    return readout_lines(rows, cfg, win_from_json(wj), cached, narrow=True, quota_ts=quota_ts)


# THE BOARD'S NARROW VARIANTS (2026-10-18). The board above is laid out for 76 cells, and a
# SessionStart systemMessage gets pane − 5, so below an 81-column pane every one of its rows wrapped
# mid-cell (operator screenshot, ~55 columns). The producer has no pane to measure — it is a launchd
# job — so it renders the same rows once per width on this ladder, with the pane presentation
# (`readout_lines(pane=N)`, the layout `/accounts` draws into a pane), and hooks/accounts-board.sh
# picks the widest variant that fits the pane it is drawing into. 35 is a 40-column pane; 70 the
# last step below the board itself. One renderer, laid out N ways: no fact is re-derived here.
# 12-30 (2026-10-22) follow the pane ladder to its floor: a phone over SSH or a thin split is a
# 17-25-column pane, and below 35 the nearest variant used to be the 35-cell one, wrapped.
BOARD_LADDER = (12, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 55, 60, 65, 70)


def _board_variants(cfg, rows, wj, cached, quota_ts=None):
    """{width: lines} for every width on BOARD_LADDER, from a COPY of the rows (as _render_board)."""
    rows = json.loads(json.dumps(rows))
    refresh_login_countdown(rows)
    apply_assignments(rows, cfg)
    apply_burn(rows, cfg)
    win = win_from_json(wj)
    out = {}
    for n in BOARD_LADDER:
        try:
            out[n] = readout_lines(rows, cfg, win, cached, quota_ts=quota_ts, pane=n)
        except Exception:   # noqa: BLE001 — a missing variant costs that width, never the board
            pass
    return out


# THE BOARD'S WIDTH LADDER (2026-10-21). The variants above are static text too: a pane that
# started at ~50 columns and was widened to ~115 kept its ~45-cell board (operator screenshot).
# Claude Code has no render component for a SessionStart hook message, so mods/accounts-readout
# draws the board as a band above the prompt instead and re-lays it out at every width change; this
# is what it draws from, `pane_ladder` over the same rows (one renderer, no new layout logic).
# docs/research/accounts-board-band-2026-10-21/. ~2 s of a keepwarm tick at load ~130, measured.
def _board_ladder(cfg, rows, wj, cached, quota_ts=None):
    """pane_ladder over a COPY of the rows (as _board_variants), or None: a missing ladder costs
    the band (the hook then prints its static board), never the board."""
    try:
        rows = json.loads(json.dumps(rows))
        refresh_login_countdown(rows)
        apply_assignments(rows, cfg)
        apply_burn(rows, cfg)
        win = win_from_json(wj)
        return pane_ladder(lambda n: readout_lines(rows, cfg, win, cached, quota_ts=quota_ts, pane=n))
    except Exception:   # noqa: BLE001
        return None


LOCAL_K_RENDER_MIN_S = 60
LOCAL_K_RENDER_MAX_LOAD_PER_CPU = 10.0


def _local_board(cfg, state):
    """D8: re-render the board when the cache's signature differs from the board's. An auth or
    error difference re-renders at once; a live-count-only difference at most once per 60 s, and
    only when load/cpu <= 10. Returns a `local: …` line when it rendered, else None."""
    c = _read_cache_any_age(cfg)
    path = board_path(cfg)
    if not c or not os.path.exists(path):
        return None
    sig = _board_sig(c["rows"])
    old = (_read_json_obj(path + ".meta.json") or {}).get("sig")
    if old == sig:
        return None
    auth_diff = not old or [x[:3] for x in old] != [x[:3] for x in sig]
    if not auth_diff:
        if time.time() - (state.get("last_k_render") or 0) < LOCAL_K_RENDER_MIN_S:
            return None
        if os.getloadavg()[0] / (os.cpu_count() or 1) > LOCAL_K_RENDER_MAX_LOAD_PER_CPU:
            return None
    lines = _render_board(cfg, c["rows"], c.get("window") or {}, True, quota_ts=c.get("ts"))
    if write_board(cfg, lines, sig=sig, keep_mtime=True, rendered_from_ts=c.get("ts"),
                   producer="local",
                   variants=_board_variants(cfg, c["rows"], c.get("window") or {}, True,
                                            quota_ts=c.get("ts")),
                   ladder=_board_ladder(cfg, c["rows"], c.get("window") or {}, True,
                                        quota_ts=c.get("ts"))) is None:
        return None
    if not auth_diff:
        state["last_k_render"] = time.time()
    return f"local: board re-rendered ({'auth' if auth_diff else 'live counts'})"


def _prev_snapshot(cfg):
    """Last sweep's percents (TTL-ignored, cfg-key-checked) — feeds the burn-delta display."""
    try:
        with open(cfg["cache_file"]) as f:
            old = json.load(f)
        if old.get("cfg_key") != _cfg_key(cfg):
            return None
        # quota_as_of/stale_quota ride along so inherit_lastgood can carry provenance forward
        # instead of re-dating inherited numbers to "now" on every sweep. The burn-delta loop
        # (the only other consumer) reads a fixed field tuple and ignores the extra keys.
        # k/k_stale/k_stale_as_of are inherit_k's input: the pane census this sweep may not be
        # able to take, plus the stamp that bounds how long it may be carried. THIS FUNCTION
        # IGNORES THE TTL — that is why the age bound lives in inherit_k and is measured against
        # the stamp, never against "there was a snapshot".
        return {"ts": old.get("ts"),
                "rows": {r["acct"]: {k: r.get(k) for k in
                                     ("session_pct", "weekly_pct", "fable_pct",
                                      "quota_as_of", "stale_quota", "k_as_of",
                                      "k", "k_stale", "k_stale_as_of")}
                         for r in old.get("rows", [])}}
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError):
        return None


class FreshLockWedged(Exception):
    """The --fresh single-flight lock could not be taken within its wall-clock budget.

    Raised instead of returning False because the two outcomes are NOT interchangeable: False
    means "serve a grace-TTL cache", which is precisely what --fresh may never do. A distinct
    type makes it impossible to fall into the degrade path by accident."""


class CacheOnlyUnavailable(Exception):
    """A cache-only read (--max-wait 0) found nothing servable inside its staleness bound.

    ABSTENTION, not failure — and a distinct type for the same reason FreshLockWedged is one: the
    alternative outcomes are not interchangeable. The whole point of the cache-only mode is that an
    interactive launcher must NEVER inherit the sweeper's tail, whose documented worst case is
    minutes (get_data's own comment: 4 accounts x keychain + a 90 s heal subprocess + two
    fetch_usage retry ladders) with a hard 240 s + exit 5 floor behind a wedged holder. Returning
    False here would drop into exactly that path; raising cannot.

    Maps to exit 3 (data unavailable — callers may degrade to a proxy), NOT a new exit code: a new
    enum member lands in every existing consumer's `*)` arm, and both 2 and 3 already mean
    "no answer", so a fourth value would strand the callers it was invented to inform."""


def _fresh_lock_wait_s(cfg):
    """Wall-clock budget for the --fresh path. Deliberately generous: a LEGITIMATE holder can own
    this for minutes (4 accounts x keychain read + a 90s heal subprocess + two fetch_usage retry
    ladders), and a bound shorter than a lawful hold would turn a healthy sweep into a failure —
    the exoneration-bound lesson: a bound under what it bounds can only convict."""
    env = os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_FRESH_LOCK_WAIT_S")
    if env:
        try:
            return float(env)
        except ValueError:
            pass
    return cfg.get("fresh_lock_wait_s", 240)


def _acquire_lock(lock, cfg, allow_degrade):
    """Take the single-flight lock. -> True when held; False only when allow_degrade is set
    and the wait budget expired (caller should then serve a grace-TTL cache).

    allow_degrade is FALSE for --fresh, which must never be satisfied by cached data:
    handoff-fire runs `--fresh --json` specifically to force a live sweep WITH in-process
    auto-heal before firing a wave, and then reads the resulting rows to decide the fleet is
    healthy. Handing it a 91-second-old cache would report "all accounts healthy (or
    auto-healed)" when no heal had run at all, and the wave would fire at a stranded account.

    THE --fresh WAIT IS BOUNDED (a15/D7). It used to be a bare blocking `flock(LOCK_EX)` with no
    deadline at all. fcntl releases on holder DEATH, so a crashed holder is harmless — but an
    ALIVE-but-wedged one (hung heal, network stall) is not: every concurrent --fresh caller blocks
    forever, and handoff-fire runs `--fresh --json` before firing a wave, so the wave stalls
    indefinitely with no diagnostic. Bounding it does not weaken the no-stale-data contract,
    because expiry raises rather than degrading: the caller is told the sweep could not run, and
    never handed cache dressed as fresh."""
    if not allow_degrade:
        deadline = time.time() + _fresh_lock_wait_s(cfg)
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                return True
            except BlockingIOError:
                if time.time() >= deadline:
                    raise FreshLockWedged(
                        f"--fresh could not take the single-flight lock within "
                        f"{_fresh_lock_wait_s(cfg):.0f}s: a live holder is wedged "
                        f"(hung heal or network stall). NOT serving cache — --fresh must not be "
                        f"satisfied by stale data.")
                time.sleep(0.15)
    deadline = time.time() + cfg.get("lock_wait_s", 5)
    while True:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            return True
        except BlockingIOError:
            if time.time() >= deadline:
                return False
            time.sleep(0.15)


def _acquire_lock_until(lock, deadline):
    """Non-blocking attempts on the single-flight lock until a monotonic deadline -> held?
    The --max-wait N>0 path: a caller that named a wall-clock bound waits for NOTHING past it."""
    while True:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            return True
        except BlockingIOError:
            left = deadline - time.monotonic()
            if left <= 0:
                return False
            time.sleep(min(0.1, left))


def _arm_sweep_bound(deadline):
    """Bound the sweep by the time --max-wait has left -> a disarm callable (no-op when unbounded).

    A timer, not an exception: the sweep blocks on worker threads (probes, the census walk, a heal
    subprocess), and an exception raised into the main thread would sit in the executor's join —
    the very tail --max-wait exists to refuse. On expiry the handler writes ONE stderr line and
    exits 3 (data unavailable) at once; the flock dies with the process, no cache is written.
    Signals exist only on the main thread, so any other caller runs unbounded, as before."""
    if deadline is None or threading.current_thread() is not threading.main_thread():
        return lambda: None

    def _expire(_sig, _frm):
        os.write(2, b"claude-accounts: --max-wait expired mid-sweep; no answer (exit 3)\n")
        os._exit(3)

    old = signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, _expire)
    signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, max(deadline - time.monotonic(), 0.01))

    def _disarm():
        signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, old)
    return _disarm


def _arm_hold_ceiling():
    """Kernel-enforced ceiling on how long THIS process may hold the single-flight lock -> disarm.

    --keepwarm got a SIG_DFL deadline on 2026-09-24 (b8dbe299a) after one tick wedged holding this
    lock for 52 min. Every OTHER holder stayed unbounded, and on 2026-09-30 the same outage came
    back through one of them: cc-relogin-poll's `--login-status`, launched in the Background QoS
    band, sat 21 min at 0.38 s of CPU inside SSL_CTX_new while the cache aged past its 600 s grace,
    and a limit recovery PARKED on "no routable target" exactly as on the 24th. The bound belongs
    to the lock, not to one caller. SIG_DFL, so the kill needs no cooperation from a wedged or
    starved interpreter; the fd closes with the process and cache writes are os.replace'd.

    Skipped when a timer is already pending (--keepwarm's own deadline, or --max-wait's sweep
    bound, which is armed after this and replaces it) and off the main thread, where signals do
    not exist. Default 300 s: above every completed sweep in the keepwarm log (p99 146 s over the
    last ~2,500), below the 510 s at which a sweep begun on a TTL-expired cache pushes it past the
    600 s grace. CC_ACCOUNTS_HOLD_DEADLINE_S=0 disables it."""
    try:
        hold_s = int(os.environ.get("CC_ACCOUNTS_HOLD_DEADLINE_S", "300"))
    except ValueError:
        hold_s = 300
    if hold_s <= 0 or threading.current_thread() is not threading.main_thread() \
            or signal.getitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL)[0] > 0:
        return lambda: None
    old = signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, signal.SIG_DFL)
    signal.alarm(hold_s)

    def _disarm():
        signal.alarm(0)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, old)
    return _disarm


def get_data(cfg, fresh=False, no_heal=False, max_wait=None, max_age=None, src=None):
    # --max-wait N>0 is a REAL wall-clock bound from here (it used to be parsed and ignored):
    # a cache inside TTL is served as always; otherwise the lock is tried only until the deadline,
    # a miss serves the grace cache or abstains (exit 3), and a won lock sweeps under a timer.
    deadline = ((_PROC_T0 if _CLI else time.monotonic()) + max_wait) \
        if max_wait is not None and max_wait > 0 else None
    if not fresh:
        # A CACHE-ONLY reader can never heal, so letting the no_heal refusal apply to it only ever
        # made the launcher abstain on a `cc-relogin --fresh --no-heal` cache with any errored row
        # (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D4). In-memory boolean, no I/O.
        cache_only = max_wait is not None and max_wait <= 0
        c = cache_read(cfg, want_heal=not no_heal and not cache_only, src=src, grace_s=max_age)
        if c:
            return c["rows"], c["window"], True, c.get("prev")
    # CACHE-ONLY: abstain before touching the lock. Everything below this line can block —
    # _acquire_lock waits lock_wait_s, the degrade path re-reads, and the second flock loop waits
    # fresh_lock_wait_s (240 s) before raising. A caller that asked for max_wait 0 has declared it
    # would rather have no answer than any of that, and "no answer" is a legitimate routing input
    # (exit 3) because every consumer already has a degrade path for it.
    if max_wait is not None and max_wait <= 0:
        raise CacheOnlyUnavailable(
            f"no cache within {max_age if max_age is not None else cfg.get('cache_ttl_s', 90)}s "
            f"and --max-wait 0 forbids sweeping")
    lock_path = cfg["cache_file"] + ".lock"
    with open(lock_path, "w") as lock:
        # single-flight: first caller sweeps, rest reuse. The holder can own this for MINUTES
        # in the worst case — 4 accounts x (keychain read + a 90s heal subprocess + two
        # fetch_usage retry ladders) — and a bare blocking flock made every other caller
        # (statuslines, cc-board, a handoff wave) wait it out with no way to fall back to the
        # cache it had just declined by a few seconds.
        if deadline is not None:
            if not _acquire_lock_until(lock, deadline):
                c = None if fresh else cache_read(cfg, want_heal=not no_heal, src=src,
                                                  grace_s=cfg.get("cache_grace_s", 600))
                if c:
                    return c["rows"], c["window"], True, c.get("prev")
                raise CacheOnlyUnavailable(
                    f"--max-wait {max_wait:g}s expired behind the single-flight lock with no "
                    f"servable cache (grace {cfg.get('cache_grace_s', 600)}s)")
        elif not _acquire_lock(lock, cfg, allow_degrade=not fresh):
            c = cache_read(cfg, want_heal=not no_heal, src=src,
                           grace_s=cfg.get("cache_grace_s", 600))
            if c:
                return c["rows"], c["window"], True, c.get("prev")
            # Nothing servable — waiting is the only correct option, but it is still BOUNDED
            # (a15/D7). This was the second bare blocking flock: a caller with no cache at all sat
            # here forever behind an alive-but-wedged holder. The budget is the same generous
            # fresh-path one, and expiry raises the same distinct type rather than inventing a
            # second failure mode. Its OWN name, never `deadline`: that one is the monotonic
            # --max-wait bound _arm_sweep_bound reads below, and reusing it for this epoch stamp
            # asked setitimer for ~1.8e9 s, which macOS refuses with EINVAL (keepwarm died on it
            # 2026-09-30; tests/claude-accounts-fresh-lock-bound.bats test 6).
            wedge_at = time.time() + _fresh_lock_wait_s(cfg)
            while True:
                try:
                    fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                    break
                except BlockingIOError:
                    if time.time() >= wedge_at:
                        raise FreshLockWedged(
                            f"no servable cache and the single-flight lock stayed held for "
                            f"{_fresh_lock_wait_s(cfg):.0f}s: a live holder is wedged.")
                    time.sleep(0.15)
        hold_disarm = _arm_hold_ceiling()
        try:
            if not fresh:
                # grace_s=max_age, NOT the default TTL. This post-lock re-read exists to catch the
                # case where another caller swept while we waited, but it must honour the SAME
                # staleness bound the caller asked for. Passing no bound here meant `--max-age 30`
                # was silently upgraded to the 90s default the moment the first read missed —
                # which made the keep-warm producer a NO-OP: every tick found a <90s cache at this
                # line, reported "served-cache", and never refreshed, so the cache expired anyway
                # and the daemon's entire job went undone while it logged success. Measured on
                # --max-age 0, which served a 15s cache instead of sweeping.
                c = cache_read(cfg, want_heal=not no_heal, src=src, grace_s=max_age)
                if c:
                    return c["rows"], c["window"], True, c.get("prev")
            prev = _prev_snapshot(cfg)
            disarm = _arm_sweep_bound(deadline)
            try:
                win = frontier_window(cfg)
                rows = collect(cfg, no_heal=no_heal, prev=prev)
                record_utilization(rows)
            finally:
                disarm()
            wj = {"active": win["active"], "end": win["end"],
                  "permanent": win.get("permanent", False),
                  # permanent is carried explicitly: without it a null deadline cannot be told
                  # apart from "the SSOT deadline was unparseable" by any --json consumer.
                  "deadline": win["deadline"].isoformat() if win["deadline"] else None}
            cache_commit(cfg, {"ts": time.time(), "cfg_key": _cfg_key(cfg), "no_heal": no_heal,
                               "rows": rows, "window": wj, "prev": prev})
            # D1's strand-side stop rule rides the LIVE sweep (keepwarm, every 180 s), never a
            # cache read: a recycle's cache-only --rank must not pay for a ledger read. Advisory.
            try:
                repick_strand_check(cfg)
            except Exception:  # a stop-rule fault must never cost the sweep
                pass
            return rows, wj, False, prev
        finally:
            hold_disarm()


def mark_self(rows, cfg):
    """Stamp is_self on each row — ALWAYS after get_data(), never inside collect().

    The sweep result is written to ONE shared cache file whose key hashes only the SSOT path
    plus the account names, and that path is $HOME/.claude/accounts.json for every session
    regardless of CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. All four accounts therefore share a single cache entry,
    so an is_self computed during the sweep would be served to a DIFFERENT account's session
    for the rest of the TTL — marking the wrong row as "you", which is the exact error this
    field exists to prevent. Computing it per-invocation from the reader's own env is the
    only correct placement.

    A bare ~/.claude (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR unset) attributes to the 'next' account, the same
    mirror rule concurrency() uses."""
    self_dir = os.path.expanduser(
        os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or os.path.join(HOME, ".claude")).rstrip("/")
    by_name = {a["name"]: a["config_dir"].rstrip("/") for a in cfg["accounts"]}
    mirror = next((a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]
                   if a["config_dir"].rstrip("/").endswith(".claude-next")), None)
    for r in rows:
        d = by_name.get(r["acct"])
        r["is_self"] = bool(d and d == self_dir) or (
            r["acct"] == mirror and self_dir == os.path.join(HOME, ".claude"))
    return rows


def refresh_login_countdown(rows):
    """Re-derive login_expires_h/_expired from the ABSOLUTE stamp — ALWAYS after get_data(),
    for the same reason mark_self is.

    A countdown decays from the instant it is serialized, and these rows are replayed from a
    shared cache: 90s on the normal path, up to cache_grace_s (600s) on the lock-degrade path,
    and _prev_snapshot ignores the TTL altogether. So a served login_expires_h is an
    as-of-the-sweep number, and --login-status classifies REQUIRED vs EXPIRING on exactly that
    boundary — an account that lapsed during the TTL would still be reported as expiring, with
    a positive number next to it. This codebase already learned the rule on the reset
    countdowns (inherit_lastgood re-derives *_reset_h from the stored absolute stamps, and the
    /accounts readout is forbidden from doing `now + *_reset_h` arithmetic); the absolute
    stamp is the durable form and the only thing worth trusting after a cache round-trip.

    Deliberately scoped to the login fields. The *_reset_h countdowns have the same staleness,
    but they FEED THE ROUTER (score_general/score_fable consume weekly_reset_h through
    horizon()), so re-deriving them would shift scores mid-TTL on a scorer that was verified
    against the current semantics — a separate change needing its own verification, not a
    drive-by."""
    for r in rows:
        at = r.get("login_expires_at")
        if at:
            h = hrs_until(at)
            r["login_expires_h"] = h
            r["login_expired"] = h is not None and h <= 0
    return rows


def win_from_json(wj):
    d = None
    if wj.get("deadline"):
        d = datetime.fromisoformat(wj["deadline"])
    return {"active": wj.get("active"), "end": wj.get("end"), "deadline": d,
            "permanent": bool(wj.get("permanent"))}


# ---- output ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Human table only: ANSI truecolor with graceful degradation (pipe / NO_COLOR / TERM=dumb /
# --no-color → plain). Machine surfaces (--json/--route/--rank/--relogin-info) stay plain, always.

CLAY = (215, 119, 87)         # Anthropic clay — accents, matches CC's own TUI palette
PERI = (177, 185, 249)        # periwinkle — informational (healed, cache note)
GREEN = (140, 190, 120)
AMBER = (255, 193, 7)
RED = (224, 90, 90)
GRAY = (150, 150, 150)

COLOR = False


def _color_on(args):
    if "--no-color" in args or os.environ.get("NO_COLOR"):
        return False
    if os.environ.get("FORCE_COLOR"):
        return True
    return sys.stdout.isatty() and os.environ.get("TERM", "") != "dumb"


def c(txt, rgb=None, bold=False, dim=False):
    # NEVER emit SGR-2 (dim): its rendering is profile-dependent and collapses to invisible
    # on dark iTerm themes (observed 2026-07-10). "dim" = an explicit darker truecolor gray —
    # the same technique CC's own /context uses (38;2;153;153;153-style, no dim attribute).
    if not COLOR or (rgb is None and not bold and not dim):
        return txt
    if dim:
        rgb = tuple(int(v * 0.62) for v in (rgb or (170, 170, 175)))
    seq = ["1"] if bold else []
    if rgb:
        seq.append(f"38;2;{rgb[0]};{rgb[1]};{rgb[2]}")
    return f"\x1b[{';'.join(seq)}m{txt}\x1b[0m"


def usage_rgb(p):
    return GREEN if p < 50 else AMBER if p < 80 else RED


EIGHTHS = "▏▎▍▌▋▊▉█"


def cut_marker(p, cut, width=10):
    """'▲' once usage is at/over the router's hard cutoff, else ' '.

    The ┆ tick inside bar() can only sit on a CELL boundary, so on a 10-cell track it marks
    80%, not S_CUT=85% — and it is overwritten by the fill as soon as usage reaches it, i.e.
    it disappears from the 81-100% band, exactly where it decides routing. Widening the bar
    blows the 82-column budget; recoloring the fill is invisible under --no-color (which is
    how the agent path invokes this); substituting a glyph destroys the 1/8-cell magnitude
    the fill carries.

    So the crossing is signalled OUTSIDE the bar, in the separator that already sits between
    the bar and the percentage — width-neutral, a real character rather than a color, and an
    EXACT `p >= cut` comparison instead of cell arithmetic. Net effect: below the cutoff the
    ┆ tick shows how much room is left; at/over it the ▲ shows the line is crossed. Between
    the two — where the fill has swallowed the tick cell but usage has not yet reached the
    cutoff (82-84% on a 10-cell track) — the ┆ simply relocates here, so the decision line is
    visible at EVERY usage level rather than blinking out for the last few points before it
    matters most."""
    if p is None or cut is None:
        return " "
    if p >= cut * 100:
        return "▲"
    q = max(0.0, min(100.0, p)) / 100.0 * width
    full = int(q)
    fill_len = full + (1 if int((q - full) * 8) else 0)
    return "┆" if fill_len > int(width * cut) else " "


def bar(p, width=10, cut=None):
    """0-100 used% → `████▌···┆·` at 1/8-cell resolution, colored by severity.
    cut (0..1) draws a boundary tick in the TRACK where the router's hard cutoff sits, so the
    bar shows the DECISION LINE and not just magnitude (5h column: S_CUT). The tick is
    cell-granular (±5% on a 10-cell track) and is covered once the fill reaches it —
    cut_marker() carries the exact crossing signal from there on."""
    if p is None:
        return c("·" * width, GRAY, dim=True)
    q = max(0.0, min(100.0, p)) / 100.0 * width
    full, sub = int(q), int((q - int(q)) * 8)
    fill = "█" * full + (EIGHTHS[sub - 1] if sub else "")
    track = ["·"] * (width - len(fill))
    out = c(fill, usage_rgb(p))
    ci = int(width * cut) - len(fill) if cut is not None else -1
    if 0 <= ci < len(track):
        return (out + c("".join(track[:ci]), GRAY, dim=True) + c("┆", RED, dim=True)
                + c("".join(track[ci + 1:]), GRAY, dim=True))
    return out + c("".join(track), GRAY, dim=True)


def pct(p):
    # The number IS the signal (user feedback 2026-07-10: dimmed/textured data reads worse
    # than crisp numerals) — full-brightness severity color, bold once it's decision-relevant.
    if p is None:
        return c("   ?", GRAY, dim=True)
    return c(f"{p:>3.0f}%", usage_rgb(p), bold=p >= 80)


def fmt_h(h, narrow=False):
    """Countdown → the coarsest form that still answers "when do I act?".

    Past 24h a decimal-day figure ("2.3d", "54.4h") is arithmetic the reader has to
    finish — nobody plans against 0.3 of a day (operator feedback 2026-07-30, on a
    table showing `in 54.4h`). Days+hours is the form a human already thinks in, so
    anything ≥24h renders `2d 6h`.

    `narrow=True` is the FIXED-WIDTH contract for the mid-row 5h-reset cell, whose
    overflow skews every column to its right: it collapses the day form to `2d` so the
    rendering stays ≤4 chars for ANY input, including a corrupt or far-future stamp.
    The weekly cell is the LAST on its line and prose is unconstrained, so both take
    the full form. Whatever this emits, cc-relogin-poll's `hours_secs` must parse —
    it reads this string back out of the --login-status TSV to derive a deadline.
    """
    if h is None:
        return "?"
    if h < 0:
        # An elapsed stamp means the bucket ALREADY rolled — fresh capacity, not an
        # expiry. Rendering it through the h<1 branch produced raw negative minutes
        # ("↻-2499m" observed live), which also overflowed the :>5 / :>6 reset cells and
        # skewed every column to the right of it. Kept to 3 chars so both fields hold.
        return "now"
    if h < 1:
        return f"{int(h * 60)}m"
    if h < 24:
        return f"{h:.1f}h"
    if h >= 2400:                                # ≥100d
        return "99d+"
    d, rem = int(h // 24), int(h % 24)
    # `4d` not `4d 0h` — a zero remainder is noise, and it keeps the narrow and wide
    # forms identical in the one case where they can be.
    if narrow or rem == 0:
        return f"{d}d"
    return f"{d}d {rem}h"


def fmt_board_t(h):
    """Countdown for the narrow board's `resets` and `login` cells: always TWO units.

    fmt_h mixes forms ("7.4h" beside "2d 15h"), which a column scan has to decode twice; the
    board wants one shape per column. It stays out of fmt_h on purpose — fmt_h's output is parsed
    by cc-relogin-poll and must not move. Its one other reader is _reset5_clause, whose 5h
    countdown rides the readout's ▲/△ and out-of-5-hour sentences in BOTH presentations, so the
    chat table and the board say a reset the same way. Width is <=7 for every input ("12h 24m"),
    matching _NW_WKR."""
    if h is None:
        return "—"
    if h < 0:
        return "now"                              # elapsed: the bucket already rolled (as fmt_h)
    m = int(h * 60)
    if m < 60:
        return f"{m}m"
    if m < 24 * 60:
        return f"{m // 60}h {m % 60}m"
    if h >= 2400:                                 # ≥100d: a corrupt/far-future stamp stays narrow
        return "99d+"
    return f"{int(h // 24)}d {int(h % 24)}h"


def _reset5_clause(r):
    """`; resets in 1h 12m` for a row's 5-hour window, or "" when no reset time is known.

    The absolute stamp first: `session_reset_h` is a countdown taken at the SWEEP, so on a cached
    row it is as stale as the cache, while hrs_until(session_reset_at) is re-derived now. A stamp
    already past says so instead of `now`: the percent beside it was read before that reset, and
    a reader must not take it as the window's current state."""
    h = hrs_until(r.get("session_reset_at"))
    if h is None:
        h = r.get("session_reset_h")
    if not isinstance(h, (int, float)) or isinstance(h, bool) or not math.isfinite(h):
        return ""                                 # NaN/inf would raise inside fmt_board_t
    if h <= 0:
        return "; its 5-hour window has reset since this reading"
    if h < 1 / 60:                                # fmt_board_t floors to minutes: "0m" reads as elapsed
        return "; resets in <1m"
    return f"; resets in {fmt_board_t(h)}"


def _wire_note(r):
    """A throttled row's substitute server read, stated as figures in plain words."""
    w = r.get("wire") or {}
    if not r.get("wire_substitute"):
        return ""
    f = [f"{lbl} {w[k] * 100:.0f}% ({_plain_status(w.get(st))})"
         for lbl, k, st in (("5-hour", "5h_util", "5h_status"), ("weekly", "7d_util", "7d_status"))
         if w.get(k) is not None]
    return f"; Anthropic's server reads {', '.join(f)}" if f else ""


def _plain_status(s):
    """The server's allowed/allowed_warning/rejected verdict, as words a reader needs no key for."""
    return {"allowed": "accepting work", "allowed_warning": "accepting work, near the limit",
            "rejected": "refusing work"}.get(s, "status unknown")


def _retry_hint(r):
    """What to tell a reader about re-polling a throttled row. `--fresh` used to be the advice, and
    it is the one thing that cannot help: the throttle is per token and sticky (a re-poll inside
    30 s is another 429 97.6% of the time), and every caller now holds off a 429'd account for a
    shared backoff — so the honest hint is when the next poll will happen."""
    if not r.get("poll_throttled"):
        return " · --fresh to retry"
    held = usage_backoff_left(r.get("acct")) if r.get("acct") else 0
    return f" · next poll in ~{held / 60:.0f} min (429 backoff)" if held >= 30 else \
        " · re-polled at the next sweep"


def _fmt_asof(iso):
    """A last-good quota_as_of stamp → 'HH:MM' when captured today, else 'Mon DD', in LOCAL time
    (matches the table's header clock). None / unparseable ⇒ '' (caller omits the '@ …')."""
    if not iso:
        return ""
    try:
        dt = datetime.fromisoformat(str(iso).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return ""
    # A naive stamp is UTC, as hrs_until reads it: skipping the conversion printed a UTC wall
    # clock as local, off by the offset beside a countdown that was right.
    if dt.tzinfo is None:
        dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    dt = dt.astimezone()                         # → local, to match 'live · Mon HH:MM'
    now = datetime.now()
    return dt.strftime("%H:%M") if dt.date() == now.date() else dt.strftime("%a %d")


def _fmt_when(iso):
    """A FUTURE stamp → absolute LOCAL wall-clock: 'Sat 19:39' inside a week, 'Sat Aug 08'
    beyond it. Same rule the /accounts chat readout applies to resets, and for the same
    reason: a bare weekday a week out is ambiguous with today. Absolute, not a countdown,
    because a login deadline is something the operator has to find a slot for."""
    if not iso:
        return ""
    try:
        dt = datetime.fromisoformat(str(iso).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return ""
    if dt.tzinfo is None:                        # naive = UTC, as hrs_until reads it
        dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    dt = dt.astimezone()
    return (dt.strftime("%a %H:%M")
            if (dt - datetime.now(dt.tzinfo)).days < 6 else dt.strftime("%a %b %d"))


def _same_instant(a, b, tol_s=120.0):
    """Do two stamps name the same reset moment? Tolerance, never string equality.

    The weekly and weekly-Fable buckets roll together but are stamped a fraction of a
    second apart, so any exact or truncated comparison is a coin flip on whether that
    fraction happens to cross a boundary. Unparseable on either side ⇒ False: the caller
    footnotes a divergence it cannot rule out rather than silently merging two columns.
    """
    try:
        da = datetime.fromisoformat(str(a).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
        db = datetime.fromisoformat(str(b).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return False
    return abs((da - db).total_seconds()) <= tol_s


def _md_when(iso):
    """Absolute LOCAL wall-clock for the markdown readout: `Sat 07:21`, dated beyond ~6 days
    (`Sat Jul 18 03:59`) because a bare weekday a week out is ambiguous with today.

    Distinct from _fmt_when, which DROPS the time past a week — fine for a login deadline
    the operator only needs to find a day for, wrong for a reset the readout must answer to
    the minute. Empty string when there is no parseable stamp; the caller renders that as
    an em dash, never as a past date and never as a negative countdown.
    """
    if not iso:
        return ""
    try:
        dt = datetime.fromisoformat(str(iso).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return ""
    # Naive = UTC, the reading hrs_until gives the same stamp: a clock printed in the wrong zone
    # beside a correct countdown is off by the UTC offset (5 h in Chicago) and looks plausible.
    if dt.tzinfo is None:
        dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    dt = dt.astimezone()
    near = (dt - datetime.now(dt.tzinfo)).days < 6
    return dt.strftime("%a %H:%M") if near else dt.strftime("%a %b %d %H:%M")


# The desk lane's rungs, in the words the footer prints. Keyed on the SAME constants desk_keys
# returns, so a renamed rung cannot leave a renderer describing a ladder that no longer exists.
DESK_TIER_LABEL = {DESK_TIER_SAFE: "safe set",
                   DESK_TIER_5H: "5h-safe only",
                   DESK_TIER_ANY: "no floor met"}


def desk_why(r, cfg):
    """The desk route line's clause, in the DESK lane's OWN terms — one builder, both renderers.

    W2.2 is explicit that this string must speak the two-key rule (5h-safe set, then EARLIEST
    WEEKLY RESET) and not general's. A footer clause is read as an explanation of the pick beside
    it, so a clause carrying the wrong lane's reasoning does worse than omitting one: it makes a
    correct decision look like a mistake. The order here is the rule's own — the weekly reset is
    the sort key, the 5h figure is the gate it had to pass, and the rung says which of the two
    keys survived (`desk_keys`' ladder, never inferred from the numbers).

    `sticky` names hysteresis (W1.4): this account kept the desk on incumbency, so the operator
    can tell "it is best" from "it is where you already are and nothing beat it by the margin"."""
    tier = desk_keys(r, cfg)[2]
    return (f"weekly ↻ {fmt_board_t(_until(r, 'weekly_reset_at', 'weekly_reset_h'))}"
            f" · 5h {(r.get('session_pct') or 0):.0f}%"
            f" · {DESK_TIER_LABEL.get(tier, '?')}"
            + (" · sticky" if r.get("desk_incumbent") else ""))


# The desk rungs in plain words, for the chat readout's line 1 (desk_plain). Each states only what
# the ladder GUARANTEES: the pick's rung is the highest any eligible account reached. Nothing here
# says "soonest reset": inside a rung the order also carries the login soft band and the incumbent
# margin, so that claim is false whenever either moved the pick.
DESK_TIER_PLAIN = {
    DESK_TIER_SAFE: "room in both its 5-hour and weekly limits",
    DESK_TIER_5H: "on pace to stay under its 5-hour limit, but weekly room is thin",
    DESK_TIER_ANY: "every usable account is on pace to hit its 5-hour limit",
}
# The same three rungs in a pane too narrow for the sentence (2026-10-22, _pane_head): each says
# what its long form says, shorter, so the picks block stays together at 15-25 cells.
DESK_TIER_SHORT = {
    DESK_TIER_SAFE: "room in both limits",
    DESK_TIER_5H: "weekly room is thin",
    DESK_TIER_ANY: "all on pace to hit 5h limit",
}


def desk_plain(r, cfg, drop=0):
    """The desk pick's clause in plain words — the CHAT twin of desk_why (2026-10-18).

    desk_why keeps its codes (`weekly ↻ 1d 23h · 5h 25% · safe set · sticky`) for the board and
    `claude-accounts`, where they are pinned; line 1 of the chat readout is read by someone who
    asked "which account do I use", so it says the same rung and the same weekly countdown in
    words. Incumbency says FAVORED, never "kept because": the margin is set even when the
    incumbent would have won without it, so the clause cannot claim it was the cause."""
    # The desk lane picks the SOONEST weekly reset, so its pick is the row most likely to sit at
    # that reset: the same cases as _resetw_clause, never "resets in now" / "in 0m" / "in —".
    h = _until(r, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h")
    wk = ("" if h is None else "; its weekly window has reset since this reading" if h <= 0
          else "; weekly resets in <1m" if h < 1 / 60 else f"; weekly resets in {fmt_board_t(h)}")
    # `drop` is the pane's (2026-10-22): 1 gives up the incumbency clause, the lowest-value part;
    # 2 the weekly countdown too, which the pick's own grid row carries. The rung always stays.
    return (DESK_TIER_PLAIN.get(desk_keys(r, cfg)[2], "rung unknown") + (wk if drop < 2 else "")
            + ("; favored because your last `claude` opened here"
               if r.get("desk_incumbent") and drop < 1 else ""))


# General-router exclusions in plain words, for the chat readout's Skipped line. An unknown
# reason renders as its raw token so a new one stays visible rather than hidden. The 5-hour,
# weekly and login entries print only when the readout printed no line of its own for that
# account; their words never claim a wall the server did not confirm.
GENERAL_SKIP_PLAIN = {
    "concurrency-unmeasured": "live sessions could not be counted",
    "no-session-data": "no 5-hour reading",
    "no-weekly-data": "no weekly reading",
    "zero-score": "no usable headroom",
    "5h-cutoff": "no 5-hour room left",
    "weekly-exhausted": "weekly room under the router floor",
}


def general_skips_line(rows, reasons, cfg, explained=frozenset()):
    """`Skipped by the general router: **x** (why) · …` for exclusions with no line of their own,
    or None. Wide readout only.

    `reasons` is the general lane's reason map from ranked(). `explained` holds the (account,
    reason) pairs the readout ACTUALLY printed a line for, recorded where those lines are emitted
    and never inferred from the reason name: the router also returns `weekly-exhausted` for
    headroom under its floor with no server refusal (the 98% target with extra usage on, or a
    server reading of 0.996 still `allowed`), and a drain set by CC_ROUTE_CLIFF_DRAIN_H past
    login_warn_h has no login line. Before this, an account at its live-session cap was named on
    no line at all. A row a ↻ line covers (its reason IS its error, or it is stale or rolled) is
    left off. None means every exclusion is on screen in a line of its own."""
    R = cfg["router"]
    parts = []
    for r in rows:
        acct = r.get("acct")
        why = (reasons or {}).get(acct)
        if not why or why == r.get("error") or r.get("stale_quota") or r.get("rolled_since"):
            continue
        if (acct, why) in explained:
            continue
        if why == "kmax-concurrency":
            n, cap = k_eff(r), k_cap(r, R)
            # "sessions", not "working": k_eff falls back to the LIVE count when k_work is unmeasured.
            plain = f"{n} sessions, {'over' if n > cap else 'at'} its cap of {cap}"
        elif why == CLIFF_DRAIN_REASON:
            lx = _until(r, "login_expires_at", "login_expires_h")
            plain = ("login expires " + (f"in {fmt_board_t(lx)}, " if lx is not None else "")
                     + "inside the router's drain window")
        else:
            plain = GENERAL_SKIP_PLAIN.get(why, f"`{why}`")
            if why == "weekly-exhausted" and r.get("credits_on"):
                plain += ", counted against a 98% target because extra usage is on"
        parts.append(f"**{acct}** ({plain})")
    return ("Skipped by the general router: " + " · ".join(parts)) if parts else None


# ---- narrow (board) presentation ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The SessionStart board's width budget, and the reason it is not the readout's ~100. The board
# renders INSIDE the TUI frame (`⎿ SessionStart:startup says: …`), where a row wider than the pane
# wraps mid-token — measured as "unreadable soup" in §2.7 of the plan. A wrapped row is strictly
# worse than a narrower one: it costs the same vertical space AND loses the column alignment that
# makes a table readable at a glance. 76 leaves margin for the TUI's own left gutter at the
# 80-column floor.
NARROW_W = 76
# Column widths, as constants rather than literals in three places: the header, the rows and the
# test all have to agree, and a header that disagrees with its rows is the classic silent drift.
# _NW_WKR is 7, not 6: the board's two-unit countdown (`fmt_board_t`) is up to 7 cells
# ("12h 24m"), and an overflowing reset cell would skew the login column off its gap.
_NW_MARK, _NW_NAME, _NW_PCT, _NW_WKR = 3, 13, 5, 7

# Column widths, as constants rather than literals in three places: the header, the rows and the
# test all have to agree, and a header that disagrees with its rows is the classic silent drift.
# The 2026-09-22 layout adds two columns the prose block used to carry (`bar`, `strand`) and
# narrows `name`, which `_clip` already guards. Every cell is padded as PLAIN text and coloured
# afterwards (`_cell`), because `f"{s:<8}"` counts escape bytes as columns and would silently
# shred the alignment the colour was added to sharpen.
#
# 2026-09-27: `strand` gave its slot to `live`. Early in a week the strand nowcast restated the
# unused headroom (84pp of 100) in red, and on the one account where it mattered — the next to
# reset — it abstained (`—`) whenever that account's readings had gone stale. The operator could
# not act on it, but did ask for the per-account session count /accounts already shows. The
# drain question survives as ONE line under the table, about the soonest-resetting account.
_NW_BAR, _NW_STR, _NW_LIVE = 8, 6, 4


# ── BOARD COLOUR ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MEASURED 2026-09-22 against the live CC 2.1.260 binary under a real pty (the probe is
# docs/research/R5b-sessionstart-render-probe.py, re-run with an SGR payload; raw capture and
# verdicts in docs/research/board-render-2026-09-22.md). Three facts, none of them inferred:
#
#   · the harness paints a SessionStart `systemMessage` in FLAT #999 GREY — literally
#     `\x1b[38;2;153;153;153m` at the head of every rendered row, and NOT the dim attribute.
#     That grey is the entire reason the board read as "relatively unreadable" (operator,
#     2026-09-22): eighteen rows of numbers, labels, prose and alarms at one single weight.
#   · ANY SGR the payload carries SURVIVES VERBATIM and OVERRIDES that grey — truecolor
#     foreground, truecolor background and bold all rendered, as did box-drawing and block
#     glyphs. The channel was never monochrome; nothing had ever asked it for colour.
#   · usable width is PANE − 5 (a 5-column continuation indent), so the ≤76 budget stays right
#     for the 80-column floor. Line 1 alone also carries the harness's ~31-column
#     `⎿ SessionStart:startup says: ` prefix, which is why the header line below is SHORT —
#     at 80 columns the old 46-character header wrapped, and a wrapped line 1 is the worst
#     one to lose.
#
# Gated separately from `COLOR`, and ON by default, for a mechanical reason: the board is
# written to a FILE by the `com.claude.accounts-keepwarm` launchd producer, whose stdout is
# never a tty, so `_color_on()` is False in exactly the one caller that must emit colour.
# `NO_COLOR` still wins (the standard), and `CC_BOARD_COLOR=off` is the kill switch for the day
# a harness starts ESCAPING these sequences instead of rendering them — the one failure mode
# that would be worse than the flat grey.
BOARD_LABEL = (125, 133, 144)      # column labels, rules, subordinated prose
BOARD_TEXT = (230, 237, 243)       # the values a reader acts on
BOARD_MUTE = (110, 118, 129)       # an account that cannot be routed to at all
BOARD_ACCENT = (88, 166, 255)      # the ➤ picks — the one thing on the board that is a verdict
BOARD_TRACK = (48, 54, 61)         # the unfilled half of a bar

_SGR_RE = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;]*m")


def board_color_on():
    return (not os.environ.get("NO_COLOR")) and \
        os.environ.get("CC_BOARD_COLOR", "on").lower() not in ("off", "0", "false", "no")


def bc(txt, rgb=None, bold=False, bg=None):
    """Colour ONE board cell. Independent of `COLOR` — see the block comment above."""
    if not txt or not board_color_on() or (rgb is None and not bold and bg is None):
        return txt
    seq = ["1"] if bold else []
    if rgb:
        seq.append("38;2;%d;%d;%d" % tuple(rgb))
    if bg:
        seq.append("48;2;%d;%d;%d" % tuple(bg))
    return "\x1b[" + ";".join(seq) + "m" + txt + "\x1b[0m"


def _cell(txt, w, align="<", rgb=None, bold=False):
    """Pad to w cells as PLAIN text, THEN colour. Never the other way round."""
    return bc(_clip(str(txt), w).ljust(w) if align == "<" else _clip(str(txt), w).rjust(w),
              rgb, bold)


def strand_rgb(pp):
    """A strand is percentage points that DIE unspent at the reset, so high is BAD — the same
    polarity as usage_rgb, but keyed on a loss rather than on a fill."""
    return RED if pp >= 50 else AMBER if pp >= 20 else BOARD_LABEL


def board_eff(r, key):
    """(percent, exhausted) for one board meter — the server's own reading first.

    The endpoint integer rounds UP and clamps at 100, so it draws "99% used, still allowed" and
    "refused" as the same full red bar (operator, 2026-10-01: leaving ~1% of a weekly on the
    table is a lot). The wire carries both apart: `exhausted` is True only when the server says
    `rejected` (or reads >= 100%), False for any other wire reading — whose percent is the
    server's WHOLE percent, capped at 99 — and None when there is no wire reading, i.e. the
    endpoint integer alone, where a 100 is a ceiling nobody verified.

    Whole, not a tenth (2026-10-06 incident): the server sends hundredths, ROUNDED and held at 0.99
    until it refuses (docs/research/sessionstart-readout-2026-10-06/README.md, R2; 0 of 6,793
    readings carried a third decimal). So 0.99 means somewhere from ~98.5% to just under 100%,
    and the old floor-to-a-tenth printed "99.0%", a digit nobody measured. round, never floor:
    0.57 * 100 is 56.99999999999999 in binary floating point."""
    if r.get(key) is None:          # withheld (rolled since the reading) or never read
        return None, None
    wf = WIRE_FIELD.get(key)
    wire = r.get("wire") or {}
    wv = wire.get(wf) if wf else None
    if wv is not None:
        if wire.get(wf.replace("_util", "_status")) == "rejected" or wv >= 1.0:
            return 100.0, True
        return float(min(round(wv * 100), 99)), False
    v = r.get(key)
    return (None, None) if v is None else (float(v), None)


# THE THREE STATES AT THE WALL, INLINE (operator, 2026-10-03: "inline in the graph, not some
# subtext below it", in BOTH the percent column and the bar). One function decides the text and
# one the colour, and every percent renderer goes through them, so the column, the bar and the
# /accounts table can never disagree about which state an account is in.
#   confirmed out   (server refused)            "100%"   red bar, full
#   nearly out      (server: still accepting)    "99%"    orange bar, last sliver left open
#   not confirmed   (meter's rounded 100 only)   "≥99%"   gray bar
# The text carries it without colour too (CC_BOARD_COLOR=off, a colour-blind reader): only a
# confirmed refusal ever prints "100%", and only an unverified meter prints "≥99%". The nearly-out
# cell is the server's own whole percent, never a tenth (2026-10-06; see board_eff).
NEAR_WALL_RGB = (255, 140, 40)


def pct_text(v, exhausted):
    """A percent as the reader should see it, given board_eff's (percent, exhausted)."""
    if exhausted is None and v >= 100:
        return "≥99%"               # the meter rounds UP: true value is somewhere from 99% up
    if exhausted is False:
        return f"{math.floor(v):.0f}%"
    return f"{v:.0f}%"


def pct_rgb(v, exhausted):
    """The colour for that percent: red is reserved for a refusal the server confirmed."""
    if exhausted is None and v >= 100:
        return GRAY
    if exhausted is False and v >= 99:
        return NEAR_WALL_RGB
    return usage_rgb(v)


def num_rgb(v, text, wall_rgb):
    """The colour for a percent CELL's digits (never the bar): quiet until a value matters.

    Nine green numbers say nothing, so a shown 0% is muted and anything under 50% is plain
    text; amber/red and the wall colours (wall_rgb, from pct_rgb) only appear once they mean
    something. Keyed on the TEXT so a 0.4% that prints "0%" is muted like a true zero."""
    if text == "0%":
        return BOARD_MUTE
    if v < 50:
        return BOARD_TEXT
    return wall_rgb


def board_bar(pct, exhausted=None, width=_NW_BAR):
    """Magnitude the eye reads without parsing digits — the single largest readability win
    available on a monochrome-by-default surface, and the reason `weekly` gets one and the other
    two columns do not: weekly is the bucket the strand nowcast is about.

    Drawn LOW, not full height: filled cells are "▆" and the track is a "▁" baseline, so the top
    quarter of every cell stays empty and each row's bar stands apart from the rows above and
    below (full-height █/░ cells on adjacent rows fused into one slab). No glyph is both lower-
    three-quarters and left-n-eighths, so the bar fills in WHOLE cells (12.5% each).

    A BAR THAT CAN STILL BE SPENT NEVER READS FULL: below 100% the last cell stays empty, so a
    99%-and-allowed account is visibly not the solid bar of one the server is refusing. (Whole cells rounded 94% up to a full bar.)"""
    if pct is None:
        return bc("·" * width, BOARD_TRACK)
    p = max(0.0, min(100.0, float(pct)))
    rgb = pct_rgb(p, exhausted)
    # Not confirmed by the server: the whole bar drawn at HALF height ("▄"), so it differs from a
    # confirmed refusal's solid "▆" bar even with colour off. Only "100% confirmed" is ever drawn
    # solid and full. (A full-height "▓" would re-fuse with the neighbouring rows.)
    if exhausted is None and p >= 100.0:
        return bc("▄" * width, rgb)
    # WHOLE cells, one height: a partial cell can only step in height here, and a stepped last
    # cell read as a glitch (operator review 2026-10-03). The digits beside the bar carry the
    # precision; the bar carries the gestalt. Any nonzero use shows at least one cell.
    full = int(round(p / 100.0 * width))
    if p > 0:
        full = max(full, 1)
    if exhausted is not True and p < 100.0:
        full = min(full, width - 1)
    return bc("▆" * full, rgb) + bc("▁" * (width - full), BOARD_TRACK)


def _bpct(r, key, mute=False):
    """One percentage cell: right-aligned, coloured by usage, `*` INSIDE the coloured run.

    The `*` stays welded to the digits (`11%*`, never `11% *`) because it is the
    inherited-reading suffix and tests/accounts-board.bats asserts that exact token — and
    because a reader scanning a column must not have to decide whether a floating asterisk
    belongs to the row above. Right-aligning the value-plus-suffix as ONE string is what keeps
    the digits in a column while the suffix comes and goes."""
    t, rgb = _bpct_text(r, key)
    # `mute`: the row is out of weekly quota, so this cell is context, not a reason to route.
    # Same text and padding — only the colour steps down — so the colour-off board is unchanged.
    return bc(t.rjust(_NW_PCT + 1), BOARD_MUTE if mute else rgb)


def _bpct_text(r, key):
    """A percent cell's TEXT (with its `*`) and colour — shared by the board cell and the chat grid,
    so the two surfaces can never print different numbers for one meter."""
    v, ex = board_eff(r, key)
    if v is None:
        return "—", BOARD_MUTE
    star = "*" if (r.get("stale_quota") or r.get("error")) else ""
    if key not in WIRE_FIELD:       # no server reading exists for it: plain percent, as before
        # Digits use num_rgb (quiet below 50%); the bar keeps usage_rgb/pct_rgb untouched.
        t = f"{v:.0f}%"
        return t + star, num_rgb(v, t, usage_rgb(v))
    t = pct_text(v, ex)
    return t + star, num_rgb(v, t, pct_rgb(v, ex))


def _nrow(mark, name, live, p5, bar, pwk, pfb, resets, login):
    """The ONE place the narrow table's column order and spacing live.

    Header and data rows both go through it, which is the drift the width constants were
    already trying to prevent and could not: they pinned the widths and left two independent
    format strings free to disagree about the ORDER and the gaps."""
    # `login` arrives UNPADDED on purpose: padding inside a coloured run leaves the spaces
    # before the reset sequence, where .rstrip() cannot see them, and they ride into every
    # copy-paste as invisible trailing ink.
    # The bar sits RIGHT of the weekly percent it draws ("35% ███░░░░░"): between 5h and weekly
    # it read as 5h's bar, since nothing but adjacency says which number a bar belongs to.
    # Three spaces before `login`, not one: both trailing cells are countdowns of the same
    # shape, and a single space let "2d 15h 20d 19h" read as one string.
    return (" ".join([mark + name, live, p5, pwk, bar, pfb, resets])
            + "   " + login).rstrip()


# ---- wide (chat) presentation ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The /accounts chat readout is a fenced ```text grid in the BOARD's visual language (2026-10-18,
# operator: "as beautiful, clear, clean, and concise as possible", after the board proved the look).
# The old 8-column markdown table rendered as a heavy boxed grid with "Sun 03:59 (in 3.5h)" cells;
# a monospace fence keeps the board's columns, its bar and its mark column, and has room for the
# two things the board cannot carry: the 5-hour reset and the absolute clock beside each countdown.
# Widths: live, a percent, the 5h countdown, the 5h clock (`Sun 03:59`), the weekly countdown.
_CW_LIVE, _CW_PCT, _CW_CD5, _CW_ABS5, _CW_CDW = 4, 5, 6, 9, 7

# Flag severity, the order the chat AND board print their one-line flags in: money first (it is
# the standing guard), then anything that blocks an account, then the near-wall readings, then
# staleness. A stable sort keeps accounts in row order within a level.
(_SEV_MONEY, _SEV_AUTH, _SEV_OUT, _SEV_NEAR, _SEV_CUT5, _SEV_CLIFF, _SEV_STALE, _SEV_INFO,
 _SEV_FOOT) = range(9)


def _until(r, at_key, h_key):
    """Hours until a row's reset, re-derived from the STAMP when there is one (`_reset5_clause`'s
    precedence: `*_reset_h` is a countdown taken at the sweep, so on a cached row it is as stale as
    the cache). None unless a finite, non-bool number results."""
    h = hrs_until(r.get(at_key))
    if h is None:
        h = r.get(h_key)
    if not isinstance(h, (int, float)) or isinstance(h, bool) or not math.isfinite(h):
        return None
    return h


def _reset_pair(r, at_key, h_key):
    """One reset as (countdown, clock): `("3h 29m", "Sun 03:59")`, countdown FIRST because it is
    the half a reader acts on. Null or elapsed is `("—", "")`: a past clock reads as a deadline
    that still stands. Under a minute is `<1m`, since fmt_board_t floors and `0m` reads elapsed."""
    h = _until(r, at_key, h_key)
    if h is None or h <= 0:
        return "—", ""
    clk = _md_when(r.get(at_key)).split()
    # Dated (past ~6 days) drops the weekday — `Oct 24 23:00`, 12 cells, not 16: the date already
    # disambiguates, and the 4 cells are what keeps a long-named fleet inside the 100-cell grid.
    return ("<1m" if h < 1 / 60 else fmt_board_t(h)), " ".join(clk[1:] if len(clk) == 4 else clk)


def _resetw_clause(r):
    """The weekly twin of `_reset5_clause`, for the out-of-weekly-quota sentences."""
    h = _until(r, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h")
    if h is None:
        return ""
    if h <= 0:
        return "; its weekly window has reset since this reading"
    if h < 1 / 60:
        return "; resets in <1m"
    return f"; resets in {fmt_board_t(h)}"


def _login_cell(r, warn_h, narrow, h=None):
    """The login cell, ONE builder for both surfaces: a countdown, `⚠ ` inside the warn band, and
    the state instead of a stamp when a /login is already required (a future date there reads as
    "fine until then" when the grant was in fact rejected).

    `h` is the chat grid's countdown, re-derived from `login_expires_at` the way every other grid
    cell is; the board keeps the sweep's `login_expires_h` (absent `h`), byte for byte. Given one,
    an expiry that has passed since the sweep reads `⊘ EXPIRED`, never a stale `⚠ 6m` or a `—`."""
    if r.get("auth") == "login-required":
        return "⊘ REQ" if narrow else "⊘ REQUIRED"
    if h is not None:
        return "⊘ EXPIRED" if h <= 0 else ("⚠ " if h <= warn_h else "") + fmt_board_t(h)
    lxh = r.get("login_expires_h")
    if lxh is None or lxh < 0:
        return "—"
    return ("⚠ " if lxh <= warn_h else "") + fmt_board_t(lxh)


def _wrow(mark, name, live, p5, cd5, abs5, pwk, bar, pfb, cdw, absw, login, nw, ww):
    """The ONE place the chat grid's column order and gaps live (header and rows alike, as `_nrow`
    is for the board). Plain text only: no SGR reaches chat, and every glyph used in a cell is one
    cell wide, so ljust/rjust are exact."""
    return (mark.ljust(_NW_MARK) + name.ljust(nw) + " " + live.rjust(_CW_LIVE)
            + " " + p5.rjust(_CW_PCT) + "  " + cd5.rjust(_CW_CD5) + " " + abs5.ljust(_CW_ABS5)
            + "  " + pwk.rjust(_CW_PCT) + " " + bar.ljust(_NW_BAR) + " " + pfb.rjust(_CW_PCT)
            + "  " + cdw.rjust(_CW_CDW) + " " + absw.ljust(ww) + "  " + login).rstrip()


# ---- pane (hook-drawn) presentation ------------------------------------------------------------
# The chat readout's content, in the board's COLOUR, laid out for the pane it lands in (2026-10-18,
# operator: "get color into this just like our SessionStart readout … best designed at all pane
# widths and … doesn't just break like it does now"). Chat cannot carry colour (the model's text is
# markdown-rendered and no SGR survives it), and its fixed ~95-cell fence hard-wraps in any narrower
# pane — at 45 and 37 columns, the widths the operator was actually in, every grid row split in two.
#
# THE CHANNEL, MEASURED on CC 2.1.293 under a real pty (2026-10-18; method as
# docs/research/board-render-2026-09-22.md, with the hook on PostToolUse:Bash and UserPromptSubmit):
# a PostToolUse `systemMessage` renders as `⎿ PostToolUse:Bash says: …` under the collapsed
# "Ran 1 shell command" line, payload truecolor and bold survive, continuation rows are indented 5,
# and a hook reads the right width from Claude's own tty. So `--readout`, run inside Claude Code,
# also writes this presentation to pane_file() and hooks/relay-verbatim.sh draws it.
#
# ONE SET OF FACTS, a third LAYOUT. Every value — marks, `*` suffixes, notes, picks, the drain
# sentence — is decided in readout_lines exactly as for chat; only placement and colour differ.
PANE_GUTTER = 5         # the TUI's continuation indent: usable cells = pane columns − 5 (measured)
PANE_MIN = 12           # the narrowest layout shipped (a 17-column pane less the gutter)
PANE_FLOOR = 44         # never size below this for a split that has not happened (pane_width)


def pane_width(cols):
    """The cell budget to lay the pane render out in: the pane must SURVIVE ONE SPLIT.

    Drawn text is static: when the pane narrows afterwards, Claude Code re-wraps it at the new
    width and every grid row breaks in two. Measured 2026-10-18, the first live run: drawn for 80
    columns (70-cell rows), the pane was ~51 columns two minutes later, after a split opened beside
    it, and the operator saw the wrapped soup this presentation exists to prevent. Splits are this
    fleet's normal motion (every handoff fires split-right), so the budget is what is left after
    halving the pane — never under PANE_FLOOR, which a 100-column pane split in two still clears
    (49 − 5 gutter = 44), and never over what the pane has now."""
    usable = max(PANE_MIN, cols - PANE_GUTTER)
    return min(usable, max(PANE_FLOOR, (cols - 1) // 2 - PANE_GUTTER))


def pane_cols():
    """Columns of the pane Claude Code is drawing in, or None.

    Read from the tty of `CLAUDE_PID` (the claude process the Bash tool exports), not from our
    own stdio: the Bash tool's shell has no tty (`??`), and $COLUMNS is 0 there. The expect-
    wrapped launch puts claude on its own pty, which mirrors the pane's size, so the read is the
    same either way (measured: both 62x45). `CC_PANE_COLS` overrides, for tests and replays."""
    ov = os.environ.get("CC_PANE_COLS")
    if ov:
        return int(ov) if ov.isdigit() and int(ov) > 0 else None
    pid = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PID", "")
    if not pid.isdigit():
        return None
    try:
        tty = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "tty=", "-p", pid], capture_output=True, text=True,
                             timeout=2).stdout.strip()
        if not tty or tty.startswith("?"):
            return None
        fd = os.open("/dev/" + tty, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOCTTY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
        try:
            return os.get_terminal_size(fd).columns or None
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
    except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None


def pane_file(sid):
    """Where `--readout` leaves the pane render for hooks/relay-verbatim.sh, keyed by session so
    two panes never draw each other's readout. The hook consumes (deletes) it."""
    d = os.environ.get("CC_PANE_DIR") or "/tmp/claude-accounts-pane"
    safe = re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9_-]", "", sid or "")
    return os.path.join(d, safe + ".txt") if safe else None


# THE WIDTH LADDER (2026-10-19). Every layout `_pane_at(cells)` can produce from PANE_MIN cells
# (a 17-column pane less the gutter; 35 until 2026-10-22) to 195 (a 200-column one),
# deduplicated: consecutive widths that lay out identically keep only the narrowest, so "the
# widest variant whose `cells` fits" is exactly the layout the renderer would have chosen for
# that budget. mods/accounts-readout reads it and re-draws on resize; the layout logic stays
# here, the mod only picks a row of it.
PANE_LADDER = range(PANE_MIN, 196)


def pane_ladder_file(sid):
    """The ladder beside pane_file(sid). Not consumed: the mod reads it after the tool call."""
    fp = pane_file(sid)
    return fp[:-len(".txt")] + ".ladder.json" if fp else None


PANE_BUDGET_COLS = range(PANE_MIN, 401)


def pane_ladder(pane_at):
    """{v, written_ms, gutter, budget, variants: [{cells, width, lines}]}, narrowest first.

    `budget` is pane_width() for every pane width from PANE_MIN to 400 (`cells[cols - from]`),
    so the mod lays out for a width exactly as `_pane(cols)` would: the widest variant whose
    `cells` is within it. pane_width keeps its survive-one-split rule under the mod because a
    resize repaints only what is on screen (measured, docs/research/accounts-responsive-mod-
    2026-10-19/): a readout that has scrolled away stays at the width it was drawn at, and only
    the half-pane budget keeps that copy whole through a split. `width` is the widest line's
    display cells, measured here so the mod never re-implements _disp_w."""
    variants = []
    for n in PANE_LADDER:
        lines = pane_at(n)
        if variants and variants[-1]["lines"] == lines:
            continue
        variants.append({"cells": n, "width": max((_disp_w(x) for x in lines), default=0),
                         "lines": lines})
    return {"v": 1, "written_ms": int(time.time() * 1000), "gutter": PANE_GUTTER,
            "budget": {"from": PANE_BUDGET_COLS.start,
                       "cells": [pane_width(c) for c in PANE_BUDGET_COLS]},
            "variants": variants}


def _pane_wrap(s, width, first="", cont="  "):
    """Greedy word wrap measured in DISPLAY cells with the `**` markers excluded — textwrap counts
    each marker as two columns and wrapped lines that fit. Splits on plain spaces only, so an NBSP
    binds its neighbours; leading indentation on the source line is kept on row one.

    A ` · ` separator never sits at a row edge (operator screenshot 2026-10-22: `  ·` alone on a
    row at 35 cells): it is placed only when the item after it fits beside it, and otherwise the
    row break IS the separator. A `·` that LEADS the line is its glyph (a footnote, the Skipped
    line) and stays."""
    ind = s[:len(s) - len(s.lstrip(" "))]
    rows, cur = [], first + ind

    def w(t):
        return _disp_w(t.replace("**", ""))
    # A bound token too wide even for a fresh row gives its NBSPs back: a wrapped lane pair beats
    # a row past the pane (`➤ fable → next-quaternary` at 25 cells).
    words = [x for word in s.lstrip(" ").split(" ")
             for x in (word.split("\u00a0") if w(cont + word) > width else [word])]
    room = max(8, width - _disp_w(cont))

    def chop(word):
        """A word wider than a row (a config path, a long account name) breaks after its last
        `.`/`:`/`/`/`_`/`=` past the row's middle, or after any `-` past its third cell (a name
        cut mid-word reads as another name: `next-quatern` / `ary-`), else at the edge — never
        past the pane."""
        out = []
        while w(word) > room:
            cut = ""
            for ch in word:
                if w(cut + ch) > room:
                    break
                cut += ch
            k = max(cut.rfind(c) for c in ".:/_=")
            kh = cut.rfind("-")
            k = max(k if k >= room // 2 else -1, kh if kh >= 3 else -1)
            cut = cut[:k + 1] if k >= 0 else cut
            out.append(cut)
            word = word[len(cut):]
        return out + [word]
    words = [x for word in words for x in (chop(word) if w(word) > room else [word]) if x]
    for i, word in enumerate(words):
        lead = cur.strip() == ""
        if word == "·" and (rows or not lead):
            nxt = words[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(words) else ""
            if not lead and nxt and w(cur) + 3 + w(nxt) <= width:
                cur += " ·"
            elif not lead:
                rows.append(cur)
                cur = cont
            continue
        if lead or w(cur) + 1 + w(word) <= width:
            cur += ("" if lead else " ") + word
        else:
            rows.append(cur)
            cur = cont + word
    rows.append(cur)
    return [r for r in rows if r.strip()] or [""]


_PANE_GLYPH_RGB = {"➤ᵍ": BOARD_ACCENT, "➤ᶠ": BOARD_ACCENT,   # the lane marks, longest first
                   "➤": BOARD_ACCENT,
                   "🚨": RED, "✖": RED, "⊘": RED, "○": RED, "⚠": AMBER, "△": AMBER, "▲": AMBER}


def _pane_prose(line, width, first="", cont="   "):
    """One markdown line → wrapped, coloured pane rows. `**bold**` becomes bold value-colour text
    (a run split by the wrap is closed and reopened, so no row bleeds into the next); backticks go.
    The leading glyph takes its severity colour; the rest is label grey, or value white on a line
    whose glyph is an alarm, so a warning is never drawn in the colour of a footnote."""
    # `next3 ← you` and every code span (a command) wrap as ONE token: NBSP is not a break point
    # for _pane_wrap, and renders as a space. A span too wide for a row gives its NBSPs back there.
    plain = re.sub(r"`([^`]*)`", lambda m: m.group(1).replace(" ", "\u00a0"), line)
    plain = plain.replace("`", "").replace(" ← ", "\u00a0←\u00a0")
    # A VALUE NEVER LEAVES ITS OWNER OR ITS UNIT (2026-10-22): `next3` over `26%` reads as two
    # facts, `resets in 1h` over `11m` as another time, `2 minutes` over `ago` as an orphan, and
    # `run claude3 →` over `/login` as an instruction cut off. NBSP, so a pane wide enough for the
    # whole phrase lays out exactly as before.
    plain = re.sub(r"(\S+) (\d+%)", "\\1\u00a0\\2", plain)
    plain = re.sub(r"\b(\d+[dh]) (\d+[hm])\b", "\\1\u00a0\\2", plain)
    plain = re.sub(r"\b(\d+) (seconds?|minutes?) ago\b", "\\1\u00a0\\2\u00a0ago", plain)
    plain = plain.replace(" → ", " →\u00a0")       # an arrow leads its row, never ends one
    rows = _pane_wrap(plain, width, first, cont) if plain.strip() else [""]
    g = next((k for k in _PANE_GLYPH_RGB if plain.lstrip().startswith(k)), None)
    base = BOARD_TEXT if g else BOARD_LABEL
    out, bold = [], False
    for n, row in enumerate(rows):
        lead = ""
        if n == 0 and g:
            ind = len(row) - len(row.lstrip())
            lead, row = row[:ind] + bc(g, _PANE_GLYPH_RGB[g], bold=True), row[ind + len(g):]
        segs = []
        for j, t in enumerate(row.split("**")):
            if j:
                bold = not bold
            if t:
                segs.append(bc(t, BOARD_TEXT, bold=True) if bold else bc(t, base))
        out.append(lead + "".join(segs))
    return out


def _pane_grid(cells, width):
    """The account grid for a `width`-cell pane: as many columns as fit, never a wrapped row.

    Six columns are the readout's reason to exist (operator directive 2026-07-11: when does the 5h
    limit expire, when does the weekly) and are kept at every width: mark, name, 5h used, 5h
    countdown, weekly used, weekly countdown. The rest join in priority order while they fit — the
    weekly bar (8 cells, else 4), live sessions, login, fable, then the two absolute clocks. Under
    the six-column minimum each account takes TWO rows (5-hour over weekly), so a 30-column pane
    still reads as a table rather than as wrapped soup."""
    if not cells:
        return []
    # (key, header, width, align, group). Display order; groups get a two-space gap before them.
    nmw = max(len(c[1]) + 2 * c[2] for c in cells)
    lgw = max([5] + [len(c[12]) for c in cells])
    ww = max([_CW_ABS5] + [len(c[11]) for c in cells])
    spec = [("live", "live", _CW_LIVE, ">", None), ("p5", "used", _CW_PCT, ">", "5h"),
            ("cd5", "resets", _CW_CD5, ">", "5h"), ("abs5", "", _CW_ABS5, "<", "5h"),
            ("pwk", "used", _CW_PCT, ">", "wk"), ("bar", "", _NW_BAR, "<", "wk"),
            ("pfb", "fable", _CW_PCT, ">", "wk"), ("cdw", "resets", _CW_CDW, ">", "wk"),
            ("absw", "", ww, "<", "wk"), ("login", "login", lgw, "<", "lg")]
    must = {"p5", "cd5", "pwk", "cdw"}
    prio = ["bar", "live", "login", "pfb", "abs5", "absw"]

    def total(keys, bw, nw):
        w, prev = _NW_MARK + nw, None
        for k, _h, cw, _a, grp in spec:
            if k not in keys:
                continue
            cw = bw if k == "bar" else cw
            w += (2 if grp != prev and grp is not None else 1) + cw
            prev = grp
        return w

    # A name clipped under 9 cells (`ne… ←`) no longer says which account it is: below that the
    # two-row form, which gives the name the whole left column, is the better trade.
    nw = min(_NW_NAME, nmw)
    while nw > min(9, nmw) and total(must, 0, nw) > width:
        nw -= 1
    if total(must, 0, nw) > width:
        return _pane_stacked(cells, width)
    keys, bw = set(must), 0
    for k in prio:
        if k == "bar":
            for b in (_NW_BAR, 4):
                if total(keys | {"bar"}, b, nw) <= width:
                    keys.add("bar")
                    bw = b
                    break
        elif total(keys | {k}, bw, nw) <= width:
            keys.add(k)

    last = [k for k, *_x in spec if k in keys][-1]

    def line(vals):
        """vals: key → (text, rgb, bold). Pads PLAIN text, then colours (`_cell`). The last cell
        is left-aligned and NOT padded: spaces inside a coloured run sit before its reset, where
        .rstrip() cannot reach them, and ride into every copy as invisible trailing ink."""
        s, prev = "", None
        for k, _h, cw, al, grp in spec:
            if k not in keys:
                continue
            cw = bw if k == "bar" else cw
            if k == last and al == "<":
                cw = _disp_w(vals.get(k, ("",))[0])
            s += "  " if grp != prev and grp is not None else " "
            prev = grp
            t, rgb, b = vals.get(k, ("", None, False))
            # The bar arrives pre-coloured and already `bw` wide, so it is appended as is; the
            # header row's bar cell is EMPTY and must still be padded to `bw`, or every header to
            # its right sits a bar's width left of its column (operator screenshot, 2026-10-19).
            s += t if k == "bar" and t else _cell(t, cw, al, rgb, b)
        return s

    def cols_of(grp):
        """(start, width) of a group's span in the plain row, for the group rule above it."""
        pos, prev, start, end = _NW_MARK + nw, None, None, None
        for k, _h, cw, _a, g in spec:
            if k not in keys:
                continue
            cw = bw if k == "bar" else cw
            pos += 2 if g != prev and g is not None else 1
            prev = g
            if g == grp:
                start = pos if start is None else start
                end = pos + cw
            pos += cw
        return start, end

    # The group rule over each span (`5-hour ────`), padded by VISIBLE width: `head` carries SGR.
    head = ""
    for grp, lab in (("5h", "5-hour"), ("wk", "weekly")):
        a, b = cols_of(grp)
        head += " " * max(0, a - _disp_w(head))
        head += bc(lab, BOARD_LABEL) + " " + bc("─" * max(0, b - a - len(lab) - 1), BOARD_TRACK)
    out = [head.rstrip()]
    out.append(_cell("", _NW_MARK) + _cell("account" if nw >= 7 else "acct", nw, "<", BOARD_LABEL)
               + line({k: (h, BOARD_LABEL, False) for k, h, _w, _a, _g in spec}))
    for (mk, acct, me, live, p5, cd5, abs5, pwk, _bar, pfb, cdw, absw, login, r) in cells:
        usable = r.get("auth") not in ("login-required", "logged-out", "keychain-error") \
            and not r.get("auth_broken")
        wk_out = board_eff(r, "weekly_pct")[1] is True
        live_row = usable and not wk_out
        nm = _clip(acct, nw - 2) + " ←" if me else _clip(acct, nw)
        vt = BOARD_TEXT if usable else BOARD_MUTE
        out.append(
            _cell(mk, _NW_MARK, "<", BOARD_ACCENT, bold=True)
            + _cell(nm, nw, "<", BOARD_TEXT if live_row else BOARD_MUTE, bool(mk))
            + line({"live": (live, BOARD_TEXT if live_row and live != "?" else BOARD_MUTE, False),
                    "p5": (p5, BOARD_MUTE if wk_out else _bpct_text(r, "session_pct")[1], False),
                    "cd5": (cd5, vt, False), "abs5": (abs5, BOARD_LABEL, False),
                    "pwk": (pwk, _bpct_text(r, "weekly_pct")[1], False),
                    "bar": (board_bar(*board_eff(r, "weekly_pct"), width=bw), None, False),
                    "pfb": (pfb, BOARD_MUTE if wk_out else _bpct_text(r, "fable_pct")[1], False),
                    "cdw": (cdw, vt, False), "absw": (absw, BOARD_LABEL, False),
                    "login": (login, RED if login.startswith(("⊘", "⚠")) else BOARD_LABEL,
                              False)}).rstrip())
    return out


def _meter_cols(cells):
    """(percent width, countdown width) for the narrow forms: the widest value actually present,
    so a fleet with no `100%*` and no `12h 24m` gives those cells back."""
    return (max([2] + [_disp_w(c[k]) for c in cells for k in (4, 7)]),
            max([3] + [_disp_w(c[k]) for c in cells for k in (5, 10)]))


def _meter_rgb(c):
    """(name, 5h percent, weekly percent, countdown) colours for one account: the grid's rules."""
    r = c[13]
    usable = r.get("auth") not in ("login-required", "logged-out", "keychain-error") \
        and not r.get("auth_broken")
    wk_out = board_eff(r, "weekly_pct")[1] is True
    vt = BOARD_TEXT if usable else BOARD_MUTE
    return (BOARD_MUTE if wk_out else vt, BOARD_MUTE if wk_out else _bpct_text(r, "session_pct")[1],
            _bpct_text(r, "weekly_pct")[1], vt)


def _meter_head(used_end, resets_end):
    """The legend over the narrow forms' two value columns: `used` ending where the percents end,
    `resets` where the countdowns end. The rows' `5h`/`wk` say WHICH limit; this says what the two
    numbers are, which the rows alone never did (`4%  25m` is not self-evidently a reset). A label
    wider than its column overhangs left, and `used` gives way to `resets`, never the reverse."""
    used_end = min(used_end, resets_end - len("resets") - 1)
    s = " " * max(0, used_end - len("used")) + "used"
    return bc(s + " " * max(1, resets_end - len("resets") - len(s)) + "resets", BOARD_LABEL)


def _pane_stacked(cells, width):
    """Two rows per account, for a pane too narrow for one: name over live count on the left,
    5-hour over weekly on the right, countdowns aligned under a `used`/`resets` legend, and the
    bar on the weekly row when the pane has the cells for it.

    EVERY NAME WHOLE (2026-10-22). This form used to clip names to `nex…` and then, narrower,
    every countdown (operator screenshot, a 27-column band). A clipped name no longer says which
    account a row is, so a pane that cannot hold the longest name whole takes _pane_cards, which
    gives each name a row of its own. Columns are as wide as the values in them. What goes first
    as the cells run out: the weekly bar, then the second space before `5h`/`wk` (with one, a live
    count and its label read as one grey run, `6 live wk 44%`)."""
    pw, cw = _meter_cols(cells)
    nw = max([_disp_w(c[1]) + 2 * c[2] for c in cells]
             + [_disp_w(f"{c[3]} live") for c in cells if c[3] not in ("", "?")])
    for gap, bw in ((2, 4), (2, 0), (1, 0)):
        if _NW_MARK + nw + gap + 3 + pw + (bw + 1 if bw else 0) + 1 + cw <= width:
            break
    else:
        return _pane_cards(cells, width)
    vend = _NW_MARK + nw + gap + 3 + pw               # where the percents end
    out = [_meter_head(vend, vend + 1 + cw)]
    for c in cells:
        (mk, acct, me, live, p5, cd5, _a5, pwk, _bar, _pfb, cdw, _aw, _lg, r) = c
        nrgb, rgb5, rgbw, vt = _meter_rgb(c)
        bar = (" " + board_bar(*board_eff(r, "weekly_pct"), width=bw)) if bw else ""
        out.append(_cell(mk, _NW_MARK, "<", BOARD_ACCENT, bold=True)
                   + _cell(acct + (" ←" if me else ""), nw, "<", nrgb, bool(mk))
                   + " " * gap + bc("5h", BOARD_LABEL) + " " + _cell(p5, pw, ">", rgb5)
                   + " " + _cell(cd5, cw, ">", vt))
        out.append(_cell("", _NW_MARK) + _cell(f"{live} live" if live not in ("", "?") else "",
                                               nw, "<", BOARD_LABEL)
                   + " " * gap + bc("wk", BOARD_LABEL) + " " + _cell(pwk, pw, ">", rgbw)
                   + " " + _cell(cdw, cw, ">", vt) + bar)
    return out


# THE CARD FORM's give-back order, one step per width: (name gutter, value-row offset under the
# name, gap before the countdown). The gutter holds the lane marks; giving it up puts the names at
# the margin with the value rows still indented under them, so a name never sits inside its own
# values (the inverted 15-cell card, judged 2026-10-22). The marks are not lost: the lane block
# above names every pick in words, and a picked account's name stays bold. No weekly bar: on a
# card it left an empty run on every 5h row between a percent and its reset.
_CARD_STEPS = ((_NW_MARK, 2, 2), (_NW_MARK, 2, 1), (_NW_MARK, 1, 1), (0, 2, 1), (0, 1, 1))


def _card_chunks(s, room):
    """A name wider than its row, in row-sized pieces: after the last `-` (past the third cell)
    or `.`/`_` that fits, else at the edge. Never an ellipsis: on a card a name is never cut."""
    out = []
    while _disp_w(s) > room:
        cut = ""
        for ch in s:
            if _disp_w(cut + ch) > room:
                break
            cut += ch
        kh = cut.rfind("-")
        k = kh if kh >= 3 else max(cut.rfind(c) for c in "._")
        cut = cut[:k + 1] if k >= 3 else cut
        out.append(cut)
        s = s[len(cut):]
    return out + [s]


def _pane_cards(cells, width):
    """THE CARD FORM (2026-10-22), under the two-row form: one card per account, a name row and
    then one row per limit, trading width for height.

            used  resets
        ➤ᵍ next  5 live
             5h  6%   16m
             wk  2% 2d 21h

    Every fact the readout exists for survives to 15 cells (a 20-column pane less the gutter): the
    name, whole (wrapped at hyphens, never clipped), `← you`, the live count (beside the name, or
    on its own row when the name fills it), and each limit's use and reset, under one legend.
    _CARD_STEPS gives back the gap, the indent and the gutter, in that order. Under the
    floor (12-14 cells) each countdown moves under its percent, right-aligned to the same edge, and
    the legend takes two rows to match (`used` over `resets`): a value is wrapped, never cut."""
    pw, cw = _meter_cols(cells)
    split = False
    for gut, off, gap in _CARD_STEPS:
        if gut + off + 3 + pw + gap + cw <= width:
            break
    else:
        gut, off, gap, split = 0, 1, 1, True
    ind = gut + off
    vend = ind + 3 + (max(pw, cw) if split else pw)        # where the percents end
    if split:
        out = [bc("used".rjust(vend), BOARD_LABEL), bc("resets".rjust(vend), BOARD_LABEL)]
    else:
        out = [_meter_head(vend, vend + gap + cw)]
    room = max(4, width - gut)
    for c in cells:
        (mk, acct, me, live, p5, cd5, _a5, pwk, _bar, _pfb, cdw, _aw, _lg, _r) = c
        nrgb, rgb5, rgbw, vt = _meter_rgb(c)
        names = _card_chunks(acct, room)
        if me:
            # `← you` is one token: it rides the name's last row, or takes the next one whole.
            names = names[:-1] + ([names[-1] + " ← you"] if _disp_w(names[-1]) + 6 <= room
                                  else [names[-1], "← you"])
        lv = f"{live} live" if live not in ("", "?") else ""
        for i, part in enumerate(names):
            row = (_cell(mk if i == 0 else "", gut, "<", BOARD_ACCENT, bold=True) if gut else "") \
                + bc(part, nrgb, bool(mk))
            if i == len(names) - 1 and lv:
                sp = next((g for g in (2, 1) if _disp_w(part) + g + _disp_w(lv) <= room), 0)
                if sp:
                    row, lv = row + " " * sp + bc(lv, BOARD_LABEL), ""
            out.append(row)
        if lv:
            out.append(" " * gut + bc(lv, BOARD_LABEL))
        for lab, pct, prgb, cd in (("5h", p5, rgb5, cd5), ("wk", pwk, rgbw, cdw)):
            if split:
                out.append(" " * ind + bc(lab, BOARD_LABEL) + " "
                           + _cell(pct, vend - ind - 3, ">", prgb))
                out.append(" " * (vend - _disp_w(cd)) + bc(cd, vt))
            else:
                out.append(" " * ind + bc(lab, BOARD_LABEL) + " " + _cell(pct, pw, ">", prgb)
                           + " " * gap + _cell(cd, cw, ">", vt))
    return out


_DESK_NONE = "no account routable; the launcher falls back to its pinned account"


def _pane_head(rank_i, pick_i, pick_g, pick_f, cfg, width):
    """The three lanes for a `width`-cell pane (2026-10-22), as markdown lines that _pane_prose
    wraps and colours. The picks are the answer, so every rule keys on whether ITS line fits:

      · `➤ desk (bare claude) → pick` fits one row: the chat head, with the desk clause on its own
        indented rows giving up its lowest-value parts (desk_plain `drop`) while it would take
        more than 4 rows: `favored because…` first, then the weekly countdown (on the pick's row).
      · `➤ general → x · ➤ fable → y` does not fit one row: one lane per row at the margin, each
        led by the mark its pick's grid row carries (`➤ᵍ`, `➤ᶠ`; the bare `➤` when a louder lane
        took the row), so the pair is also the legend for those marks. It used to wrap at the `·`
        and print fable two cells in, as if it were part of general.
      · the desk verdict does not fit one row: the lanes as one block, arrows aligned, `(bare
        claude)` gone and the clause cut to its rung, in its short words (DESK_TIER_SHORT) once
        the rung would take more than 2 rows. Where the aligned block cannot fit, each lane is its
        own row with the bare `➤`, and a lane too wide for that takes two, the arrow leading the
        second (never `general →` ending a row). `← you` rides the desk row only where it fits
        whole; the pick's own row says `← you` too."""
    nb = "\u00a0"
    me = bool(rank_i and rank_i[0][1].get("is_self"))
    fits = (lambda s: _disp_w(_demark(s)) <= width)
    # The marks readout_lines puts on the grid, by the same de-dup rule (a louder lane's mark wins).
    mg = "➤ᵍ" if pick_g and pick_g != pick_i else "➤"
    mf = "➤ᶠ" if pick_f and pick_f not in (pick_i, pick_g) else "➤"

    def nrows(line):
        return len(_pane_prose(line, width, cont=line[:len(line) - len(line.lstrip())] or "  "))
    verdict = f"➤ desk (bare `claude`) →{nb}**{pick_i or 'none'}**" + (" ← you" if me else "")
    if fits(verdict):
        if rank_i:
            cl = next((c for c in ("   " + desk_plain(rank_i[0][1], cfg, drop=d) for d in (0, 1))
                       if nrows(c) <= 4), "   " + desk_plain(rank_i[0][1], cfg, drop=2))
            head = [verdict, cl]
        else:
            # ABSTENTION IS AN ANSWER: printed as "none", never omitted (see the board branch).
            head = [f"➤ desk (bare `claude`) → **none** — {_DESK_NONE}"]
        pair = (f"➤{nb}general{nb}→{nb}**{pick_g or 'none'}** · "
                f"➤{nb}fable{nb}→{nb}**{pick_f or 'none'}**")
        return head + ([pair] if fits(pair) else
                       [f"{mg}{nb}general{nb}→{nb}**{pick_g or 'none'}**",
                        f"{mf}{nb}fable{nb}→{nb}**{pick_f or 'none'}**"])
    lanes = (("➤", "desk", pick_i), (mg, "general", pick_g), (mf, "fable", pick_f))
    aligned = [m + nb * (_NW_MARK - _disp_w(m)) + n + nb * (8 - len(n)) + f"→{nb}**{p or 'none'}**"
               for m, n, p in lanes]
    you = f"{nb}←{nb}you" if me else ""
    if all(fits(x) for x in aligned):
        blocks, cind = [[x] for x in aligned], " " * _NW_MARK
    else:
        blocks, cind = [], "  "
        for _m, n, p in lanes:
            one = f"➤{nb}{n}{nb}→{nb}**{p or 'none'}**"
            blocks.append([one] if fits(one) else [f"➤{nb}{n}", f"{cind}→{nb}**{p or 'none'}**"])
    if you and fits(blocks[0][-1] + you):
        blocks[0][-1] += you
    clause = cind + (desk_plain(rank_i[0][1], cfg, drop=2) if rank_i else _DESK_NONE)
    if rank_i and nrows(clause) > 2:
        clause = cind + DESK_TIER_SHORT.get(desk_keys(rank_i[0][1], cfg)[2], "rung unknown")
    # the desk lane's row(s), its clause under them, then general and fable
    return blocks[0] + [clause] + blocks[1] + blocks[2]


def _read_time_line(cfg, cached, brief=False):
    """The chat readout's last line: WHEN the quota was read, never when the page was drawn.

    The old footer printed `datetime.now()` beside `cached 28s`, so its clock was render time and
    the read time was arithmetic left to the reader; `live sweep` carried no time at all. The age
    is the cache's own `ts` (_quota_age_s), so an unknowable age says so rather than printing 0."""
    now = datetime.now()
    if not cached:
        return f"Quota read live at {now:%H:%M}"
    age = _quota_age_s(cfg)
    if brief:
        # The pane's narrow form (2026-10-22), once `claude-accounts --readout --fresh` cannot sit
        # whole on one row: the CLOCK alone. The hint is help, not a reading, and a relative age
        # printed into the band's static ladder rots while the band waits (the board's own rule,
        # D8/C11); the clock stays true.
        return ("Quota read from cache, age unknown" if age is None
                else f"Quota read at\u00a0{now - timedelta(seconds=age):%H:%M}")
    again = "`claude-accounts --readout --fresh` reads it again now"
    if age is None:
        return f"Quota read from cache, age unknown · {again}"
    ago = (f"{age} second{'' if age == 1 else 's'}" if age < 90
           else f"{age // 60} minute{'' if age // 60 == 1 else 's'}" if age < 90 * 60
           else fmt_board_t(age / 3600.0))
    return f"Quota read at {now - timedelta(seconds=age):%H:%M}, {ago} ago · {again}"


_DRAIN_PACE = "At the last 2 days' pace"


def _drain_line(rows, routable_g, pick_g=None, terse=False):
    """The weekly-drain block as ONE decision: which routable accounts to give heavy work, because
    their quota dies unused at reset. Same producer as pace_line (`wk_strand_pp`, same sort); it
    names only accounts the general lane can already route to, so it is never a second ranking.
    The full per-account block (burst percentiles, start-by clauses) stays in `claude-accounts`.

    PLAIN WORDS (2026-10-18). `wk_strand_pp` is points of the FULL weekly limit at the 48 h pace,
    so "83% of its weekly limit" is exact and `~83pp of 99` was jargon. `pick_g` is the general
    lane's pick, the account handoff-fire will actually send work to: it leads the names when it
    is a target, and when it is not, the line states the forecast without a directive, because a
    "give heavy work to X" that the router will not follow cannot be acted on without a pin.

    TERSE (pane, 2026-10-22, once the full line would take more than 4 rows): the same decision and
    the same figures, without the basis (`at the last 2 days' pace`), the `no forecast yet`
    caveat or the list of unread accounts, and with each figure bound to its account (NBSP).
    Never a figure dropped."""
    def _key(x):
        s = wk_strand_pp(x)
        return -(s if s is not None else -1.0)
    targets, also, small, unknown = [], [], [], []
    for r in sorted((x for x in rows if pace_need_ppd(x) is not None), key=_key):
        left = max(0.0, 100.0 - r["weekly_pct"])
        if left < 0.5:
            continue
        s = wk_strand_pp(r)
        if s is None:
            unknown.append(r["acct"])
        elif s >= 20 and r["acct"] in routable_g:
            targets.append((r["acct"], s))
        elif s >= 20:
            also.append(f"{r['acct']} {s:.0f}% (not usable now)")
        elif s >= 0.5:
            small.append(f"{r['acct']} {s:.0f}%"
                         + ("" if r["acct"] in routable_g else " (not usable now)"))

    def _join(xs, word):
        return xs[0] if len(xs) == 1 else ", ".join(xs[:-1]) + f" {word} " + xs[-1]
    unk = [f"no forecast yet for {', '.join(unknown)}"] if unknown and not terse else []
    lead = "Unused at weekly reset: " if terse else f"{_DRAIN_PACE}, unused at weekly reset: "
    if terse:
        small, also = ([x.replace(" ", "\u00a0") for x in xs] for xs in (small, also))
    if targets and pick_g is not None and pick_g not in [a for a, _s in targets]:
        # The router sends new work elsewhere: state the forecast, never a directive it won't follow.
        return " · ".join([lead + " · ".join(
            [f"**{a}** {s:.0f}%" for a, s in targets] + small + also)] + unk)
    if targets:
        # The general pick leads when it is a target: the first name is the one work goes to.
        targets.sort(key=lambda t: t[0] != pick_g)
        names = _join([f"**{a}**" for a, _s in targets], "or")
        figs = _join([f"{s:.0f}%" for _a, s in targets], "and")
        line = (f"Give heavy work to {names}: "
                + ("else " if terse else f"{_DRAIN_PACE.lower()}, ") + f"{figs} of "
                + ("its weekly limit goes" if len(targets) == 1 else "their weekly limits go")
                + (" unused" if terse else " unused at reset"))
        # Smaller strands still ride the line: the decision names the big ones, never hides the rest.
        return " · ".join([line] + ["also " + x for x in also + small] + unk)
    if small or also:
        return " · ".join([lead + " · ".join(small + also)] + unk)
    if unknown and not any(wk_strand_pp(x) is not None
                           for x in rows if pace_need_ppd(x) is not None):
        # NO account was measured: a verdict here would be stated about nobody's data.
        return ("Not enough recent readings to forecast unused weekly quota"
                + ("" if terse else ": " + ", ".join(unknown)))
    return " · ".join(["No weekly quota goes unused" if terse
                       else f"{_DRAIN_PACE}, no weekly quota goes unused"] + unk)


def _disp_w(s):
    """Display CELLS, not characters — the one measure the width budget can be stated in.

    `len()` is wrong for exactly the thing this budget exists to prevent: `🚨` (which the
    unauthorized-spend note carries) is ONE character and TWO cells, so a line that passes a
    len()-based check can still wrap. Ambiguous-width characters are counted as 1, which is what
    a Western terminal renders them as — `➤`, `↻`, `—` and the lane superscripts are all in that
    class, so the table rows measure identically either way and only prose with emoji differs.
    """
    # SGR-BLIND since 2026-09-22: a colour sequence occupies ZERO columns, so measuring the raw
    # string would count ~11 phantom cells per coloured cell and the wrap/clip budget — the thing
    # this function exists to enforce — would shred exactly the rows it was protecting.
    return sum(2 if unicodedata.east_asian_width(ch) in ("W", "F") else 1
               for ch in _SGR_RE.sub("", s))


def _demark(s):
    """Markdown → plain text, at the PRESENTATION edge.

    The notes are authored once and read on both surfaces. In a terminal `**bold**` and
    `backticks` are literal noise rather than emphasis, but stripping them here — instead of
    keeping a second, terminal-flavoured copy of every note string — is what preserves the
    single-renderer rule: there is still exactly one place that decides what a note SAYS.
    """
    return s.replace("**", "").replace("`", "")


def _clip(s, n):
    """Truncate to n cells, marking the truncation. A silently-cut account name is a different
    account name; `next-quaternar…` at least says a name was shortened."""
    if _disp_w(s) <= n:
        return s
    out = ""
    for ch in s:
        if _disp_w(out + ch) > n - 1:
            break
        out += ch
    return out + "…"


def _wrap_narrow(s, first="", cont="   ", width=NARROW_W):
    """Wrap one prose line to the budget, VERIFIED in cells.

    textwrap measures in characters, so on a line carrying a wide glyph its output can still
    breach the budget. Rather than reimplement wrapping, wrap at a candidate width and check the
    real measure, narrowing until it holds — the check is the contract, the width is just a knob.
    """
    w = width
    while w > 24:
        lines = textwrap.wrap(s, width=w, initial_indent=first, subsequent_indent=cont,
                              break_long_words=False, break_on_hyphens=False) or [first]
        if all(_disp_w(ln) <= width for ln in lines):
            return lines
        w -= 4
    return [_clip(first + s, width)]


def render_readout(rows, cfg, win, cached, narrow=False):
    """Print the readout. See readout_lines — this is the thin stdout wrapper."""
    print("\n".join(readout_lines(rows, cfg, win, cached, narrow)))


def readout_lines(rows, cfg, win, cached, narrow=False, quota_ts=None, pane=None):
    """Build the /accounts readout as finished lines — the SINGLE renderer, in TWO presentations.

    Why this exists: the readout used to be ~200 lines of prose in commands/accounts.md
    instructing the model to rebuild this table by hand from --json on every invocation —
    column order, which flag is a bullet and which a column, staleness suffixes, bolding,
    the relative-time format. Every one of those facts is already computed here, so the
    project had two renderers for one table kept in sync by discipline alone: a single
    format change (`2.3d` → `2d 6h`) had to be made in both, and nothing but care stopped
    them drifting. Prose cannot be tested; this can. The doc keeps what genuinely needs
    judgment — how to INTERPRET a stale row, an exclusion, a cliff — and stops restating
    what the code already knows.

    `narrow=True` is the ≤76-col board presentation (W3.6), and it is a MODE rather than a second
    function for that same reason. Every fact below — which row the desk picked, which percent
    carries the `*` inherited-reading suffix, which bucket has rolled, what the desk's clause
    says — is computed exactly once and then laid out one of two ways. A hand-built second table
    would re-derive them, and the two would drift the first time a format changed: that is the
    history this function was extracted from, and a board is a worse place to discover it than a
    chat readout, because nobody reads a startup banner adversarially.
    """
    warn_h = float(cfg.get("login_warn_h", 72.0))
    s_cut = cfg["router"]["S_CUT"] * 100
    s_show = cfg["router"].get("S_RESET_SHOW", S_RESET_SHOW_DEFAULT) * 100
    # THREE lanes, and the DESK one leads (W2.1-2). Until now this surface computed general and
    # fable only, so the lane bare `claude` actually launches on was rendered NOWHERE — and the
    # lanes are not cosmetically different: replayed on two synthetic accounts they invert
    # completely (`➤ general → soon` printed while bare `claude` launched onto `far`, R6 §3).
    rank_i, _ire = ranked(rows, cfg, win, "interactive")
    rank_g, _gre = ranked(rows, cfg, win, "general")
    rank_f, _fre = ranked(rows, cfg, win, "fable")
    pick_i = rank_i[0][1]["acct"] if rank_i else None
    pick_g = rank_g[0][1]["acct"] if rank_g else None
    pick_f = rank_f[0][1]["acct"] if rank_f else None

    if narrow:
        # THE BOARD STATES ITS OWN AGE, on line 1, before any number it is about to print.
        # `cached`/`*` already say "this row is inherited"; neither says how old the sweep is,
        # and a board is read at a glance by someone who did not ask for it — the one reading
        # posture where an unlabelled stale number is most likely to be believed. The hook adds
        # the second axis (how old the pre-rendered FILE is); this is the first (how old the
        # QUOTA reading is), and they are genuinely different failures.
        age = _quota_age_s(cfg)
        # LINE 1 IS SHORT ON PURPOSE. It is the ONE row that also carries the harness's
        # `⎿ SessionStart:startup says: ` prefix (~31 columns, measured), so its own budget is
        # pane−31 rather than pane−5: the previous 46-character header wrapped at an 80-column
        # pane, and a wrapped line 1 costs the reader the age of the very numbers below it.
        out = [bc("CLAUDE MAX", BOARD_TEXT, bold=True)
               + bc(" · " + datetime.now().strftime("%a %H:%M")
                    + " · " + ("cached" if cached else "live")
                    + (f" {age}s" if age is not None else ""), BOARD_LABEL),
               _nrow(_cell("", _NW_MARK, "<", BOARD_LABEL),
                     _cell("account", _NW_NAME, "<", BOARD_LABEL),
                     _cell("live", _NW_LIVE, ">", BOARD_LABEL),
                     _cell("5h", _NW_PCT + 1, ">", BOARD_LABEL),
                     # A rule over the bar carries the `weekly` label across the pair (_nrow
                     # puts the bar right of the percent), so the bar is no longer unlabelled.
                     _cell("─" * _NW_BAR, _NW_BAR, "<", BOARD_TRACK),
                     _cell("weekly", _NW_PCT + 1, ">", BOARD_LABEL),
                     _cell("fable", _NW_PCT + 1, ">", BOARD_LABEL),
                     _cell("resets", _NW_WKR, ">", BOARD_LABEL),
                     bc("login", BOARD_LABEL))]
    else:
        # THE ANSWER LEADS (2026-10-18). The desk line answers "which account do I use", so it is
        # line 1, above the grid, where bold renders (it would not inside the fence). Its clause is
        # desk_plain: the same rung and weekly countdown desk_why prints on the board, in words.
        if pane:
            # THE PANE HEAD (2026-10-22): _pane_head lays the three lanes out for the width, so
            # the picks keep row one and stay whole at every width down to the ladder's floor.
            out = _pane_head(rank_i, pick_i, pick_g, pick_f, cfg, pane)
        elif rank_i:
            out = [f"➤ desk (bare `claude`) → **{pick_i}**"
                   + (" ← you" if rank_i[0][1].get("is_self") else "")
                   + f" — {desk_plain(rank_i[0][1], cfg)}"]
        else:
            # ABSTENTION IS AN ANSWER: printed as "none", never omitted (see the board branch).
            out = ["➤ desk (bare `claude`) → **none** — no account routable; "
                   "the launcher falls back to its pinned account"]
        # Chat puts general and fable on one line; the pane lays every lane out in _pane_head.
        if not pane:
            out.append(f"➤ general → **{pick_g or 'none'}** · ➤ fable → **{pick_f or 'none'}**")
        out.append("")
    notes, cells = [], []
    # A pane too narrow for `claude-accounts --readout --fresh` on one row: the narrow prose rules.
    tight = bool(pane) and _disp_w("  claude-accounts --readout --fresh") > pane
    # (account, general-lane reason) pairs some line below explains — recorded where the line is
    # emitted, so the Skipped line after the loop names only exclusions nothing else printed.
    explained = set()

    def _note(sev, text):
        notes.append((sev, text))
    for r in rows:
        acct, a = r["acct"], r.get("auth")
        # THE BARE ➤ IS THE DESK PICK, in both renderers (W2.1). It used to be the general pick
        # here and in render_table, which made one glyph mean "what handoff-fire will do" on a
        # surface a human reads to answer "which account do I use". The lane-specific marks keep
        # their superscripts, and general now carries one too rather than borrowing the bare form:
        # a reader must not have to know which renderer they are looking at to know what ➤ means.
        # De-dup follows the existing rule — a lane's mark is suppressed when a louder lane
        # already marked the same row, so a row picked by everything reads `➤`, not `➤ ➤ᵍ ➤ᶠ`.
        marks = ""
        if pick_i and acct == pick_i:
            marks += " ➤"
        if pick_g and acct == pick_g and pick_g != pick_i:
            marks += " ➤ᵍ"
        if pick_f and acct == pick_f and pick_f not in (pick_i, pick_g):
            marks += " ➤ᶠ"

        # ONE weekly-resets column: both weekly buckets reset at the same instant, and the
        # CLI stamps them a fraction of a second apart — so compare with a TOLERANCE and
        # footnote only a genuine split, rather than adding a column the operator then has
        # to reconcile. Minute-precision truncation (what the prose rule used to say) is
        # NOT enough: a sub-second gap that straddles a minute boundary (03:59:59.80 vs
        # 04:00:00.01) truncates to two different minutes, and this printed a bogus
        # "they differ" footnote on two of four accounts the first time it ran for real.
        wr, fr_ = r.get("weekly_reset_at"), r.get("fable_reset_at")
        if wr and fr_ and not _same_instant(wr, fr_):
            fh = _until(r, "fable_reset_at", "fable_reset_h")
            fcd, fclk = _reset_pair(r, "fable_reset_at", "fable_reset_h")
            # "passed" only when a countdown SAYS so; an unparseable stamp is unknown, not elapsed.
            fwhen = ("Fable reset has passed" if fh is not None and fh <= 0
                     else f"Fable resets {fclk} (in {fcd})" if fclk
                     else f"Fable resets in {fcd}" if fh is not None else "Fable —")
            notes.append((_SEV_FOOT, f"· `{acct}` weekly and Fable reset at different minutes: "
                          + fwhen))

        # login expires: never a quota, never a reset (`_login_cell`, one builder for both).
        # Chat re-derives it from the stamp like every other grid cell, so an expiry that passed
        # since the sweep reads EXPIRED; the board keeps the sweep's countdown, byte for byte.
        lxh = r.get("login_expires_h")
        if not narrow:
            lxh = _until(r, "login_expires_at", "login_expires_h")

        # ---- the row: ONE set of facts, laid out for the surface that asked for it.
        # Only the LAYOUT branches here. Every value — the marks, the `*` suffixes, the em dash
        # rules, the warn threshold — was decided above, once, for both.
        if narrow:
            # Narrow carries `live` (the same k_shown the wide table prints) since 2026-09-27 —
            # "how many sessions are already on it" turned out to be exactly what a startup
            # glance is for. It renders both time columns as COUNTDOWNS rather than `absolute (in relative)` pairs: the
            # wide cell is ~20 cells and a board has ~14 to give it. The countdown is the half a
            # reader acts on; the absolute stamp stays one `claude-accounts` away.
            login_n = _login_cell(r, warn_h, narrow=True)    # same shape as `resets`
            nm = _clip(acct + (" ←" if r.get("is_self") else ""), _NW_NAME)
            # Board-only two-unit form ("7h 24m", "2d 15h"): one shape for the whole column.
            wk_n = fmt_board_t(_until(r, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h"))
            # An account that cannot be routed to is MUTED rather than dropped: its row is still
            # a fact the reader needs (its quota is real, and stranding on it is the loudest
            # number on the board), but it must not read as a candidate.
            usable = a not in ("login-required", "logged-out", "keychain-error") \
                and not r.get("auth_broken")
            ks = k_shown(r)
            # A weekly refusal the SERVER confirmed (exhausted is True — never 99.x% or ≥99%,
            # which can still be spent) dims the row too: it looked as routable as its
            # neighbours with only one red cell. The red weekly cell and bar stay loud (they
            # are why), and `resets` stays bright (it is when the account comes back).
            wk_out = board_eff(r, "weekly_pct")[1] is True
            live_row = usable and not wk_out
            out.append(_nrow(
                _cell(marks.strip(), _NW_MARK, "<", BOARD_ACCENT, bold=True),
                _cell(nm, _NW_NAME, "<", BOARD_TEXT if live_row else BOARD_MUTE,
                      bold=bool(marks.strip())),
                _cell(ks, _NW_LIVE, ">", BOARD_TEXT if live_row and ks != "?" else BOARD_MUTE),
                _bpct(r, "session_pct", mute=wk_out),
                board_bar(*board_eff(r, "weekly_pct")),   # drawn after its percent: see _nrow
                _bpct(r, "weekly_pct"),
                _bpct(r, "fable_pct", mute=wk_out),
                _cell(wk_n, _NW_WKR, ">", BOARD_TEXT if usable else BOARD_MUTE),
                bc(login_n, RED if login_n.startswith(("⊘", "⚠")) else BOARD_LABEL)))
        else:
            # Laid out after the loop, once every name and clock width is known.
            cells.append((marks.strip(), acct, bool(r.get("is_self")), k_shown(r),
                          _bpct_text(r, "session_pct")[0],
                          *_reset_pair(r, "session_reset_at", "session_reset_h"),
                          _bpct_text(r, "weekly_pct")[0],
                          _SGR_RE.sub("", board_bar(*board_eff(r, "weekly_pct"))),
                          _bpct_text(r, "fable_pct")[0],
                          *_reset_pair(r, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h"),
                          _login_cell(r, warn_h, narrow=False, h=lxh), r))

        # ---- flags: bullets, never extra columns, and enumerated by SOURCE not by literal
        if PENDING_RE.match(str(r.get("error") or "")):
            # D8: a just-logged-in account, recovered by the local pass, not yet read
            _hm = time.strftime("%H:%M", time.localtime(_as_epoch(r.get("auth_recovered_at"))
                                                        or time.time()))
            _note(_SEV_STALE, f"↻ `{acct}` · logged in {_hm}; quota read pending, excluded from routing")
        elif r.get("stale_quota") or r.get("error"):
            asof = _fmt_asof(r.get("quota_as_of"))
            if r.get("poll_throttled"):
                _note(_SEV_STALE, f"↻ `{acct}` — poll throttled (NOT a usage cap); numbers are "
                             f"last-known" + (f" as of {asof}" if asof else "")
                             + _wire_note(r) + _retry_hint(r))
            else:
                _note(_SEV_STALE, f"↻ `{acct}` — last-known"
                             + (f", as of {asof}" if asof else "")
                             + "; not polled this sweep, excluded from routing")
        if r.get("rolled_since"):
            _note(_SEV_STALE, f"↻ `{acct}` — {'/'.join(r['rolled_since'])} RESET since that reading"
                         + (f" ({'; '.join(r['reset_evidence'])})" if r.get("reset_evidence")
                            else "")
                         + "; pre-reset figures are withheld (`—`) until a read replaces them")
        # The SAME number the table's 5h cell prints — wire first. Two renderings of one meter
        # disagreeing by a point in one message is exactly the second renderer this file exists
        # to delete, and here it would land beside a cell the operator is reading for precision.
        wu5 = (r.get("wire") or {}).get("5h_util")
        wire5 = (r.get("wire") or {}).get("5h_status")
        sp = wu5 * 100 if wu5 is not None else r.get("session_pct")
        # The reset rides the line (operator ask 2026-10-11): an account near or past the cutoff
        # is one the reader wants back, and WHEN is the half they act on. △ starts at
        # S_RESET_SHOW (80%) so the time shows before routing stops, and only with a known reset:
        # without one it would repeat the 5h cell beside it. One line per account either way.
        r5 = _reset5_clause(r)
        if sp is not None and sp >= s_cut:
            explained.add((acct, "5h-cutoff"))
            # NARROW PANE (2026-10-22): the server's ✖ out-of-5-hour line below says this and more
            # (the same percent, the same reset), so the ▲ beside it is a duplicate the narrowest
            # panes pay rows for. Wider panes keep both, byte for byte as chat.
            if not (tight and wire5 == "rejected"):
                _note(_SEV_CUT5, f"▲ `{acct}` — 5h at {sp:.0f}%, "
                                 f"at/over the {s_cut:.0f}% routing cutoff" + r5)
        elif sp is not None and sp >= s_show and r5:
            _note(_SEV_CUT5, f"△ `{acct}` — 5h at {sp:.0f}%" + r5)
        # THE THREE-WAY SPLIT THIS BULLET USED TO COLLAPSE. `weekly_pct >= 100` is not a fact
        # about exhaustion — the endpoint rounds UP and clamps, so it covers "99% used and the
        # server says allowed", "exactly full", and "102% used and rejected". Saying **LIMITED**
        # over the first of those is a false refusal, and it is the one that costs: measured over
        # the recorded series an account sits pinned at `100` for a mean ~21 h before its weekly
        # reset (max 56.5 h), excluded from routing the whole time. So the bullet now states which
        # of the three it is, and says so in the language of who decided: the SERVER, or a
        # rounding we did.
        #
        # BOTH SURFACES PRINT THE SENTENCE, and it leads with the answer in plain words (operator,
        # 2026-10-03). The 2026-10-01 board carried this distinction only in the bar — a solid
        # bar for refused, a one-eighth sliver for still-allowed — and in the ABSENCE of a line:
        # the refused account got no sentence at all, while the unverified one got one. Nobody
        # can read "confirmed out" off a missing line, and the operator could not. Each state now
        # names itself, with who decided it: Anthropic's server, or our rounding.
        wire = r.get("wire") or {}
        w7, s7 = wire.get("7d_util"), wire.get("7d_status")
        resets = _resetw_clause(r)
        _bank_g = bank_live(acct)
        if _bank_g and bank_at_wall(r):
            # AT THE WALL WITH A GRANT IN HAND is not "out of quota": it is the one moment the
            # grant can be redeemed, and the grant is lost at its expiry if nobody does.
            explained.add((acct, "weekly-exhausted"))
            _note(_SEV_OUT, "✖ " + f"`{acct}` is at its weekly limit and holds a banked full reset "
                         f"(expires {_bank_day(_bank_g)}): **redeem it now at {BANK_REDEEM_URL}**")
        elif s7 == "rejected":
            explained.add((acct, "weekly-exhausted"))
            _note(_SEV_OUT, "✖ " + f"`{acct}` is **out of weekly quota**, confirmed by Anthropic's server"
                         + (f" ({w7 * 100:.0f}% used)" if w7 is not None else "") + resets)
        elif w7 is not None and (r.get("weekly_pct") or 0) >= 100:
            # A SNAPSHOT, SAID AS ONE (2026-10-06): this sentence used to read "still accepts work
            # (about 1% of the week left, roughly 5% of one 5-hour window)", and sessions on that
            # account were refused about a minute later with 11 of them live on it. Both figures
            # were the CEILING of a reading the server rounds to hundredths and holds at 0.99 until
            # it refuses (research README, R2/R3: every recorded >=99% stay ended in a refusal), so
            # no amount is stated; the read's clock time and the load on the account are, because
            # those are what tell a reader how long "accepting" can still be true.
            age_q = _quota_age_s(cfg)
            at = (" at " + (datetime.now() - timedelta(seconds=age_q)).strftime("%H:%M")
                  if age_q is not None else "")
            k, kw = r.get("k"), r.get("k_work")
            load = (f", with {k} live session{'' if k == 1 else 's'}"
                    + (f" ({kw} working)" if kw is not None else "") + " on it"
                    if isinstance(k, int) else "")
            # Not out by the server, but held back by the ROUTER (headroom under its floor, e.g.
            # 0.996 still `allowed`): say so in chat, or the sentence reads as an all-clear for an
            # account no lane will pick. Chat only — the board's sentence stays byte-identical.
            held = not narrow and _gre.get(acct) == "weekly-exhausted"
            if held:
                explained.add((acct, "weekly-exhausted"))
            _note(_SEV_NEAR, "△ " + f"`{acct}` was **not out of weekly quota**{at}: the meter showed 100%, "
                         f"but Anthropic's server read {pct_text(*board_eff(r, 'weekly_pct'))} used "
                         f"and was still accepting work{load}; that reading cannot say how much was "
                         f"left, and it may be none"
                         + ("; the router still holds it back from new work" if held else ""))
        elif (r.get("weekly_pct") or 0) >= 100:
            _note(_SEV_NEAR, "△ " + f"`{acct}` shows 100% weekly, **not confirmed**: the usage meter rounds "
                         f"up, so a little may be left; `claude-accounts --wire` asks the server")
        if wire.get("5h_status") == "rejected":
            explained.add((acct, "5h-cutoff"))
            _note(_SEV_OUT, "✖ " + f"`{acct}` is **out of 5-hour quota**, confirmed by Anthropic's server"
                         + (f" ({wire['5h_util'] * 100:.0f}% used"
                            + ("; a lower-priority lane is still open"
                               if wire.get("fallback") == "available" else "")
                            + ")" if wire.get("5h_util") is not None else "")
                         + r5)
        if (r.get("route_reasons") or {}).get("fable") == "fable-exhausted":
            _note(_SEV_INFO, f"○ `{acct}` — Fable exhausted")
        # ¢ keys on SPEND, not on the toggle: an account read credits_on=false with $176.91
        # already spent. And the dollar field, never credits_used — that one is in CENTS.
        #
        # BREACH vs STAT. This used to render the same neutral bullet whether or not the spend
        # was sanctioned, which made it a number to read past rather than a guard that can go
        # red — the standing cost guard existed only as a backlog row, with no SSOT field and
        # nothing that could fail. accounts.json:spend.usage_credits_authorized is now the
        # operator's standing answer, so unsanctioned spend states that it is unsanctioned.
        if (r.get("credits_used") or 0) > 0:
            authorized = bool((cfg.get("spend") or {}).get("usage_credits_authorized"))
            usd = r.get("credits_used_usd", 0.0)
            if authorized:
                _note(_SEV_INFO, f"¢ `{acct}` — ${usd:.2f} extra-usage spend "
                             f"(toggle currently {'on' if r.get('credits_on') else 'off'}) "
                             f"— authorized in accounts.json:spend")
            else:
                _note(_SEV_MONEY, f"🚨 `{acct}` — **${usd:.2f} extra-usage spend, NOT authorized** "
                             f"(toggle currently {'on' if r.get('credits_on') else 'off'}; "
                             f"accounts.json:spend.usage_credits_authorized=false)")
        # THE LEADING INDICATOR — the same guard one step earlier, and deliberately NOT a ¢ line.
        # ¢ keys on SPEND because spend is the reliable signal (an account read credits_on=false
        # with $176.91 already gone), and this readout is pinned silent at zero spend. But the
        # standing guard's actual ask is that the TOGGLE stay off: with it on, hitting a plan limit
        # BILLS instead of stopping the session, so by the time a ¢ line can fire the money has
        # already moved. render_table has surfaced the toggle at zero spend all along; this readout
        # — the operator-facing one — said nothing, and still routed the desk to that account.
        # `elif` so a spending account gets the louder 🚨 line and never both.
        elif r.get("credits_on") and not bool((cfg.get("spend") or {}).get(
                "usage_credits_authorized")):
            _note(_SEV_MONEY, f"⚠ `{acct}` — extra-usage toggle is **ON** and not authorized "
                         f"(accounts.json:spend.usage_credits_authorized=false). No spend yet: "
                         f"a plan limit will now BILL instead of stopping the session.")
        if a not in ("ok", None):
            _note(_SEV_AUTH, f"○ `{acct}` — auth `{a}`"
                         + (f" → {r['email']} · Dia “{r['dia_profile']}”" if r.get("email") else ""))
        # the login cliff, with the identity that makes it actionable
        if a != "login-required" and lxh is not None and lxh <= warn_h:
            explained.add((acct, CLIFF_DRAIN_REASON))
            gone = lxh <= 0
            _note(_SEV_AUTH if gone else _SEV_CLIFF, ("⊘ " if gone else "⚠ ")
                         + f"`{acct}` login {'EXPIRED' if gone else 'expires'} "
                         + (_md_when(r.get("login_expires_at")) or "?")
                         + ("" if gone else f" (in {fmt_board_t(lxh)})")
                         + " — a refresh does NOT renew it · run "
                         + f"`{r.get('launcher') or '?'}` → `/login`"
                         + (f" · {r['email']} · Dia “{r['dia_profile']}”" if r.get("email") else ""))

    # EVERY EXCLUSION ON SCREEN (chat, 2026-10-18). The lines above explain the ones they cover;
    # this names the rest — an account at its live-session cap, uncountable sessions, weekly room
    # under the router floor with no server refusal, a login drain past the warn band — which
    # otherwise appeared only as an unmarked grid row. Severity OUT: it says why an account is off.
    if not narrow:
        sk = general_skips_line(rows, _gre, cfg, explained)
        if sk:
            _note(_SEV_OUT, "· " + sk)

    # NO MARKER, SO NO LEGEND (2026-10-03). The table used to suffix a server-read cell with `ʷ`
    # and explain it in a footnote; even with the legend the operator could not read it at a
    # glance ("not intuitively clear"). Each account at the wall now carries its own sentence
    # above, so the cell is a plain percent and nothing on screen needs decoding.
    pre = set()         # pane: indices of rows already laid out and coloured (the grid)
    if pane:
        for ln in _pane_grid(cells, pane):
            pre.add(len(out))
            out.append(ln)
        out.append("")
    elif not narrow:
        # THE GRID, laid out now that every name and clock width is known. The name column fits
        # the longest name (clipped at the board's cap, never the ← suffix); the weekly clock
        # column fits `Sat Oct 24 22:59` only when some reset is past ~6 days out.
        ww = max([_CW_ABS5] + [len(c[11]) for c in cells])
        # The name column takes what the 100-cell budget leaves (66 fixed cells, the clock and
        # the widest login cell), capped at the board's 13; `_clip` marks any shortened name.
        budget = 100 - (66 + ww + max([5] + [len(c[12]) for c in cells]))
        nw = min(_NW_NAME, budget, max([7] + [len(c[1]) + 2 * c[2] for c in cells]))
        out.append("```text")
        out.append((" " * (_NW_MARK + nw + 1 + _CW_LIVE + 1) + "5-hour "
                    + "─" * (_CW_PCT + 2 + _CW_CD5 + 1 + _CW_ABS5 - 7) + "  weekly "
                    + "─" * (_CW_PCT + 1 + _NW_BAR + 1 + _CW_PCT + 2 + _CW_CDW + 1 + ww - 7)
                    ).rstrip())
        out.append(_wrow("", "account", "live", "used", "resets", "", "used", "", "fable",
                         "resets", "", "login", nw, ww))
        for (mk, acct, me, live, p5, cd5, abs5, pwk, bar, pfb, cdw, absw, login, _r) in cells:
            nm = _clip(acct, nw - 2) + " ←" if me else _clip(acct, nw)
            out.append(_wrow(mk, nm, live, p5, cd5, abs5, pwk, bar, pfb, cdw, absw, login,
                             nw, ww))
        out += ["```", ""]
    # The bank line rides the MONEY tier: an unredeemed grant past its expiry is quota lost for good.
    _bank_line = bank_readout_line(rows)
    if _bank_line:
        _note(_SEV_MONEY, _bank_line)
    # Severity order (money, auth, out, near, 5h cutoff, cliff, stale, info, footnote), stable.
    notes.sort(key=lambda t: t[0])
    for _sev, n in notes:
        # Same note text, both surfaces: a glyph-led line in chat, plain wrapped prose on the
        # board. The SENTENCE is identical, which is the property that matters — an operator who
        # reads the board and then runs /accounts must not meet two different claims.
        out.extend(_wrap_narrow(_demark(n)) if narrow else [n])

    # ---- footer: the router's own answer, never a re-rank of score_*
    if notes or narrow:
        out.append("")
    # THE DESK LINE COMES FIRST (W2.2). It answers "what will bare `claude` do" — the question the
    # operator actually asked, and the only one on this surface whose answer they act on by typing
    # a command. general/fable keep their own line, byte-identical, because they answer a different
    # question (what handoff-fire and the frontier lane will do) and are consumed programmatically.
    if not narrow:
        pass            # chat already led with the desk/general/fable lines, above the grid
    elif rank_i:
        # W3.6 REQUIRES the board carry this line: it is the only thing on the surface that says
        # what the next bare `claude` will actually DO, which is the question a startup banner is
        # read to answer. Narrow puts the clause on its own indented line rather than truncating
        # it — the clause is the desk lane's reasoning, and a half-clause reads as a wrong one.
        if narrow:
            # The one line on this surface that is a VERDICT rather than a reading, so it is the
            # one that gets the accent. `desk (bare claude)` stays a contiguous run — the suite
            # asserts that literal, and a colour boundary inside a phrase is the classic way to
            # break a substring assertion without changing a visible character.
            out.append(bc("➤ ", BOARD_ACCENT, bold=True)
                       + bc("desk (bare claude) → ", BOARD_LABEL)
                       + bc(pick_i, BOARD_TEXT, bold=True))
            out.extend(bc(x, BOARD_LABEL)
                       for x in _wrap_narrow(desk_why(rank_i[0][1], cfg), first="   "))
    else:
        # ABSTENTION IS AN ANSWER: the desk lane never yields the login-cliff term, so it can
        # refuse where general routes — and the launcher then falls back to its PINNED account.
        # Printed as "none", never omitted: a missing line reads as "not computed".
        out.extend(_wrap_narrow("desk (bare claude) → none — no account routable; the "
                                "launcher falls back to its pinned account", first="➤ "))
    if narrow:
        out.append(bc(f"  general → {pick_g or 'none'} · fable → {pick_f or 'none'}",
                      BOARD_LABEL))
        sr = soonest_reset_line(rows, cfg)
        if sr:
            out.extend(bc(ln, BOARD_LABEL) for ln in _wrap_narrow(sr, first="· "))
    # The fleet's one pace conclusion, above the per-account drain rows it sums. AMBER on the
    # board — a warning, never the BOARD_LABEL grey the no-dim-text ruling forbids; plain here.
    # Chat formats every countdown the way its grid cells do, re-derived from the stamp; the board
    # keeps fmt_h on the sweep's countdown (the defaults), byte for byte.
    _wk_left = (lambda x: fmt_board_t(_until(x, "weekly_reset_at", "weekly_reset_h")))
    fl = fleet_pace_line(rows) if narrow else fleet_pace_line(rows, fmt=fmt_board_t)
    if fl:
        if narrow:
            out.extend(bc(ln, AMBER) for ln in _wrap_narrow(fl))
        else:
            out.append(fl)
    pl = pace_line(rows) if narrow else pace_line(rows, left=_wk_left)
    if pl:
        # S3: the weekly-drain block is now one row PER ACCOUNT, sorted by pp at risk. Still one
        # renderer (L3) — pace_line owns every word of it; this splits what it produced.
        #
        # NARROW COLLAPSES IT, and only narrow (2026-09-22). The board now carries a `strand`
        # COLUMN fed by `wk_strand_pp` — the same function pace_line computes from — so four of
        # these five rows restated, in a sentence each, a number already sitting beside the
        # account it belongs to. That is ~28% of the board's rows spent on a repeat, on the one
        # surface read at a glance by someone who did not ask for it. What the column CANNOT
        # carry is the wall alarm, so an alarming row is kept verbatim and the rest become one
        # legend line. This is a PRESENTATION split of one producer's output — the same rule
        # `narrow` already follows everywhere else — never a second strand calculation.
        #
        # CHAT COLLAPSES IT TOO (2026-10-18), into the one decision it supports: `_drain_line`
        # names the routable accounts whose quota dies unused. Alarm rows stay verbatim on both
        # surfaces; the full block (burst percentiles, start-by clauses) is plain `claude-accounts`.
        _alarm = [x for x in pl.split("\n") if "⚠" in x]
        for _pln in _alarm:
            out.extend(_wrap_narrow(_demark(_pln)) if narrow else [_pln.strip()])
        if not narrow:
            _rg = {r["acct"] for _s, r in rank_g}
            dl = _drain_line(rows, _rg, pick_g)
            # The pane keeps the decision and its figures, and gives up the basis first: past 4
            # rows the full sentence was as tall as the table it sums (2026-10-22).
            if pane and len(_pane_prose(dl, pane, cont="  ")) > 4:
                dl = _drain_line(rows, _rg, pick_g, terse=True)
            out.append(dl)
    if narrow:
        if win.get("permanent"):
            # permanent means NO expiry — never quote a countdown off the sentinel `end` date
            wl = "Fable window: **permanent** (no expiry)."
        elif win.get("active"):
            wl = f"Fable window: open through {win.get('end')}."
        else:
            wl = "Fable window: **inactive** — router excluding Fable."
        out.extend(_wrap_narrow(_demark(wl)))
    else:
        # ONE footer line in chat (2026-10-18): WHEN the quota was read, and the Fable window only
        # when it is news. `active` is tested BEFORE `permanent`: frontier_window parses the two
        # flags independently, so `permanent` over an inactive window printed "permanent" beside
        # `➤ fable → none`, and an unparsed window (active None) printed as inactive. A
        # permanent, active window is the standing state and prints nothing.
        wa = win.get("active")
        wl = ("Fable window: **unknown** — frontier_access in model-config.yaml did not parse, "
              "so the Fable lane is refused" if wa is None
              else "Fable window: **inactive** — the Fable lane routes nowhere" if not wa
              else None if win.get("permanent")
              # active with no parseable `end`: never "open through None"
              else "Fable window: open, end date **unknown**" if not win.get("end")
              else f"Fable window: open through {win.get('end')}")
        line = (f"{wl} · " if wl else "") + _read_time_line(cfg, cached)
        if not pane or (not tight and (not wl or _disp_w(_demark(line)) <= pane)):
            out.append(line)
        elif not tight:
            # A footer that wraps puts the read time on a line of its own, never as a continuation
            # of the Fable sentence (2026-10-22).
            out += [wl, _read_time_line(cfg, cached)]
        else:
            # NARROW (2026-10-22), once the refresh command cannot sit whole on one row: the Fable
            # window keeps its verdict (the clause before ` — `; `claude-accounts` has the rest)
            # and the read time its clock.
            out += ([wl.split(" — ")[0]] if wl else []) + [_read_time_line(cfg, cached, brief=True)]
    if narrow:
        # D8 / C11: CLOCK times only — a relative age would rot inside the shared file.
        if quota_ts is None:
            quota_ts = (_read_json_obj(cfg["cache_file"]) or {}).get("ts")
        al = [t for r in rows for t in (_as_epoch(r.get("auth_as_of")),
                                        _as_epoch(r.get("k_native_as_of"))) if t is not None]
        hms = (lambda t: time.strftime("%H:%M:%S", time.localtime(t)) if t else "—")
        out.append(bc(f"auth/live {hms(max(al) if al else None)} · quota {hms(_as_epoch(quota_ts))}",
                      BOARD_LABEL))
    if pane:
        # Every row the grid did not lay out is chat markdown: wrap it to the pane and colour it.
        out = [x for i, ln in enumerate(out)
               for x in ([ln] if i in pre else _pane_prose(
                   ln, pane, cont=ln[:len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())] or "  "))]
    return out


def _iso_utc(iso):
    """A stamp → `2026-08-02T20:21:49Z`, the ONE form a machine consumer can parse.

    The row's own `login_expires_at` carries microseconds and a `+00:00` offset
    (`...:49.116000+00:00`), and BSD `date -j -f` — what cc-relogin-poll's iso_epoch
    reaches for first — accepts no fractional seconds. Handing a consumer the raw field
    gives it something that LOOKS like an ISO stamp and parses as nothing. Normalising
    here is what makes the machine surface actually machine-readable.
    """
    if not iso:
        return ""
    try:
        dt = datetime.fromisoformat(str(iso).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return ""
    if dt.tzinfo is None:
        dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    return dt.astimezone(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


AUTH_GLYPH = {
    "ok": ("●", GREEN), "healed": ("●", PERI), "stale": ("◐", AMBER),
    "logged-out": ("○", RED), "token-invalid": ("✕", RED), "keychain-error": ("△", AMBER),
    "no-oauth-blob": ("△", AMBER), "probe-error": ("✕", RED), "login-required": ("⊘", RED),
}
# auth states that need an operator ACTION, surfaced as a bullet under the table
ACTIONABLE_AUTH = ("logged-out", "token-invalid", "keychain-error", "no-oauth-blob",
                   "probe-error", "login-required")
# ...of which THESE are the ones an interactive /login actually fixes. keychain-error (locked
# keychain / ACL) and probe-error (a bug in this tool) are not credential states, and telling
# the operator to log in would send them at the wrong problem.
LOGIN_FIXABLE = ("logged-out", "token-invalid", "no-oauth-blob", "login-required")


def render_table(rows, cfg, win, cached, prev=None):
    W = 82                                     # visible width budget (right-aligned header meta)
    src = "cached" if cached else "live"
    stamp = f"{src} · {datetime.now().strftime('%a %H:%M')}"
    title = "Claude Max accounts"
    print()
    print("  " + c(title, bold=True)
          + " " * max(2, W - 2 - len(title) - len(stamp)) + c(stamp, GRAY))
    print()
    cell = 15                                   # bar(10) + space + pct(4)
    # the 5h header self-documents the ┆ tick: "5h session ┆85%" (router cutoff);
    # ↻5h heads the per-row 5-hour-window reset countdown — it was invisible until a
    # window silently rolled 81%→0% between sweeps (2026-07-11 feedback).
    login_warn_h = float(cfg.get("login_warn_h", 72.0))
    cutlbl = f"{int(cfg['router']['S_CUT'] * 100)}%"
    h5 = c("5h session ", GRAY) + c("┆", RED, dim=True) + c(cutlbl, GRAY, dim=True)
    h5 += " " * (cell - 12 - len(cutlbl))                 # pad "5h session ┆85%" to the cell
    h5 += " " + c(f"{'↻5h':>5}", GRAY)                    # 5h-reset sub-column (matches the row width)
    print("  " + c(f"{'account':<8}   {'live':>4}  ", GRAY) + h5 + "  "
          + c(f"{'weekly all':<{cell}}  {'weekly Fable':<{cell}}  {'↻week':>6}", GRAY))
    # Ranked ONCE, here rather than at the footer, so the table itself can mark the row the
    # router picked. The answer to "which account do I use" was previously only in the footer,
    # four lines below the numbers it is derived from, leaving the reader to scan back up and
    # re-find that account by name (operator feedback 2026-07-30). Same lists feed the footer
    # below — this is a hoist, not a second ranking pass.
    # NOT `g`/`f`: the per-row loop below rebinds `f` as a field name ("for f, tag in ..."),
    # which silently turned the fable list into a string when these were hoisted over it.
    rank_i, ire = ranked(rows, cfg, win, "interactive")
    rank_g, gre = ranked(rows, cfg, win, "general")
    rank_f, fre = ranked(rows, cfg, win, "fable")
    pick_desk = rank_i[0][1]["acct"] if rank_i else None
    alerts = []
    for r in rows:
        a = r.get("auth")
        glyph, grgb = AUTH_GLYPH.get(a, ("·", GRAY))
        # Quota-staleness OVERRIDES the auth glyph. A poll-throttled row keeps auth="ok",
        # so it rendered with the green ● and full-brightness bars — visually identical to
        # live data, while actually showing ledger numbers that can be days old AND being
        # silently dropped from routing. The bars stay bright on purpose (pct()'s comment
        # records that dimmed data reads worse than crisp numerals); the GLYPH carries the
        # warning. A row that was both healed and throttled loses the healed glyph here —
        # acceptable only because the "healed headlessly" alert fires independently below.
        if r.get("stale_quota"):
            glyph, grgb = "↻", AMBER
        # the invoking session's own account, resolved in probe_account from config_dir
        label = r["acct"] + (" ←" if r.get("is_self") else "")
        # ➤ = the DESK pick — what bare `claude` will launch onto (W2.1). It marked the GENERAL
        # pick from 2026-07-30 until now, and that was never a decision: the mark exists because
        # operator feedback asked "which account do I use", a HUMAN question, and on 2026-07-30 the
        # desk lane did not exist yet, so the machine's answer was the only one available. It
        # existed 11 days later, and this row went on answering the other question — while the two
        # lanes can invert completely (R6 §3). The general pick is consumed by handoff-fire, which
        # reads stdout, not tables; it keeps the footer line below.
        # Still exactly ONE mark, in the same glyph and colour the footer's route line uses: the
        # 8-wide column holds "next3 ←➤" exactly, so a second marker would overflow and skew the
        # row. Bold cannot carry this either — every healthy row is already bold.
        mark = "➤" if (pick_desk and r["acct"] == pick_desk) else ""
        pad = " " * max(0, 8 - len(label) - len(mark))
        name = (c(label, bold=True) if a in ("ok", "healed") else label) \
            + c(mark, CLAY, bold=True) + pad
        # `k` is None when `ps` could not be read (concurrency()'s None contract). The DISPLAY is
        # the one consumer that may degrade — it renders "?" rather than a fabricated 0, so the
        # board never shows an idle fleet it did not observe. It is NOT dimmed: unknown is news,
        # and so is `N*`, the last sweep's count inherited by inherit_k (k_shown owns both).
        kv = r.get("k")
        k = c(f"{k_shown(r):>4}", GRAY, dim=(kv == 0))
        if "error" in r and not r.get("poll_throttled"):
            # auth-broken (logged-out / token-invalid / keychain-error / expired-stale / no-data):
            # error text prominent, plus the STRANDED last-known weekly when the ledger has it
            # (inheritance populated session_pct, which the old `is None` guard would have hidden).
            msg = r["error"]
            lastknown = ""
            if r.get("stale_quota") and r.get("weekly_pct") is not None:
                asof = _fmt_asof(r.get("quota_as_of"))
                lastknown = c(f"  · last-known wk {r['weekly_pct']:.0f}%"
                              + (f" @ {asof}" if asof else ""), AMBER, dim=True)
            print(f"  {name} {c(glyph, grgb)} {k}  "
                  + c(msg, RED if a in ("logged-out", "token-invalid") else AMBER)
                  + lastknown
                  + c(f"  ({r.get('email') or '-'})", GRAY, dim=True))
        else:
            srh = r.get("session_reset_h")
            # the 5h reset sits WITH the 5h usage: a high 5h% + soon reset = capacity relief
            # incoming. Amber when <1h (a rollover is imminent), else a quiet gray.
            s5r_rgb = AMBER if (srh is not None and srh < 1) else GRAY
            # narrow: this cell sits MID-ROW, so its width contract is the strict one.
            s5reset = c(f"{('↻' + fmt_h(srh, narrow=True)):>5}", s5r_rgb,
                        dim=(srh is None or srh >= 1))
            cells = "  ".join([
                bar(r.get("session_pct"), cut=cfg["router"]["S_CUT"])
                + c(cut_marker(r.get("session_pct"), cfg["router"]["S_CUT"]), RED, bold=True)
                + pct(r.get("session_pct")) + " " + s5reset,
                bar(r.get("weekly_pct")) + " " + pct(r.get("weekly_pct")),
                bar(r.get("fable_pct")) + " " + pct(r.get("fable_pct"))])
            wrh = r.get("weekly_reset_h")
            reset_rgb = AMBER if (wrh is not None and wrh < 6) else GRAY   # imminent = act on it
            print(f"  {name} {c(glyph, grgb)} {k}  {cells}  "
                  # dim when there is no stamp, so "?" reads as an ABSENCE rather than as
                  # a value — the 5h column already does exactly this, and dim-for-absent is
                  # the established idiom (null bars, k==0). No new colour is introduced.
                  + c(f"{fmt_h(wrh):>6}", reset_rgb, dim=(wrh is None)))
        # conditional detail — only states that need action surface below the table
        if a in ACTIONABLE_AUTH:
            # Name the operator's OWN lever first. For a credential state that is exactly
            # `<launcher>` then `/login` — one command, guaranteed, no agent in the loop —
            # and the relogin skill is the agent-driven alternative, not the headline.
            fix = (f"{r['launcher']} → /login  (or: account-relogin skill)"
                   if a in LOGIN_FIXABLE else f"claude-accounts --relogin-info {r['acct']}")
            alerts.append(c("○ ", RED) + c(f"{r['acct']} {a}", RED, bold=True)
                          + c(f" → {r.get('email') or '-'} · Dia “{r.get('dia_profile') or '-'}”", GRAY)
                          + c(f" · fix: {fix}", GRAY, dim=True))
        elif a == "healed":
            alerts.append(c("↻ ", PERI) + c(f"{r['acct']} token was stale — healed headlessly", PERI)
                          + c(" · ~/.claude/logs/claude-accounts.log", GRAY, dim=True))
        elif a == "stale":
            # "heal skipped" was printed unconditionally, including when the heal RAN and the
            # server REFUSED it (next3, 2026-07-24). "Skipped" reads as a choice we made and
            # therefore as benign; "FAILED" reads as a fact about the account. Same row, same
            # colour, opposite operator response — so the label has to track what happened.
            note = r.get("heal_note") or ""
            what = ("heal skipped" if note.startswith("skipped:")
                    else "heal FAILED" if note else "not healed")
            alerts.append(c("◐ ", AMBER) + c(f"{r['acct']} token stale — {what}", AMBER)
                          + c(f" · {note or r.get('error', '')}", GRAY, dim=True))
        # The login cliff, with lead time. Suppressed once auth is already login-required —
        # that row's own red bullet is the same message, arrived.
        # A stamp that lapsed since the sweep is the case with no `auth` signal at all: the row
        # was probed as ok and its credentials died inside the cache TTL, so nothing else on
        # this row is going to say so. An earlier `0 < lxh` guard silently dropped exactly that
        # row — the one moment the warning is no longer a warning.
        lxh = r.get("login_expires_h")
        if a != "login-required" and lxh is not None and lxh <= login_warn_h:
            gone = lxh <= 0
            # The IDENTITY travels with the instruction, never a line away from it. A
            # bare "next → /login" names an account NUMBER, and the operator authenticates
            # a mailbox — so the row that says "log in" must also say WHICH mailbox and
            # which browser profile, or a mis-paired credential lands silently and the
            # account you meant is still expiring (operator directive 2026-07-30). Same
            # email · Dia pairing the ACTIONABLE_AUTH bullet above already carries.
            alerts.append(c("⊘ " if gone else "⚠ ", RED if gone else AMBER)
                          + c(f"{r['acct']} login {'EXPIRED' if gone else 'expires'} "
                              f"{_fmt_when(r.get('login_expires_at'))}"
                              + ("" if gone else f" ({fmt_h(lxh)})"),
                              RED if gone else AMBER, bold=True)
                          + c(" — a refresh does NOT renew it", GRAY)
                          + c(f" · {r['launcher']} → /login", GRAY, dim=True)
                          + c(f" · {r.get('email') or '-'} · Dia “{r.get('dia_profile') or '-'}”", GRAY, dim=True))
        if r.get("stale_quota"):
            # The numbers on this row are historical, and the row is router-excluded (every
            # inheritance path leaves row["error"] set, which _excluded() bails on) UNLESS it is a
            # 429 throttle with a young enough read (throttle_admissible). Say which — a number
            # with no provenance reads as current, and a wrong "excluded" hides a candidate.
            asof = _fmt_asof(r.get("quota_as_of"))
            routing = ("routed on a fresh wire read" if wire_admissible(r)
                       else "routed on it (429 throttle, read within the admit bound)"
                       if throttle_admissible(r) else "excluded from routing")
            alerts.append(c("↻ ", AMBER)
                          + c(f"{r['acct']} showing LAST-KNOWN quota, not polled this sweep", AMBER)
                          + c(f" · as of {asof or 'unknown'} · {routing}"
                              + _retry_hint(r), GRAY, dim=True))
            if r.get("rolled_since"):
                # The most actionable derived fact available for a stranded account: those
                # windows have reset since the reading, so it is probably fresh again — and the
                # evidence is named, because "likely fresh" with no reason reads as a guess.
                alerts.append(c("  ⤷ ", GREEN)
                              + c(f"{'/'.join(r['rolled_since'])} RESET since that reading"
                                  + (f" ({'; '.join(r['reset_evidence'])})"
                                     if r.get("reset_evidence") else "")
                                  + " — pre-reset figures withheld", GREEN))
        sp = r.get("session_pct")
        # these two alerts imply a LIVE, routable account — never fire them on a stale/last-known
        # row (its number is historical; the account is currently unreachable/excluded).
        if sp is not None and sp >= 85 and not r.get("stale_quota"):
            alerts.append(c("▲ ", RED) + c(f"{r['acct']} 5h window at {sp:.0f}% — routing cutoff", RED)
                          + c(f" · resets in {fmt_h(r.get('session_reset_h'))}", GRAY, dim=True))
        # Fire on SPEND or on the toggle — never on the toggle alone. `credits_on` is only the
        # switch's current position, so keying the alert on it hid $176.91 of metered spend on
        # an account reading is_enabled=false (2026-07-26). Money that has already moved is the
        # louder fact of the two, and it stays true after the toggle is flipped back off.
        spend_usd = r.get("credits_used_usd") or 0.0
        if (r.get("credits_on") or spend_usd > 0) and not r.get("stale_quota"):
            state = "ON" if r.get("credits_on") else "off"
            alerts.append(c("¢ ", AMBER)
                          + c(f"{r['acct']} extra-usage spend ${spend_usd:,.2f}", AMBER)
                          + c(f" · toggle {state}", GRAY, dim=True))
    if alerts:
        print()
        for a in alerts:
            print("  " + a)
    # burn since last sweep — the snapshot becomes an instrument (Δ<0.5% stays silent;
    # a NEGATIVE delta means that limit reset in between)
    if prev and prev.get("ts") and prev.get("rows"):
        chips = []
        for r in rows:
            p = prev["rows"].get(r["acct"]) or {}
            for f, tag in (("session_pct", "5h"), ("weekly_pct", "weekly"), ("fable_pct", "Fable")):
                a, b = p.get(f), r.get(f)
                if a is not None and b is not None and abs(b - a) >= 0.5:
                    # 5h moves fast — only surface it when the window ROLLED OVER (negative
                    # delta = reset). Its growth is noise; weekly/Fable show both directions.
                    if f == "session_pct" and b >= a:
                        continue
                    chips.append(c(f"{r['acct']} {b - a:+.0f}% {tag}", GRAY))
        if chips:
            # _prev_snapshot deliberately ignores the TTL, so this stamp can be days old.
            # Reuse the same today/not-today switch _fmt_asof uses — a bare "since 02:45"
            # reads as this morning even when it is from last week.
            since = _fmt_asof(datetime.fromtimestamp(prev["ts"], timezone.utc).isoformat())
            print()
            print("  " + c("Δ ", PERI) + c(f"since {since}  ", GRAY, dim=True)
                  + c(" · ", GRAY, dim=True).join(chips))
    print()
    if win["active"] is None:
        wline = c("◌ ", RED) + c("Fable window UNKNOWN", RED, bold=True) \
            + c(" — model-config.yaml frontier_access unreadable", GRAY, dim=True)
    elif not win["active"]:
        wline = c("○ ", GRAY, dim=True) + c("Fable window inactive", GRAY, dim=True)
    elif win.get("permanent"):
        # No countdown: there is no deadline to press against. The old code derived one from
        # the far-future sentinel `end` and advertised "26826.6d left".
        wline = c("◉ ", GREEN) + c("Fable permanent", GREEN) \
            + c(" — standard Max/Team-Premium inclusion at ≤50% of weekly limits", GRAY)
    else:
        left_h = None
        if win["deadline"]:
            left_h = (win["deadline"] - datetime.now(timezone.utc)).total_seconds() / 3600.0
        # left_h stays truthful to the printed `end` date — subtracting the router's safety
        # margin here would contradict the date on the same line. The divergence that
        # mattered is the LABEL: score_fable stops routing Fable once left_h drops below
        # deadline_margin_h, so the footer must not still read "open" at that point.
        margin = cfg["frontier"].get("deadline_margin_h", 0.0)
        ended = (left_h is not None and left_h - margin <= 0
                 and not cfg["frontier"].get("credits_authorized"))
        urgent = left_h is not None and left_h < 24
        if ended:
            wline = c("○ ", RED) + c(f"Fable window ended {win['end']}", RED, bold=True) \
                + c(" — router excluding Fable", GRAY)
        else:
            wline = c("◉ ", AMBER if urgent else GREEN) \
                + c(f"Fable window open through {win['end']}", AMBER if urgent else GREEN) \
                + (c(f" · {fmt_h(left_h)} left", GRAY) if left_h is not None else "")
    print("  " + wline)
    # rank_g/rank_f/gre/fre were computed before the table so the picked row carries ➤.

    def route_line(label, lst, reasons, why_fields):
        head = c("➤ ", CLAY) + f"{label:<7} → "
        if not lst:
            return head + c("none", RED, bold=True) \
                + c("  " + " · ".join(f"{a} {w}" for a, w in reasons.items()), GRAY, dim=True)
        row = lst[0][1]
        why = " · ".join(w for w in (why_fields(row),) if w)
        # A COUNT, not the reason map: the footer lives in the same 82-col budget as the
        # table, and three "acct reason" pairs overflow it. The count is what changes the
        # read — "best of 4" and "best of the 1 we could see" are different claims — and the
        # per-account detail is already in the alert block above and --json route_reasons.
        excl = c(f"  · {len(reasons)} excluded", GRAY, dim=True) if reasons else ""
        return head + c(f"{row['acct']:<6}", CLAY, bold=True) + c(f" {why}", GRAY) + excl

    def fable_why(r):
        # Lead with the REASON. An imminent reset is a use-it-or-lose-it pick — naming it
        # first stops the "but that account is at 74%?!" first-read contradiction.
        frh = r.get("fable_reset_h")
        if frh is not None and frh < 24:
            return f"resets in {fmt_h(frh)} — use it or lose it · Fable {r['fable_pct']:.0f}%"
        return f"Fable {r['fable_pct']:.0f}% ↻ {fmt_h(frh)} · weekly {r['weekly_pct']:.0f}%"

    # DESK FIRST (W2.2): the one line on this surface that answers what the operator's next bare
    # `claude` will do. `desk` is 4 chars, inside route_line's f"{label:<7}", so the three lines
    # stay column-aligned and inside the 82-col budget without widening anything.
    print("  " + route_line("desk", rank_i, ire, lambda r: desk_why(r, cfg)))
    print("  " + route_line("general", rank_g, gre, lambda r:
          f"5h {r['session_pct']:.0f}% · weekly {r['weekly_pct']:.0f}% ↻ {fmt_h(r.get('weekly_reset_h'))}"))
    print("  " + route_line("fable", rank_f, fre, fable_why))
    # M7 pace: quota stranded at a weekly reset is the one unrecoverable waste, and nothing on
    # this surface said whether the fleet was on track to 100% until the reset had already
    # answered. Same 82-col budget: one line, soonest reset first.
    fl = fleet_pace_line(rows)              # the fleet's sum of those rows, as one conclusion
    if fl:
        print("  " + c(fl, AMBER))
    pl = pace_line(rows)
    if pl:
        for _pln in pl.split("\n"):          # S3: one row per account, sorted by pp at risk
            print("  " + c(_pln, GRAY))
    # A winner whose LOGIN dies inside the warn window is still the correct routing answer —
    # it genuinely has the headroom, and quota is what the scorer is for. But a session fired
    # onto it now can outlive its own credentials. The alert above states the fact; this
    # states the CONSEQUENCE, at the point where the decision is actually taken. Kept off the
    # route lines themselves: those are inside the 82-col budget and already carry a clause.
    seen = set()
    # rank_i FIRST and included at all (W2.4). Without it a pick that only the DESK lane makes —
    # and the desk lane is the one that NEVER yields the cliff term, so it is also the lane whose
    # winner is most likely to be lane-specific — carried a dying login with no warning on any
    # surface: the alert block above fires per-ROW, and this is the only place that says "the
    # account you are about to launch onto is the one whose credentials are expiring".
    for lst in (rank_i, rank_g, rank_f):
        w = lst[0][1] if lst else None
        lxh = w.get("login_expires_h") if w else None
        if w and lxh is not None and lxh <= login_warn_h and w["acct"] not in seen:
            seen.add(w["acct"])
            # Same lapsed-inside-the-TTL case: such a row keeps auth=ok and no error, so it is
            # still fully routable and can BE the winner. That is the loudest moment to say it.
            print("  " + c("  ⚠ ", AMBER)
                  + c(f"{w['acct']} is the pick, but its login "
                      + (f"EXPIRED {_fmt_when(w.get('login_expires_at'))}" if lxh <= 0 else
                         f"expires {_fmt_when(w.get('login_expires_at'))} ({fmt_h(lxh)})")
                      + " — /login first, or a long session outlives its credentials", AMBER)
                  # identity travels with the /login instruction — see the cliff bullet above
                  + c(f" · {w['email']}", GRAY, dim=True))
    if cached:
        print("  " + c(f"cache ≤{cfg.get('cache_ttl_s', 90)}s old · --fresh for a live sweep",
                       GRAY, dim=True))
    print()


# ---- relogin status board (RELOGIN_BUILD_CONTRACT §6) -----------------------------------------
# Thresholds mirror cc-relogin-poll's defaults (§5): the loop starts attempting at T-7d so one
# fragile attempt becomes ~168 chances to catch a k==0 window, and escalates at T-48h — loud
# BEFORE the deadline, never after. Read-only by construction: this surface never heals, never
# forces a sweep, and never touches a credential.
# The attempt window is SSOT'd in accounts.json (relogin_trigger_h), not owned here: this board
# only RENDERS the state, while cc-relogin-poll schedules inside it and cc-relogin's gate decides
# whether to act inside it. A constant owned by the renderer is a constant the actuator can drift
# from — measured 2026-07-29, when this board rendered DUE + "NEXT ACTION: cc-relogin next" for
# 72-168h while cc-relogin still gated on login_warn_h=72 and exited 2 REFUSED. This default is
# the fallback for a config that predates the key, and MUST stay equal to cc-relogin's.
RELOGIN_TRIGGER_H_DEFAULT = 7 * 24
RELOGIN_ESCALATE_H = 48
RELOGIN_POLL_LOG = os.environ.get(
    "CC_RELOGIN_POLL_LOG", os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "logs", "cc-relogin-poll.log"))
# The detection surface (--login-status / the login_expires_* row fields) is NOT on every build.
# When it is missing the answer is UNKNOWN + exit 3, never OK + exit 0: "cannot detect" must
# never read as "nothing to do". Precedence when accounts disagree — act-now beats cannot-see
# beats all-clear; UNKNOWN deliberately outranks OK.
RELOGIN_EXIT = {"ESCALATED": 2, "DUE": 1, "UNKNOWN": 3, "OK": 0}
RELOGIN_PRECEDENCE = ("ESCALATED", "DUE", "UNKNOWN", "OK")
# UNKNOWN = this row carries no login_expires_* at all. That USED to mean the build lacked the
# fields, and this string said so ("land feat/accounts-login-cliff"). The branch landed, so the
# only way a row reaches UNKNOWN on this build is that probe_account returned early on
# kstate != "present" (:588-594, before :603 sets the stamp) — i.e. the account's keychain could
# not be read — or the row came from a ledger written before the fields existed. Both are answered
# by a re-probe, and a persistent one is a broken account, not a missing feature.
RELOGIN_UNKNOWN_ACTION = "claude-accounts --fresh; if it persists, cc-relogin {acct}"


def relogin_last_attempt(acct: str, path: str = "") -> dict:
    """Last cc-relogin attempt for ONE account, from the poller's log; {} when unknowable.

    The line format belongs to cc-relogin-poll, so parse tolerantly: JSONL objects first (the
    house convention), else a plain-text line naming the account. Last match wins. A missing or
    unreadable log is UNKNOWN, never an error — the poller may simply never have run yet."""
    hit: dict = {}
    try:
        with open(path or RELOGIN_POLL_LOG, errors="replace") as f:
            for raw in f:
                line = raw.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    row = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    row = None
                if isinstance(row, dict):
                    if str(row.get("acct") or row.get("account") or row.get("name") or "") != acct:
                        continue
                    hit = {"ts": row.get("ts"),
                           "result": str(row.get("result") or row.get("outcome")
                                         or row.get("status") or "?")}
                elif re.search(r"\b%s\b" % re.escape(acct), line):
                    m = re.search(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}[T ][0-9:]{5,8}\S*", line)
                    hit = {"ts": m.group(0) if m else None, "result": line}
    except OSError:
        return {}
    return hit


def relogin_row(r: dict, trigger_h: float = RELOGIN_TRIGGER_H_DEFAULT) -> dict:
    """One account's renewal state, derived ONLY from fields actually present on this build."""
    acct = r["acct"]
    at, h = r.get("login_expires_at"), r.get("login_expires_h")
    if r.get("auth") in ("logged-out", "token-invalid"):
        # Already past the cliff, and visible from a field that exists on EVERY build — an
        # account that IS signed out must never render as UNKNOWN.
        state, reason = "ESCALATED", f"auth={r['auth']} — sign-in required now"
    elif at is None and h is None:
        state, reason = "UNKNOWN", "login_expires_* absent — detection surface missing"
    elif r.get("login_expired") or (h is not None and h <= 0):
        state, reason = "ESCALATED", "login window already expired"
    elif h is None:
        state, reason = "UNKNOWN", "login_expires_at without login_expires_h — cannot age it"
    elif h <= RELOGIN_ESCALATE_H:
        state, reason = "ESCALATED", f"T-{h:.0f}h — inside the {RELOGIN_ESCALATE_H}h escalation window"
    elif h <= trigger_h:
        state, reason = "DUE", f"T-{h:.0f}h — inside the {trigger_h / 24:.0f}d attempt window"
    else:
        state, reason = "OK", f"T-{h:.0f}h"
    last = relogin_last_attempt(acct)
    return {"acct": acct, "state": state, "reason": reason, "auth": r.get("auth"),
            "login_expires_at": at, "login_expires_h": h,
            "login_fixable": r.get("login_fixable"),
            "last_attempt_ts": last.get("ts"), "last_attempt_result": last.get("result"),
            "next_action": ("-" if state == "OK"
                            else RELOGIN_UNKNOWN_ACTION.format(acct=acct)
                            if state == "UNKNOWN" else f"cc-relogin {acct}")}


def render_relogin_status(rows: list, args: list, trigger_h: float = RELOGIN_TRIGGER_H_DEFAULT) -> int:
    """Render the board (plain always — a machine surface) and return the process exit code."""
    out = [relogin_row(r, trigger_h) for r in rows]
    worst = next((s for s in RELOGIN_PRECEDENCE if any(o["state"] == s for o in out)), "OK")
    code = RELOGIN_EXIT[worst]
    blind = [o["acct"] for o in out if o["state"] == "UNKNOWN"]
    if "--json" in args:
        print(json.dumps({"exit": code, "state": worst,
                          "detection": "unavailable" if blind else "available",
                          "rows": out}, indent=2))
    else:
        print(f"{'ACCT':<8} {'STATE':<10} {'EXPIRES':<26} {'HOURS':>6}  "
              f"{'LAST ATTEMPT':<32} NEXT ACTION")
        for o in out:
            h = "-" if o["login_expires_h"] is None else f"{o['login_expires_h']:.0f}"
            last = ("never" if not (o["last_attempt_ts"] or o["last_attempt_result"])
                    else f"{o['last_attempt_ts'] or '?'} {o['last_attempt_result'] or '?'}")
            print(f"{o['acct']:<8} {o['state']:<10} {str(o['login_expires_at'] or '-'):<26} "
                  f"{h:>6}  {last[:32]:<32} {o['next_action']}")
    if blind:
        # Loud, every run, naming the missing surface. The whole point of this mode is that a
        # status board which cannot see is worse than useless if it claims "all clear".
        print("claude-accounts --relogin-status: DETECTION-UNAVAILABLE — login_expires_at/"
              "login_expires_h absent for " + ", ".join(blind)
              + ". This build DOES emit them, so the probe could not read that account's "
                "keychain (or the row predates the fields): re-run with --fresh, and if it "
                "persists treat the account as broken. Reporting UNKNOWN, NOT OK.",
              file=sys.stderr)
    return code


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provider surface (non-Claude agent backends).
#
# ADDITIVE BY CONSTRUCTION. The Claude rows, the router footer, the --json field names, the
# --rank/--route/--login-status exit codes and the readout's columns are consumed by four callers
# and pinned by tests/claude-accounts-core.bats, so nothing below reshapes any of them: this is a
# SEPARATE SECTION with its own table, a new `providers` key alongside the existing --json ones,
# and a new flag. A provider registry that cannot be read yields [] and the Claude surface renders
# exactly as it did before — the dashboard must never go dark because a second vendor's file moved.
#
# WHY IT LIVES HERE and not in a sibling `cc-agents`: render_readout's own docstring records that
# it exists to BE the single renderer, having replaced ~200 lines of prose in commands/accounts.md
# that made the model rebuild the table by hand. A second program rendering "what agent capacity do
# I have" would recreate precisely the drift that function was written to delete. One renderer per
# artifact — so render_providers() below is the ONLY place provider rows are formatted, and both
# entry points (--readout appends it, --agents prints it alone) call that one function.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PROVIDERS_JSON_DEFAULT = "~/.claude/providers.json"


def load_providers(path=None):
    """Read the provider registry. Absent or malformed ⇒ [], never an exception.

    The Claude dashboard is the load-bearing surface; a provider registry is an accessory to it.
    An accessory that can take the whole readout down with a JSON typo would be a worse trade than
    not having it, so every failure here degrades to "no provider section" (same discipline as
    cache_read's non-dict → miss).
    """
    p = os.path.expanduser(path or os.environ.get("CC_PROVIDERS_JSON") or PROVIDERS_JSON_DEFAULT)
    try:
        with open(p) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return []
    provs = d.get("providers") if isinstance(d, dict) else None
    return provs if isinstance(provs, list) else []


def _provider_pin(pr):
    """The model actually pinned on disk, read from the provider's OWN config.

    Never the registry's opinion of what should be pinned: the whole point of the pin-proof rule
    is that a config file governs and a remembered value does not.
    """
    mp = pr.get("model_pin")
    if not isinstance(mp, dict) or not mp.get("path"):
        return None
    p = os.path.expanduser(mp["path"])
    try:
        if p.endswith(".toml"):
            txt = open(p).read()

            def grab(key):
                m = re.search(r'^\s*%s\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"' % re.escape(key), txt, re.M)
                return m.group(1) if m else None

            model = grab(mp.get("key") or "model")
            eff = grab(mp["effort_key"]) if mp.get("effort_key") else None
            return f"{model} @ {eff}" if (model and eff) else model
        with open(p) as f:
            cur = json.load(f)
        for part in (mp.get("key") or "").split("."):
            cur = cur.get(part) if isinstance(cur, dict) else None
        return cur if isinstance(cur, str) else None
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return None


# ---- provider probe cache ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# probe_provider() measured 3.68s of a 3.7s `claude-accounts --json` against the live registry (6
# providers, 8 child CLI processes), and every consumer re-paid it on every invocation —
# bin/cc-context, bin/cc-value, bin/cc-board, bin/cc-wave-plan, bin/cc-blockers.
#
# WHAT IS MEMOISED IS ONLY THE CHILD PROCESSES, never probe_provider() itself, and that is an
# invariant rather than an implementation detail. `pinned_model` is read from the provider's OWN
# config precisely so that a config file governs and a remembered value does not (_provider_pin's
# docstring), and `installed` must answer for the PATH as it is right now. A whole-probe cache
# would turn both into remembered values — the very defect the pin-proof rule exists to prevent.
# The suite already pins it: "the model pin is read from the provider's OWN config" edits a
# provider's settings.json between two runs and demands the render change, with the registry
# untouched. That test is this cache's standing control; it must stay green.
#
# The key carries everything that can change a child's answer: the argv, the resolved binary's
# identity (realpath + mtime + size, so an upgrade or reinstall invalidates), and the registry's
# own mtime + size (so editing providers.json invalidates every probe — the invalidation the work
# item asked for, and the reason a registry edit is visible on the very next call).
PROVIDER_CACHE_TTL_S = 900
# A FAILED probe expires far sooner than a good one. Caching failures at all is deliberate: a
# provider whose CLI hangs costs its full `timeout_s` on EVERY invocation, which is most of what
# this cache exists to remove. But a transient failure must not pin a wrong auth_state for a
# quarter of an hour, so recovery is visible within about one sweep instead.
PROVIDER_CACHE_FAIL_TTL_S = 60


def _provider_cache_path():
    """Where the memo lives. HOME-derived, so a fixtured $HOME is isolated by construction —
    the suite pins HOME before it pins anything else, and a cache keyed off /tmp would have made
    every provider test a function of the operator's real registry."""
    return os.environ.get("CC_PROVIDER_CACHE") or os.path.join(
        HOME, ".claude", "cache", "provider-probe.json")


def _provider_cache_key(argv):
    parts = ["\x00".join(argv)]
    reg = os.path.expanduser(os.environ.get("CC_PROVIDERS_JSON") or PROVIDERS_JSON_DEFAULT)
    for p in ((argv[0] if argv else ""), reg):
        try:
            st = os.stat(p)
            parts.append(f"{os.path.realpath(p)}|{st.st_mtime_ns}|{st.st_size}")
        except OSError:
            parts.append("absent")
    return sha256("|".join(parts).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]


def _provider_cache_load():
    """Malformed, unreadable, or valid-JSON-but-not-an-object ⇒ {}, never an exception. Same
    discipline as cache_read: an accessory store must degrade to a miss, never wedge the tool."""
    try:
        with open(_provider_cache_path()) as f:
            c = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return {}
    return c if isinstance(c, dict) else {}


def _provider_cache_store(entries):
    path = _provider_cache_path()
    tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}.tmp"
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path) or ".", exist_ok=True)
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(entries, f)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass


def _provider_cmd(argv, timeout_s, fresh=False):
    """A provider's own CLI, run at most once per TTL. → (rc, stdout, stderr, ran).

    `ran` is False when the child could not be run to completion at all (what the callers' except
    arms used to see). It is a returned FIELD rather than a raised exception so that a failure is
    cacheable in its own right, at its own shorter TTL.
    """
    argv = list(argv)
    key = _provider_cache_key(argv)
    entries = _provider_cache_load()
    if not fresh:
        e = entries.get(key)
        if isinstance(e, dict) and isinstance(e.get("ts"), (int, float)):
            ttl = PROVIDER_CACHE_TTL_S if e.get("ran") else PROVIDER_CACHE_FAIL_TTL_S
            # The lower bound rejects an entry stamped in the FUTURE: a clock step backwards
            # would otherwise pin it until the clock caught up, which for a 900s TTL can be
            # arbitrarily long.
            if 0 <= time.time() - e["ts"] <= ttl:
                return e.get("rc"), e.get("stdout") or "", e.get("stderr") or "", bool(e.get("ran"))
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout_s)
        res = {"rc": r.returncode, "stdout": r.stdout or "", "stderr": r.stderr or "", "ran": True}
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        res = {"rc": None, "stdout": "", "stderr": "", "ran": False}
    entries[key] = dict(res, ts=time.time())
    # Bounded on the way out. Every key rotates whenever the argv, the binary, or the registry
    # changes, so an upgraded binary would otherwise leave its predecessor's row behind forever;
    # dropping anything past the longer TTL keeps the store the size of the live registry.
    cutoff = time.time() - PROVIDER_CACHE_TTL_S
    entries = {k: v for k, v in entries.items()
               if isinstance(v, dict) and isinstance(v.get("ts"), (int, float))
               and v["ts"] >= cutoff}
    _provider_cache_store(entries)
    return res["rc"], res["stdout"], res["stderr"], res["ran"]


def probe_provider(pr, timeout_s=8.0, fresh=False):
    """One provider → its LIVE state, merged onto its registry facts.

    Three separate questions, deliberately not collapsed (see providers.json _the_detection_rule):
    `installed` (binary on PATH), `auth_state` (credentials usable), and `routable` (the registry's
    `headless` field — does a non-interactive agent mode exist at all). Only the third decides
    whether this fleet can send work there, and it is the one a presence check silently skips.
    """
    out = dict(pr)
    exe = shutil.which(pr.get("bin") or "") if pr.get("bin") else None
    out["installed"] = bool(exe)
    out["exe"] = exe
    out["version"] = None
    out["pinned_model"] = _provider_pin(pr) if exe else None
    # routable = has a headless mode AND the registry cleared it on cost. in_scope already
    # encodes the cost verdict, so a cost-failed provider can never render as routable.
    out["routable"] = bool(pr.get("headless")) and bool(pr.get("in_scope"))

    if not exe:
        out["auth_state"] = "absent"
        return out

    _, vso, vse, vran = _provider_cmd([exe] + list(pr.get("version_args") or ["--version"]),
                                      timeout_s, fresh=fresh)
    if vran:
        lines = [ln.strip() for ln in (vso + vse).splitlines() if ln.strip()]
        out["version"] = lines[0] if lines else None
    else:
        out["version"] = None

    a = pr.get("auth")
    if not isinstance(a, dict):
        out["auth_state"] = "unknown"
        return out

    if a.get("kind") == "json_file":
        try:
            with open(os.path.expanduser(a.get("path") or "")) as f:
                d = json.load(f)
        except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
            out["auth_state"] = "unreadable"
        else:
            # An API key present is not a health state — it is a BILLING state, and the one the
            # cost gate cares about, so it gets its own label rather than folding into "ok".
            akf = a.get("api_key_field")
            if akf and str(d.get(akf) or "").strip():
                out["auth_state"] = "api-key"
            else:
                blob = d.get("tokens") if isinstance(d.get("tokens"), dict) else d
                blob = blob if isinstance(blob, dict) else {}
                out["auth_state"] = "ok" if any(blob.get(k) for k in
                                                ("access_token", "refresh_token")) else "logged-out"
    elif a.get("kind") == "cmd_json":
        cmd = list(a.get("cmd") or [])
        exe2 = shutil.which(cmd[0]) if cmd else None
        if not exe2:
            out["auth_state"] = "absent"
        else:
            _, aso, _ase, aran = _provider_cmd([exe2] + cmd[1:], timeout_s, fresh=fresh)
            if not aran:
                out["auth_state"] = "unreadable"
            else:
                try:
                    j = json.loads(aso.strip() or "{}")
                    st = j.get("status")
                    out["auth_state"] = ("ok" if st == "ready"
                                         else (j.get("reason") or st or "not-ready"))
                # AttributeError: valid JSON that is not an OBJECT (a bare list or string) reached
                # .get() and raised out of a probe whose whole job is to report a state — the same
                # non-dict hazard cache_read was fixed for.
                except (json.JSONDecodeError, AttributeError):
                    out["auth_state"] = "unreadable"
    else:
        out["auth_state"] = "unknown"
    return out


def render_providers(provs):
    """Provider rows → finished markdown lines. THE single renderer for this section.

    Returns a list of lines rather than printing, so --readout can append it and --agents can
    print it alone without a second formatter existing anywhere.
    """
    if not provs:
        return []
    out = ["", "**Agent backends beyond Claude** — registry `~/.claude/providers.json`",
           "",
           "| backend | routable | version | auth | plan | bills outside it? | model pinned |",
           "|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|"]
    skips = []
    for p in provs:
        label = p.get("label") or p.get("name") or "?"
        # Reason precedence matters: a provider can fail several ways at once, and naming the
        # wrong one is worse than naming none. The registry's own skip_reason is authoritative
        # when it exists — Grok has no headless mode measured AND fails the cost gate, and
        # rendering "no headless mode" there would misreport WHY it is not wired.
        if p.get("routable"):
            route = "✅"
        elif p.get("skip_reason"):
            route = "⊘ skipped"
        elif not p.get("headless"):
            route = "✖ no headless mode"
        else:
            route = "⊘ skipped"
        ver = p.get("version") or ("—" if p.get("installed") else "not installed")
        auth = {"ok": "ok", "absent": "—", "api-key": "🚨 API key (metered)"}.get(
            p.get("auth_state"), p.get("auth_state") or "UNKNOWN")
        plan = p.get("plan") or "UNKNOWN"
        b = p.get("bills_outside_plan")
        bills = "🚨 **YES**" if b is True else ("no" if b is False else "UNKNOWN")
        pin = p.get("pinned_model") or "—"
        if p.get("pinned_model") and p.get("proven_by"):
            pin += " ✓proven"
        elif p.get("pinned_model"):
            pin += " ⚠unproven"
        out.append(f"| {label} | {route} | {ver} | {auth} | {plan} | {bills} | {pin} |")
        if not p.get("in_scope") and p.get("skip_reason"):
            skips.append(f"⊘ `{p.get('name')}` — {p['skip_reason']}")

    for s in skips:
        out.append("- " + s)

    routable = [p for p in provs if p.get("routable")]
    ready = [p for p in routable if p.get("auth_state") == "ok"]
    out.append("")
    out.append(f"➤ non-Claude backends ready now: **{len(ready)} of {len(routable)} routable** "
               f"({len(provs)} known)")
    # The distinction the whole registry exists to preserve: a backend riding a subscription we
    # ALREADY hold is capacity; one that bills per token is a meter, and it is never rendered as
    # though it were the same thing.
    if any(p.get("bills_outside_plan") for p in provs):
        out.append("- 🚨 rows marked **YES** bill OUTSIDE a plan we hold — not wired, by policy "
                   "(`accounts.json spend.usage_credits_authorized=false`)")
    return out


def render_providers_brief(provs):
    """The provider section as the chat readout's footer lines (2026-10-18). The full 7-column
    table stays one command away (`--agents`, same render_providers); what must stay on screen is
    what is ready to take work now, and which backends bill OUTSIDE a plan we hold. Line 1 is who
    is ready; 🚨 and ⚠ lines follow only for an exception (a routable backend that bills outside
    a plan or might, or one on a metered API key); the standing not-wired set closes it."""
    if not provs:
        return []
    def _label(p):
        return p.get("label") or p.get("name") or "?"
    def _and(xs):
        return xs[0] if len(xs) == 1 else ", ".join(xs[:-1]) + " and " + xs[-1]
    routable = [p for p in provs if p.get("routable")]
    # READY means it can take work now AND it is known to run inside a plan we hold: `routable`
    # keys on headless + in_scope only and never reads the billing field, so a routable backend
    # whose billing is True or unknown used to be called "ready now" (2026-10-18).
    ready = [p for p in routable if p.get("auth_state") == "ok" and p.get("installed", True)
             and p.get("bills_outside_plan") is False]
    # An API key is a BILLING state, not a broken login (probe_provider): render_providers flags
    # it 🚨 metered, so the brief names it as money too rather than as "auth not ok".
    keyed = [p for p in provs if p.get("auth_state") == "api-key"]
    r_bills = [p for p in routable if p.get("bills_outside_plan") is True and p not in keyed]
    r_unknown = [p for p in routable if p.get("bills_outside_plan") is None and p not in keyed]
    other = [p for p in routable
             if p not in ready and p not in keyed and p not in r_bills and p not in r_unknown]
    # The cause, not "auth not ok": probe_provider reports a missing binary as auth `absent`,
    # and installing is a different fix from signing in.
    missing = sum(1 for p in other if not p.get("installed", True))
    causes = ([f"{missing} not installed"] if missing else []) \
        + ([f"{len(other) - missing} not signed in"] if len(other) - missing else [])
    if ready:
        head = ", ".join(f"**{_label(p)}**" for p in ready)
        if causes:
            head += f" (also routable: {', '.join(causes)})"
    else:
        head = f"none of {len(routable)} routable" + (f" ({', '.join(causes)})" if causes else "")
    out = [f"Other agents ready now: {head} — all {len(provs)} in `claude-accounts --agents`"]
    money = []
    if r_bills:
        money.append(f"{_and([_label(p) for p in r_bills])} "
                     f"{'is' if len(r_bills) == 1 else 'are'} ROUTABLE and "
                     f"bill{'s' if len(r_bills) == 1 else ''} OUTSIDE a plan we hold")
    if keyed:
        money.append(f"{_and([_label(p) for p in keyed])} "
                     f"{'is' if len(keyed) == 1 else 'are'} on a metered API key")
    if money:
        out.append("🚨 " + " · ".join(money))
    if r_unknown:
        out.append(f"⚠ {_and([_label(p) for p in r_unknown])} "
                   f"{'is' if len(r_unknown) == 1 else 'are'} routable, but whether "
                   f"{'it bills' if len(r_unknown) == 1 else 'they bill'} outside a plan is unknown")
    # THE STANDING SET carries no alarm glyph (2026-10-18): it printed 🚨 on every run, and an
    # alarm that always fires carries no signal. Bold names keep a label with its own ` · `
    # (`Pi · Claude backend`) from reading as two clauses.
    standing = [_label(p) for p in provs
                if p.get("bills_outside_plan") is True and not p.get("routable")]
    if standing:
        out.append(f"Not wired, because {'it' if len(standing) == 1 else 'they'} would bill "
                   f"outside our plans: {_and([f'**{x}**' for x in standing])}")
    return out


def _startup_health_fold():
    """The keepwarm tick's second job: fold the startup-health fleet line for the accounts board.

    docs/research/startup-hooks-2026-10-18/README.md §5 unit 3.5. `cc-startup-health --fleet` writes
    ~/.claude/state/startup-health/fleet-line.txt, which hooks/accounts-board.sh only `cat`s, so the
    fold runs here, off every hook path, on a job that already ticks every 180 s (no new daemon,
    README §6). Resolved beside this file's REAL path, so it runs the same checkout as this tick and
    needs no ~/.claude/bin link. Bounded and fully swallowed: a slow or broken fold costs the line
    (which then reads "fold stale" on the board), never this tick's cache or board.
    """
    if os.environ.get("CC_STARTUP_HEALTH_FOLD", "1") == "0":
        return
    try:
        # __file__ is absent when this program is exec'd as `python3 -c` (tests/accounts-board.bats).
        me = globals().get("__file__") or sys.argv[0]
        tool = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(me)), "cc-startup-health")
        if os.access(tool, os.X_OK):
            subprocess.run([tool, "--fleet"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                           stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=30, check=False)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — the board line is best-effort by design
        pass


def main():
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    if "-h" in args or "--help" in args:
        print(__doc__)
        return
    # --wire: force the unified-header read on EVERY account, not just the ones whose endpoint
    # integer is ambiguous. Set here, on the main thread, before any fan-out reads it.
    global WIRE_FORCE
    if "--wire" in args:
        WIRE_FORCE = True
    # --agents: the provider section ALONE, and deliberately the FIRST branch — it returns before
    # load_cfg(), before the keychain, before the usage endpoint and before the lock. Two reasons,
    # and the second is the one that bit: (1) asking "what else can I route to" must never block
    # behind a 4-account sweep or force one; (2) it must not require a Claude accounts.json to
    # exist at all. Placed after load_cfg() this exited non-zero the moment $HOME was fixtured,
    # which is exactly how a hermetic test found it — a provider question depending on Claude
    # config is a coupling, not a convenience.
    if "--agents" in args:
        # `"--fresh" in args` spelled inline at all three provider call sites rather than read from
        # the `fresh` local: this branch deliberately returns BEFORE that local is bound (it must
        # answer without load_cfg()), and one spelling that works everywhere beats two that differ
        # by position in the file.
        provs = [probe_provider(p, fresh="--fresh" in args) for p in load_providers()]
        if "--json" in args:
            print(json.dumps({"providers": provs}, indent=2))
            return
        lines = render_providers(provs)
        if not lines:
            print("no provider registry (~/.claude/providers.json) — Claude-only fleet")
            return
        print("\n".join(lines).lstrip("\n"))
        return

    # --strand-score: M3c, and it sits HERE — before load_cfg(), before the keychain, before the
    # usage endpoint and before the lock — for the same reason --agents does. It scores HISTORY
    # off the utilization series alone; it must never force a 4-account sweep, and it must answer
    # on a box with no accounts.json at all (which is exactly how a hermetic test can run it).
    #
    # L3 EXCEPTION, ARGUED. L3 says there is ONE renderer of "what are the accounts doing now".
    # This renders no live state: it renders scored PAST windows, nothing routes on it, and it is
    # the mutation-test of the planner rather than a second opinion about the fleet.
    #
    # A FLAG, not a new scripts/*.sh, and that is deployment truth rather than taste:
    # ~/.claude/bin/claude-accounts is an existing symlink into the checkout, so a flag is an EDIT
    # that rides it and converges on the trunk fast-forward. A new file is an ADD with no symlink
    # — absent from every path the live layer can reach until the converger runs.
    if "--strand-score" in args:
        hours = _num_flag(args, "--hours") or STRAND_SCORE_LOOKBACK_H
        sam, _span = _util_tail(hours=hours)
        sc = strand_score(sam)
        if "--json" in args:
            print(json.dumps({"hours": hours, "windows": sc["windows"], "accts": sc["accts"],
                              "buckets": sc["buckets"], "cells": sc["cells"]}, indent=2))
            return
        print("\n".join(render_strand_score(sc, hours)))
        return

    # --stranded [ACCT]: the realised half of --strand-score, as its own answer. Same placement,
    # same reasons — history off the utilization series alone, no sweep, no accounts.json.
    if "--stranded" in args:
        i = args.index("--stranded")
        tok = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) and not args[i + 1].startswith("--") else None
        acct = _stranded_acct(tok) if tok is not None else None
        if tok is not None and acct is None:
            print(f"claude-accounts: --stranded takes an account ({'|'.join(STRANDED_ACCTS)} "
                  f"or 1-4; got {tok!r})", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(64)
        hours = _num_flag(args, "--hours") or STRAND_SCORE_LOOKBACK_H
        sam, _span = _util_tail(hours=hours)
        wins = [w for w in stranded_windows(sam) if acct is None or w["acct"] == acct]
        if "--json" in args:
            print(json.dumps({"hours": hours, "acct": acct, "windows": wins}, indent=2))
            return
        print("\n".join(render_stranded(wins, hours, acct)))
        return

    # --census-ab-report: the Phase 3 soak verdict (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D1). Before load_cfg()
    # for the --strand-score reasons: it reads the event log alone and must never force a sweep.
    if "--census-ab-report" in args:
        sys.exit(census_ab_report())

    # --local-refresh: the 20 s local job's pass (ACCOUNTS_BOARD_FRESHNESS D7). Before the global
    # modifiers: it loads cfg without resolving the claude binary, and its kill switch answers
    # before cfg is read at all.
    if "--local-refresh" in args:
        sys.exit(local_refresh_main())

    # --note-bank ACCT --left N --expires ISO [--source WHO]: record one read of an account's bank
    # of limit resets (see bank_path). A file write only — no sweep, no network, no lock.
    if "--note-bank" in args:
        sys.exit(note_bank_main(args))

    # --max-wait / --max-age are GLOBAL modifiers, parsed before load_cfg() because --max-wait 0
    # also decides whether load_cfg may fork the claude-bin resolver. Shaped exactly like
    # `--login-status --window-h N`: positional value, malformed ⇒ exit 64, absent ⇒ None ⇒ every
    # existing caller byte-identical. Deliberately NOT a `--route-cached` flag — that would need a
    # `--rank-cached` twin, and the doubling is the tell that it belongs on the axis, not the verb.
    global _CLI, _UTIL_MEMO_ON
    _CLI = True
    # D8: the _util_tail memo serves the board-rendering verbs only; --route/--rank stay plain.
    _UTIL_MEMO_ON = "--keepwarm" in args or "--readout" in args
    max_wait = _num_flag(args, "--max-wait")
    max_age = _num_flag(args, "--max-age")
    cache_only = max_wait is not None and max_wait <= 0
    cfg = load_cfg(need_claude_bin=not cache_only)
    fresh = "--fresh" in args
    if fresh and cache_only:
        sys.exit("claude-accounts: --fresh and --max-wait 0 are contradictory "
                 "(--fresh must sweep; --max-wait 0 forbids it)")
    # --relogin-status is a read-only status surface: it reports what the renewal loop should
    # do, so it must never perform an account operation itself as a side effect of being read.
    no_heal = "--no-heal" in args or "--relogin-status" in args

    if "--keepwarm" in args:
        # PRODUCER for com.claude.accounts-keepwarm. Its whole job is to keep the shared cache
        # inside its TTL so that readers on the interactive launch path (--max-wait 0) find
        # something to serve instead of abstaining.
        #
        # A FLAG, not a new scripts/*.sh, and that is a deployment fact rather than taste:
        # ~/.claude/bin/claude-accounts already exists as a symlink into the checkout, so a flag
        # is an EDIT that rides it and converges on the trunk fast-forward. A new script file is
        # an ADD with no symlink — absent from every path the box can reach — so the plist's
        # ProgramArguments would point at nothing and the job would fail every single tick.
        #
        # REFRESH-AHEAD, not refresh-on-expiry. Sweeping only once the cache has already expired
        # leaves a hole: with StartInterval 60 against a 90s TTL, a tick at t=60 sees age 60 (<90,
        # no sweep) and the next is at t=120 — so the cache is stale for the whole t=90..120
        # window and every reader in it abstains. Passing `--max-age <interval/2>` makes the
        # producer refresh while the cache is still valid, which is the only arrangement where a
        # consumer never sees an expired one.
        #
        # Deliberately NOT --no-heal: cache_read refuses a no-heal payload to a heal-wanting
        # caller whenever any row is BAD_AUTH, so a no-heal producer would poison the cache for
        # every other consumer exactly on the day one account is logged out — the day it matters.
        # A WALL-CLOCK DEADLINE, enforced by the kernel. launchd never starts a second tick while
        # one is running, so a producer that wedges holding the single-flight lock blocks routing
        # machine-wide for as long as it lives: measured 2026-09-24, one tick sat 52 min at 0.43 s
        # of CPU inside the 3.14 interpreter (_PyThreadState_Attach under an `_strptime` import),
        # every `--rank` exited 5 after its 240 s wait, and a limit recovery PARKED on "no routable
        # target". The out-log had 14 ticks over 30 min and 131 over 240 s. SIGALRM is left at
        # SIG_DFL, so the kill needs no cooperation from a wedged interpreter; the kernel closes
        # the lock fd, and cache writes are os.replace'd, so a killed tick leaves the previous
        # cache intact for the next one. Default sits under the readers' 240 s lock wait.
        # CC_KEEPWARM_DEADLINE_S=0 disables it.
        try:
            _kw_deadline = int(os.environ.get("CC_KEEPWARM_DEADLINE_S", "225"))
        except ValueError:
            _kw_deadline = 225
        if _kw_deadline > 0:
            signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, signal.SIG_DFL)
            signal.alarm(_kw_deadline)
        t0 = time.time()
        # RESET EVIDENCE OUTRANKS THE AGE SKIP. A cache younger than --max-age is served — unless
        # some account's last good read is now KNOWN to be pre-reset (a stamp passed): then that cache is wrong in a way its age cannot show, and
        # this tick sweeps so the board and the router stop carrying it. A throttled account is
        # still not polled (its backoff holds); its wire substitute is what this buys.
        pending = reset_evidence_pending(cfg)
        rows, wj, was_cached, _prev = get_data(cfg, fresh=bool(pending), no_heal=no_heal,
                                               max_age=max_age)
        # ---- ALSO the board producer (W3.6), and a FLAG on this job rather than a new script for
        # the deployment reason the block above already gives: ~/.claude/bin/claude-accounts is a
        # symlink into the checkout, so extending this verb is an EDIT that rides the link and goes
        # live on the trunk fast-forward, while a new scripts/*.sh is an ADD with no symlink —
        # absent from every path the box can reach, so the plist would exec nothing forever.
        #
        # And it must be produced HERE rather than read on demand, because `--readout` measured
        # 5.33s against a 5s hook timeout (§2.7): a SessionStart hook that called this program
        # would recreate the very latency bug W0 just removed. The hook is a file read; this job,
        # already running every 60s for the cache, is where the 5s can be afforded.
        #
        # mark_self is deliberately NOT applied. It resolves "← you" from the CURRENT process's
        # config dir — which for a launchd producer is launchd's, and for the readers is four
        # different accounts. A shared artifact cannot carry a per-reader fact, and a wrong one
        # here would point the arrow at somebody else's account.
        # D8: render from the COMMITTED cache — the overlay-merged rows cache_commit wrote — so
        # keepwarm can never overwrite a newer local board with the sweep's pre-overlay rows. The
        # mtime is still stamped now: it is the hook's producer-liveness signal.
        _c = _read_cache_any_age(cfg)
        _src = _c["rows"] if _c else rows
        board = write_board(cfg, _render_board(cfg, _src, wj, was_cached,
                                               quota_ts=(_c or {}).get("ts")),
                            sig=_board_sig(_src), rendered_from_ts=(_c or {}).get("ts"),
                            producer="keepwarm",
                            variants=_board_variants(cfg, _src, wj, was_cached,
                                                     quota_ts=(_c or {}).get("ts")),
                            ladder=_board_ladder(cfg, _src, wj, was_cached,
                                                 quota_ts=(_c or {}).get("ts")))
        age = _quota_age_s(cfg)
        print(f"keepwarm: {'served-cache' if was_cached else 'swept'} rows={len(rows)} "
              f"reset_pending={','.join(pending) or '-'} "
              f"age_s={age if age is not None else '-'} "
              f"board={board or 'FAILED'} "
              f"took_ms={int((time.time() - t0) * 1000)}")
        _startup_health_fold()
        return

    if "--relogin-info" in args:
        if args.index("--relogin-info") + 1 >= len(args):
            sys.exit("claude-accounts: --relogin-info requires an account name")
        name = args[args.index("--relogin-info") + 1]
        acct = next((a for a in cfg["accounts"] if a["name"] == name), None)
        if not acct:
            sys.exit(f"unknown account: {name} ({'|'.join(a['name'] for a in cfg['accounts'])})")
        dia_dir = None
        try:
            ls = json.load(open(cfg["dia_local_state"]))
            dia_dir = {v.get("name"): k for k, v in ls["profile"]["info_cache"].items()}.get(acct["dia_profile"])
        except (OSError, KeyError, json.JSONDecodeError):
            pass
        # file_fallback=False: cc-relogin and handoff-fire gate on THE KEYCHAIN's state, so a
        # .credentials.json fallback must never make a missing item read present here.
        creds, kstate = read_creds(acct["config_dir"], cfg["keychain_account"], file_fallback=False)
        print(json.dumps({**{k: v for k, v in acct.items()}, "dia_profile_dir": dia_dir,
                          "keychain_service": keychain_service(acct["config_dir"]),
                          "keychain_state": kstate, "claude_bin": cfg["claude_bin"],
                          "oauth_scopes": cfg["oauth_scopes"],
                          "has_refresh_token": bool(creds and creds.get("refreshToken")),
                          # PRESENT is not USABLE. The relogin skill's Phase 1 (headless
                          # refresh-token grant) cannot succeed once this stamp has passed,
                          # so a caller that gates only on has_refresh_token spends 90s
                          # proving what this field already knows. Phase 2 (browser OAuth)
                          # is the only path from here.
                          "refresh_token_expires_at": _iso_ms(
                              creds and creds.get("refreshTokenExpiresAt")),
                          "refresh_token_expired": _expired_ms(
                              creds and creds.get("refreshTokenExpiresAt"))}, indent=2))
        return

    # RESET TELEMETRY READBACK — a file read, no network, dispatched before the get_data anchor.
    # Resets are detected by the sweep itself (watch_resets); nobody records one by hand.
    if "--reset-report" in args:
        days = 30.0
        if "--days" in args:
            i = args.index("--days")
            try:
                days = float(args[i + 1])
                if not (days > 0 and math.isfinite(days)):
                    raise ValueError
            except (IndexError, ValueError):
                print("claude-accounts: --days needs a positive number", file=sys.stderr)
                sys.exit(64)
        sys.exit(reset_report(days))

    # FLEET_V2 C5: batch recovery placement. Cache-only by construction (cmd_place reads with
    # max_wait 0), so it is dispatched before the get_data anchor below and can never sweep.
    if "--place" in args:
        sys.exit(cmd_place(args, cfg))
    # D2b (W16): the late-band verdict, cache-only for the same reason.
    if "--shed-band" in args:
        sys.exit(cmd_shed_band(args, cfg))

    # M7: record a fire-time assignment and exit — a WRITE, deliberately dispatched BEFORE the
    # get_data anchor below so telling the router about a fire can never itself trigger a sweep
    # (the caller is handoff-fire mid-launch; a 90s sweep here would sit inside every fire).
    if "--assign" in args:
        i = args.index("--assign")
        name = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
        names = [a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]]
        if name not in names:
            # 64 = usage, same contract as --window-h: a misspelled account must never be a
            # silent no-op — the caller believes it spread the load when it charged nobody.
            print(f"claude-accounts: --assign requires an account name "
                  f"({'|'.join(names)}; got {name!r})", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(64)
        src = None
        if "--src" in args:
            j = args.index("--src")
            src = args[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(args) else None

        def _opt(flag, conv):
            if flag not in args:
                return None
            j = args.index(flag)
            try:
                return conv(args[j + 1])
            except (IndexError, ValueError):
                raise ValueError(f"{flag} needs a value of the right type "
                                 f"(got {args[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(args) else None!r})")
        try:
            err = record_assignment(cfg, name, src, id=_opt("--id", str), sid=_opt("--sid", str),
                                    w=_opt("--w", int), ttl_s=_opt("--ttl-s", float))
        except ValueError as e:
            print(f"claude-accounts: --assign: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(64)
        if err:
            print(f"claude-accounts: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(5)
        return
    # v2 ledger writes (lr-reconciler) — same pre-sweep placement and exit contract as --assign:
    # 64 = a malformed charge (nothing written), 5 = the append itself failed.
    for verb in ("--assign-many", "--unassign"):
        if verb not in args:
            continue
        i = args.index(verb)
        arg = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
        try:
            if verb == "--unassign":
                err = record_void(arg)
            else:
                if arg is None:
                    raise ValueError("needs a JSONL file path")
                try:
                    with open(arg) as f:
                        text = f.read()
                except OSError as e:
                    print(f"claude-accounts: --assign-many: cannot read {arg}: {e}",
                          file=sys.stderr)
                    sys.exit(5)
                err = append_assignments(parse_assign_many(cfg, text))
        except ValueError as e:
            print(f"claude-accounts: {verb}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(64)
        if err:
            print(f"claude-accounts: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(5)
        return

    # `--rank/--route … --src <acct>`: the account the caller is moving work OFF (W6 resolution 11).
    # Set as the module default rather than passed to get_data, because the call below is a
    # source-patch anchor and must stay byte-identical. Name or config dir, like --place's `src`.
    if ("--rank" in args or "--route" in args) and "--src" in args:
        global CACHE_SRC
        j = args.index("--src")
        sv = args[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(args) else None
        dirs = {os.path.expanduser(a["config_dir"]).rstrip("/"): a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
        names = {a["name"] for a in cfg["accounts"]}
        sname = sv if sv in names else dirs.get(os.path.expanduser(sv or "").rstrip("/"))
        if sname is None:
            print(f"claude-accounts: --src requires an account name or config dir "
                  f"({'|'.join(sorted(names))}; got {sv!r})", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(64)
        CACHE_SRC = (sname,)
    # NOTE FOR EDITORS: the next line is an ANCHOR. tests/claude-accounts-core.bats patches this
    # file's SOURCE by exact string match on it (to stub rows without a live sweep), so changing its
    # text or its indentation silently turns that patch into a no-op and the test then exercises a
    # different program than it claims to. That is why FreshLockWedged is handled at the __main__
    # entry instead of wrapped around this call. Keep it byte-identical.
    # 🚨 SOURCE-PATCH ANCHOR — keep this call on ONE line, verbatim.
    # tests/claude-accounts-core.bats replaces this exact string to inject fixture rows. A literal
    # replace of a string that is no longer there is a SILENT no-op: the patch does not apply, the
    # test runs the unpatched program against live data, and it fails for a reason that has nothing
    # to do with what it is testing. Reformatting this across two lines did exactly that (2026-08-10)
    # — the same trap the FreshLockWedged handler at the bottom of this file already documents.
    rows, wj, cached, prev = get_data(cfg, fresh, no_heal, max_wait, max_age)
    mark_self(rows, cfg)                          # per-invocation: MUST be after the cache read
    refresh_login_countdown(rows)                 # ditto — a cached countdown is a stale one
    apply_assignments(rows, cfg)                  # ditto — phantoms must act INSIDE the cache TTL
    apply_burn(rows, cfg)                         # burn/pace rates from the utilization series
    win = win_from_json(wj)

    # Before the generic --json branch: `--relogin-status --json` is its own shape, not the
    # dashboard's rows.
    if "--relogin-status" in args:
        sys.exit(render_relogin_status(
            rows, args, float(cfg.get("relogin_trigger_h", RELOGIN_TRIGGER_H_DEFAULT))))

    if "--json" in args:
        for r in rows:
            g, gr = score_general(r, cfg)
            f, fr_ = score_fable(r, cfg, win)
            # score_interactive rides here for the same reason the readout gained a desk line
            # (W2.3): a lane emitted by no machine surface is a lane no consumer can check. The
            # keys are ADDITIVE — `score_general`/`score_fable` and both maps keep their names,
            # shapes and meanings, so every existing consumer is untouched.
            i, ir = score_interactive(r, cfg)
            r["score_general"], r["score_fable"] = g, f
            r["score_interactive"] = i
            # The desk lane's own decision inputs, as DATA rather than as a number a consumer
            # would have to re-derive from the score: which rung of the degradation ladder this
            # row sits on, and whether hysteresis is holding the desk here.
            r["desk_tier"] = desk_keys(r, cfg)[2]
            r["route_reasons"] = {"general": gr, "fable": fr_, "interactive": ir}
            # The band is emitted as its own field, not left implicit in a reason string:
            # cc-route records it per decision (route.jsonl cliff_band) so "did routing see the
            # cliff?" is answerable from disk rather than inferred from prose.
            r["cliff_band"] = cliff_band(r)
            # Parallel map, not a reshape of route_reasons: the reason strings are prose
            # (one carries an emoji), so a consumer classifying them had to string-match.
            # None where the account routed — there is nothing to classify.
            r["route_reason_class"] = {
                "general": reason_class(r, gr) if gr else None,
                "fable": reason_class(r, fr_) if fr_ else None,
                "interactive": reason_class(r, ir) if ir else None}
            # M7 pace surface: what the account needs to burn/day to finish its week at 100%.
            # burn_wk_ppd (the measured recent rate) already rides the row when the series has
            # enough span; the pair is the machine form of the renderers' pace line.
            r["weekly_need_pct_per_day"] = pace_need_ppd(r)
        # s_cut is config, not per-account state, so it rides at top level once. Emitted so
        # consumers read the routing cutoff as DATA instead of hardcoding 85 in prose that
        # silently diverges the day the SSOT constant changes.
        # `providers` rides alongside the existing keys — window/cached/s_cut/rows keep their
        # names, shapes and meanings, because four callers and 61 pinned tests read them. A
        # consumer that has never heard of providers is unaffected by an extra sibling key.
        print(json.dumps({"window": wj, "cached": cached,
                          "s_cut": cfg["router"]["S_CUT"], "rows": rows,
                          "providers": [probe_provider(p, fresh="--fresh" in args)
                                        for p in load_providers()]}, indent=2))
        return

    if "--login-status" in args:
        # The one fact a dashboard read cannot be relied on to carry, because it is only
        # actionable BEFORE it becomes visible as a failure. The exit code is the answer so a
        # hook or poller can gate on it without parsing, and it reads the shared cache like
        # every other mode — a recurring check must never force an endpoint sweep.
        warn_h = float(cfg.get("login_warn_h", 72.0))
        # --window-h N: the value is read POSITIONALLY, never as a membership test over argv —
        # the same lesson --route learned when `"fable" if "fable" in args` silently handed a
        # caller asking for a frontier account a general one. A malformed value is a USAGE error
        # (64), never a fallback to the default: this whole flag exists because a consumer whose
        # policy is 168h was silently answered at 72h, and a quiet fallback would recreate that
        # exact defect. 64 is outside the 0/1/2 verdict range so `rc <= 2` consumers cannot read
        # it as an answer.
        if "--window-h" in args:
            i = args.index("--window-h")
            raw = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
            try:
                w = float(raw)
                if w < 0:
                    raise ValueError(raw)
                warn_h = w
            except (TypeError, ValueError):
                print(f"claude-accounts: --window-h requires a non-negative number of hours "
                      f"(got {raw!r})", file=sys.stderr)
                sys.exit(64)
        worst = 0
        for r in rows:
            lxh = r.get("login_expires_h")
            if r.get("login_fixable"):
                state = "REQUIRED"
            elif lxh is not None and lxh <= warn_h:
                # <=0 with a still-working auth state: the stamp says the grant is dead but
                # the access token has not been rejected yet. Still REQUIRED — the account is
                # living on borrowed minutes and no refresh will follow.
                state = "REQUIRED" if lxh <= 0 else "EXPIRING"
            else:
                continue
            worst = max(worst, 2 if state == "REQUIRED" else 1)
            # An account can need /login for a reason that is NOT its deadline — next3 was
            # REQUIRED (refresh grant rejected) with 14 days still on its stamp, and printing
            # that stamp beside REQUIRED read as "required, and it expires in 14 days". The
            # deadline columns are therefore filled only when the deadline is what is driving
            # the verdict; otherwise the reason column carries the cause and they read "—".
            reason = ("login-expired" if r.get("login_expired")
                      else r.get("auth") if r.get("login_fixable") else "login-expiry")
            by_deadline = lxh is not None and lxh <= warn_h
            # 🚨 MACHINE FIELDS — normalised ISO and a bare decimal hour count, NEVER the
            # rendered forms a human reads. Those are _fmt_when ("Sun 13:21") and fmt_h
            # ("2d 6h"), and emitting them here made this surface unparseable in the first
            # column and format-coupled in the second: iso_epoch("Sun 13:21") returns 0, so
            # the poller's preferred ISO path was dead by construction and its ONLY working
            # deadline source was a regex over fmt_h's display string — which is why a
            # cosmetic table change had to be matched by an awk edit in another program.
            # A display format must never be an API. Humans read the table; this is for jq,
            # awk and date.
            print("\t".join([r["acct"], state, reason,
                             (_iso_utc(r.get("login_expires_at")) or "?") if by_deadline else "—",
                             f"{lxh:.1f}" if by_deadline else "—",
                             r.get("launcher") or "?"]))
        sys.exit(worst)

    if "--route" in args or "--rank" in args:
        if "--route" in args and "--rank" in args:
            sys.exit("claude-accounts: --route and --rank are mutually exclusive")
        flag = "--route" if "--route" in args else "--rank"
        # Read the flag's VALUE positionally. The old `"fable" if "fable" in args` was a
        # membership test over all of argv, so `--route fabel`, `--route FABLE` and a bare
        # `--route` all fell through to general and exited 0 with a confident account name —
        # a caller asking for a frontier account silently got a general one.
        i = args.index(flag)
        kind = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
        if kind not in ("general", "fable", "interactive"):
            sys.exit(f"claude-accounts: {flag} requires general|fable|interactive (got {kind!r})")
        # `--recovery` is a MODIFIER on the lane, never a fourth lane (U13 4b): a `recovery` KIND
        # would need a recovery-general and a recovery-fable twin, and the doubling is the tell.
        # Under it a target must SURVIVE the session being placed on it, not merely have room for
        # a new one.
        recovery = "--recovery" in args
        if recovery and kind == "interactive":
            # Refused, never silently ignored: the desk lane picks a HUMAN's account, not a host
            # for a transplanted session, and a modifier that reads as applied while doing
            # nothing is the exact failure shape this wave exists to remove.
            sys.exit(f"claude-accounts: --recovery is not defined for {flag} interactive "
                     "(the desk lane hosts no transplanted session)")
        cand, reasons = ranked(rows, cfg, win, kind, recovery=recovery)
        # W2.6 — the desk decision goes on disk BEFORE either exit path, so an abstention is
        # recorded as faithfully as a pick. `--rank` is diagnostic and deliberately silent here:
        # only a `--route` is a decision something acts on.
        if flag == "--route":
            log_route_decision(kind, cand, reasons, cfg, cached)
        if not cand:
            print("none")
            print(f"claude-accounts: no routable account for {kind}: "
                  + "; ".join(f"{a}={w}" for a, w in reasons.items()), file=sys.stderr)
            # exit 3 = data unavailable (callers may degrade to a proxy); exit 2 = data was
            # fine, no account routable by POLICY (exhausted / window / cutoff — callers must
            # NOT fire blind). Classified by REASON, not by the presence of a row error.
            sys.exit(3 if all(reason_class(r, reasons.get(r["acct"])) == "data" for r in rows)
                     else 2)
        # Excluded accounts are named even when a route WAS found. Without this the caller
        # sees a confident winner and no hint that a roomier account was dropped for a
        # TRANSIENT reason (a 90s poll throttle scores null and vanishes) — which reads as
        # "this is the best account" rather than "this is the best account we could see".
        # stderr, so the stdout contract for both flags is untouched.
        if reasons:
            print(f"claude-accounts: {kind} excluded — "
                  + "; ".join(f"{a}={w}" for a, w in reasons.items()), file=sys.stderr)
        # A yielded login-cliff drain is never silent: the caller is told that the account it just
        # received is inside its drain band and was re-admitted only because nothing else was
        # routable. Stdout stays exactly the contract (account name / acct score lines).
        # ROUTE-META (ACCOUNT_ROUTING_V2 M3b): the INPUTS of the decision, in a machine-parseable
        # token, so "did routing read live quota, and did it see the cliff?" is answerable from disk.
        # route.jsonl recorded only the OUTPUT (ts/slot/outcome/detail), which makes the frozen
        # scope's second clause unfalsifiable — the campaign's most-repeated defect.
        # On STDERR, never stdout: the stdout contract is one account name (--route) or `acct score`
        # lines (--rank), and row 5's cc-wave-plan parses stdout BY SHAPE and discards stderr.
        if os.environ.get("CC_ROUTE_RECORD_INPUTS", "on").strip().lower() not in ("off", "0", "false"):
            w = cand[0][1]
            lxh = w.get("login_expires_h")
            age = _quota_age_s(cfg)
            held_sets = repick_held_sets(cand, cfg.get("router"))
            print("route-meta: "
                  + " ".join([f"acct={w['acct']}",
                              f"cliff_band={cliff_band(w)}",
                              f"cliff_h={lxh:.1f}" if isinstance(lxh, (int, float)) else "cliff_h=-",
                              f"cliff_at={w.get('login_expires_at') or '-'}",
                              # quota_as_of is set ONLY on an inherited (stale) row, which the router
                              # excludes — so on a routed account it is absent BY CONSTRUCTION and
                              # `-` is the honest value. The falsifiable freshness number is the age
                              # of the sweep this decision read.
                              f"quota_as_of={w.get('quota_as_of') or '-'}",
                              f"quota_age_s={age if age is not None else '-'}",
                              f"cached={1 if cached else 0}",
                              f"yielded={1 if w.get('cliff_yielded') else 0}",
                              # W6a — whether the SURVIVAL floors were in force for this
                              # decision. Without it a recovery pick and a dispatch pick are
                              # indistinguishable on disk, and "why was this account chosen for
                              # a transplant" is unanswerable after the fact — the same
                              # unfalsifiability route-meta itself exists to close.
                              f"recovery={1 if recovery else 0}",
                              # W1.7 — WHICH INSTRUMENT charged concurrency for this decision.
                              # k_work is None whenever the sweep's walk went over budget, and the
                              # router then silently re-charges the PANE census: measured swing
                              # k_eff 2 -> 7 for one account, i.e. the difference between routable
                              # and kmax-concurrency, with nothing on disk saying the instrument
                              # had changed. `kwork_to` counts over-budget walks in THIS process
                              # (0 on a cache read, which is why k_src is reported too).
                              # k_src() is the ONE derivation (it also picks the cap and the KF
                              # denominator); 'off' is reported separately because the kill switch
                              # is a different fact from the walk having failed.
                              f"k_src={'off' if not _term_on('CC_ROUTE_KWORK') else k_src(w)}",
                              f"k_cap={k_cap(w, cfg['router'])}",
                              f"k_work={w.get('k_work') if w.get('k_work') is not None else '-'}",
                              f"k={w.get('k') if w.get('k') is not None else '-'}",
                              # HOW OLD the pane census is when this decision was charged one it
                              # did not take (k_src=panes-stale). `-` on every measured row, so
                              # the field is silent except where the new policy actually fired.
                              # §6's lesson, applied to the change that closes §4: a policy that
                              # alters which accounts are routable must be answerable from disk,
                              # and the ONE number that evaluates it is the staleness it accepted.
                              f"k_stale_s={_ks if (_ks := _k_stale_s(w)) is not None else '-'}",
                              # D2 — the age of the native census this row carries, charged or
                              # not, so a panes-native decision is answerable from disk too.
                              f"k_native_s={int(_kn) if (_kn := _k_native_s(w)) is not None else '-'}",
                              f"k_eff={k_eff(w)}",
                              f"kwork_to={KWORK_TIMEOUTS}",
                              # §14's re-pick threshold, DECLARED BY THE ROUTER (backlog
                              # 2dc6906b6e0b). handoff-fire's recycle_repick reads this rather than
                              # carrying a constant of its own: a threshold on the M7 score that
                              # lives outside the scorer is free to drift from it silently. Emitted
                              # on every --route/--rank so the consumer never has to ask twice, and
                              # so a decision's own threshold is recoverable from its log line.
                              f"repick_ratio={_rr:g}" if (_rr := repick_ratio(cfg.get('router')))
                              else "repick_ratio=off"]
                             # The dispatch floor's verdict on every THIN candidate, with the
                             # spare pp it was judged on — so "why was (or was not) the account
                             # about to reset picked" is answerable from this line. `-` = no thin
                             # row. One token, no spaces or glob characters: cc-route word-splits
                             # this line unquoted.
                             + (["dispatch_floor=" + (",".join(
                                 f"{r['acct']}:{r['dispatch_floor']}:"
                                 + (f"{r['dispatch_spare_pp']:.1f}"
                                    if _is_num(r.get("dispatch_spare_pp")) else "-")
                                 for _s, r in cand
                                 if r.get("dispatch_floor") not in (None, "roomy")) or "-")]
                                if kind in ("general", "fable") and not recovery else [])
                             # D1 (W15): the HELD set, the subset a recycle LEAVES, the subset
                             # held on an unknown, and the mode — appended AFTER dispatch_floor so
                             # the pinned " repick_ratio=… dispatch_floor=" adjacency survives.
                             # recycle_repick tests membership in these; it owns no verdict name.
                             + ([f"dispatch_held={','.join(held_sets[0]) or '-'}",
                                 f"repick_leave={','.join(held_sets[1]) or '-'}",
                                 f"repick_unknown={','.join(held_sets[2]) or '-'}",
                                 f"repick_held={repick_held_mode(cfg.get('router'))}"]
                                if kind in ("general", "fable") and not recovery else [])
                             # The desk lane's own decision inputs: which rung of the degradation
                             # ladder the winner came from, and whether hysteresis was in play.
                             + ([f"desk_tier={desk_keys(w, cfg)[2]}",
                                 f"desk_incumbent={1 if w.get('desk_incumbent') else 0}",
                                 f"desk_k_eff={k_eff_desk(w)}"] if kind == "interactive" else [])),
                  file=sys.stderr)
        drained = [r["acct"] for _s, r in cand if r.get("cliff_yielded")]
        if drained:
            print(f"claude-accounts: login-cliff drain YIELDED for {', '.join(drained)} — no other "
                  f"account was routable and a drained account still works (the cliff has not "
                  f"passed). Re-login is due: cc-relogin {drained[0]}", file=sys.stderr)
        if flag == "--route":
            print(cand[0][1]["acct"])
        else:
            for s, r in cand:
                print(f"{r['acct']} {s:.6f}")
        return

    # --bank-wall: one TAB-separated line per account at its weekly wall that holds a live grant —
    # `acct<TAB>grant-key<TAB>sentence`. autonomy-sweep pages each grant-key once. Empty = nothing
    # to redeem. Meant to run with --max-wait 0 (cache only), so it never forces a sweep.
    if "--bank-wall" in args:
        for acct, key, text in bank_wall_lines(rows):
            print(f"{acct}\t{key}\t{text}")
        return

    if "--readout" in args:
        # `--narrow` is a MODE on this renderer (W3.6), not a second table. Absent, every byte of
        # this surface is what it was.
        narrow = "--narrow" in args
        provs = (render_providers_brief([probe_provider(p, fresh="--fresh" in args)
                                         for p in load_providers()])
                 if "--no-agents" not in args and not narrow else [])

        def _pane_at(w):
            return readout_lines(rows, cfg, win, cached, pane=w) + [
                x for ln in provs for x in _pane_prose(ln, w, cont="  ")]

        def _pane(cols):
            return _pane_at(pane_width(cols))
        if "--ladder" in args:
            # The width ladder the accounts-readout mod draws from, on stdout (tests, a look).
            print(json.dumps(pane_ladder(_pane_at)))
            return
        if "--pane" in args:
            # The hook-drawn presentation on stdout, for a test or a look at another width:
            # `--cols N` is the PANE's columns (the gutter is taken off here), else the live pane.
            ci = args.index("--cols") + 1 if "--cols" in args else None
            cols = (int(args[ci]) if ci is not None and ci < len(args) and args[ci].isdigit()
                    else pane_cols())
            if not cols:
                print("claude-accounts: --pane: pane width unknown (no CLAUDE_PID tty, no "
                      "CC_PANE_COLS); pass --cols N", file=sys.stderr)
                sys.exit(64)
            print("\n".join(_pane(cols)))
            return
        render_readout(rows, cfg, win, cached, narrow)
        # APPENDED, never interleaved: the Claude table and its router footer render byte-identical
        # to before, then the provider section follows as its own block. An empty or unreadable
        # registry appends nothing, so the canonical readout degrades to exactly its old output.
        # `--no-agents` exists for a caller that wants the historical readout verbatim.
        # Suppressed under --narrow: render_providers builds to its own ~82-col budget, so
        # appending it would breach the board's width on a surface that cannot scroll sideways.
        if provs:
            print("\n".join(provs))
        # INSIDE CLAUDE CODE, ALSO LEAVE THE PANE RENDER FOR THE HOOK (2026-10-18). Same rows, same
        # run, so the coloured copy hooks/relay-verbatim.sh draws can never disagree with the text
        # the model reads. Best-effort by construction: any failure leaves the chat readout as the
        # only one, which is exactly what the hook falls back to (it tells the model to paste).
        sid = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID")
        # THE LADDER TOO (2026-10-19), for mods/accounts-readout: the text above is static, so a
        # pane narrowed after the draw re-wraps it into soup. The mod re-draws on every width
        # change and picks the widest layout here that fits. Written before the pane file, and
        # without needing the pane's width, so the mod has it whatever pane_cols() could read.
        lp = pane_ladder_file(sid)
        if not narrow and lp:
            try:
                os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(lp), exist_ok=True)
                tmp = f"{lp}.{os.getpid()}"
                with open(tmp, "w") as f:
                    json.dump(pane_ladder(_pane_at), f)
                os.replace(tmp, lp)
            except Exception:       # noqa: BLE001 — same contract as the pane file below
                pass
        if not narrow and sid and board_color_on():
            try:
                cols, fp = pane_cols(), pane_file(sid)
                if cols and fp:
                    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(fp), exist_ok=True)
                    tmp = f"{fp}.{os.getpid()}"
                    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
                        f.write("\n".join(_pane(cols)) + "\n")
                    os.replace(tmp, fp)
            except Exception:       # noqa: BLE001 — a convenience copy must never fail the readout
                pass
        return

    # human table
    global COLOR
    COLOR = _color_on(args)
    render_table(rows, cfg, win, cached, prev)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    # A wedged single-flight lock is REPORTED, never silently converted into stale rows, and never a
    # bare traceback. It must be parseable as well as visible: handoff-fire reads `--fresh --json` to
    # decide a wave is safe to fire, so it needs a machine-readable failure rather than — far worse —
    # a plausible-looking table it would read as "fleet healthy". Exit 5 = io/lock, the convention the
    # rest of the toolchain uses.
    #
    # Handled HERE rather than around the get_data call because that call site is a source-patch
    # anchor for tests/claude-accounts-core.bats (see the note there); wrapping it re-indented the
    # line and silently disabled that patch.
    try:
        main()
    except CacheOnlyUnavailable as e:
        # ABSTAIN, reported in each surface's own vocabulary. On --route/--rank stdout carries the
        # answer, and "none" is the value those callers already handle (it is what an unroutable
        # fleet prints), so the launcher needs no new parsing to fall back to its pinned account.
        # The REASON rides route-meta, which cc-route:126-131 already parses — a consumer that
        # wants to distinguish "cache absent" from "policy says nobody" can, and one that does not
        # keeps working unchanged.
        argv = sys.argv[1:]
        if "--json" in argv:
            print(json.dumps({"error": "cache_only_unavailable", "detail": str(e),
                              "rows": [], "cached": False}, indent=2))
        else:
            if "--route" in argv or "--rank" in argv:
                print("none")
            print(f"claude-accounts: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        print("route-meta: cache=absent mode=cache-only waited_ms=0", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(3)
    except FreshLockWedged as e:
        if "--json" in sys.argv[1:]:
            print(json.dumps({"error": "fresh_lock_wedged", "detail": str(e),
                              "rows": [], "cached": False}, indent=2))
        else:
            print(f"claude-accounts: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(5)
